R0002,ผลกระทบจากอาชญากรรมคอมพิวเตอร์: ศึกษาเฉพาะกรณีการพนันทายผลฟุตบอลออนไลน์,"วารสารดุษฎีบัณฑิตทางสังคมศาสตร์, 6, 1",2559,สราวุฒิ ปรีดากรณ์,วารสารดุษฎีบัณฑิตทางสังคมศาสตร์,10.14456/phdssj.2016.17,,https://doi.org/10.14456/phdssj.2016.17,,datacite:Text/Article,datacite,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R0005,A New Revolution in the Gambling Business in India,"Gambling in India is undergoing a radical shift in the digital world that is driven by widespread smartphone adoption, Unified Payments Interface (UPI)-driven payments, the rise of online casinos, and fantasy sports apps.This has moved the gambling activities off the traditional physical sites to easily accessible virtual space.The online gambling market in India is experiencing a robust growth in recent years, which can be explained by the technological changes, young population, and changing consumer behaviour (Grand View Research, 2024; IMARC, 2025;Nishith Desai Associates, 2024).However, the digital revolution brings new issues, such as gambling addiction, regulatory gaps, offshore illegal organizations, and the increased social risks.This paper is a survey of the contemporary condition of gambling revolution in India and how it can be explained, the effects, the complications of the regulation, and policy demands.",2026,,International Research Journal of Modernization in Engineering Technology and Science,10.56726/irjmets85468,,https://doi.org/10.56726/irjmets85468,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0006,Deportes y prácticas democráticas en América Latina,"Tabla de contenidos: Prólogo / Wanderley Marchi Júnior. Prácticas deportivas y sociedad: claves para su comprensión en las coyunturas regionales / Jorge Ricardo Saraví, Alejo Levoratti, Débora Paola Di Domizio, Daniela Negri. Acceso democrático al deporte y al ejercicio físico: la desigualdad social como barrera / Renato Francisco Rodrigues Marques. Ensamblajes socioecológicos para analizar políticas públicas de educación, deporte y salud de una manera ""más que humana"" / Rodrigo Soto-Lagos. El fútbol como territorio de luchas colectivas / Verónica Moreira. Desmontando la ceguera formativa del deporte. El papel de la educación deportiva física escolar / Mariana Sarni. El deporte en la formación superior: de objeto-cosa a objeto-multidimensional situado / Osvaldo Omar Ron. Políticas públicas en los países de América Latina: convergencias y divergencias / Fernando Augusto Starepravo. Deportes, prácticas democráticas y sociedad en México / Oswaldo Gurrola. Enfoque de investigación en las prácticas y subjetividad deportiva / Gabriel Armando Cachorro. Miradas antropológicas sobre los deportes y las prácticas deportivas en Brasil / Mariane da Silva Pisani. Educación física, identidades transgénero, intersexual y no binaria en los deportes / Lorena Irene Berdula. El deporte y la competencia deportiva como arena pública. Anotaciones para el debate sobre la relación deporte, competencia y ética / Liber Benítez. Entre la investigación y la extensión. Debates emergentes en los estudios sociales del deporte / Julia Hang. Apuntes para pensarlos diálogos entre universidad-territorio-feminismo en Uruguay: el caso del fútbol femenino / Martina Pastorino. Deporte y literatura: líneas de fuga para la resistencia y subversión de los cuerpos / Daniel Zambaglione. La investigación como recurso para desvelar y aprender sobre nuestra sociedad en la relación entre el deporte, los medios de comunicación y las tecnologías / Cristiano Mezzaroba. Diversión, riesgo y dinero: jóvenes y apuestas deportivas on line en Argentina / Juan Branz. ¿Tecnologías para qué? / Nemesia Hijós. El deporte en la Red Social Global: perspectivas del Sur y el compromiso de la Sociología Pública / Billy Graeff. Deporte y progresismo en el Uruguay. Entre las políticas de alto rendimiento y los deportistas clasificados a Tokio 2020+1 / Bruno Mora. Producción y trasmisión de conocimientos en los estudios sociales del deporte. Enfoques, saberes, instituciones y especialistas / Pablo Scharagrodsky..",2026,,"Memoria Académica (Facultad de Humanidades y Ciencias de la Educación, Universidad Nacional de La Plata)",,,,es,openalex_non_en:book,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0007,Do Sports Bettors Remember Their Bets? The Role of Problem Gambling in Bet Recall,,2026,,Journal of Gambling Issues,10.4309/ikva7818,,https://doi.org/10.4309/ikva7818,,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0008,Evaluating the Psychometric Properties of the Sports Betting Motivations Scale – Revised (SBMS-R) in Canadian Sports Bettors,,2026,,Journal of Gambling Issues,10.4309/wkwq5305,,https://doi.org/10.4309/wkwq5305,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0009,In Case You Haven't Heard…,"Gambling addiction is not just due to sports anymore. The so‐called “prediction market” is now open for bets on anything from the military operation in Venezuela leading to the arrest of President Nicolas Maduro, to guessing Google's most‐searched terms. Whatever it is, the gambler loses and the industry — now more commonly a website and not a casino with distractions like entertainment and fine dining — wins. Sports betting still accounts for the majority of transactions on prediction websites Kalshi and Polymarket, according to a 2025 survey from Sacred Heart University , which found that 6 in 10 Americans are concerned about the rising prevalence of sports betting in particular, which accounts for most transactions on the prediction sites. That gambling is addictive is not a question. Just because some people gamble and don't become addicted doesn't prove anything, any more than the fact that some people drink without becoming addicted proves that alcohol is not addictive. As with alcohol, the vast majority of money spent on gambling comes from the minority of people who are addicted — and that minority is growing. Last year, the American Psychiatric Association found that 28% of people in the United States gamble at least once a day.",2026,,Alcoholism & Drug Abuse Weekly,10.1002/adaw.34792,,https://doi.org/10.1002/adaw.34792,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article,openalex;openaire,2,False,False,False,,R0010;R0011;R0012;R0013;R3237,,,
R0010,In Case You Haven't Heard…,"Last month the Heather Maurer, executive director of the National Council on Problem Gambling resigned after taking $2 million from Kalshi, a prediction market. The problems of gambling addiction and problem gambling have been growing with online sports betting and prediction markets. With no money from the federal government for problem gambling, and a growing commercial investment in gambling, it's hard to see how young people will have a treatment or even a respite from the lure. A year ago FanDuel signed gambling addiction advocate, until then NCPG, chief, Keith Whyte.",2026,,Alcoholism & Drug Abuse Weekly,10.1002/adaw.35086,,https://doi.org/10.1002/adaw.35086,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0009;R0011;R0012;R0013;R3237,,,
R0011,In Case You Haven't Heard…,"On a recent weekend in a popular college town we were surprised by the large number of restaurants, pizza places, cafes and other spots catering to students which offered alcohol. Basically, all of them did. We asked a student who had recently returned to the town after summer break, how were underage patrons able to drink? “Fake IDs” she responded immediately. Oh. Great. We didn't bother to ask about the cannabis dispensary, but we have a feeling fake IDs won't work there. So we looked it up. In fact, fake IDs don't work well in cannabis dispensaries, certainly not legal dispensaries, and not ones located in New York. But there are other places that sell so‐called cannabis, such as gas stations. Still, what is apparently legal for underage users: online sports betting. In New York, the attorney general is trying to put a stop to that (see story, page 4). Maybe it's time to write yet another story on underage drinking.",2026,,Alcoholism & Drug Abuse Weekly,10.1002/adaw.35015,,https://doi.org/10.1002/adaw.35015,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0009;R0010;R0012;R0013;R3237,,,
R0012,In Case You Haven't Heard…,"The legal battle to label Facebook and other social media sites as addictive in a court of law continues. Last week, the Ninth U.S. Circuit Court of Appeals rejected an appeal by the named social media sites, who were seeking to overturn a lower court ruling that said the companies had appealed too early. Many parents, school districts and others have claimed that the sites' business goals were aimed at targeting and addicting children, and they have demanded restitution. Also in contention as addictive are speculative online businesses such as predictive markets, online sports betting and other gambling sites. Whether money or public health will win out is anyone's guess, but some public officials are doing their best to fight for health. Meanwhile, gambling addiction has legitimate treatments. Social media use has been normalized already beyond what could have been imagined. It's now up to teachers and parents to help prevent social media use problems experienced by young people. The rest of us have to turn off our phones and set a good example, until the experts come up with a better solution.",2026,,Alcoholism & Drug Abuse Weekly,10.1002/adaw.35023,,https://doi.org/10.1002/adaw.35023,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0009;R0010;R0011;R0013;R3237,,,
R0013,In Case You Haven't Heard…,"A lawsuit filed last week accused FanDuel and DraftKings of using data supplied by the NFL to offer a “known addictive product,” ESPN reported. In addition, both sportsbooks were charged with using VIP hosts to encourage two plaintiffs to keep betting, eventually leading to gambling disorders and financial losses. Also named in the suit are the NFL and its data partner, Genius Sports, in addition to five sportsbook employees who were assigned as VIP hosts to plaintiffs Christopher Sage and Terry Thompson. According to ESPN, the complaint alleges that DraftKings and FanDuel used the NFL's live data feed to offer in‐game microbetting, a form of gambling believed to be more addictive. “Attorneys for Sage and Thompson say the two Pennsylvania men developed severe gambling disorders and lost more than $2 million combined to the sportsbooks over several years,” according to ESPN. Both men had gambled without any problems for almost two decades, according to the attorneys. “Within just a few years of placing their first microbets on the sportsbook apps, Plaintiffs nearly lost everything,” according to ESPN. “Sage placed himself on Pennsylvania's self‐exclusion gambling list on March 15, 2025, the same month he was formally diagnosed with a gambling addiction disorder, but continued to receive messages from his DraftKings VIP host, according to the lawsuit.”",2026,,Alcoholism & Drug Abuse Weekly,10.1002/adaw.34862,,https://doi.org/10.1002/adaw.34862,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0009;R0010;R0011;R0012;R3237,,,
R0014,Störungen durch Substanzgebrauch oder Verhaltenssüchte,,2026,,Elsevier eBooks,10.1016/b978-3-437-21237-6.00019-3,,https://doi.org/10.1016/b978-3-437-21237-6.00019-3,en,citations:book-chapter,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0015,The impact of bookmakers’ sports sponsorship on the image of football clubs,"Objective: The aim of this article is to determine the impact of sports betting sponsorship on the image of football clubs.The study seeks to answer the question of whether greater reputational benefits are achieved by the clubs themselves or by providers of betting services.An additional objective is to diagnose fans' attitudes toward betting on sports events.Design/Methodology/Approach: The article examines the attitudes of football supporters toward sponsors and sponsored clubs.Data were collected in spring 2024 using an online survey method (CAWI) among 165 football supporters (purposive sampling).From this group, 66 respondents who had ever placed sports bets were ultimately selected for the analysis of bookmakers' influence on the image of football clubs. Findings:The study demonstrated the existence of a social stigma associated with betting services, mainly due to concerns about gambling and past scandals.Respondents' close social circles often view betting negatively, suggesting a need for educational campaigns promoting responsible gambling.The betting sector should emphasize player responsibility and autonomy.Innovations in betting applications, such as interactive in-play wagering features, may increase user engagement.Fans are more inclined to change bookmakers if a bookmaker begins sponsoring their favorite club than if it becomes a partner of a rival team.Sponsorship should therefore be considered in terms of both economic benefits and potential reputational risks.While sponsorship does not directly translate into an increased number of bets, it supports bookmakers' brand image among fans.An in-depth analysis was also carried out of the sponsorship relationship between Lech Poznań and Superbet.This cooperation yields moderate benefits for both parties.Positive fan attitudes toward sponsors may generate long-term financial benefits and brand trust, which are crucial for future success in the betting industry.Research limitations: Despite efforts to obtain a representative sample of fans from clubs across the entire league, the study exhibited an overrepresentation of Lech Poznań supporters.Fan communities of other Ekstraklasa clubs were underrepresented, which requires cautious generalization of the findings.Future research should extend the data collection period to ensure adequate representation of all supporter communities within the league. Originality/Value:The study attempts to evaluate the sponsorship relationships of all football clubs in a given league during a single competitive season that were sponsored by bookmakers.The overall assessment of perceptions of relationships between bookmakers and sponsored clubs was enriched by an in-depth analysis of the partnership between KKS Lech Poznań and Superbet.",2026,,Scientific Papers of Silesian University of Technology Organization and Management Series,10.29119/1641-3466.2026.247.1,,https://doi.org/10.29119/1641-3466.2026.247.1,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0016,Understanding Gambling Culture and the Normalisation of Sports Betting in Elite Female Sports Clubs,,2026,,Journal of Gambling Issues,10.4309/lmne7799,,https://doi.org/10.4309/lmne7799,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0023,Motivational Factors Influencing Sports Betting Behavior of Dual Career Upper Secondary School Students in Iceland : a Qualitative Study Based on The Theory of Planned Behavior,"Sports betting is gaining more popularity around the world and that also applies to Iceland, as it has one of the highest participation rates in Europe. Young people are participating at higher rates than before and research indicates that sports betting may be more normalized among athletes. This study examines how attitude, subjective norms, and perceived behavioral control influences sports betting behavior among Icelandic upper secondary dual career students, using The Theory of Planned Behavior as a framework. Focus group interviews were used to collect data, and it was analyzed using thematic analysis. The sample consisted of 14 participants, divided into two gendered focus groups. The results revealed three themes revolving around psychological factors, social influences and perceived control over outcomes that may affect sports betting participation. Findings suggest that the TPB could be a useful framework and that a positive attitude towards sports betting, stronger subjective norms and higher perceived behavioral control may increase sports betting participation. This study provides a better understanding of factors that motivate sports betting behavior among dual career students in Iceland. The result could help in the development of prevention efforts to lower participation rates of young people participating in sports betting in Iceland. Keywords: Sports betting, The Theory of Planned Behavior, attitude, subjective norms, perceived behavioral control, athletes.",2026,2002-,Skemman,,,,en,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0024,1503 - Schema on the Inside: A Two-Phase Fine-Tuning Method for High-Efficiency Text-to-SQL at Scale,"Applying large, proprietary API-based language models to text-to-SQL tasks poses a significant industry challenge: reliance on massive, schema-heavy prompts results in prohibitive per-token API costs and high latency, hindering scalable production deployment. We present a specialized, self-hosted 8B-parameter model designed for a conversational bot in CriQ, a sister app to Dream11—India’s largest fantasy sports platform with over 250 million users—that answers user queries about cricket statistics. Our novel two-phase supervised fine-tuning approach enables the model to internalize the entire database schema, eliminating the need for long-context prompts. This reduces input tokens by over 99%, from a 17k-token baseline to fewer than 100, and replaces costly external API calls with efficient local inference. The resulting system achieves 98.4% execution success and 92.5% semantic accuracy, substantially outperforming a prompt-engineered baseline using Google’s Gemini Flash 2.0 (95.6% execution, 89.4% semantic accuracy). These results demonstrate a practical path toward high-precision, low-latency text-to-SQL applications using domain-specialized, self-hosted language models in large-scale production environments.",2026,2026,Underline Science Inc.,10.48448/6mth-0z77,,https://doi.org/10.48448/6mth-0z77,,datacite:Audiovisual/Conference talk | openalex:other,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0025,1508 - Driving Engagement in Daily Fantasy Sports with a Scalable and Urgency-Aware Ranking Engine,"In daily fantasy sports (DFS), match participation is highly time-sensitive. Users must act within a narrow window before a game begins, making match recommendation a time-critical task to prevent missed engagement and revenue loss. Existing recommender systems, typically designed for static item catalogs, are ill-equipped to handle the hard temporal deadlines inherent in these live events. To address this, we designed and deployed a recommendation engine using the Deep Interest Network (DIN) architecture. We adapt the DIN architecture by injecting temporality at two levels: first, through real-time urgency features for each candidate match (e.g., time-to-round-lock), and second, via temporal positional encodings that represent the time-gap between each historical interaction and the current recommendation request, allowing the model to dynamically weigh the recency of past actions. This approach, combined with a listwise neuralNDCG loss function, produces highly relevant and urgency-aware rankings. To support this at industrial scale, we developed a multi-node, multi-GPU training architecture on Ray and PyTorch. Our system, validated on a massive industrial dataset with over 650k users and over 100B interactions, achieves a +9% lift in nDCG@1 over a heavily optimized LightGBM baseline with handcrafted features. The strong offline performance of this model establishes its viability as a core component for our planned on-device (edge) recommendation system, where online A/B testing will be conducted.",2026,2026,Underline Science Inc.,10.48448/e0vv-0370,,https://doi.org/10.48448/e0vv-0370,,datacite:Audiovisual/Conference talk | openalex:other,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0027,Driving Engagement in Daily Fantasy Sports with a Scalable and Urgency-Aware Ranking Engine,"In daily fantasy sports (DFS), match participation is highly time-sensitive. Users must act within a narrow window before a game begins, making match recommendation a time-critical task to prevent missed engagement and revenue loss. Existing recommender systems, typically designed for static item catalogs, are ill-equipped to handle the hard temporal deadlines inherent in these live events. To address this, we designed and deployed a recommendation engine using the Deep Interest Network (DIN) architecture. We adapt the DIN architecture by injecting temporality at two levels: first, through real-time urgency features for each candidate match (e.g., time-to-round-lock), and second, via temporal positional encodings that represent the time-gap between each historical interaction and the current recommendation request, allowing the model to dynamically weigh the recency of past actions. This approach, combined with a listwise neuralNDCG loss function, produces highly relevant and urgency-aware rankings. To support this at industrial scale, we developed a multi-node, multi-GPU training architecture on Ray and PyTorch. Our system, validated on a massive industrial dataset with over 650k users and over 100B interactions, achieves a +9\% lift in nDCG@1 over a heavily optimized LightGBM baseline with handcrafted features. The strong offline performance of this model establishes its viability as a core component for our planned on-device (edge) recommendation system, where online A/B testing will be conducted.",2026,2026,Underline Science Inc.,10.48448/hgt5-0g70,,https://doi.org/10.48448/hgt5-0g70,en,crossref:journal-article | datacite:Audiovisual/Conference talk | datacite:Preprint/Article | openaire:Article; Conference object; Preprint | openalex:conference-paper | openalex:other | openalex:preprint | s2:JournalArticle;Conference,openalex;s2;crossref;datacite;openaire,5,False,False,False,,,,,
R0029,Dataset Generation Framework Guided by the Online Gambling Disorder Questionnaire,"Gambling disorder is a public health concern, but research on the early detection of gambling-related harm is limited by the scarcity of ethically shareable online conversations. This study presents a framework for generating an entirely synthetic dataset for natural language processing research on gambling-related behavioral signals. Behavioral dimensions from the Online Gambling Disorder Questionnaire were used to construct 2100 risk-aligned user profiles and generate X/Twitter-style monologues and multi-user threads across gambling and non-gambling subtopics. The dataset contains 35,346 monologues and 1716 threads. The generated text was evaluated using automatic metrics, manual target-consistency assessment, and downstream classification under user-disjoint and parent-topic-disjoint protocols. Automatic and manual evaluations indicated acceptable linguistic quality and consistency with predefined behavioral targets. In the stricter parent-topic-disjoint setting, logistic regression achieved a macro-F1 of 0.884 for binary gambling-content detection, whereas four-level risk prediction remained more difficult. These results show that the dataset contains learnable signals for computational detection of gambling-related content without exposing real-user data. However, the dataset was not externally validated against authentic social-media conversations and is not intended for clinical diagnosis. The resulting resource is designed to support reproducible research on gambling disorder and mental health while preserving user privacy.",2026,Abdulgafer,Electronics,10.3390/electronics15163644,,https://doi.org/10.3390/electronics15163644,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0030,"Odds, Ends, and Empty Wallets: The Rise of Sports Betting in Cameroon and the Hidden Costs for Low-Income Families","This study explores the determinants of betting behavior in Cameroon, focusing on the socio-economic, technological, and cultural influences. Using data from 650 respondents collected via structured questionnaires, a binary logistic regression model was employed to examine the impact of key predictors such as unemployment, low entry cost, technological access, peer influence, popularity of football, entertainment value, youth-driven market, and urban concentration on betting decisions. The control variables used in the study included socioeconomic status, education level, income, and gender that were incorporated to refine the analysis. Findings indicate that low entry cost, technological access, peer influence, youth-driven market dynamics, and urban concentration significantly increase the likelihood of betting participation. In contrast, unemployment, football popularity, and entertainment value were not statistically significant. The model demonstrated a low explanatory power with a Nagelkerke R2 of 0.19, and the survey instrument showed strong internal consistency with the Cronbach’s alpha with a value of 0.81. Multicollinearity diagnostics confirmed the reliability of the predictors with a Variance inflation factor that is less than 2. These results suggest that betting behavior in Cameroon is primarily driven by affordability, digital accessibility, social dynamics, and urban youth culture, rather than economic hardship or for recreational motives. The study recommends the use of targeted policy interventions, digital literacy initiatives, and youth engagement programs to promote responsible betting and mitigate associated risks.",2026,Abenwi,Modern Economy,10.4236/me.2026.173028,,https://doi.org/10.4236/me.2026.173028,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0031,SPORT BETTING ADVERTISMENT AND ATTITUDE OF CHRISLAND UNIVERSITY FEMALE STUDENTS TOWARDS ONLINE GAMBLING,"Gambling has over the years been seen as a means to obtain quick money in such a time where economic growth has dwindled. Similarly, gambling was previously frowned at in the stone days but the advent of technology and several betting platforms available has given room for different persons and social strata of the society to participate without flinching as it calls for concerns that custodians of knowledge and societal morals are at the fore of this decadence with the expectation of winning exorbitant funds in exchange for using minute sum of money to stake the odds in the betting pool. In light of the aforementioned, the study examined sport advertisement and attitude of female Chrisland University students on online gambling. The theoretical tenets of agenda setting and information processing theories were the basis which the study was hinged upon thereby reviewing relevant concepts and propelled by empirical facts from literature. The survey research design was adopted as the study’s methodology thereby 933 female students of Chrisland University were the population of the study as 280 female students were proportionately drawn from the departments in the colleges of arts, management sciences(CAMASS), college of law(COLAW), college of basic medical and applied science(COBAMS) and college of natural applied science(CONAS). The findings of this study revealed that 48.9% of the respondents agree that sports betting is a form of entertainment while 51.8% of the respondents strongly agreed that sports betting possess potential risks associated with online gambling. The study recommends that regulatory bodies should introduce stricter guidelines for sports betting advertisement, also educational institutions and parents should monitor their ward’s exposure to sports betting advertisement and online gambling activities.",2026,ADENUSI,Journal of Humanities and Social Science,10.70382/bjhss.v10i6.065,,https://doi.org/10.70382/bjhss.v10i6.065,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0032,"Psychometric analysis of brief adolescent gambling screen, problem gambling severity index, and gambling motives questionnaire-financial among Ghanaian adolescents","The purpose of this study was to evaluate the psychometric properties of the Brief Adolescent Sport Betting Screen (BASBS), Problem Sport Betting Severity Index (PSBSI), and Sport Betting Motives Questionnaire–Financial (SBMQF) among Ghanaian adolescents. A cross-sectional survey was conducted among 514 adolescents (352 males and 162 females) recruited from six public senior high schools in Accra, Ghana. Confirmatory factor analysis and multigroup confirmatory factor analysis were performed to examine the factorial validity, internal consistency, measurement invariance across sex, and convergent validity of the three instruments. The PSBSI demonstrated a one-factor structure, whereas the SBMQF supported the hypothesised four-factor structure. Both instruments exhibited satisfactory internal consistency, measurement invariance across sex, and expected convergent validity, supporting their suitability for assessing sports betting severity and betting motives among Ghanaian adolescents. Although the BASBS demonstrated an acceptable one-factor structure, it showed lower internal consistency, lack of full measurement invariance, and unexpected convergent validity, indicating that further cultural adaptation and validation are required. These findings provide initial psychometric evidence supporting the use of the PSBSI and SBMQF in adolescent sports betting research, school-based screening, and prevention programmes in Ghana.",2026,Adjorlolo,Discover Mental Health,10.1007/s44192-026-00568-4,,https://doi.org/10.1007/s44192-026-00568-4,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0033,Current Trends in Religious Studies in Nigeria (Volume Three),"Volume Three of Current Trends in Religious Studies in Nigeria presents a compelling portrait of religion as a dynamic and contested public reality. The chapters assembled here move across the everyday practices of youth, workers, families, worshippers, and citizens, showing that faith is always negotiated within concrete social, economic, political, and technological contexts. Whether the discussion concerns sports betting and Christian ethics, digital worship, interfaith dialogue, climate justice, or the re-emergence of indigenous religious identity, the recurring insight is clear: religion in Nigeria cannot be studied in abstraction from lived experience. The volume also reveals that Nigerian religious life is deeply plural and deeply consequential. Christianity, Islam, and African Traditional Religion do not exist in isolation; they interact, compete, borrow, provoke, and coexist in ways that shape public life. Several chapters demonstrate that misunderstanding, institutional failure, and social exclusion often intensify conflict, while dialogue, accountability, contextual preaching, and ethical leadership create openings for peace and mutual recognition. In this sense, the volume insists that religion is not only a source of meaning, but also a site of civic responsibility. A striking feature of this collection is its refusal to treat modernity as a simple threat to faith. Instead, it engages modernity critically and constructively. The chapters on digital religion, artificial intelligence, media framing, youth culture, and online worship show that technology is now part of the terrain of religious formation and expression. At the same time, the ethical questions raised by these developments remind us that innovation without discernment can erode community, distort identity, and deepen vulnerability. The task before scholars and religious leaders is therefore not to reject the digital age, but to interpret it wisely and guide it toward human flourishing. The volume likewise brings to the foreground the importance of culture, memory, and context in theological and historical interpretation. Chapters on African Traditional Religion, oral tradition, theatre, historiography, and missional frameworks demonstrate that African knowledge systems are not peripheral to religious studies; they are foundational. Any attempt to understand Nigerian Christianity, Islam, or indigenous religion must reckon with the cultural worlds in which these traditions are received, adapted, resisted, and reimagined. This is especially important in a post-colonial setting where questions of authority, identity, and decolonization remain unresolved. Several chapters also press home a vital moral concern: religion must speak to development, justice, and human dignity. Economic life, education, entrepreneurship, family stability, and public governance are all shaped by religious values and religious failures. The volume repeatedly asks whether faith communities are cultivating integrity, accountability, compassion, and social responsibility, or whether they are merely reproducing inequality, silence, and dependency. In this regard, the collection offers not only analysis but also a challenge to churches, mosques, temples, and scholars to embody the ethical claims they profess. Another important thread running through the volume is the vulnerability of the human person. Chapters on marital abuse, prosperity theology, fertility, parenting, and youth migration remind us that religious discourse can either heal or harm. When theology becomes detached from lived pain, it may legitimize suffering; when it is rooted in wisdom and empathy, it can become a source of restoration. The volume therefore calls for religious scholarship that is attentive to psychosocial burden, generational change, and the fragile realities of ordinary people. Taken together, the chapters in this volume demonstrate the richness, urgency, and relevance of religious studies in Nigeria today. They show that the field must be interdisciplinary, historically grounded, ethically serious, and socially engaged. If there is a single lesson that emerges from this collection, it is that religion remains one of the most powerful interpretive and transformative forces in Nigerian life, and therefore deserves scholarship that is both critical and constructive. May this volume inspire further research, deeper dialogue, and more responsible public engagement. And may it encourage scholars, clergy, policymakers, and communities to pursue a religious life that is intellectually rigorous, socially just, culturally rooted, and genuinely life-giving. Adebayo Ola AFOLARANMI, PhD Department of Religious Studies and Intercultural Studies, Faculty of Arts, Lead City University, Ibadan, Nigeria",2026,Afolaranmi,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.21628672,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21628672,und,datacite:Book/ | openaire:Book | openalex:book,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0034,Hierarchical Contextual Uplift Bandits for Catalog Personalization,"Contextual Bandit (CB) algorithms are widely adopted for personalized recommendations but often struggle in dynamic environments typical of fantasy sports, where rapid changes in user behavior and dramatic shifts in reward distributions due to external influences necessitate frequent retraining. To address these challenges, we propose a Hierarchical Contextual Uplift Bandit framework. Our framework dynamically adjusts contextual granularity from broad, system-wide insights to detailed, user-specific contexts, using contextual similarity to facilitate effective policy transfer and mitigate cold-start issues. Additionally, we integrate uplift modeling principles into our approach. Results from large-scale A/B testing on the Dream11 fantasy sports platform show that our method significantly enhances recommendation quality, achieving a 0.4% revenue improvement while also improving user satisfaction metrics compared to the current production system. We subsequently deployed this system to production as the default catalog personalization system in May 2025 and observed a further 0.5% revenue improvement.",2026,Agrawal,arXiv (Cornell University),10.48550/arxiv.2601.14333,,https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2601.14333,und,datacite:Preprint/Article | openaire:Article; Preprint | openalex:preprint | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;datacite;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0035,The gamblification of digital leisure: The introduction of gambling among new publics,"In recent years, the internet and digitalization have propelled a phenomenon of ‘gamblification’ across various sectors. This event extends beyond traditional areas like sports betting, introducing gambling elements into new domains such as video games and streaming platforms. The rise of loot boxes in online games exemplifies this trend, merging gambling mechanics with gaming. Not only does this process spread activities that may lead to problematic behaviors, but also normalizes gambling in contexts not typically associated with it, exposing individuals to a broader range of potential harms. Our study explores the ‘gamblification’ and normalization of gambling in digital leisure by examining the correlation between online and land-based gambling, electronic device-based leisure activities, and online sports betting with other kinds of online gambling. Utilizing data from a 2022 survey of the Spanish population (n = 1429), we identify a segmentation and introduction of gambling to new audiences who do not engage in traditional gambling ways. These findings, which address the evolving landscape of gambling in the digital age, have significant implications for the development of public prevention and intervention policies.",2026,Aguerri,Journal of Consumer Culture,10.1177/14695405261440730,,https://doi.org/10.1177/14695405261440730,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0036,Social Acceptance of Sportsbooks and their Influence on the Ecuadorian Market,,2026,Aguilar,Lecture notes in networks and systems,10.1007/978-3-032-09077-5_21,,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-09077-5_21,en,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0037,A narrative review of school-based preventive interventions on mental disorders and addictions,"In recent years, there has been considerable concern about the deterioration of mental health in children and adolescents, and there is a coincidence between mental disorders and addictive disorders. In view of this situation, it is important to review preventive interventions from the educational field, since it is considered an area of socialization par excellence of the subjects that can avoid or reduce exposure to adversities at an early age. For this purpose, a narrative review was carried out. The databases PubMed, PsycINFO and ERIC were searched. A total of 2096 records were retrieved from which 16 studies were selected. Most of the interventions reviewed adopted a universal approach. Most of the articles reviewed refer to interventions around 13-15 years of age and, in general terms, no specific actions are specified by groups according to their needs or risks. The intervention is usually applied by teachers in collaboration with other health experts. The focus is mainly on depression, anxiety, alcohol, cannabis and tobacco, but mental health is also promoted with reinforcement of emotional competence and training in social skills. Also, there is a scarce presence of programs on non-substance addictions and long-term follow-up studies. Based on what has been studied in this research, preventive initiatives are at a point of unquestionable reflection that requires redirecting efforts based on solid scientific evidence that determines approaches adjusted to the realities of the target populations and that helps to make decisions on when to carry out universal or specific interventions.",2026,Aguilar-Moya,Discover Mental Health,10.1007/s44192-026-00532-2,42839111,https://doi.org/10.1007/s44192-026-00532-2,en,citations:review,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0038,"Student Gambling Behaviour: A Comprehensive Review of Motivational Drivers, Risk Determinants, and Regulatory Frameworks","Purpose: Student gambling has become a significant public health concern, with rising prevalence and increasing vulnerability to associated harms among young people. This paper presents a state-of-the-art review of the literature on the motivations, risk factors, and policy and regulatory measures related to student gambling. Design/Methodology/Approach: This study employed a mixed-methods approach combining bibliometric analysis and qualitative content analysis of 112 peer-reviewed articles retrieved from Scopus, Web of Science, IEEE Xplore, and ScienceDirect, following the PRISMA protocol. Research Limitation: The review is restricted to English-language peer-reviewed journal articles retrieved from four databases, which may exclude relevant grey literature, non-English scholarship, and emerging regional research. Findings: Existing research predominantly focuses on sociological and psychological dimensions of gambling. Four major motivations for student gambling were identified: financial pressures, fun and excitement, the pursuit of status or easy money, and peer influence. Key risk factors include social and environmental determinants, economic and socioeconomic vulnerabilities, accessibility and exposure to gambling platforms, and student-specific situational and contextual conditions. The review also highlights several policy and regulatory responses, including evolving legal frameworks, controls on advertising and access, institutional governance strategies, and lessons from international regulatory models. Practical Implication: The findings inform universities and policymakers to embed gambling awareness and financial literacy into student curricula, counselling services, and orientation programmes. It also strengthens digital age-verification systems and restricts gambling advertising near campuses. Social Implication: Addressing peer-driven normalisation of gambling and providing targeted support for financially vulnerable and socially isolated students can reduce disproportionate harm within higher education communities. Originality/Value: This study contributes to the broader gambling research landscape by providing a structured global synthesis of scholarly trends, thematic developments, and research gaps.",2026,Agyekum,African Journal Of Applied Research,10.26437/b903xw43,,https://doi.org/10.26437/b903xw43,,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0039,Metacognitive beliefs and addictions: A systematic review and meta-analysis with emphasis on the mediating role of desire thinking,,2026,Akbari,Addictive Behaviors,10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108613,41579781,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108613,en,citations:review,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0040,"Matraquage médiatique, addiction et risques sanitaires liés aux paris sportifs en ligne à Abidjan (Côte d’Ivoire): Media masking, addiction and health risks linked to online sports betting in Abidjan (Ivory Coast)","Étrange en apparence qu’une étude scientifique de se pencher sur la question des paris sportifs en ligne et de l’engouement qui s’est développement autour ces dernières années. Certainement, le contexte, les réformes et le déploiement du numérique y ont contribué. Nul besoin de se déplacer désormais pour être acteurs majeurs d’une compétition. Entre campagnes de communication et la présence des réseaux sociaux numériques, l’habitude de se frotter à ces jeux a fini par s’installer dans le quotidien des populations. Selon une étude le nombre de parieurs est déjà à plus de 54% en Afrique subsaharienne1 , il est important de se poser les questions suivantes à savoir : comment les médias impactent-ils la pratique des paris sportif numériques ? Quels est le niveau de pratique de ces jeux ? Les populations connaissent-elles les risques sanitaires liés à l’addiction aux paris sportif ? Il ressort de notre étude menée au travers d’entretiens semi-directifs et de questionnaires que les campagnes de communication accrues autour des paris sportifs développent une addiction chez les populations ; la fréquence de paris est très élevée eu égard aux messages diffusés excessivement sur les médias, les populations dans leur majorité ignorent qu’ils existent des risques sanitaires liés à l’addiction aux paris sportifs en ligne. It may seem strange that a scientific study would examine the issue of online sports betting and the craze that has developed around it in recent years. Certainly, the context, reforms, and the rollout of digital technology have contributed to this. Nowadays, there is no need to travel to be a major player in a competition. Between advertising campaigns and the presence of digital social networks, the habit of engaging in these games has become part of everyday life for many people. According to a study, the number of bettors is already over 54% in sub-Saharan Africa, so it is important to ask the following questions: How do media impact the practice of digital sports betting? How widespread is the practice of these games? Are people aware of the health risks associated with sports betting addiction? Our study, conducted through semi-structured interviews and questionnaires, shows that increased advertising campaigns for sports betting are creating addiction among the population. The frequency of betting is very high due to excessive advertising in the media, and the majority of the population is unaware of the health risks associated with online sports betting addiction.",2026,Ake,AKIRI (Revue des sciences humaines et sociales lettres langues et civilisations),,,https://www.ajol.info/index.php/akiri/article/view/318100,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex;openalex_non_en;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0041,"Sports Betting and Christian Youth in Nigeria: Emerging Trends, Ethical Tensions and Ecclesial Responses",,2026,AKERELE,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.21990761,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21990761,,datacite:BookChapter/ | openaire:Part of book or chapter of book | openalex:book-chapter,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0042,"Technology and Gambling: A Comprehensive Analysis of Social, Ethical, Religious and Moral Decadence Caused by Cyber Activities","The emergence of technology has given rise to multifaceted moral, ethical, and other ramifications, many of which remain inadequately comprehended. This study aims to provide a comprehensive analysis of the multifaceted impact of technology-driven gambling on societal ethics and individual well-being. Through systematic exploration of databases (PubMed, IEEE, ScienceDirect, Google Scholar, JSTOR), a discernible gap in the literature on technology and gambling was identified. The ongoing technological revolution necessitated an in-depth exploration of its ethical dimensions. Key methodologies included purposive sampling for diverse perspectives, collaborative engagements for enhanced diversity, critical appraisal for significance, and thematic synthesis for a nuanced understanding. The results of the study showed that the increased use of the internet by a large number of individuals to perform activities like gambling and social networking has led to cyber decadence and made it easy for cybercriminals to perform malicious activities such as stealing confidential information, such as credit care details form organisations and use for their own benefit. The key themes discussed in the results section include cyber decadence, statistics in gambling, effects of technology on gambling, and effects of gambling addictions on our society. The main finding of this study is that the persistent expansion of technology and gambling is the main cause of ethical, Biblical, and moral decadence in society and globally. Technology has resulted in various immoral behaviours and activities such as cybercrime, cyber-bullying, identity theft, and social media scams, particularly impacting sports betting. In conclusion, the ethical implications of gambling were expounded, highlighting potential violations of responsible stewardship principles. Future research is encouraged to delve deeper into the evolving landscape of technology-driven gambling, exploring emerging challenges and refining mitigation strategies. There is a need for ongoing discourse on responsible technology use, ethical considerations in gambling, and the necessity for regulatory frameworks prioritising societal well-being.",2026,Akhonya,Journal of Science Innovation and Creativity,10.58721/jsic.v5i1.1488,,https://doi.org/10.58721/jsic.v5i1.1488,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0043,"Gendered motivations and behaviour in online sports betting: perspectives from university students in Kumasi, Ghana",,2026,Akite,SN Social Sciences,10.1007/s43545-026-01348-1,,https://doi.org/10.1007/s43545-026-01348-1,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0044,Price Discovery across Political Prediction Markets: Evidence from the 2024 U.S. Presidential Election,,2026,Aktuğ,SSRN Electronic Journal,10.2139/ssrn.7051166,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7051166,,openalex:preprint,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0045,,,
R0045,Price Discovery Across Political Prediction Markets: Evidence From The 2024 U.S. Presidential Election,,2026,Aktuğ,SSRN Electronic Journal,10.2139/ssrn.6624604,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6624604,,openalex:preprint,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0044,,,
R0046,Finnish Sports Betting Study 2025 - Vedonlyontiyhtiot.com,"The ""Suomalainen vedonlyöntitutkimus 2025"" by Vedonlyontiyhtiot.com (the Finnish sports betting study 2025) collected responses from 1,007 Finnish bettors to examine how they bet, where they bet, and what types of licences dominate the market. The results provide one of the clearest snapshots to date of the behaviour of active sports bettors in Finland.One of the clearest findings in the Suomalainen Vedonlyöntitutkimus 2025 is the overwhelming preference for tax-free betting sites.According to the survey, 98.50% of respondents primarily use a platform that offers tax-free winnings through an EU or ETA licence, such as Malta or Estonia. This result is strikingly consistent and leaves very little ambiguity about what Finnish bettors value when choosing where to play.The dominance of Malta and Estonia in the licensing breakdown explains much of this preference. Together, these two EU jurisdictions account for more than 75% of all primary sites selected by respondents.Åland’s PAF licence adds another sizeable share, while non-EU licences remain extremely marginal. Taken as a whole, the numbers show that Finnish bettors overwhelmingly choose environments where winnings are tax-free by default, and where the regulatory structure is familiar and stable.It’s important to note that the study does not measure whether tax-free status is the reason players chose these sites, but the end result is clear: almost every respondent ends up on a tax-free platform regardless of their initial motivation. This makes tax-free play one of the strongest behavioural trends in the dataset.",2026,Alanen,Figshare,10.6084/m9.figshare.31932309,,https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.31932309,,openalex:other,openalex,1,False,False,True,,,,,
R0047,"Evaluating the impact of a gambling harm focused educational intervention on medical students’ self-reported confidence: a non-randomised, single-group pre-post study","Abstract Background Gambling harm is a significant public health concern that is systematically under-recognised in clinical practice. Despite the recent inclusion of gambling disorder in the General Medical Council’s Medical Licensing Assessment content map, gambling harm has been largely absent from undergraduate medical education in the United Kingdom, and structured evaluations of gambling harm teaching delivered to medical students have not, to our knowledge, been reported. Methods A single-group, pre-post study of a teaching session on gambling harm was conducted across three sequential cohorts of Year 4 medical students at King’s College London during the 2025-2026 academic year. Outcome measures were collected immediately after the teaching with no follow-up. The session was delivered online by Gambling Harm UK, a registered UK charity, and comprised lived experience testimony and teaching with public health and clinical components. Self-reported confidence across six domains was assessed pre-and post-session on a five-point scale, alongside nine post-session attitudinal statements. Paired confidence data were analysed using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test with Hodges-Lehmann estimates of the median paired difference, with a Bonferroni-corrected significance threshold of p < 0.008 applied within each cohort. Results Sixty-eight paired responses were analysed (completion rates of 28%, 24%, and 32%). Statistically significant improvements in self-reported confidence were observed across all six domains in each cohort. Median confidence rose from’slightly confident’ before the session to’quite confident’ afterwards, with median paired improvements of 1.5 to 2.5 points (all p ≤ 0.001). Post-session attitudinal responses were positive across all nine items, with lived experience the most strongly endorsed. Conclusions A single online teaching session on gambling harm was associated with significant and consistent improvements in self-reported confidence in recognising and responding to gambling harm across three cohorts of medical students. Whether these gains translate into changes in clinical behaviour requires longer-term, multi-site evaluation with behavioural outcome measures. Medical schools should consider how gambling harm teaching might fit within their curricula and evaluate its introduction, with the aim of normalising asking about gambling in routine social history taking alongside alcohol and smoking.",2026,Albukai,medRxiv,10.64898/2026.09.01.26361237,,https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.09.01.26361237,,citations:preprint,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0048,"Take steps to raise awareness about risks, consequences of gambling","The changing landscape of gambling and sports betting in the United States is causing increased concern, particularly when it comes to young people. Although gambling is federally permitted in the United States, states have differing regulations related to casinos, state lotteries, online gambling, and sports betting. More than half of U.S. states have legalized mobile/online gambling or sports betting, and since the U.S. Supreme Court overturned the Professional and Amateur Sports Protection Act in 2018, almost 40 states have legalized retail and/or mobile sports betting. The expansion of online and mobile gambling platforms and the fast‐paced, highly engaged design of these digital gambling products has contributed to increasing participation and greater potential for harm. The commercial gambling sector has also prioritized advertising efforts, using social media and consumer data to deliver personalized marketing to increase gambling engagement.",2026,Allen,College Athletics and the Law,10.1002/catl.70012,,https://doi.org/10.1002/catl.70012,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0049,ENTRE APOSTAS E IMPOSTOS: DESAFIOS NA REGULAMENTAÇÃO DAS APOSTAS ESPORTIVAS E A ATUAÇÃO DO STJD NA PREVENÇÃO DE CRIMES FISCAIS,"O presente trabalho aborda os desafios enfrentados pela regulamentação das apostas esportivas no Brasil, com foco na Lei nº 14.790/2023 e os impactos econômicos e tributários dessa atividade. A análise abrange a evolução histórica e jurídica das apostas no país, destacando a legalização das apostas de quota fixa pela Lei nº 13.756/2018 e os avanços proporcionados pela Lei nº 14.790/2023. Embora o marco regulatório tenha avançado, ainda persistem dificuldades relacionadas à fiscalização eficaz, à arrecadação tributária e ao combate a práticas ilícitas, como a lavagem de dinheiro e a evasão fiscal, especialmente no contexto das plataformas digitais.O trabalho também examina a atuação do Superior Tribunal de Justiça Desportiva (STJD) e seu papel na preservação da integridade das competições esportivas, combatendo práticas fraudulentas como a manipulação de resultados, frequentemente associadas a apostas ilegais. Embora o STJD não exerça funções fiscais ou penais diretas, sua atuação disciplinar tem impacto indireto na prevenção de crimes fiscais, uma vez que a manipulação de resultados afeta tanto a credibilidade das competições quanto a arrecadação tributária.A pesquisa conclui que, para que o mercado de apostas esportivas seja regulamentado de forma eficaz e sustentável, é necessária uma fiscalização mais rigorosa, um sistema tributário mais justo e a implementação de políticas de compliance eficientes. A cooperação entre os órgãos reguladores, como o Ministério da Fazenda, a Receita Federal, o COAF e o STJD, é essencial para garantir a transparência e a integridade do setor. Ao integrar essas medidas, o Brasil poderá consolidar um mercado de apostas responsável, que contribua positivamente para a economia, ao mesmo tempo em que previne práticas ilícitas e protege a ordem econômica e tributária.",2026,Almeida,Revista de Estudos Jurídicos do UNI-RN,,,,pt,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0050,Influence of sponsorship on betting intentions: from linear to deep learning models.,"Sports sponsorship by commercial betting providers is an essential source of income for sports entities worldwide. This study examines the formation of betting behavior in sporting events using predictor variables, namely, self-congruence, perceived exposure, subjective norms, goodwill, and involvement, to predict betting intention and actual gambling behavior, comparing linear (PLS-SEM) and nonlinear (artificial neural networks-ANN) methods. Data from 661 Spanish subjects were analyzed. The results of the PLS-SEM model identified subjective norms and self-congruence as the main predictors of betting intention, whereas goodwill showed limited effects. However, the ANN analysis revealed complex patterns not detected by linear methods, where variables such as goodwill acquire greater relative importance when considering complex interactions between variables. The research contributes theoretically by highlighting the need to adopt conceptual frameworks that consider the complex interaction between psychosocial variables, overcoming traditional models that analyze the determinants of gambling behavior in isolation. Additionally, it provides new knowledge about the differentiated and complementary influence of self-congruence, perceived exposure, goodwill, and involvement in predicting gambling behavior, constructs that had not been systematically integrated into a joint predictive model. Methodologically, it contributes by demonstrating that the integration of SEM and ANN approaches improves the robustness and validity of the analysis, offering a more complete view of the factors that influence the intention to gamble. The findings have practical implications for the design of more effective problem gambling prevention strategies and regulations.",2026,Alonso Dos Santos,Harm reduction journal,10.1186/s12954-026-01485-z,42289703,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12954-026-01485-z,eng,pubmed:Journal Article,pubmed,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0051,THE WORLD CUP and New York KNICKS MAKE DYNAMIC IMPACTS ON U.S. Sportsbooks IN JUNE,United States sportsbooks saw a sharp decline in June revenue thanks to two distinct events: the U.S. men's soccer World Cup opening match and the New York Knicks ending a 53-year title drought in the NBA.,2026,Altruda,Gaming Law Review,10.1177/25725300261493692,,https://doi.org/10.1177/25725300261493692,en,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0052,Propuesta de mejoramiento en el alistamiento del servicio en la compañía Redcolsa,"Redcolsa es una empresa vallecaucana dedicada a la comercialización de productos y servicios como juegos, giros, apuestas deportivas, pagos y recargas. Con el objetivo de aumentar sus ingresos y mantener una utilidad sostenible, cuenta con más de 1.200 puntos de servicio, 10 sedes de atención y un centro de distribución donde se almacenan insumos, repuestos y activos. En este centro también opera el taller de mantenimiento y reparación de activos. Además, en el centro de distribución se lleva a cabo el proceso de alistamiento del servicio, el cual consiste en el abastecimiento y atención de solicitudes del canal de ventas. En línea con su razón de ser, la empresa busca generar valor económico y social. Por ello, Redcolsa se enfoca en optimizar el desempeño de todas las actividades de la cadena de suministro. Para lograrlo, es indispensable que todos los actores de la cadena estén operativos; en caso de presentarse alguna interrupción, esta debe ser solucionada lo más pronto posible. Actualmente, se evidencian demoras en los tiempos de ejecución del proceso de alistamiento del servicio, lo que puede afectar la atención de solicitudes del canal de ventas y, en consecuencia, interrumpir la comercialización de los servicios. Esta situación se debe a la falta de seguimiento al proceso, ya que no se ha realizado un diagnóstico actualizado que permita identificar oportunidades de mejora para su optimización. ¿Qué oportunidades de mejora se podrían proponer para optimizar los tiempos del proceso de alistamiento del servicio en Redcolsa? Esta pregunta será respondida durante el desarrollo del proyecto aplicado. Para ello, se realizará un diagnóstico de la situación actual del proceso de alistamiento del servicio, utilizando herramientas como estudios de métodos y tiempos, encuestas y diagramas de Pareto. Estas permitirán recopilar de manera sistemática la información necesaria para analizar el proceso, identificar los factores que ocasionan las demoras y, finalmente, diseñar una propuesta de mejora que permita reducir los tiempos del proceso en un 20%. Esto aumentará las oportunidades de atención al canal de ventas y mejorará la prestación del servicio a los clientes. Palabras clave: logística, Alistamiento, Almacén.",2026,Alvarez,Institutional Repository of the National University Open and Distance UNAD (Universidad Nacional Abierta y a Distancia),,,,,openalex_non_en:other,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0053,Restricting Online Sports Betting,"In December 2025, Brazil barred recipients of its cash transfer program from registering with licensed sports betting operators. We evaluate this restriction using synthetic difference-indifferences and administrative data covering the universe of registered bettors. Before the restriction, we find that betting is most prevalent in municipalities which are populous, economically active, and with more intensive use of instant payments, and least prevalent where a larger share of the population receives social assistance. Afterward, betting activity contracted sharply in the municipalities most exposed to the policy: the number of active bettors fell by 12% to 18%, the number of bets by 20% to 22%, and total wagering by 12% to 14%. Moreover, we find no evidence that this restriction was circumvented through the accounts of unrestricted individuals. We conservatively estimate that this policy saved at least 25 million USD in losses from vulnerable bettors from December 2025 to April 2026. Turning to household credit, we find no corresponding improvement. Hence, our paper shows that restricting online sports betting can reduce such wagering, but these restrictions do not seem to improve household debt levels in the short run. Thus, our results speak to the design of consumer financial protection in fast-growing digital markets.",2026,Alves,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.7501578,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7501578,,crossref:posted-content | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0055,Sports Betting and Financial–Health Outcomes Among Young Adults: A Scoping Review,"Abstract Background Sports betting has grown significantly over the last few years, especially amongst young adults, due to digital media, aggressive advertising, and integration of betting into the sporting culture. Although the available literature is mostly oriented to psychological and behavioural damages, recent facts reveal a high risk of sports betting to the financial health, such as debts, financial stress, and loss of savings. However, these financial impacts on young adults in terms of their scope, nature, and contextual determinants are still not integrated into the literature. Methods This scoping review has been conducted according to the system of methodology developed by Arksey and O’Malley and presented according to the PRISMA-ScR recommendations. Primary empirical research published since 2020 was determined with a systematic search in academic databases and grey literature sources. Eligible studies met the criteria of research on sports betting in young adults and provided outcomes that are related to financial health. The thematic and content analyses were used to chart and synthesise data. The quality of methodological approach was evaluated with the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) critical appraisal instruments and Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT). Results Twenty-five major studies were eligible to inclusion. The data shows that the sport betting is extremely normalised in young adults and promoted by the ease of access online and promotional deals. The terms that were repeatedly included in the reported financial outcomes were financial stress, accumulation of debts, loss of savings, and poor financial resiliency. The behavioural drivers of these harms included perceived skill and loss chasing, and institutional and socio-technical settings, such as regulatory environments and mobile payment systems. The vast majority of the studies were set in the high-income countries, and there is little evidence related to the low- and middle-income environments. Conclusion Betting in sports is linked with initial and prolonged economic damage among young adults. The results highlight the importance of responding to marketing policy-level, enhanced regulation of marketing behaviours, and combined interventions affecting the financial welfare of a population. Longitudinal designs and context-specific evidence should be initiated in future studies so as to make appropriate prevention strategies.",2026,Amakye,Research Square,10.21203/rs.3.rs-9125426/v1,,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9125426/v1,und,crossref:posted-content | europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR | openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,europepmc;openalex;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0056,DAMPAK JUDI ONLINE BOLA DARI HIBURAN KE KECANDUAN DI KALANGAN MAHASISWA FISIP UNHAS= THE IMPACT OF ONLINE FOOTBALL GAMBLING FROM ENTERTAINMENT TO ADDICTION IN UNHAS FISIP STUDENTS,"ABSTRAK ANDI IRMA NURSAFITRI AMIRUDDIN. DAMPAK JUDI ONLINE BOLA DARIHIBURAN KE KECANDUAN DI KALANGAN MAHASISWA FISIP UNHAS (dibimbing oleh Dr.Yahya, MA.) Penelitian ini membahas fenomena meningkatnya keterlibatan mahasiswa FISIP Universitas Hasanuddin dalam judi online bola, yang dipengaruhi oleh perkembangan teknologi digital, budaya penggunaan internet, serta dinamika pergaulan mahasiswa di Kota Makassar. Kota Makassar sebagai pusat pendidikan dan aktivitas urban menyediakan lingkungan yang sangat mendukung akses dan penggunaan platform digital, termasuk aplikasi judi online. Penelitian ini bertujuan untuk memahami makna judi online bola bagi mahasiswa, dampak yang ditimbulkannya, serta upaya yang dilakukan mahasiswa dalam menangani kecanduan terhadap aktivitas tersebut. Hasil penelitian menunjukkan bahwa mahasiswa memaknai judi online bola secara beragam. Sebagian melihatnya sebagai hiburan yang mengikuti tren digital, sebagian lain memandangnya sebagai bentuk kompetisi yang mempererat hubungan pertemanan, dan ada pula yang memaknainya sebagai peluang finansial. Namun, seiring meningkatnya intensitas bermain, makna tersebut berubah ketika mahasiswa mulai mengalami kerugian dan tekanan psikologis. Dampak yang muncul mencakup penurunan konsentrasi belajar, terganggunya performa akademik, masalah finansial akibat kerugian dan utang, serta gangguan sosial seperti konflik antar teman dan distorsi relasi sosial dalam lingkungan kampus. Aktivitas ini juga membentuk pola interaksi baru, menciptakan kelompok pergaulan berbasis minat pada taruhan bola yang secara tidak langsung menormalisasi praktik judi di lingkungan mahasiswa. Adapun upaya mahasiswa untuk mengatasi kecanduan judi online bola sebagian besar masih bersifat mandiri, seperti mengontrol pengeluaran, menghapus aplikasi, menghindari lingkungan yang mendorong berjudi, atau mencari aktivitas pengganti. Beberapa mahasiswa juga mendapatkan dukungan dari teman atau keluarga, namun minim yang memanfaatkan layanan konseling kampus. Hal ini menunjukkan bahwa kesadaran mahasiswa untuk berhenti sudah ada, tetapi belum ditopang oleh dukungan institusional yang memadai. Hasil penelitian ini menegaskan perlunya peran aktif pihak kampus dalam menyediakan edukasi, ruang konseling, dan program pencegahan yang terstruktur untuk membantu mahasiswa menghadapi risiko kecanduan judi online bola. Kata kunci: Judi Online Bola, Mahasiswa FISIP Unhas, Kecanduan Digital, Dampak Sosial, Perilaku Mahasiswa.",2026,AMIRUDDIN,Hasanuddin University Repository,,,,id,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0057,Sports Betting as an Asset class: SLR and Bibliometric Analysis,,2026,Anand,Unpublished,10.13140/rg.2.2.35624.12805,,https://doi.org/10.13140/rg.2.2.35624.12805,,datacite:Text/Preprint | openalex:article,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0058,A behavioral economic investigation of the effect of sports wagering on alcohol demand and relations to gambling motives.,"Sports wagering is associated with alcohol use and alcohol-related harm, and gambling can increase alcohol consumption and drinking urges. However, the effect of sports wagering on alcohol reward value remains unclear. The present study explored the interactive effect of placing a single-event sports wager and social context on alcohol demand (a behavioral economic measure of alcohol reward value) using context-adapted alcohol purchase tasks in a sample of individuals reporting past-month alcohol use and sports wagering ( n = 425; M age = 31.28; 71.29% male). Contrary to hypotheses, there were no differences in alcohol demand between context-adapted alcohol purchase tasks as a function of concurrent sports wagering ( p adj = .876-.924) or social context ( p adj = .170-.667). Exploratory analyses investigated relations between gambling motives and alcohol demand and the relations between various behavioral economic constructs and problem gambling. O max was associated with an increased likelihood of problem gambling ( OR = 1.02, p adj = .03). O max and elasticity were associated with financial gambling motives, but less so during concurrent gambling events, with concurrent sports wagering attenuating the relation between elasticity and financial motives ( b = 0.34, p adj = .003). Financial motives for gambling were positively associated with alcohol use broadly, but not with simultaneous drinking while gambling. These findings suggest relations between sports wagering and alcohol demand are complex-highlighting the need for future research into the sports wagering experience, specific sports wagering forms, and alcohol's role in sports culture to better understand the relation between alcohol use and sports wagering. (PsycInfo Database Record (c) 2026 APA, all rights reserved).",2026,Andersland,Experimental and clinical psychopharmacology,10.1037/pha0000871,42474983,https://doi.org/10.1037/pha0000871,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex:supplementary-materials | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,6,False,False,False,,,,,
R0059,"Drink in Hand, Limit in Mind: Simultaneous Alcohol Use Does Not Attenuate the Relation Between Monetary Limit Setting and Gambling Harm","Responsible gambling strategies include a range of behavioral strategies to minimize gambling harm. One such strategy is setting monetary limits before gambling, which has been associated with reduced gambling expenditure. The simultaneous use of alcohol while gambling is associated with greater gambling-related harm severity, reduced receptivity to warning messages during play, and may affect gambling behavior when intoxicated. The present study explored the relation between monetary limit setting and gambling- and alcohol-related harm, and the extent to which these relations are moderated by the simultaneous alcohol use while gambling, in a sample of 495 individuals reporting sports betting and using alcohol during the past month. Limit setting was negatively associated with both gambling- and alcohol-related harm and drinking while gambling was positively associated with gambling- and alcohol-related harm. The proportion of gambling time spent drinking potentiated the strength of the relation between limit setting and harm, such that the negative association between limit setting and gambling harm was greater for those with relatively higher proportions of gambling time spent drinking. These findings suggest that setting monetary limits is associated with reduced gambling harm, irrespective of the extent to which individuals report simultaneous alcohol use while gambling.",2026,Andersland,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0060,Home bias and investor sentiment: Evidence from a quasi-experimental financial market,"We use the sports betting market as a real-world market laboratory to study the home bias in individuals’ behavior. In contrast to traditional financial markets, where institutional frictions and the joint hypothesis problem confound explanations of the home bias, the sports betting market’s quasi-experimental features enable us to cleanly test whether preferential betting on home teams reflects informational advantages or behavioral forces. We find that individuals systematically favor local teams, domestic teams, and teams featuring players from their home country. This home bias does not yield superior performance but distorts individuals’ portfolios, generating welfare costs of similar magnitude to those in the stock market. Our findings help strengthen the foundation of behavioral explanations of the home bias in similar market environments.",2026,Andrikogiannopoulou,International Review of Financial Analysis,10.1016/j.irfa.2026.105331,,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.irfa.2026.105331,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0061,Mobile Gambling Addiction and Its Effects on the Academic Performance and Mental Health of Criminology Students at CICOSAT Colleges,"This study investigated the prevalence and effects of mobile gambling addiction among criminology students at CICOSAT Colleges. Specifically, it examined the level of addiction to online betting and gambling activities, the factors influencing gambling behavior, and the effects of mobile gambling addiction on students’ academic performance and mental health. The study utilized an Explanatory Sequential mixed-methods research design, combining quantitative and qualitative approaches to gain a comprehensive understanding of the phenomenon. Quantitative data were gathered from 330 criminology students through structured survey questionnaires, while qualitative data were obtained through interviews with 20 purposively selected students involved in online gambling activities. Weighted mean and thematic analysis were used to analyze the collected data. The findings revealed that criminology students were moderately addicted to online gambling activities, showing early signs of gambling dependency such as increased engagement, cognitive preoccupation, and tolerance. Social, environmental, financial, and technological factors were found to be influential contributors to gambling behavior. Peer influence, easy access to gambling applications, availability of e-wallet services such as GCash, and continuous exposure to gambling advertisements significantly encouraged students to participate in mobile gambling. Moreover, the study found that mobile gambling addiction negatively affected students’ academic performance by reducing concentration, causing absenteeism, poor time management, and declining academic motivation. In terms of mental health, students experienced stress, anxiety, emotional instability, sleep deprivation, and compulsive behavior associated with gambling activities. The study concluded that although mobile gambling has not yet become a dominant routine among most respondents, its increasing prevalence poses serious risks to students’ academic achievement and psychological well-being. The researchers recommended the implementation of stricter anti-gambling policies within educational institutions, the establishment of counseling and mental health support programs, integration of financial literacy education into the curriculum, and restrictions on gambling access within campus internet networks. These measures may help reduce gambling-related harm and promote healthier academic and personal development among criminology students.",2026,Angiwan,International Journal For Multidisciplinary Research,10.36948/ijfmr.2026.v08i03.78483,,https://doi.org/10.36948/ijfmr.2026.v08i03.78483,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0062,"EVALUATING THE INFLUENCE OF DIGITAL ADVERTISING METRICS ON MULTIDIMENSIONAL CUSTOMER ENGAGEMENT IN THE ONLINE BETTING INDUSTRY IN AKWA IBOM STATE, NIGERIA.","This study evaluated the influence of digital advertising metrics on multidimensional customer engagement in the online betting industry in Akwa Ibom State, Nigeria. The research was motivated by increasing reliance on quantifiable digital advertising indicators such as cost per click (CPC), ad personalization, and ad duration/exposure time. Anchored on Customer Engagement Theory, the study adopted a survey research design and collected primary data from 323 online betting customers selected through convenience sampling. Simple regression analysis was employed to test three hypotheses at a 0.05 significance level. Findings showed that all three digital advertising metrics significantly influenced multidimensional customer engagement. Cost per click demonstrated a strong positive effect (R = 0.821; R² = 0.831; F = 2632.807; p < 0.05), indicating that higher-quality, competitively placed paid ads enhanced user engagement. Ad personalization exhibited the strongest influence (R = 0.788; R² = 0.873; F = 2772.641; p < 0.05), suggesting that tailored advertising powerfully promoted cognitive, emotional, and behavioural engagement. Ad duration/exposure time also significantly affected engagement (R = 0.780; R² = 0.849; F = 2592.731; p < 0.05), confirming that optimal exposure frequency and duration improved attention, recall, and interactive responses. The study concluded that CPC, ad personalization, and exposure time are critical drivers of multidimensional customer engagement in the online betting industry. The study recommended increased investment in optimized CPC strategies, the adoption of data-driven personalized advertising, and careful adjustment of ad exposure lengths to maintain attention while preventing user fatigue. These insights provide strategic guidance for betting operators and policymakers seeking to enhance responsible and effective digital advertising practices",2026,Aniekan,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.18784174,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18784174,en,datacite:Text/ | openaire:Other literature type | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0064,China's Sports Lottery is not a betting market - it's a formula,"897 Chinese Sports Lottery (竞彩) football matches, 2026-05-17 to 2026-09-25, each paired with the same fixture's European ""99 bookmakers average"" closing odds. Overround is 1/home + 1/draw + 1/away: the sum of implied probabilities. If the odds were fair it would be exactly 1.0; the excess is the margin taken before your opinion about the match is relevant. Measured over 897 matches: - Chinese Sports Lottery 1X2: 12.92% margin, sigma = 0.0006, range 1.1251-1.1299 - Chinese Sports Lottery handicap: 12.91% margin, sigma = 0.0006 - European average (99 books): 6.85% margin, sigma = 0.0109, range 1.0403-1.1085 The lottery is more expensive in 897 of 897 matches (mean gap 6.07 percentage points). The findable part is the sigma: the lottery's price barely moves across 897 matches because it is computed from a fixed payout parameter, not set by a market. Two independent play types in the same lottery land on 12.92% and 12.91% - the same parameter applied to everything. A price that does not move cannot be wrong, which is why there is no mispricing to find here. Also included: 15 betting rules over a 1,617-bet sample (12 negative, 9 significantly so; 3 positive with confidence intervals crossing zero; 0 passing a pre-registered threshold of n &gt;= 300 and 95% CI lower bound &gt; 0). Files: raw odds CSV, per-bet results for all 15 rules, recompute scripts (standard library only), and the full report as PDF. Code: https://github.com/quietnumbers/china-sports-lottery-overround Analysis, not betting advice.",2026,Anonymous,OSF Preprints (OSF Preprints),10.17605/osf.io/8gjrv,,https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/8gjrv,,datacite:Text/Project | openalex:preprint,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0065,Sports betting and college students’ subjective well-being: The role of peers and family norms,August2026,2026,Anthonia,Iowa State University Digital Repository (Iowa State University),,,,en,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0066,O jogo compulsivo e a hipervulnerabilidade do consumidor nas plataformas de apostas esportivas online,"O presente artigo analisa a expansão das plataformas de apostas esportivas online no Brasil, especialmente sob a perspectiva do Direito do Consumidor, com enfoque na hipervulnerabilidade do apostador-consumidor em contextos de jogo compulsivo. A utilização de uma arquitetura digital orientada por inteligência artificial, diversas técnicas de gamificação e “nudges”, resultam na restrição da autonomia decisória do apostador. Com isso, questiona-se a política do “jogo responsável” como instrumento ambíguo de arquitetura da escolha, que ao ser utilizada de forma unilateral, opaca ou seletiva, pode operar na modulação comportamental dos usuários. À luz do Código de Defesa do Consumidor e da jurisprudência atual, sustenta-se possíveis caminhos para distinguir intervenções legítimas de proteção ao consumidor em face de estratégias voltadas à maximização de lucros das plataformas de apostas.",2026,Araujo,DELOS Desarrollo Local Sostenible,10.55905/rdelosv19.n76-147,,https://doi.org/10.55905/rdelosv19.n76-147,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0067,ปจจยสวนบคคล สงคม และสอทมผลตอการเลนพนนฟตบอล ความเสยงตดพนน และผลกระทบตอประชาชน จงหวดเชยงใหม,"Chiang Mai Province faces ongoing football gambling problems and consistent arrest statistics, reflecting those residents are at risk of gambling addiction. This research aimed to study the general characteristics, football gambling behaviors, risk of gambling addiction, influencing factors, and impacts under the framework of Football Gambling, Social Learning Theory, Uses and Gratifications Theory, and Technological Determinism. Data were collected from 400 respondents who had engaged in football gambling using convenience sampling. A questionnaire and the Problem Gambling Severity Index (PGSI) were used as research instruments, considering research ethics. Data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, Pearson correlation, and multiple regression. The results showed that most football gamblers were male and initiated gambling due to peer influence. More than half were at risk of football gambling addiction. Personal, social, and media factors strongly influenced football gambling behaviors, including enjoyment, peer influence, and betting odds. Moderate impacts were reported, such as stress and insomnia. Hypothesis testing indicated that individuals who had won or lost large amounts of money had a higher risk of gambling addiction than other groups. All three factors were positively associated with gambling risk. Multiple regression analysis showed that these factors significantly predicted the risk of football gambling addiction (Adjusted R² = .257, F = 20.751, p < .001). This study proposes the G-SHIELD (Gambling Shield) Model as a theoretical framework and policy measures to prevent and address football gambling problems, aiming to reduce risks at the individual, family, social, and media levels.",2026,Archaphet,Journal of Social Innovation and Lifelong Learning,,,,th,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0068,Incentivos de marketing en la percepción de riesgo y comportamiento de apuestas deportivas,"El presente estudio tuvo como objetivo analizar los incentivos de marketing en la percepción de riesgo y comportamiento de las apuestas deportivas en las decisiones de los usuarios. La metodología siguió un enfoque cuantitativo, con un diseño no experimental de alcance descriptivo, mediante una encuesta aplicada a 625 personas de la ciudad de Machala que realizan apuestas deportivas, seleccionadas a través de un muestreo por conveniencia. Los resultados evidenciaron que los bonos de bienvenida y las apuestas gratuitas son los incentivos más frecuentes que se encuentran los apostadores, mientras que las redes sociales son el principal medio de difusión de estas promociones. Más de la mitad de los participantes percibe un menor riesgo de perder dinero al recibir estos beneficios y reconoce que apuesta con mayor frecuencia y montos más altos cuando recibe una promoción. Se concluye que los incentivos de marketing funcionan como un mecanismo psicológico en la manera en que los usuarios evalúan el riesgo al apostar y en el comportamiento que adoptan después de apostar, lo que refuerza la necesidad de promover estrategias publicitarias más responsables.",2026,Argudo,Ciencia Latina Revista Científica Multidisciplinar,10.37811/cl_rcm.v10i4.25080,,https://doi.org/10.37811/cl_rcm.v10i4.25080,,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0070,Sports Betting Legalization Amplifies Emotional Cues and Intimate Partner Violence,"Abstract This study explores the relationship between legalized sports gambling, unexpected emotional cues stemming from NFL home team upset losses and reported intimate partner violence (IPV). Using 1995–2022 crime data from NIBRS, replicating and extending Card and Dahl (2011)’s model, we find that legalized gambling increases the impact of upset losses on IPV by 10 percentage points. The e!ect is larger in states with mobile betting, where higher bets were placed, around paydays, and for teams on a winning streak. These results suggest that financial losses from gambling amplify emotional reactions to unexpected team losses.",2026,Arnesen,The Review of Economics and Statistics,10.1162/rest.a.1784,,https://doi.org/10.1162/rest.a.1784,en,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref,3,False,False,False,,R2526,,,
R0071,The Rational Mind's Blind Spot: Why Do People Make Irrational Financial Decisions.,"Standard economic theory imagines a decision-maker who has preferences, process information, weighs costs and benefits consistently, faces constraints and chooses the option that maximizes expected utility. Everyday financial behaviour repeatedly departs from this benchmark as real people, and especially those living in this app-driven economy of 2026, do not behave this way. A person may know that saving is beneficial but still spends impulsively; understands that gambling has a negative expected value but still place a bet; intend to hold a long-term investment but still sell it during a market drop. This paper asks why, using four everyday behaviours: impulse spendings, sports betting among teenagers, panic selling during market falls and buy-now-pay-later (BNPL) borrowings. For each behaviour I set the textbook &ldquo;should&rdquo; against the observed &ldquo;does&rdquo;, trace the psychological mechanisms behind the gap (present bias, loss aversion, mental accounting, overconfidence, social-norms effects and hot emotional states) and asks who profits when those mechanisms are triggered. The central argument is that these behaviours are not four unrelated forms of financial &lsquo;irrationality&rsquo; , they are different expressions of a recurring decision architecture: immediate rewards are psychologically salient, delayed costs are discounted, losses are felt disproportionately, social information changes perceived norms and risk and modern financial technologies reduce friction between intention and action, turns out that these four behaviours share one structure, I call it &ldquo;asymmetric friction&rdquo;. The paper also considers the role of supply side: the firms and platforms, retailers, betting platforms, brokers and lenders because incentives are not located only inside the individuals but also the supply side as they design the environments that make certain choices easier, faster and more emotionally compelling. Irrationality is therefore systematic, and because it is systematic, it is predictable, measurable, and designable. The final synthesis proposes that improving these behaviours requires more than financial literacy, it requires understanding the interaction between preferences, cognitive limits, emotions and redesigning the environments in which people choose.&nbsp;",2026,Arora,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.23260571,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23260571,en,datacite:Preprint/ | openaire:Preprint | openalex:preprint,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0072,Machine Learning Approach to Multi-Sport Betting,,2026,Arriba-Pérez,Lecture notes in networks and systems,10.1007/978-3-032-10721-3_56,,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-10721-3_56,en,crossref:book-chapter | openaire:Part of book or chapter of book | openalex:conference-paper,openalex;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0073,"Apuestas deportivas de contrapartida online, delito de estafa y el artículo 286 BIS.4 del Código Penal","Gambling has traditionally been fertile ground for criminal behaviour, particularly when financial gains are involved. In the context of sports betting, and especially with the rise of online platforms, certain behaviours have emerged that verge on fraud. When determining the legal significance of these behaviours, the principle of the victim's self-responsibility for their financial loss undoubtedly plays a key role. However, recent decisions of the Spanish Supreme Court have reduced the number of cases in which a court could invoke such a principle. This article aims to determine whether this principle applies in betting cases and, if so, how it might limit the offender's criminal liability. In addition, the possibility of classifying some of the cases described above under the offense set forth in Article 286 bis.4 of the Spanish Criminal Code will be evaluated.",2026,Arribas,Estudios Penales y Criminológicos,10.15304/epc.49.10779,,https://doi.org/10.15304/epc.49.10779,,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | openalex_non_en:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;openalex_non_en;s2;crossref,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0077,A Case Report on Gambling Disorder in the Context of Hopelessness and Values: A Narrative Therapy Intervention,"In recent years, the increasing prevalence of gambling disorder has prompted mental health professionals to explore various treatment modalities. Although several therapeutic approaches have been proposed, there is still a lack of robust empirical evidence regarding their effectiveness. Intervention-based studies have emphasized the need to move beyond symptom reduction and behavioral control by incorporating multidimensional aspects of recovery, thereby highlighting the necessity for innovative therapeutic frameworks. This case report aims to describe and analyze the clinical story and therapeutic process of a 25-year-old male who was considered to have a gambling disorder due to repetitive engagement in online betting and who underwent treatment through the Narrative Therapy approach. The client's symptoms were assessed using the DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for gambling disorder, along with the South Oaks Gambling Screen (SOGS). The client was followed for over a year, during which 28 individual therapy sessions were conducted. This case report discusses the role of values and hopelessness within the context of Narrative-based psychotherapy. Narrative-specific techniques such as externalization and alternative story development are addressed in relation to diagnosis, intervention, and follow-up. Rather than aiming to eliminate the gambling behavior directly, the intervention focused on uncovering and engaging with the underlying values and hopes that drive such behaviors—underscoring a shift from pathology to meaning-making. Narrative Therapy offers practical and applicable techniques for addressing gambling disorder, especially by facilitating a value-oriented and individualized therapeutic process.",2026,Aslan,Cyprus Turkish Journal of Psychiatry and Psychology,10.35365/ctjpp.26.3.26,,https://doi.org/10.35365/ctjpp.26.3.26,,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0078,Behavioral addiction tendencies and associated psychosocial factors in faculty members,"Objective: Behavioral addictions may be associated with psychological symptoms and work-related factors. However, studies evaluating multiple behavioral addiction domains among faculty members remain limited. This study aimed to investigate behavioral addiction tendencies and associated psychosocial factors among faculty members. Methods: This cross-sectional online study included 202 faculty members. Participants completed an online survey consisting of a sociodemographic questionnaire, the Depression Anxiety Stress Scale-21 (DASS-21), Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI), Bergen Work Addiction Scale (BWAS), Smartphone Addiction Scale–Short Version (SAS-SV), Bergen Social Media Addiction Scale (BSMAS), Internet Gaming Disorder Scale–Short Form (IGDS9-SF), Modified Yale Food Addiction Scale (mYFAS 2.0), and Online Gambling Addiction Scale (OGAS). Group comparisons and correlation analyses were performed. Results: Behavioral addiction tendencies were observed at varying levels among faculty members, with work addiction, smartphone addiction, and social media addiction showing the highest scores. Depression, anxiety, and stress were positively associated with work addiction, smartphone addiction, social media addiction, and internet gaming disorder scores. Faculty members working more than 40 hours per week had significantly higher work addiction scores. Smartphone use, social media use, and gaming-related behaviors were positively associated with one another, whereas online gambling/betting tendencies demonstrated a distinct pattern. Conclusion: Behavioral addiction tendencies in faculty members appear to be associated with psychological symptoms and selected work-related characteristics. These findings contribute to the limited literature examining multiple behavioral addiction domains among faculty members and highlight the importance of considering behavioral addictions within a broader psychosocial context.",2026,Aslan,Journal of Dependence,10.51982/bagimli.623,,https://doi.org/10.51982/bagimli.623,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0080,"Differences in online gambling expenditure between players from Germany, Spain, Netherlands, Great Britain, US and Canada: A largescale online player tracking study","The present study aimed to compare the expenditure of real-world online gamblers (N = 926,086) from six countries (Great Britain, Netherlands, Germany, Spain, Canada [Ontario], and US) to identify potential impacts of specific regulatory aspects on the amount of money gambled by players. The dataset comprised 233,221 British players, 167,826 Dutch players, 89,456 German players, 55,229 Spanish players, 18,463 Canadian players, and 361,891 US players. Data were analyzed using the total amount of money bet, lost, and deposited. German players bet (median = €74) and lost (median = €15) the lowest amounts of money gambling. Moreover, the average bet per game (median = €0.17) was the lowest among German players. Canadian (mean = €11,165) and US players (mean = €6434) bet the highest amounts. The US and Canada were the two countries with the largest outliers with respect to amount bet. Analysis also showed that a small percentage of players were responsible for the majority of the revenue in each of the six jurisdictions. More specifically, in the US, the 20% highest spending players accounted for 95% of the amount of money bet. The respective percentages for Canada, Great Britain, Germany, Spain and the Netherlands were 94%, 88%, 86%, 91%, and 79%. The present study's findings suggest that less restrictive regulation is associated with higher gambling expenditure, and that very strict regulation is associated with lower gambling expenditure.",2026,Auer,Acta Psychologica,10.1016/j.actpsy.2026.106864,42030823,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.actpsy.2026.106864,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0081,Using Machine-Learning Algorithms to Predict Self-Reported Problem Gambling Among a Sample of Online Gamblers,"Abstract Studies suggest that algorithms can effectively be used to predict self-reported problem gambling using player tracking data. The present study analyzed a sample of real-world online gamblers ( N = 1,611) who engaged in lottery playing, casino gambling, bingo playing, and sports betting. The data also comprised each player’s actual gambling activity, as well as age and gender, in the 30 days prior to answering the Problem Gambling Severity Index (PGSI). Players who engaged in at least one lottery game 30 days prior to answering the PGSI were less likely to be problem gamblers compared to players who did not play lottery games. For all other game-categories the relationship was reversed. The results also indicated that specific behavioral tracking features—such as the average number of monetary deposits per session, total amount of money bet per day, session length, and casino gambling involvement—were among the most significant predictors of self-reported problem gambling. When evaluating different machine algorithms, logistic regression and random forest emerged as the most effective in predicting self-reported problem gambling. The present study is among the few which predicts self-reported problem gambling using a sample of online lottery players, casino gamblers, bingo players and sports bettors, and provides further empirical evidence supporting the use of machine learning models to identify self-reported problem gamblers based on player tracking data. These findings can inform responsible gambling strategies by enabling operators to identify and intervene before gambling-related problems escalate.",2026,Auer,International Journal of Mental Health and Addiction,10.1007/s11469-025-01602-2,,https://doi.org/10.1007/s11469-025-01602-2,en,citations:article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;citations;openaire,4,False,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0082,O DIREITO PREVIDENCIÁRIO FRENTE AO PROBLEMA DAS BETS: O CUSTO SOCIAL DAS APOSTAS ONLINE E OS DESAFIOS DA SEGURIDADE SOCIAL BRASILEIRA,"O presente artigo investiga se a ludopatia, enquanto transtorno mental reconhecido pelas classificações médicas internacionais, pode ensejar incapacidade previdenciária quando comprometida a aptidão laboral do segurado. A expansão das plataformas digitais de apostas esportivas online, conhecidas como bets, impulsionada pela Lei nº 14.790/2023, produziu uma crise silenciosa de saúde pública, com crescimento exponencial de casos de jogo patológico e reflexos diretos sobre o sistema de seguridade social brasileiro. O estudo desenvolve-se em quatro eixos temáticos encadeados: o marco regulatório das bets e sua insuficiência protetiva; a estrutura dos benefícios previdenciários por incapacidade no Regime Geral de Previdência Social, com exame dos requisitos legais para sua concessão; a caracterização clínica e neurobiológica da ludopatia e seus impactos documentados sobre a aptidão para o trabalho; e, por fim, a análise jurídica da ludopatia como causa de incapacidade laboral previdenciária, respondendo afirmativamente à hipótese central e identificando as lacunas normativas que obstaculizam a proteção efetiva do segurado. Conclui-se que a regulamentação vigente falhou ao desconsiderar o impacto previdenciário das apostas, gerando um paradoxo em que o Estado arrecada com o setor enquanto arca, de forma fragmentada e sem suporte normativo adequado, com os custos sociais e previdenciários do vício que contribuiu para disseminar.",2026,Avelino,Revista de Direito do Trabalho Processo do Trabalho e Direito da Seguridade Social,,,,pt,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0083,"Adicciones comportamentales: epidemiología, factores de riesgo, fenotipos y resultado terapéutico","Introducción: El TJ y la AVJ, son dos de las modalidades de AC con mayor prevalencia en las sociedades industrializadas, mostrando un crecimiento alarmantemente en las últimas décadas. Además, son cuadros clínicos cuyas características están en transformación, en gran medida como resultado de la rápida aparición y evolución de nuevas plataformas en formato presencial y virtual. Ante este escenario, los objetivos principales son: a) identificar componentes biopsicosociales que actúan como factores de riesgo del TJ (en especial la adicción a las apuestas deportivas online [AADO]) y la adicción a los videojuegos online (AVJO); b) analizar los mecanismos subyacentes que contribuyen a la expresión fenotípica y endofenotípica de estos comportamientos adictivos y c) determinar las variables con capacidad predictiva a la respuesta al tratamiento mediante la terapia cognitiva conductual (TCC). Resultados: En términos generales, las muestras analizadas estuvieron compuestas mayoritariamente por hombres, solteros, con niveles educativos primarios o secundarios y estatus socioeconómico bajo. En el primer estudio, se observó que la prevalencia de AADO fue del 13.01%, casi cuatro veces superior a la de AVJO, que alcanzó un 3.82% en una muestra de 3.036 pacientes que consecutivamente acudieron a solicitar tratamiento por comportamientos adictivos. Además, se identificó una tendencia lineal creciente en la prevalencia de AADO entre el 2012 (8.6%) y el 2022 (15.5%), mientras que la AVJO mostró solo una ligera tendencia creciente, aunque no alcanzó la significación estadística. A nivel psicopatológico, los pacientes con AADO presentaron mayores niveles de depresión, ansiedad, ideación paranoide y malestar psicológico global, así como mayores índices de impulsividad, desregulación emocional y consumo de sustancias. El perfil de personalidad de este grupo de pacientes se caracterizó por una mayor búsqueda de novedades, mayor dependencia de la recompensa, mayor persistencia y menor evitación del daño. En el segundo estudio, que se realizó mediante análisis de redes, la autotrascendencia fue identificada como el nodo “puente” y el más relevante, seguido del síntoma DSM-5 A7 “mentiras para ocultar el grado de implicación en el juego”, en los pacientes con actos ilegales relacionados al trastorno de juego (AIRTJ). En contraste, con los pacientes sin AIRTJ, el nodo con mayor relevancia y que se identificó como “puente”, fue el síntoma DSM-5 A5 “apuesta cuando se siente estresado (deprimido, culpable o ansioso)”. El tercer estudio evidenció que el riesgo de recaída durante la TCC, aplicada a pacientes con AVJO fue superior en sujetos con puntuaciones más altas en sensibilidad interpersonal, hostilidad y persistencia. Asimismo, ser hombre y presentar un inicio del comportamiento adictivo más tardío aumentó la probabilidad de abandono del tratamiento, mientras que un bajo cumplimiento terapéutico se asoció a mayores niveles de ideación paranoide, dependencia de la recompensa y bajos niveles de cooperatividad. El cuarto estudio que se incluye como anexo en esta tesis doctoral evidenció que, en comparación con sujetos controles sin adicciones, el TJ se asocia directamente con una edad más joven, mayores niveles de impulsividad, mayores déficits a nivel neuropsicológico y un nivel más bajo de LEAP2. De igual modo, se identificaron vínculos indirectos-mediadores que destacaron el papel relevante de la impulsividad y el funcionamiento neuropsicológico en la predisposición al desarrollo del TJ. Conclusiones: Los resultados de los estudios que conforman esta tesis evidencian la heterogeneidad de las AC, así como la presencia de múltiples factores biopsicosociales que influyen en su desarrollo y mantenimiento. Asimismo, se identificaron perfiles diferenciados en los grupos de pacientes con TJ (en particular con AADO) y AVJO, caracterizados por variables sociodemográficas, psicopatológicas y rasgos de personalidad específicos que permiten comprender mejor los mecanismos subyacentes que configuran la expresión fenotípica propia de cada condición clínica. Introducció: El TJ i l'AVJ, són modalitats d'AC amb més prevalença a les societats industrialitzades, mostrant un creixement alarmantment en les darreres dècades. A més, són quadres clínics les característiques dels quals estan en transformació, en gran mesura com a resultat de la ràpida aparició i evolució de noves plataformes en format presencial i virtual. Davant d'aquest escenari, els objectius principals són: a) identificar components biopsicosocials que actuen com a factors de risc del TJ (especialment l'addicció a les apostes esportives en línia [AADO]) i l'addicció als videojocs en línia (AVJO); b) analitzar els mecanismes subjacents que contribueixen a lexpressió fenotípica i endofenotípica daquests comportaments addictius i c) determinar les variables amb capacitat predictiva a la resposta al tractament mitjançant la teràpia cognitiva conductual (TCC). Resultats: En termes generals, l",2026,Ayala Rojas,Universitat Autònoma de Barcelona,,,https://hdl.handle.net/10803/697212,spa,openaire:Doctoral thesis,openaire,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R0084,Enhancing Fair Play in Fantasy Sports: The Role of Explainable AI in Team Selection,,2026,Babu,Lecture notes in networks and systems,10.1007/978-3-032-28594-2_11,,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-28594-2_11,en,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0085,Evolving fantasy cricket teams: Applying genetic algorithms for optimal player selection,"In fantasy crickets, users are allowed to make virtual teams on the basis of real performance of a player. The classical techniques, e.g. random sampling and systematic replacements restrict optimization in search of large solution space. Genetic Algorithms (GA) exploit the evolutionary strategies such as crossover, mutation and selection to maximize team configurations. In this paper, it has been argued that GA is a better solution to team selection. GA maximizes the expected performance and ensures the compliance with the credit and role limitations. GA has produced better teams than random sampling, systematic replacements and K-means clustering by continuously adapting to the play outcomes of players. This paper shows that GA is able to perform well at the optimisation of selection of fantasy sports clubs and offers a data-driven and customizable process.",2026,Babu,CRC Press eBooks,10.1201/9781003650201-126,,https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003650201-126,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R2252,,,
R0086,Retail Betting Markets,"This paper examines the rapid expansion and convergence of retail betting markets. We analyze market design elements, discuss economic utility, and highlight shared behavioral drivers of sports betting markets, prediction markets, and retail options trading. Our review underscores how technological innovation, behavioral biases, and regulatory arbitrage have shaped recent market evolution. We highlight important considerations for policy-makers facing a changing landscape and outline possibilities for further research.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Institutional subscribers to the NBER working paper series, and residents of developing countries may download this paper without additional charge at &lt;a href=""http://www.nber.org/papers/&amp;#119;35520"" TARGET=""_blank""&gt;www.nber.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;br&gt;",2026,Baker,Annual Review of Financial Economics,10.1146/annurev-financial-111424-023216,,https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev-financial-111424-023216,en,crossref:journal-article | crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,R0087;R0088,,,
R0087,Retail Betting Markets,"This paper examines the rapid expansion and convergence of retail betting markets. We analyze market design elements, discuss economic utility, and highlight shared behavioral drivers of sports betting markets, prediction markets, and retail options trading. Our review underscores how technological innovation, behavioral biases, and regulatory arbitrage have shaped recent market evolution. We highlight important considerations for policy-makers facing a changing landscape and outline possibilities for further research.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;Institutional subscribers to the NBER working paper series, and residents of developing countries may download this paper without additional charge at &lt;a href=""http://www.nber.org/papers/&amp;#119;35520"" TARGET=""_blank""&gt;www.nber.org&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;br&gt;",2026,Baker,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.7188672,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7188672,,crossref:posted-content | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref,2,False,False,False,,R0086;R0088,,,
R0088,Retail Betting Markets,"This review examines the rapid expansion and convergence of retail betting markets. We analyze market design elements, discuss economic utility, and highlight shared behavioral drivers of sports betting markets, prediction markets, and retail options trading. Our review underscores how technological innovation, behavioral biases, and regulatory arbitrage have shaped recent market evolution. We highlight important considerations for policy makers facing a changing landscape and outline possibilities for further research.",2026,Baker,Annual Review of Financial Economics,10.3386/w35520,,https://doi.org/10.3386/w35520,,s2:CaseReport;JournalArticle;Review,s2,1,False,False,False,,R0086;R0087,,,
R0089,Risk Factors and Prevalence of Gambling Among University Students During Academic Disruptions: A Case of Lecturer’s Strike in Kenya 2025,"This study investigated risk factors and prevalence of gambling among university students during academic disruptions brought about by such a case of lecturers' strikes, more so in public universities. A survey was used to collect data with a quantitative approach; a sample size of 620 students continuing with their academic work was randomly selected from 4 public universities in the Nairobi metropolitan area. The study established that several factors, such as socioeconomic factors like financial constraints, academic disruptions like lecturer strikes, environmental factors like exposure to betting platforms, and psychological factors like idleness and stress levels, contribute to gambling behaviours among students. The findings show that students from low socioeconomic backgrounds participate in risk gambling behaviour, which in the long run affects their academic progress and causes psychological distress. The most common form of gambling was sports betting, with the motivation of making quick money from betting. From the study, it was established that there’s a significant relationship between socioeconomic factors and gambling risk behaviours among students. The regulatory framework directly contributes to the growth of gambling in Kenya. Hence, in control measures, betting companies can adopt some technology that will only allow users to bet with limitations to ensure there is no overspending, thus preventing addictions of any kind. Institutions ought to invest in centers of counseling with the establishment of academic programmes geared towards creating awareness to promote responsible gambling even during times of academic disruptions in Kenyan public universities.",2026,BALONGO,Journal of the Kenya National Commission for UNESCO,10.62049/jkncu.v6i2.604,,https://doi.org/10.62049/jkncu.v6i2.604,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0090,Screening on Someone Else's Price: Exclusion and Price Discovery in Betting Markets,"Sportsbooks limit bettors whose bets beat the closing line, a price set at sharp books and exchanges where limited bettors then trade. I show that when a dealer screens customers against a price its excluded customers help produce, exclusion and price discovery are strategic complements: a more precise benchmark improves screening, which sends more informed flow to the benchmark venue. When a moderate amount of informed flow reaches the benchmark venue from other sources, this feedback sustains both a segregated and a low-precision market. Rules that force dealers to serve everyone weaken the benchmark and can collapse the segregated market. Because exclusion substitutes for price, such rules also raise the price uninformed customers pay. Screening on the benchmark implies that many correctly limited accounts are losing money when cut.",2026,Balthrop,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.7577578,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7577578,,crossref:posted-content | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0091,Between-session chasing via time of return in online eCasino gambling,"Background and aims: Chasing refers to continuing and/or intensifying gambling following losses (loss-chasing) and/or wins (win-chasing). It is commonly captured between sessions, i.e., returning another day/time after losses or wins in prior sessions. Between-session chasing is a hallmark of problematic gambling, but the current understanding of between-session chasing in field settings remains limited. Methods: Using a large-scale behavioral tracking dataset (11,156 gamblers; ∼170 million bets), we assessed between-session chasing via time of return. Using pre-registered analyses, we assessed time of return as a function of (a) prior outcome (win/loss), (b) outcome magnitude, (c) game type (Multi-line Slots, Dice game, European Roulette and Multi-hand European Blackjack) and (d) gambler's involvement level (high/low; defined based on monetary and time expenditure). Results: Confirmatory results indicated that overall gamblers returned faster following a winning than a losing session. They returned faster when previous outcome magnitude (both wins and losses) increased. Furthermore, return times of slot gamblers were less sensitive to prior outcomes than other products. Exploratory results indicated that return times of high-involvement gamblers were less sensitive to prior outcome than low-involvement gamblers. Discussion and Conclusions: The current results indicate a pattern of both loss- and win-chasing, demonstrating that the break-even, house money and wealth effects may be at play. Decreased sensitivity to prior outcome among slot and high-involvement gamblers may be attributed to habituation. Future research could disentangle these multi-causal mechanisms, to evaluate time of return as a potential behavioral marker of harm, for better identification and mitigation of problem gambling.",2026,Banerjee,Journal of Behavioral Addictions,10.1556/2006.2025.00454,42627694,https://doi.org/10.1556/2006.2025.00454,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0092,Risk Factors Associated with Gambling in Young Adults: A Systematic Review Protocol,"Background Gambling is increasingly getting recognised as a public health problem, with young adults (18-25) being identified as a particularly vulnerable group due to their ongoing neurodevelopment and increased exposure to spreading gambling environments. Individuals who adopt problematic gambling behaviours can either classify as problem gamblers or can in more severe cases meet the criteria for a clinical diagnosis for Gambling Disorder (GD). Different characteristics and traits, known as risk factors, can influence the development of these conditions. This review aims to evaluate the quantitative studies investigating risk factors contributing to problematic gambling behaviours in the young adult populations. Methods A systematic review guided by the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) methodology will be conducted. This review will consider quantitative and mixed-methods, where quantitative data can be extracted, evidence published from 1990 onwards on risk factors which are associated with gambling in the young adult population. Four databases will be searched as well as grey literature. Methodological quality will be assessed using the JBI critical appraisal tools based on the study designs of each study. Data extraction will be conducted using the JBI standardised extraction tools. The risk of bias will be assessed using the Quality in Prognostic Studies (QUIPS) tool to evaluate the risk-factor-outcome relationship. Discussion Gambling Disorder and Problem Gambling are a growing public health concern. The results of this review are anticipated to benefit researchers, clinicians and policymakers by providing a better understanding on key predictors of gambling-related harm in this population which would then aid in informing early intervention and prevention strategies. Protocol registration number PROSPERO 2026 CRD420261288057",2026,Banozic,HRB Open Research,10.12688/hrbopenres.14344.1,,https://doi.org/10.12688/hrbopenres.14344.1,en,citations:review,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,R0093,,,
R0093,Risk Factors Associated with Gambling in Young Adults: A Systematic Review Protocol,"Background Gambling is increasingly getting recognised as a public health problem, with young adults (18–25) being identified as a particularly vulnerable group due to their ongoing neurodevelopment and increased exposure to spreading gambling environments. Individuals who adopt problematic gambling behaviours can either classify as problem gamblers or can in more severe cases meet the criteria for a clinical diagnosis for Gambling Disorder (GD). Different characteristics and traits, known as risk factors, can influence the development of these conditions. This review aims to evaluate the quantitative studies investigating risk factors contributing to problematic gambling behaviours in the young adult populations. Methods A systematic review guided by the Joanna Briggs Institute (JBI) methodology will be conducted. This review will consider quantitative and mixed-methods, where quantitative data can be extracted, evidence published from 1990 onwards on risk factors which are associated with gambling in the young adult population. Four databases will be searched as well as grey literature. Methodological quality will be assessed using the JBI critical appraisal tools based on the study designs of each study. Data extraction will be conducted using the JBI standardised extraction tools. The risk of bias will be assessed using the Quality in Prognostic Studies (QUIPS) tool to evaluate the risk-factor-outcome relationship. Discussion Gambling Disorder and Problem Gambling are a growing public health concern. The results of this review are anticipated to benefit researchers, clinicians and policymakers by providing a better understanding on key predictors of gambling-related harm in this population which would then aid in informing early intervention and prevention strategies. Protocol registration number PROSPERO 2026 CRD420261288057.",2026,Banozic,HRB Open Research,10.12688/hrbopenres.14344.2,,https://doi.org/10.12688/hrbopenres.14344.2,en,citations:review,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,R0092,,,
R0094,Network structure of gambling-related cognitions and risk behaviors among sports bettors in the Democratic Republic of the Congo: a cross-sectional EBICglasso network analysis (N = 2261).,"Background and aims: Sports betting is expanding rapidly in sub-Saharan Africa, yet the network structure of gambling-related cognitions and risk indicators remains understudied. This study analyzed these cognitions and their financial risk, psychological, and social correlates among DRC sports bettors. Methods: A cross-sectional sample of 2261 sports bettors (85.9% male; mean age = 25.59 years, SD = 5.79) was recruited at Kinshasa betting venues (March-May 2026). The Gambling-Related Cognitions Scale (GRCS; α = 0.924) was administered alongside financial, psychological, and social measures. Network structure was estimated using thresholded EBICglasso (γ = 0.5). Bridge centrality and sex-based invariance were assessed via NetworkComparisonTest (100,000 permutations). Results: The thresholded network retained 150 edges (density = 0.302). Guilt and G1 were the most central nodes. Bridge analysis identified guilt and social pressure as bridge nodes. Seven items showed zero bridge strength. Item-level network structure showed no global sex difference (M = 0.195, p = .121), whereas a 14-node sensitivity analysis detected an omnibus structural difference (M = 0.217, p = .022) not localized to any edge. After Bonferroni and FDR corrections, applied separately to 496 edge-level and 90 centrality-level tests, one edge (G1-G15), but no centrality index, differed by sex. Conclusions: This is among the first network analyses of gambling-related cognitions in sub-Saharan Africa, with Gambling Expectancies as the most connected cognitive domain, G1 as a central hub, and guilt and social pressure as bridge nodes. These findings extend the Interaction of Person-Affect-Cognition-Execution (I-PACE) model and inform intervention research.",2026,Banzulu Bomba,Addictive behaviors reports,10.1016/j.abrep.2026.100746,42757076,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.abrep.2026.100746,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:research-article;Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref,5,False,False,False,,,,,
R0095,"Impacto de apuestas deportivas en menores: responsabilidad del operador, Estado y consumidor","Las apuestas deportivas digitales han experimentado un crecimiento sostenido, generando desafíos para la protección de niños, niñas y adolescentes debido a la facilidad de acceso, publicidad digital y mecanismos de registro, la presente investigación tuvo como objetivo analizar su impacto en menores y determinar la responsabilidad jurídica del operador, el Estado y los representantes legales, se realizó una revisión bibliográfica con enfoque cualitativo mediante análisis normativo, doctrinario y jurisprudencial, los resultados evidenciaron que la Constitución, el Código de la Niñez y Adolescencia y la normativa de protección de datos establecen una protección reforzada basada en el interés superior, la prioridad absoluta y la prevención de riesgos, se determinó que los operadores deben implementar mecanismos de identificación, verificación de edad y bloqueo; el Estado debe regular, supervisar y sancionar conforme a la ley; y los representantes legales deben ejercer una supervisión razonable, el análisis jurisprudencial evidenció que la regulación de las apuestas deportivas debe respetar los principios de legalidad, proporcionalidad, igualdad y seguridad jurídica, se concluye que la protección de los menores constituye una responsabilidad compartida como diferenciada, la limitada investigación sobre ludopatía infantil exige desarrollar estudios nacionales e implementar programas escolares de prevención, detección temprana, atención psicológica coordinada.",2026,Baque,Reincisol,10.65835/reincisol.v6(10)1910,,https://doi.org/10.65835/reincisol.v6(10)1910,,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0097,"THE RISE OF OPTIONS CONTRACTS IN SPORTS, THE POST-MURPHY REGULATORY CONUNDRUM, AND IMPLICATIONS ON FEDERALISM","The Supreme Court’s decision in Murphy v. N.C.A.A. dismantled the federal prohibition on state-authorized sports betting and returned its primary regulatory authority over to the states. Following Murphy, states adopted widely varying sports-betting regimes. At the same time, advances in financial technology and the growing popularity of prediction markets created a new form of event-based speculation operating outside the traditional sportsbook model. Platforms such as Kalshi began offering “yes-no” contracts tied to sporting outcomes through federally regulated exchanges overseen by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission, framing such products not as wagers, but as financial derivatives governed by the Commodity Exchange Act. These developments have exposed a regulatory fault line left unresolved post-Murphy. State regulators have argued that these contracts are functionally indistinguishable from sports betting and therefore subject to state gambling law. Meanwhile, exchanges such as Kalshi maintain that federal regulation preempts state enforcement efforts. The resulting litigation has forced courts to confront difficult questions concerning preemption, administrative law, and federalism. Some courts have emphasized the functional similarity between prediction markets and sports wagering in preserving state authority, while others have treated federal regulatory designation as sufficient to justify preemption. This Note argues that sports-based event contracts cannot be evaluated through formal classification alone. Rather, they reveal the unresolved tension between federal derivatives regulation and the state-centered sports wagering framework that emerged after Murphy.",2026,Barboza,University of New Hampshire Scholars Repository (University of New Hampshire at Manchester),,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0098,Hot Hand Effects: Analyzing MLB Starting Pitchers in Daily Fantasy Sports and Betting Markets,"This study investigates the existence and market response to the “hot hand” effect among Major League Baseball starting pitchers. Using a novel methodology that classifies pitchers as hot, cold, or neutral based on context-adjusted recent performance, we find strong evidence that short-term streaks predict daily fantasy sports (DFS) outcomes. However, these trends are not fully priced into DraftKings salaries—hot pitchers are underpriced and cold pitchers are overpriced—indicating pricing inefficiencies. In contrast, sportsbook betting lines tend to overreact, particularly for visiting hot and cold home starters. However, simulated betting strategies exploiting these trends do not yield statistically significant positive profits, suggesting that sportsbooks adjust prices to capitalize on user biases. By comparing DFS and betting markets, this paper demonstrates how institutional structures and incentives shape responses to performance signals. The findings offer new insight into pricing dynamics, market efficiency, and behavioral responses in modern sports analytics.",2026,Baris,International Journal of Sport Finance,10.1177/15586235251403232,,https://doi.org/10.1177/15586235251403232,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0099,Financial Opportunities and Challenges in the Indian Sports Industry: An Empirical Analysis,"The Indian sports industry has been undergoing a radical transformation and growth over the past decade with the commercialisation and media rights, sponsorships and fan engagement through digital platforms, Khelo India and the Fit India Movement are just a few of the changes that have been witnessed by the industry in this period. The success of these domestic leagues, Indian Premier League (IPL), Pro Kabaddi League (PKL) and Indian Super League (ISL) has led to a lot of investments from both domestic and international sources, which has led to a variety of revenue streams, such as broadcasting rights, merchandising, sports tourism, fantasy sports and analytics. However, there are structural problems in the sport: imbalanced infrastructure, cricket-centric funding, absence of cricket sports monetisation, governance issues and absence of financial transparency. The study is based on a quantitative, descriptive approach to understand the finances opportunities and challenges of the Indian sports industry empirically. The primary data was collected on the basis of a structured questionnaire having five point likert scale from 400 respondents which included athletes, sports administrators, journalists and sports enthusiasts who were analysed through reliability analysis, descriptive statistics, Pearson correlation and regression (ANOVA). The Cronbach's α values of all the measurement scales were within the acceptable range of 0.786-0.877. The results show that financial opportunities are highly correlated with financial problems (r = 0.591, p < 0.01), there is a strong relationship between the financial opportunities and new financial-management trends (F = 532.83, p < 0.001), the financial opportunities are a strong predictor of adoption of new financial resources (F = 373.35, p < 0.001), and the financial opportunities are a significant predictor of exploration of new opportunities and problems (F = 247.03, p < 0.001). The study finds that diversifying sources of revenue, use of FinTech and Data-driven decision making and public–private partnerships are essential for the financial sustainability of Indian sport in the long run.",2026,Barma,International Journal of Research & Technology,,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0100,Financial Determinants and Beliefs About Gambling Control: A Cross-National Structural Model of Problem Gambling Severity,"Gambling-related harm is increasingly recognized as a public health concern, yet the role of financial factors in problem gambling remains insufficiently understood. This study examined the associations of financial knowledge, risk-oriented financial behaviour, prudent financial attitudes, and financial anxiety with problem gambling severity. A focused Health Belief Model-informed framework was used to investigate whether perceived benefits of reducing gambling, perceived barriers to gambling reduction, and gambling-related self-efficacy statistically accounted for indirect associations between financial variables and Problem Gambling Severity Index scores. Data were collected from 2,000 adults who had gambled during the previous 12 months in Italy and the United Kingdom. The measurement structure was evaluated through exploratory and confirmatory factor analyses and cross-country measurement invariance testing. Direct, indirect, and total associations were subsequently examined using multi-group structural equation modelling. Financial anxiety was positively associated with problem gambling severity in both countries, whereas financial knowledge and prudent financial attitudes showed inverse associations. Risk-oriented financial behaviour showed a positive direct association with gambling severity in Italy but not in the United Kingdom. Perceived barriers emerged as the strongest HBM-related correlate of gambling severity and accounted for the principal supported indirect associations involving financial anxiety, financial knowledge, and prudent financial attitudes. Perceived benefits and self-efficacy were not independently associated with gambling severity. These findings suggest that gambling-related harm is associated not only with financial competence and vulnerability, but also with the perceived difficulty of reducing gambling. Prevention may therefore benefit from combining financial education with measures that reduce practical, cognitive, and emotional barriers to gambling control.",2026,Barone,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10553-9,42778717,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10553-9,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0101,Apostas e jogos online no brasil: desafios normativos e impactos sociais Online betting and gaming in Brazil: regulatory challenges and social impacts,"Com a crescente expansão das apostas e dos jogos online, o presente artigo analisou a necessidade e os desafios da regulamentação desse setor no Brasil, destacando as lacunas existentes no atual arcabouço jurídico e os riscos decorrentes da ausência de normas consistentes. A pesquisa teve como objetivo examinar criticamente as propostas legislativas e os modelos regulatórios em comparação com a realidade do mercado nacional, considerando também a influência da publicidade e a proteção dos consumidores. Para tanto, adotou-se uma abordagem bibliográfica e documental, baseada na análise de doutrinas, legislações, artigos científicos e estudos de caso. Os resultados evidenciam que, embora o país avance com normas recentes, ainda persistem fragilidades quanto à fiscalização, à prevenção da ludopatia e à ética publicitária. Conclui-se que uma regulamentação mais clara e integrada é essencial para garantir segurança jurídica, promover práticas responsáveis e equilibrar os interesses econômicos e sociais envolvidos.",2026,Barros,Revista ft,10.69849/wtme1v82,,https://doi.org/10.69849/wtme1v82,,crossref:journal-article,crossref,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0102,Apostas esportivas online e a recente regulamentação no Brasil: enquadramento e implicações da relação jurídica - Online sports betting and recent regulation in Brazil: framework and implications of the legal relationship,"Objetivo: analisar em que medida a relação jurídica entre plataformas digitais de apostas esportivas e seus usuários pode ser qualificada como relação de consumo, à luz do ordenamento jurídico brasileiro e das diretrizes do Código de Defesa do Consumidor, considerando a expansão das apostas online no Brasil e as lacunas regulatórias existentes quanto à proteção jurídica do usuário. Método: adotou-se o método dedutivo, com abordagem qualitativa, de caráter bibliográfico, documental e jurisprudencial, fundamentada na análise de legislação vigente, doutrina especializada e dados informativos pertinentes ao tema. Resultados: a análise evidenciou que a relação entre apostador e plataforma reúne os elementos característicos da relação de consumo, destacando-se a vulnerabilidade do consumidor digital frente às assimetrias informacionais, bem como os riscos comportamentais e econômicos associados às apostas esportivas online. Considerações finais: conclui-se que o reconhecimento da natureza consumerista dessa relação impõe às plataformas digitais deveres ampliados de transparência, informação e responsabilidade civil, além de demandar uma atuação regulatória mais efetiva do Estado, com vistas à proteção do consumidor e à mitigação dos impactos sociais decorrentes da atividade.",2026,Barros,"Revista Educação, Meio Ambiente e Saúde",,,,pt,openalex:article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex;openalex_non_en,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0103,Professionals’ Perspectives On the Role of Advanced Technologies in Responsible Gambling,"Problematic gambling is associated with substantial individual and societal harms, which may be intensified by the accessibility, anonymity, and captivating design of online gambling products. In this domain, advanced technologies (e.g., artificial intelligence and data analytics) are increasingly recommended for prevention, early detection, and harm reduction. This study aimed to examine the current and potential uses of advanced technologies in gambling. Forty-five semi-structured interviews were conducted with social/legal science (n = 24) and technology (n = 21) experts. Data were analysed using thematic analysis, beginning with a deductive approach followed by an inductive analysis. A total of 182 codes were identified from social/legal science professionals, and 228 from technology experts. Four overarching themes were identified: (i) characterisation of contemporary gambling; (ii) applications and implications of advanced technologies; (iii) regulatory frameworks and challenges; and (iv) recommendations and solutions. Profit-driven design practices, heightened vulnerabilities in online gambling environments, regulatory gaps, and concerns regarding algorithmic opacity and data use were highlighted. Advanced technologies were viewed as having a dual role, with potential both to exacerbate gambling-related harm and to enable earlier identification of risky behaviours and targeted interventions. Findings indicate that responsible gambling cannot rely solely on technological solutions (e.g., behavioural monitoring and limit-setting) and should be instead integrated with community-level strategies, public education, and robust regulatory oversight. Leveraging technological innovation alongside user protection is essential, requiring interdisciplinary collaboration, a public health-oriented approach, and transparent, evidence-based and ethically governed frameworks. Implications, limitations, and future directions are discussed.",2026,Barroso,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10525-z,42393439,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10525-z,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0104,"Dermatoses in Aquatic Sports: Pools, Fresh Waters, and Marine Environments",,2026,Basto,,10.13140/rg.2.2.13570.75206,,https://doi.org/10.13140/rg.2.2.13570.75206,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0105,"Betting on health: gambling disorder, psychological comorbidity, and the neglect of oral self-care in the post-PASPA United States","In the United States, the Supreme Court’s 2018 decision in Murphy v. National Collegiate Athletic Association struck down the federal ban on sports wagering, triggering rapid state-by-state legalization; by 2025, legal sports betting was available in 38 states and the District of Columbia, generating nearly $17 billion in commercial revenue on an estimated $167 billion in total wagers. National data indicate that roughly one in five American adults placed a legal sports bet in 2025, and helpline records show a marked rise in financial distress, depression, and relationship harm among callers who identify sports betting as their primary problem. Gambling disorder (GD), reclassified by the DSM-5 as a behavioral addiction within the same diagnostic category as substance-related disorders, carries this heavy psychiatric and financial burden, yet its oral health consequences remain almost entirely unexamined in the dental literature. This narrative review synthesizes international and United States-specific evidence on the psychological consequences of gambling disorder and proposes behavioral and physiological pathways linking this addiction to oral disease and self-neglect. The literature shows psychiatric comorbidity exceeding 80% among individuals with GD, disproportionate sleep disruption compatible with nocturnal bruxism, and an established caries and periodontal disease burden among individuals with other addictive disorders that plausibly extends to problem gamblers. We argue that gambling disorder compromises oral health primarily through the erosion of self-care rather than through direct toxic exposure, a mechanism compounded in the United States by a dental care system that remains structurally separate from general medical coverage and heavily dependent on out-of-pocket payment.",2026,Bastos,South Florida Journal of Environmental and Animal Science,10.53499/sfjeasv6n2-003,,https://doi.org/10.53499/sfjeasv6n2-003,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article,openalex;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0106,"Markets Without Pause Regulating Online Gambling in an Era of Velocity, Revenue, and Risk","&lt;p&gt;Online sports betting has become structurally embedded in the American economy. Since the Supreme Court’s decision in &lt;i&gt;Murphy v. NCAA&lt;/i&gt; (2018), legal sports wagering has expanded to most U.S. states, generating more than $120 billion in annual handle and over $10 billion in gross gaming revenue (American Gaming Association, 2024). State governments now rely on gambling-related receipts for education funding, infrastructure support, and budget stabilization.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The scale of the market makes prohibition implausible. Gambling is no longer peripheral; it is fiscally and culturally integrated.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Yet the contemporary gambling environment differs fundamentally from its twentieth-century predecessor. Digital platforms have eliminated friction between impulse and wager. In-play betting multiplies the number of wagering events per hour. Payment systems allow instantaneous deposits and rapid redeposit following losses. Algorithmic personalization calibrates promotional intensity to individual behavior.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;These structural changes alter the distribution of risk.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Across mature gambling markets, revenue is not evenly distributed. A relatively small minority of high-intensity users typically generate a majority of operator revenue (UK Gambling Commission, 2022; Productivity Commission, 2010). This concentration has fiscal and political implications. Reducing high-intensity losses may reduce tax receipts; failing to moderate concentration may increase volatility and backlash risk.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;International evidence suggests that regulatory architecture — not legalization alone — determines stability. Jurisdictions that introduced centralized self-exclusion, default deposit limits, and data transparency early have generally experienced less political instability than those that expanded rapidly without structural guardrails.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;This paper argues that the United States stands at a transitional moment. The market is large but not yet politically destabilized. Concentration patterns are observable but not yet widely reported. Youth exposure is rising but not yet driving crisis legislation.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;Reform undertaken during stability can be calibrated and incremental. Reform undertaken after scandal tends to be blunt and disruptive.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;The policy objective is not prohibition. It is structural sustainability: reducing loss velocity and concentration while preserving legal channelization and fiscal realism.&lt;/p&gt;",2026,Bate,SSRN Electronic Journal,10.2139/ssrn.6344278,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6344278,und,openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,openalex;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0107,PSYTURK,"# PSY-TURK Study 1: Substance-Related and Behavioral Addiction-Risk Indicators Among University Students in Türkiye ## Project Overview This repository contains the materials associated with PSY-TURK Study 1, a cross-sectional investigation of substance-related and behavioral addiction-risk indicators among university students in Türkiye. The study examined alcohol- and drug-related risk using the CAGE-AID and assessed addiction-related burden across ten behavioral domains, including compulsive shopping, problematic social media use, food-related addictive behavior, gaming, sex/pornography, gambling, sports betting, exercise, cryptocurrency investment, and internet broadcasting/live streaming. The project aimed to estimate prevalence, examine demographic correlates, evaluate overlap between substance-related and behavioral risk indicators, and assess the robustness of findings across alternative behavioral-threshold systems. ## Repository Contents This repository includes: * Analysis scripts used to prepare, score, and analyze the data. * Variable definitions and scoring procedures. * Behavioral-threshold specifications. * Supplementary analysis materials and documentation. * Reproducibility files necessary to recreate the reported analyses. ## Data Availability The original dataset contains sensitive information regarding mental health, substance use, addictive behaviors, and related personal experiences. To protect participant confidentiality and comply with ethical obligations, the raw dataset is not publicly available. A de-identified analytic dataset may be made available upon reasonable request, subject to applicable ethical, legal, and institutional requirements. Shared datasets will exclude direct identifiers and may include additional recoding or aggregation of potentially identifying variables. ## Code Availability All analysis scripts required to reproduce the primary and supplementary analyses are available in this repository. The code is provided for transparency, reproducibility, and secondary methodological evaluation. ## Citation If you use materials from this repository, please cite the corresponding publication: Batmaz, S., et al. (in preparation). Substance-related and behavioral addiction-risk indicators among university students in Türkiye: Overlap, correlates, and threshold sensitivity. ## Contact For questions regarding the project, data access, or reproducibility materials, please contact the corresponding author.",2026,Batmaz,OSF Preprints (OSF Preprints),,,,,openalex:preprint,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0108,Statewide Mobile Sports Betting and Suicidal Ideation Among U.S. College Students,"This preregistered study will examine whether the introduction of statewide legal mobile sports betting is associated with changes in past-year serious suicidal ideation among U.S. college students ages 18-24. Using Healthy Minds Study data from 2015-2025 and staggered state adoption of mobile sports betting, the primary analysis will estimate the event +2 treatment effect using a pairwise-balanced cohort-specific difference-in-differences design. A prespecified secondary analysis will test whether associations are stronger among students legally eligible to wager.",2026,Bauer,Open Science Framework,10.17605/osf.io/hzf3j,,https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/hzf3j,,datacite:StudyRegistration/Pre-registration | openalex:other,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0109,"Title Evolution, Market Structure, and Behavioral Dynamics of the Online Sports Betting Industry Author: Minal Dutta Next Move Strategy Consulting",,2026,Baul,Unpublished,10.13140/rg.2.2.15494.95045,,https://doi.org/10.13140/rg.2.2.15494.95045,und,datacite:Text/Preprint | openaire:Preprint | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0110,Exploring the Influence of Financial Misconduct Due to Online Gambling on the Learning Behavior of College Students,"Online gambling has become a major global issue, increasingly linked to financial problems and poor academic performance among college students. This qualitative study investigated how financial misconduct arising from online gambling affects students' learning behavior. Focusing on the real-life experiences of eleven students at Isabela State University, Cauayan Campus, during the 2025–2026 academic year, the research explored how debt, borrowing, and chasing losses influenced their daily learning, academic focus, and overall well-being. Data were gathered through in-depth interviews and examined using thematic analysis. The study found that social media exposure, peer influence, curiosity, financial struggles, and gambling promotions are the primary catalysts for online betting. Financial misconduct led to severe consequences, including cognitive impairment in learning processes, the erosion of academic engagement and responsibility, and a sharp decline in academic performance, particularly in major subjects. Furthermore, students experienced debt as a survival tool, poor financial management, behavioral addiction, compulsion, deceptive behaviors, and significant threats to educational continuity, alongside emotional dysregulation and physical health deterioration. To cope, affected students utilized self-regulation, sought institutional help, and relied on external support systems. Ultimately, these findings underscore the critical need for universities to design comprehensive prevention and support programs. Such initiatives must address both the psychological and financial dimensions of student gambling to safeguard academic success and student well-being.",2026,Bautista,International Journal of Research and Innovation in Social Science,10.47772/ijriss.2026.100601186,,https://doi.org/10.47772/ijriss.2026.100601186,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0111,"The Sweepstakes Casino ""Loophole"" and the Case for State Regulation","&lt;div&gt; Ever since the advent of the internet, Americans have been seeking ways to gamble online. The Supreme Court helped spur growth in this way through its decision in Murphy v. NCAA, which opened the door to legalizing sports betting. Since then, seven states have legalized online gaming, allowing individuals to play slots, blackjack, roulette, and more on their phones. However, there are platforms that offer these exact activities and operate in most states, even though iGaming is only legal in a select few. These platforms call themselves sweepstakes casinos, claiming they fall under sweepstakes regulations rather than traditional gambling laws because they lack the consideration element required to fall under traditional gambling laws, thanks to their unique dual-currency system. Because they self-classify as a sweepstakes, they argue that their platforms operate within a legal loophole and cannot be regulated through usual methods. &lt;/div&gt; &lt;div&gt; &amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;Yet, looking deeper, these platforms seemingly offer nothing more than an easily accessible form of unregulated gambling. States have begun to take action against sweepstakes casinos; however, it has become increasingly clear that the only definite solution is to ban these platforms outright. If states are unwilling to classify sweepstakes casinos as unregulated gambling platforms under their existing regulatory scheme, they should pass statutes that provide a more precise definition of gambling within their borders. Further, courts faced with cases involving sweepstakes casinos should expand the gambling element of consideration to cover indirect forms of consideration, thereby eliminating the existing loophole these platforms exploit. A combined approach to these two solutions would not only solve this problem but also end the cat-and-mouse game that has plagued online gambling since its inception by strengthening gambling laws and limiting opportunities for future exploitation of regulatory loopholes. &lt;/div&gt;",2026,Beallis,Social Science Research Network,10.2139/ssrn.6264998,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6264998,eng,openaire:Article | openalex:preprint | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0112,"The Stranger at Home: Lived Experiences of Online Gambling Within the Family in Siirt, Türkiye",,2026,BEKTAŞ,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10552-w,42704516,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10552-w,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0113,Social media advertising by sports betting companies and its influence on consumer behavior,"Advertising for sports betting platforms on digital platforms has experienced exponential growth in recent years, capturing the attention of young people in particular through social media platforms such as X (formerly Twitter), TikTok, and YouTube. The overall objective of this study was to analyze the influence of digital sports betting advertising on the consumption behavior of young users in Ecuador, particularly in terms of their exposure to and decision-making regarding these platforms. The study employed a quantitative methodological approach supplemented by qualitative interviews, using a structured survey with a Likert scale and open-ended questions administered to users of betting platforms. The results show a high frequency of exposure to sports betting advertisements, a growing interest driven by promotions, and a widespread perception of betting as an acceptable form of entertainment, even though many users acknowledge the risks. It is concluded that advertising has a significant impact on the decision to bet, especially when it presents betting as safe and lucrative. It is recommended that clear regulatory frameworks, awareness campaigns, and critical digital literacy initiatives be implemented to mitigate the impact of advertising on vulnerable audiences.",2026,Belalcazar,Journal of business and entrepreneurial studie,10.37956/jbes.v10i3.417,,https://doi.org/10.37956/jbes.v10i3.417,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0114,"Socioeconomic Impact of Sports Betting and Youth Perspectives on Regulatory Solutions in Mwanza City, Tanzania","Sports betting has expanded rapidly across Tanzanian cities, raising concerns about its financial and social effects on youth. This study examined betting behavior and its socioeconomic consequences among youth in Mwanza City, Tanzania. A cross-sectional survey was conducted with 200 respondents aged 18 years and above who had previously engaged in sports betting. Participants were recruited from betting shops, mobile money outlets, and public gathering points across six wards. Data were analyzed using descriptive statistics, chi-square tests, and binary logistic regression. The findings revealed that betting was common, with 36.5% of respondents betting occasionally, 31.0% weekly, and 22.0% daily. Financial difficulties were widespread: 62.0% reported financial challenges, 55.5% experienced reduced savings, and 52.0% had borrowed money to continue betting. The most common economic consequence was difficulty meeting basic needs such as food and transport (34.0%). Social impacts were also evident, including family conflicts (54.0%) and social isolation (53.5%). These findings indicate that sports betting contributes to significant financial and social vulnerabilities among urban youth, highlighting the need for stronger regulation, public awareness campaigns, and accessible support services.",2026,Bella,NG Journal of Social Development,10.4314/ngjsd.v19i1.4,,https://doi.org/10.4314/ngjsd.v19i1.4,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0117,Influence of Digital Deviancy on the Learning of Behaviour the Boy-Child in Public Secondary Schools in Bayelsa State,"This study investigated influence of digital deviancy on the learning behaviour of the boy-child in public secondary schools in Bayelsa State, Nigeria. It was motivated by the increasing concerns that activities such as cyber fraud and online betting divert boys from their academic pursuits. Two research questions and null hypotheses guided the study, which employed a descriptive survey design. The population comprised all male senior secondary school students across 207 public secondary schools in the state, from which a sample of 248 students was drawn. Data were collected using a structured instrument titled the Influence of Digital Deviancy and Learning Behaviour Questionnaire (DDLBQ). Mean and standard deviation were used to address the research questions, while Pearson Product Moment Correlation (PPMC) was applied to test the hypotheses at a 0.05 level of significance. Findings indicated that involvement in cyber fraud (“Yahoo-Yahoo”) and online gambling both exert a strong negative influence on students' learning behaviour, with significant correlations observed in each case. The study concluded that digital deviancy constitutes a substantial counterforce to formal education among male students. It recommended that the Bayelsa State Ministry of Education adopt comprehensive digital citizenship programmes alongside targeted, gender- responsive interventions to mitigate these challenges.",2026,Berezi,International Journal of Innovative Research in Arts Education and Technology,10.48028/iiprds/ijiraet.v4.i1.08,,https://doi.org/10.48028/iiprds/ijiraet.v4.i1.08,,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0118,Is Access to Online Casino Gaming Responsible for Estimated Impacts of Online Sports Betting?,"The recent spread of online sports betting in the United States has led to a rapidly expanding literature which estimates impacts of this access on a wide variety of outcomes. We show, however, that much of the variation in access to online sports betting is confounded with access to online casino gaming. Additionally, a majority of industry revenues across important subsets of treated states come from casino gaming rather than sports betting. We then replicate one early published study estimating the impacts of access to mobile sports betting on self-reported mental health, and illustrate the degree to which their results are robust to the exclusion of states with legalized casino gaming. Given the data used in other studies, as well as when impacts are most pronounced relative to legalization and COVID-induced sports shutdowns in 2020, this observation calls into question whether the estimated impacts should properly be attributed to legalized sports betting.",2026,Bersak,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.6863738,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6863738,und,crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0122,NBA Player Proposition Markets: A Two-Tier Machine Learning System with Five-Month Out-of-Sample Validation,"This paper presents a validated two-tier betting system for NBA player proposition markets, developed using XGBoost, Random Forest and GLMNet models trained independently per market. A five-month walk-forward out-of-sample test across 13 markets confirmed both tiers independently profitable every month. Tier 1 (model probability >= 0.80) achieved 16.0% average monthly ROI from a $2,000 starting bankroll, reaching $12,290. Tier 2 value bets (probability 0.60-0.79) achieved 28.2% average monthly ROI, reaching $23,321. Kelly criterion sizing with per-bet and daily caps is detailed. Markets with negative out-of-sample performance were permanently excluded. Three-gate validation criteria ensured zero selection bias and zero data leakage. The system is implemented at betbetter.world.",2026,Bet Better Pty Ltd,Zenodo,10.5281/zenodo.19077987,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19077987,en,datacite:Preprint/,datacite,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R0123,Jogos instantâneos online: o que são e como funcionam em 2026,"Jogos instantâneos online: o que são e como funcionam em 2026 Os jogos instantâneos online são a categoria que mais cresce no mercado brasileiro de entretenimento digital: a rodada começa e termina em segundos, sem download, sem espera pelo apito final de um jogo e sem acompanhar animações longas de rolos. Você escolhe o título, define o valor, confirma e vê o desfecho quase imediatamente. Neste guia explicamos o que são esses jogos, como funcionam por dentro, quais formatos existem hoje, por que ganharam tanto espaço no Brasil e quais cuidados separam diversão de prejuízo. O que exatamente é um jogo instantâneo Um jogo instantâneo é um formato em que o resultado é definido por um gerador de números aleatórios (RNG) e revelado em poucos segundos. Diferente dos slots tradicionais, não há uma ""esteira"" visual longa de símbolos; diferente das apostas esportivas, não existe um evento externo cujo desfecho precise acontecer. A rodada é autossuficiente: começa, resolve e fecha no mesmo instante. Essa característica muda a experiência de três formas. Primeiro, o ritmo: em vez de uma rodada a cada trinta segundos, o jogador pode fazer várias por minuto. Segundo, a leitura do resultado, que é binária e imediata — ganhou ou não ganhou. Terceiro, o controle de orçamento, que passa a depender inteiramente da disciplina do usuário, porque a velocidade não dá tempo para pensar entre uma rodada e outra. Como funcionam por dentro Todo jogo instantâneo sério roda sobre três pilares técnicos. 1. Gerador de números aleatórios (RNG). O resultado de cada rodada é sorteado por software auditado. Em plataformas reguladas, o RNG é testado por laboratórios independentes para garantir que os resultados sejam imprevisíveis e que a margem declarada seja a real. 2. Retorno ao jogador (RTP). O RTP indica quanto, em média, volta ao jogador ao longo de milhões de rodadas. Um RTP de 96% significa que a plataforma retém 4% no longo prazo. É uma média estatística, não uma promessa para a sua sessão. 3. Volatilidade. Diz o quão raros e quão grandes são os prêmios. Baixa volatilidade paga pouco e com frequência; alta volatilidade paga raramente e em valores maiores. Dois jogos com o mesmo RTP podem ter sensações completamente diferentes por causa disso. Quais formatos existem Crash e multiplicadores: um valor sobe e o jogador precisa sacar antes de ele ""estourar"". Simples de entender e extremamente rápido. Dados e roletas instantâneas: variantes curtas que resolvem em uma única rodada. Minijogos estilo ""conquista"" e ""aviator"": curvas visuais curtas com ponto de saída definido pelo usuário. Sorteios numéricos: o usuário escolhe um intervalo e o resultado sai na hora. Todos compartilham a mesma lógica: decisão rápida, resultado imediato, rodada curta. Por que cresceram tanto no Brasil Três fatores explicam a popularidade. A penetração do celular tornou o formato acessível em qualquer lugar. O Pix reduziu o atrito de depósito e saque a poucos toques. E o próprio formato casa bem com sessões curtas de lazer — a pessoa joga cinco minutos no intervalo, não precisa reservar uma hora. O resultado é uma categoria que reúne a simplicidade dos slots com a sensação de controle das apostas de evento, mas sem a espera de nenhum dos dois. Como escolher onde jogar Antes de qualquer rodada, vale verificar quatro pontos objetivos: Regulamentação e licença da operadora. RTP e volatilidade declarados de cada jogo. Limites de depósito, perda e tempo definidos por você, não pela plataforma. Velocidade e transparência do saque, especialmente em Pix. Quem faz essa checagem antes joga com informação; quem pula direto para a rodada joga no escuro. Se você quer ver como esses formatos aparecem na prática, com regras explicadas e exemplos de rodada passo a passo, temos um material dedicado em https://betaobetcasino.online/jogos-instantaneos/ — com a mecânica descrita em detalhe, sem promessa de ganho fácil. Bônus, limites e jogo responsável Bônus têm condições: exigência de aposta, prazo de validade, teto de saque e exclusões. Um bônus só vale a pena quando as condições cabem no seu jeito de jogar — não o contrário. E o ponto mais importante de todos: jogo instantâneo é entretenimento, não fonte de renda. Defina um orçamento fixo antes de começar, nunca persiga perdas, faça pausas e use as ferramentas de autoexclusão se sentir que a linha foi ultrapassada. Se o jogo deixar de ser diversão, ele deixou de fazer sentido. Perguntas frequentes Jogo instantâneo é confiável? Quando a plataforma é licenciada e o RNG é auditado por terceiros, sim — a imprevisibilidade e o RTP podem ser verificados. Dá para ganhar dinheiro de forma consistente? Não como rotina. É entretenimento com resultado aleatório: existem ganhos e existem perdas, e o RTP garante que a casa fica com uma margem no longo prazo. Qual é a diferença para os slots? A velocidade e a apresentação. Nos instantâneos a rodada resolve em segundos e não há esteira de símbolos; nos slots, a animação é parte da experiência. Preciso baixar aplicativo? Não. Os formatos instantâneos são feitos para rodar direto no navegador do celular. Como controlar o orçamento? Definindo um limite antes de jogar, separando o dinheiro de lazer e ignorando a tentativa de ""recuperar"" perdas. A velocidade do formato é justamente o que torna esse limite obrigatório. Referências Wikipedia — Gambling Wikipedia — Problem gambling",2026,Betão,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.23212612,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23212612,pt,openaire:Report | openalex_non_en:report,openalex_non_en;openaire,2,False,False,False,,R0124,,,
