R0125,Multi-agentic Framework for Crafting Fantasy 11 Cricket Teams,,2026,Bhatnagar,Learning and analytics in intelligent systems,10.1007/978-981-95-5441-6_23,,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-981-95-5441-6_23,en,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0126,Optimal Online Bookmaking for Binary Games,"In online betting, the bookmaker can update the payoffs it offers on a particular event many times before the event takes place, and the updated payoffs may depend on the bets accumulated thus far. We study the problem of bookmaking with the goal of maximizing the return in the worst-case, with respect to the gamblers’ behavior and the event’s outcome. We formalize this problem as the Optimal Online Bookmaking game, and provide the exact solution for the binary case. To this end, we develop the optimal bookmaking strategy, which relies on a new technique called bi-balancing trees, that assures that the house loss is the same for all decisive betting sequences, where the gambler bets all its money on a single outcome in each round.",2026,Bhatt,IEEE Transactions on Information Theory,10.1109/tit.2026.3694366,,https://doi.org/10.1109/tit.2026.3694366,,s2:JournalArticle,s2,1,False,False,False,,R1440,,,
R0127,"Représentations sociales et enjeux de santé publique autour des jeux de hasard dans les villes de Cotonou, Dassa-Zoumè et Parakou au Bénin","Les jeux de hasard occupent une place croissante dans les activités récréatives et économiques au Bénin, notamment à Cotonou, Dassa-Zoumè et Parakou. Leur expansion, portée par les paris sportifs, les jeux en ligne, la loterie et les machines à sous, s’accompagne d’un intérêt accru des populations mais aussi de préoccupations de santé publique. Malgré cette popularité, les perceptions sociales qui façonnent les pratiques et influencent les comportements demeurent peu documentées. Cette étude qualitative analyse les représentations, attitudes et motivations liées aux jeux de hasard dans trois communes du pays. La collecte repose sur des entretiens semi-directifs, des focus groups et des observations de terrain, permettant de saisir la diversité des discours et des contextes. Les résultats révèlent des perceptions contrastées : le jeu est perçu à la fois comme une opportunité économique, une source de divertissement et un espace de sociabilité, mais aussi comme une activité risquée susceptible d’entraîner des pertes financières, de la dépendance et des tensions familiales. Ces perceptions influencent fortement les comportements individuels et collectifs, notamment chez les jeunes. Les données soulignent la nécessité de renforcer les actions de prévention, de sensibilisation et de régulation, afin de limiter les risques sanitaires et sociaux associés aux jeux de hasard. Gambling has become increasingly prominent within recreational and economic activities in Benin, particularly in Cotonou, Dassa-Zoumè, and Parakou. Its expansion, driven by sports betting, online gambling, lottery games, and slot machines, has sparked growing public interest, but also significant public health concerns. Despite this rise in popularity, the social perceptions that shape gambling practices and influence behavioral patterns remain poorly documented. This qualitative study explores representations, attitudes, and motivations related to gambling across three municipalities in the country. Data collection relied on semi‑structured interviews, focus group discussions, and field observations, allowing for a nuanced understanding of the diversity of discourses and contexts. The findings reveal contrasting perceptions: gambling is viewed simultaneously as an economic opportunity, a source of entertainment, and a social space, but also as a risky activity that can lead to financial loss, addiction, and family tensions. These perceptions strongly influence both individual and collective behaviors, particularly among young people. The evidence highlights the need to strengthen prevention, awareness‑raising, and regulatory measures in order to mitigate the health and social risks associated with gambling.",2026,Biaou,Revue Africaine des Sciences Sociales et de la Santé Publique,,,https://revue-rasp.org/rasp/article/view/945,fra/fre,openaire:Article,openaire,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R0128,"Social representations and public health issues related to gambling in the cities of Cotonou, Dassa-Zoumè, and Parakou, Benin","Les jeux de hasard occupent une place croissante dans les activités récréatives et économiques au Bénin, notamment à Cotonou, Dassa-Zoumè et Parakou. Leur expansion, portée par les paris sportifs, les jeux en ligne, la loterie et les machines à sous, s’accompagne d’un intérêt accru des populations mais aussi de préoccupations de santé publique. Malgré cette popularité, les perceptions sociales qui façonnent les pratiques et influencent les comportements demeurent peu documentées. Cette étude qualitative analyse les représentations, attitudes et motivations liées aux jeux de hasard dans trois communes du pays. La collecte repose sur des entretiens semi-directifs, des focus groups et des observations de terrain, permettant de saisir la diversité des discours et des contextes. Les résultats révèlent des perceptions contrastées : le jeu est perçu à la fois comme une opportunité économique, une source de divertissement et un espace de sociabilité, mais aussi comme une activité risquée susceptible d’entraîner des pertes financières, de la dépendance et des tensions familiales. Ces perceptions influencent fortement les comportements individuels et collectifs, notamment chez les jeunes. Les données soulignent la nécessité de renforcer les actions de prévention, de sensibilisation et de régulation, afin de limiter les risques sanitaires et sociaux associés aux jeux de hasard.",2026,Biaou,Revue Africaine des Sciences Sociales et de la Sante Publique,,,,fr,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0129,"Troubles liés à l’usage des jeux de hasard et d’argent chez les adolescents et jeunes adultes en Afrique subsaharienne : analyse exploratoire de la prévalence et des facteurs associés en milieu universitaire au Bénin, 2025","INTRODUCTION: Gambling is growing at an alarming rate in Benin, particularly among students. This population, seeking independence and success, is especially vulnerable to the risks of disorders associated with these practices. AIM OF THE STUDY: to determine the prevalence of, and identify factors associated with disorders related to gambling in universities in Benin. METHODS: This is a cross-sectional analytical study involving 1,360 Beninese students. Gambling-related disorders were assessed using the diagnostic criteria of the fifth edition of the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders (DSM-V). Associated factors were explored using multiple logistic regression. RESULTS: Of the 1,360 students, 455 (33.5%) were gamblers, 89 of whom were classified as having gambling-related disorders, representing a prevalence of 6.5% among all students and 19.6% [95% CI: 15.9%–23.2%] among gamblers. The distribution of disorders was as follows: 12.1% mild, 6.8% moderate, and 0.7% severe. After adjustment, factors associated with these disorders were age (p=0.007), having a gambling app on one’s phone (p<0.001), friends’ influence on decision-making (p=0.014), frequency of gambling (p=0.012), betting with money belonging to others (p=0.006), duration of engagement in online gambling (p=0.032), and anxiety (p<0.001). CONCLUSION: This study highlights the extent of gambling-related disorders in Beninese universities. It emphasizes the need for stricter regulation and targeted preventive measures to address this still insufficiently recognized public health problem.",2026,Biaou,Santé Publique,10.3917/spub.263.0279,42838904,https://doi.org/10.3917/spub.263.0279,fr,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0130,"An uneven playing field: a mixed methods, multiphase feasibility study of a programme to reduce gambling among at-risk men in a professional football club setting.","BackgroundSports betting is a growth area for the gambling industry, with football fans a key target ofadvertising. Men are particularly at risk from gambling harm. Prior research has shown thatthe delivery of health behaviour-related interventions through professional football clubscan attract and support health behaviour change among men. The 8-week Football Fansand Betting (FFAB) programme was designed as an early health behaviour changeintervention for delivery in professional football clubs by club community coaches toreduce their sports betting and other forms of gambling among men aged 18-55 with aPGSI score of 15 and under. This paper reports the acceptability and feasibility of delivering thisprogramme in football clubs in England.MethodsThe FFAB feasibility study’s objectives were to determine whether FFAB has the potentialto reduce gambling behaviour by assessing: 1) recruitment to and retention in theprogramme, 2) the acceptability of FFAB to both club coaches and participants, and 3) togather preliminary evidence on potential impact. To meet aim 1, we initiated recruitmentat 6 clubs and recorded recruitment data throughout, and conducted focus groups onrecruitment materials with fans of Club C-F. For aim 2, we collected data on retentionthroughout our programme deliveries. For aim 3, we completed delivery of FFAB at asmany clubs as possible (n=3); observed the delivery of sessions; completed semi-structuredinterviews with coaches, participants, and non-completers.Results1) Recruitment to the programmeRecruiting the intended number of participants proved highly challenging. This was partly dueto the commercial landscape, where sponsorship by gambling companies lessened club interestin the programme. Even in clubs without direct gambling sponsors, FFAB ads struggled forvisibility against more prominent gambling sponsorships in stadiums. Moreover, stigmasurrounding gambling harm hindered participant recruitment and retention, compounded bybroader commercial challenges. Engaging the target group of the FFAB model was difficultbecause they perceived gambling as normal, making it challenging to connect with FFAB'sobjectives.2) Retention in the programmeRetention to the programme faced similar challenges as recruitment – the commercial landscapeand stigma being key challenges FFAB did not overcome. Retention at our first two programmedeliveries struggled. The final delivery used a more local-hub approach to recruitment andbolted FFAB’s classroom element on to an existing social football game and was successful inretaining its small cohort from session 0-9.3) The acceptability of FFAB to both club coaches and participantsCoaches and participants reported that FFAB was acceptable and that there is a need forearly intervention delivered in this setting. Participants who went through the programmeand the coaches who delivered it reported positive experiences of FFAB.4) Gather preliminary data on potential impactAlthough we were able to carry out our research the FFAB programme facedchallenges. This meant that it was not possible to progress to an RCT to assess impactas intended. However preliminary data from men who completed the programmesuggest that 1) there is a need for an intervention to help men address gambling harm inthe sports space, 2) these men are an underserved population, with sometimes complexneeds, and 3) they often do not recognise the signs of gambling harm and experiencestigma around their behaviour.ConclusionsWe found that FFAB was acceptable to the coaches who delivered it and the participantswho attended. However, our model for recruitment did not work. We also faced difficultieswith retention. More feasibility work to develop a different approach to a gamblingreduction with men between 18-55 with a PGSI score of less than 15 is required.",2026,Biggar,BMC public health,10.1186/s12889-026-26845-z,41781913,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-026-26845-z,eng,datacite:Collection/Collection | europepmc:research-article;Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex:preprint | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;datacite;openaire,6,False,False,False,,,,,
R0131,When Sports Betting Addiction Takes Hold: Creation and Validation of the Sports Betting Addiction Index,"The mainstreaming and legalization of sports betting in over three quarters of the country has given rise to significant increases in sport betting addiction (Yeola et al., 2025). One problem in determining the overall addiction levels is that most mainstream gambling addiction measures are (a) not sport-specific and (b) several decades old. Given the role mobile media plays in easing the placement of bets and consumption of sports media in general, there is substantial need to establish a robust, valid measure that reflects contemporary sports fan betting behaviors. This presentation would present the pilot results for the establishment of a Sports Betting Addiction Index (SBAI). Using Netemeyer et. al’s (2003) index development framework as guidance, the pilot will unfold via the following structure: (a) identification of key literature to build an expert list, (b) solicitation of input from the expert panel, (c) generation of proposed measurement items, and (d) pilot testing. Findings would include the advancement of multiple subfactors of sports betting addiction along with relevant demographic correlates that could help crystallize when interventions are warranted. Implications for sports management, media, marketing, and psychology will all be imbued within the creation and advancement of this pilot data.",2026,Billings,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0133,The Crisis of Modern Sport; Toward a Phenomenology of the Athletic Body,"What does it mean to be an athlete? Philosophers of sport have largely focused on either the formal structure of games or the aesthetic unity of the skilled body in action, yet the concept of athleticism has been underdeveloped, often used to denote any participant in sport. This work develops a phenomenological conception of athletic pursuit. I argue that athletic pursuits put our bodies out of their everyday functionality, revealing what is normally lost in the mundane. In this sense, athletics serves as a horizon for the disclosure of the body. With this understanding, I consider how this path of self-discovery is put at risk by contemporary sporting structures. The tendency for commercialized sport to engage in its own simulation (televised events, sports video games, and the promulgation of sports betting) has made sports leagues billions of dollars, but it has misunderstood the purpose of sport. By returning to its phenomenological essence, we arrive at a deeper appreciation of sport and a framework for an ethics of athletic pursuit.",2026,Blair,KiltHub Repository,10.1184/r1/33416614,,https://doi.org/10.1184/r1/33416614,,datacite:Dissertation/Thesis | openalex:dissertation,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0134,Gambling and wealth acquisition in Ghana: A biblical-theological and ethical critique of Ghana’s betting culture,"The acquisition and use of wealth have significant implications for an individual’s relationship with God, society, and the environment. In Ghana, gambling—particularly sports betting—has become a widespread economic activity among the youth, largely due to high unemployment rates, the aggressive marketing strategies of betting companies, and a prevalent get-rich-quick mentality. However, the ethicality of gambling remains a subject of theological and moral debate, especially within Christian circles. The key research question that this paper addresses is: To what extent does gambling align with biblical-theological principles of wealth acquisition and ethical financial stewardship? The paper employed a literature-based research approach, analyzing biblical texts, theological discourses, and scholarly works to evaluate the moral and ethical dimensions of gambling. The methodological steps included a contextual examination of gambling in contemporary Ghana, biblical-theological analysis of relevant biblical texts on wealth creation and distribution, and a critique of gambling based on a biblical-theological ethics on wealth. The central argument of this paper is that while the Bible does not explicitly condemn gambling, its teachings on wealth acquisition, responsible stewardship, and social justice provide a framework for assessing the practice. The study found that gambling not only promotes a mindset of quick wealth accumulation (which contradicts biblical principles of diligence, honest labor, and ethical financial management) but also contributes to social and economic inequalities by promoting financial irresponsibility and exacerbating poverty. This research contributes to the discourse on theology, ethics, and economic justice by providing a biblically grounded framework for evaluating contemporary financial practices.",2026,Boaheng,E-Journal of Humanities Arts and Social Sciences,10.38159/ehass.2026747,,https://doi.org/10.38159/ehass.2026747,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0135,Gambling Research in Sport Management: A Scoping Review,"The purpose of this study was to conduct a scoping review of the sport management literature focusing on sports gambling. The authors examined n = 101 articles from two online databases (Scopus and JSTOR), using the Joanna Briggs Institute (2020) scoping review framework. Five consistently present themes were found in the literature from the thematic analysis: consumer behavior, market performance/efficiency, cheating/integrity of sport, the impact of fandom, and corporate social responsibility. Results show that the largest theme within the sport management research about gambling focuses on consumer behavior (39.6%), and researchers predominantly employ quantitative research methods (41.5%). In addition, scholars from the United States have dominated the published literature on sports gambling (49.5%), despite only recent legalization of the practice. Overall, this scoping review suggests that scholars should look for cross-cultural phenomena in the future concerning sports gambling within the sport management literature. Furthermore, researchers should place emphasis on corporate social responsibility and multimethod and multisport studies.",2026,Boddy,Journal of Sport Management,10.1123/jsm.2025-0217,,https://doi.org/10.1123/jsm.2025-0217,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0136,The Co-Development of ESPN+ and College Sports: A Case Study,"In 2018 ESPN debuted its national streaming service ESPN+. This profoundly changed the way sports fans watch college sports as well as other professional sporting events. Prior to ESPN+, a limited number of college sports games appeared on linear channels such as ESPN, ESPN2 and ESPNU, and occasionally a few other channels. Since 2018, ESPN+ streamed thousands of events per year ranging from college soccer to college softball and everything in between, including the qualifying and early rounds of professional tennis and golf tournaments from around the country. This case study describes the impact of ESPN+ on a college conference, a university, a sample of its coaches, and a sample of its student-athletes. Prior to EPSN+, league officials and college administrators reported to experience relative invisibility and funding struggles especially related to recruiting. After ESPN+ they report visibility has increased and the school is benefitting from better income streams. However, sports relying on international recruitment have yet to benefit. And like all other colleges, struggle to deliver high quality visuals to fans. For coaches, before ESPN+ recruiting meant extensive travel, time, and expense. Today digital recruiting (via video) is far more cost effective. For players, ESPN+ has dramatically increased their screentime and visibility, changed recruiting/joining practices, contributed to players’ NIL, and more. For all these stakeholders, this study also reports that a somewhat hidden economic driver of the rise of ESPN+ for all these stakeholders is linked to online sports gambling. Implications for future research are offered.",2026,Bohannon,ODU Digital Commons (Old Dominion University),10.25777/5q77-fx50,,https://doi.org/10.25777/5q77-fx50,,datacite:Text/Thesis | openalex:dissertation,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0137,"Your choice, your team: psychological ownership and AI decision-making","Purpose This study examines how psychological ownership influences user satisfaction in artificial intelligence (AI)-assisted decision-making contexts within sport consumption. While prior research has focused primarily on algorithmic accuracy and outcome quality, it has paid limited attention to users' perceived agency during the decision-making process. This study addresses this gap by investigating the role of autonomy and psychological ownership in shaping consumer responses. Design/methodology/approach A 2 × 2 experimental design was conducted using a fantasy sport decision-making scenario. Participants were randomly assigned to conditions manipulating decision autonomy and AI involvement. Data were analyzed using regression-based moderation analysis. Findings Results revealed a suppression pattern in which self-selection increased psychological ownership, producing a positive indirect effect on satisfaction, while its direct effect on satisfaction was negative. These opposing effects resulted in a nonsignificant total effect. Originality/value This study extends psychological ownership theory into AI-supported sport consumption contexts by demonstrating that decision agency can generate both psychological benefits through increased ownership and satisfaction costs through a negative direct pathway. The findings contribute to both sport consumer behavior literature and emerging research on human–AI interaction.",2026,Bok,Sport Business and Management An International Journal,10.1108/sbm-03-2026-0106,,https://doi.org/10.1108/sbm-03-2026-0106,en,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0138,Reinforcement Learning for Game Playing,"This study examines reinforcement learning (RL) in sports gambling, focusing on robots learning to navigate complex mazes. Using Q-learning and Deep Q-Networks algorithms, the agents optimize their decision-making over time. The study evaluates RL strategies’ effectiveness, considering convergence time, system performance, and dynamic environment adaptability. The findings highlight the potential of RL in navigation problems and will enhance research in independent robotics and AI-based game techniques.",2026,Boligala,CRC Press eBooks,10.1201/9781042005376-111,,https://doi.org/10.1201/9781042005376-111,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0139,"Pay to Play: Behavioral and Demographic Patterns in Problematic Gambling, Sports Betting, and Within-Title Video Game Spending","Since a federal ruling in 2018, legalized online sports betting in the US generated $26 billion of revenue in 2025. A parallel digital market is in video gaming, where in-game transactions comprised $30 billion in the US in 2025. Some in-game purchasing mechanics have risk-reward pathways identical to gambling, where real money is spent for outcomes that are not guaranteed, such as loot boxes and gachas. Research has established parallels to gambling for these types of purchases, including connections to problematic behaviors. To better understand the links between sports betting, in-game transactions, and problematic gambling, this study examined the overlap of these activities across individuals from diverse demographics. Over 500 online participants were selected for either video game play or sports betting. They reported frequency of sports betting, in-game purchasing, and completed the PGSI, a measure of gambling harm. Higher PGSI scores were associated with greater sports betting and in-game spending (rhos>.5). Behaviors varied by demographics, with higher scores for men (all measures), those identifying as Black compared to White (all measures) and Asian (sports betting), and older individuals (sports betting). This suggests that underlying mechanisms shared by sports betting and video game transactions are associated with problematic behaviors. We posit that demographic differences may be due to beliefs and norms associated within subcultures of these groups.",2026,Bonny,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
