R0490,Youth Sports Development in Ghana,"This chapter critically analyses the fragmented landscape of youth sports development in Ghana and its impacts on participation, accessibility, and equity. We argue that the current system, fragmented across the Ministry of Youth and Sports (elite-focused), the Ministry of Education (community-focused), and the Ministry of Gender, Children and Social Protection (gender and disability protection-focused) hinders progress. Youth sport sits at the heart of the nation’s identity, but participation remains uneven – existing policies and strategies, while well-intentioned, face challenges due to political implementation. The chapter further contends that the shift from traditional, freely accessible playing fields to commercially driven, gated AstroTurf pitches potentially creates barriers to access, disenfranchises, participation in community sport and fuels the rise of sport betting among Ghanaian youth. The chapter proposes a collaborative, multi-ministerial approach to youth sports development to address these issues. This approach would emphasise grassroots sport-for-all initiatives, inclusive District Sports Units, and targeted programmes for underrepresented groups. Finally, the chapter recommends stronger partnerships between state and non-state sport organisations, including national sport associations (embodiment of local sport clubs) and Sport for Development, and Peace NGOs. Ghana can cultivate a more inclusive and equitable youth sport landscape by leveraging expertise and resources across these sectors.",2026,Charway,Routledge eBooks,10.4324/9781003503910-13,,https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003503910-13,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0491,All‐cause and cause‐specific mortality in gambling disorder: Evidence from a nation‐wide matched and sibling cohort study in Taiwan,"BACKGROUND AND AIMS: Gambling disorder (GD) has been linked to suicidal ideation and suicide deaths; however, evidence on all-cause and cause-specific mortality-particularly in Asian populations-remains limited. Using a retrospective cohort study based on nationwide matched and sibling cohort, we investigated all-cause and cause-specific mortality risk in patients with GD. DESIGN, SETTING AND PARTICIPANTS: A retrospective cohort study was conducted using 2000-2022 data from Taiwan's National Health Insurance Research Database. A cohort of 961 individuals diagnosed with GD was identified. An age- and sex-matched control cohort (n = 3,844) and an unaffected sibling cohort (n = 675) were constructed. Cumulative survival was illustrated using Kaplan-Meier curves. MEASUREMENTS: Cox regression models estimated crude and adjusted hazard ratios (AHR) for all-cause, natural-cause and unnatural-cause (accidents and suicides) mortality risks. Covariates for adjustment included sociodemographic factors, physical and psychiatric comorbidities and familial confounding. FINDINGS: Over a mean follow-up of 8 years, GD was associated with elevated all-cause mortality risk [AHR 1.20, 95% confidence interval (CI) = 0.90-1.61] driven by statistically significantly elevated risk of unnatural mortality (AHR 6.15, 95% CI = 3.44-10.98) and especially suicide mortality (AHR 10.03, 95% CI = 4.71-21.33). Risk of natural mortality was statistically significantly lower in GD patients (AHR 0.66, 95% CI = 0.45-0.96). Sibling cohort analysis revealed a similar trend (all-cause mortality: AHR 1.70, 95% CI = 0.67-4.28; unnatural cause mortality: AHR 8.65, 95% CI = 1.62-46.22; suicide mortality: AHR 7.24, 95% CI = 0.74-70.59; natural cause mortality: AHR 0.48, 95% CI = 0.13-1.73). Results remained consistent after adjustment for individual psychiatric comorbidities. CONCLUSIONS: Gambling disorder patients in Taiwan appear to have a statistically significantly increased risk of unnatural-cause mortality and especially suicide mortality compared with matched controls. Policies and clinical interventions for treating GD patients should focus on suicide prevention to reduce mortality in this population.",2026,Chen,Addiction,10.1111/add.70397,41872026,https://doi.org/10.1111/add.70397,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0492,"Risk, Reward, and Survival Pressure in Artificial Agents","Risk in SPVE is not only reward seeking. The same lobster can chase payout, avoid loss, react to Tiamat pressure, respond to social pressure, and enter repair. This draft separates reward, settlement, and survival pressure so the dashboard can answer a practical question: is the agent making a profitable decision, a fearful decision, a social decision, or a repair decision?",2026,Chen,Zenodo,10.5281/zenodo.20743919,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20743919,en,datacite:Preprint/,datacite,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R0493,Gambling participation and risk over time associated with type of gambling activity: Trends in Australian general population 2015-2022.,"• Gambling participation in Australia fell from 37% in 2015 to 33% in 2022. • Higher-risk gambling increased from 3.7% to 4.7% over the same period. • Young people under 35 saw larger increases in higher-risk gambling over this time. • Slot machines, race betting, and sports betting were linked to higher-risk gambling. • Clear need for public health approaches targeting higher-risk young adults. There is limited longitudinal data examining national gambling trends in Australia. This study examines longitudinal trends in gambling participation and higher-risk gambling in Australia from 2015 to 2022 using data from the nationally representative Household Income and Labour Dynamics in Australia (HILDA) panel study. Study participants were HILDA respondents aged 15 and older in 2015, 2018, or 2022 who completed the Problem Gambling Severity Index (PGSI; N = 44,836 observations). Gambling participation was based on self-reported typical monthly expenditure on 10 gambling activities. Gambling risk was assessed using the 9-item PGSI and categorised as low-risk (0–2) or higher-risk (3+) gambling. Age and sex were included as socio-demographic variables. The results indicated that monthly gambling participation declined from 37.1% in 2015 to 32.9% in 2022 but levels of higher-risk gambling did not reduce at the same rate. Higher-risk gambling was independently associated with participation in electronic gaming machines (EGMs; OR = 8.0, p < 0.001), race betting (OR = 2.3, p < 0.001) and sports betting (OR = 1.5, p = 0.01). These were the most popular forms of gambling among those aged under 25. Although overall gambling participation in Australia has declined to 2022, this did not result in a reduction to higher-risk gambling. Young adults were the least likely to gamble, but show disproportionate participation in EGMs, race, and sports betting, and an increasing rate of higher-risk gambling.",2026,Chilver,Addictive behaviors,10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108618,41621350,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108618,eng,citations:article | europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;citations;openaire,6,False,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
