R0495,Revisiting the Legal Definition of “Gambling” in an Era of Prediction Markets and Other Gambling-Like Activities,"Absent a clear definition, there is growing confusion around what constitutes “gambling” for purposes of legislation and regulation. Prediction markets, some trading platforms, and social sports wagering apps have followed the precedent of daily fantasy sports and cryptocurrency trading in skirting the legal prescriptions applied to gambling operators on legalized platforms worldwide. Most recently, tensions increased when prediction markets began offering sports-related event contracts nationwide, threatening sportsbook revenues, state tax income, and gambling bans in non-legal states. Many view these markets as gambling, while prediction market executives argue they function as financial markets because payouts are set by market activity, not bookmakers. This presentation will outline this debate and the legal implications of public policy considerations that attempt to distinguish gambling behavior and the infrastructure for gambling-like activities. For example, an event contract is a mechanism not a behavior and can support both gambling and non-gambling uses. This distinction calls into question the claim that federal oversight by the Commodity Futures Trading Commission supplants state gambling law. The resulting tension challenges the historically state-controlled gambling framework by promoting activity that is virtually indistinguishable from illegal gambling. Applying traditional legal definitions of gambling to these emerging mechanisms will be discussed.",2026,Cho,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0496,Clinical Characteristics and Treatment Retention Rates of Investment Addiction,"Objectives: This study aims to compare the clinical characteristics, pharmacotherapy patterns, and treatment retention rates between patients with investment addiction (compulsive trading of stocks and cryptocurrencies) and those with traditional gambling disorders in South Korea. Methods: We conducted a retrospective chart review of 209 patients diagnosed with Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Dis- orders, Fifth Edition gambling disorder at an addiction clinic from November 1 to 30, 2025. Patients were divided into two groups: the investment addiction group (n=61; focusing on stocks, cryptocurrencies, and futures/options) and the non-investment gambling group (n=148; involving casinos, sports betting, and card games). We collected clinical variables, including demographics, education, employ- ment status, medication history (e.g., naltrexone, methylphenidate), and participation in structured group therapy. Statistical analyses included independent t-tests and chi-square tests for group comparisons, Kaplan–Meier survival analysis with log-rank tests for treat- ment retention rates, and Cox proportional hazards regression to identify predictors of treatment dropout. Results: The investment addiction group was significantly older (38.1±9.8 years vs. 33.3±10.3 years, p=0.002), had higher education levels (15.8±1.4 years vs. 14.6±2.0 years, p<0.001), and exhibited higher rates of marriage (57.4% vs. 39.2%, p=0.024) and full-time employment (72.1% vs. 43.2%, p<0.001). Survival analysis indicated significantly shorter treatment retention in the investment addic- tion group (mean 13.2 months vs. 24.2 months, p=0.008). Cox regression analysis revealed that age, participation in group therapy, and prescription of methylphenidate were significant protective factors for retention in both groups. Conclusion: Investment addiction is prevalent among high-functioning individuals but is associated with poor treatment retention, like- ly due to low insight and cognitive distortions. Early intensive interventions that focus on environmental control, structured group thera- py, and screening for comorbid attention-deficit hyperactivity disorder with appropriate pharmacotherapy are essential for improving long-term treatment outcomes.",2026,Choi,Korean Academy of Addiction Psychiatry,10.37122/kaap.2026.30.1.22,,https://doi.org/10.37122/kaap.2026.30.1.22,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0497,Legalized Sports Gambling and Cost of Equity,"Exploiting the staggered legalization of sports wagering across U.S. states following the Supreme Court's 2018 Murphy v. NCAA decision, we examine how a local speculative-taste shock propagates to the firm-level cost of equity. Using the imputation estimator of Borusyak et al. (2024) on a panel of U.S. publicly listed firms over 2013-2023, we find that legalization is associated with a statistically significant reduction in the average implied cost of equity of firms headquartered in treated states. The effect is concentrated among firms with low pre-existing retail investor attention and sparse analyst coverage, consistent with an investor-recognition-andattention channel through which legalization expands the effective retail investor base of otherwise-overlooked firms.",2026,Choi,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.7109938,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7109938,und,crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0498,"Betting from the margins: gambling, respectability and the invisible incorporation of women into sports betting in Gweru, Zimbabwe","Abstract Sports betting has become one of the most visible features of Zimbabwe’s urban economy and one of its most male-dominated sectors. Existing scholarship reads women’s low presence in betting shops either as evidence of exclusion or, following the feminisation of gambling literature developed in Britain and Australia, as a barrier now being dismantled by online platforms and a softening market. Drawing on thirteen in-depth interviews with women in Gweru who bet on football or work in betting shops, this paper argues that neither reading holds. Zimbabwe’s crisis economy has made betting one of the few income options open to women who have neither capital nor credentials, while the moral order governing urban femininity, forged in the colonial alliance between settler and indigenous patriarchies and reproduced by the postcolonial state, codes betting as unrespectable. Women manage this contradiction through a repertoire of tactics: seeking husbands’ permission, concealing their betting, delegating bets to male proxies, betting online from home, and entering shops only under male escort. These tactics are agentic, and they work. But they work by removing women’s bodies from betting space rather than by contesting its terms. We name this outcome invisible incorporation: women are absorbed into the betting economy as customers and as employees while the masculine and moral character of betting space is left intact and, in some respects, reinforced. Read from Gweru, feminisation is a commercial process rather than a gender transformation. The paper contributes a decolonial feminist account of gambling in Africa and cautions against transposing Northern gambling frameworks onto African settings without attending to the colonial genealogy of the moral categories through which African women’s economic conduct is judged.",2026,Choto,Research Square,10.21203/rs.3.rs-10995004/v1,,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10995004/v1,,crossref:posted-content | europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR | openalex:preprint,europepmc;openalex;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0499,Multi-Sport Match Outcome Prediction: A Unified Framework for Football and Cricket Analytics,"The global sports prediction and analytics ecosystem represents a rapidly expanding domain, with the international sports betting market valued at USD 100.9 billion in 2024 and projected to reach USD 187.4 billion by 2030 (CAGR 11%). Simultaneously, the sports analytics market is forecast to grow at a CAGR of 15.6% through 2033, driven by the proliferation of data-driven decision-making in professional sports. Accurate pre-match outcome prediction remains a technically challenging problem due to the stochastic nature of competitive sports, the heterogeneity of relevant features across disciplines, and the scarcity of unified frameworks that generalise across multiple sports. Existing approaches predominantly target a single sport or tournament, rely on limited feature sets, and fail to provide systematic ablation evidence for their design choices. Statistical models and shallow machine learning methods achieve modest accuracy in the range of 54–67% for football and 58–78% for cricket pre-match prediction, while deep learning approaches frequently underperform on structured tabular sports data due to data scarcity and overfitting. This paper proposes a unified, feature-engineered prediction framework — denoted UniSportXGB — that integrates sportspecific domain knowledge with gradient-boosted decision trees (XGBoost) to predict pre-match outcomes across association football (English Premier League, Spanish La Liga, UEFA Champions League, German Bundesliga) and cricket (Indian Premier League, ICC Cricket World Cup). The framework systematically constructs 28 features per sport, including rolling form windows, home/away performance splits, head-to-head records, venue win rates, toss influence (cricket), and squad fatigue proxies. Four models — Logistic Regression, Random Forest, XGBoost, and a Multilayer Perceptron — are trained and compared under identical experimental conditions. UniSportXGB achieves 68.3% accuracy and F1-score of 0.67 on football outcome prediction, and 71.4% accuracy with F1-score of 0.70 on cricket, outperforming the strongest baseline (Random Forest) by 4.2 and 3.8 percentage points respectively. Statistical significance is confirmed via paired t-test (p &lt; 0.01) across all comparisons. SHAP feature importance analysis reveals that rolling five-match form and venue win rate are the most discriminative predictors in both sports. UniSportXGB provides a reproducible, open-access prediction toolkit applicable to coaching analytics, fan engagement systems, and responsible sports intelligence platforms, with direct relevance to the growing sports-data industry",2026,Chougule,International Journal for Research in Applied Science and Engineering Technology,10.22214/ijraset.2026.83520,,https://doi.org/10.22214/ijraset.2026.83520,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0500,Structure-Guided Entity Resolution: Fine-Tuning LLMs for Robust Name Matching in Complex Linguistic Contexts,"Matching person names across heterogeneous records is a core challenge in entity resolution, especially within linguistically and culturally complex environments. Variations in naming conventions, inconsistent transliteration across scripts, and frequent data entry errors make it difficult to unify user identities, an essential requirement for Know Your Customer (KYC) compliance. While Large Language Models have shown promise in understanding natural language, they often struggle with the structured ambiguity present in such domain-specific settings. This paper introduces Structure-Guided Entity Resolution (SGER), a novel framework that fine-tunes an LLM through a two-phase curriculum. The model is first trained to parse the grammatical and semantic structure of personal names, then optimized for the downstream task of binary entity matching. We evaluate SGER in the challenging context of Indian identity data, one of the most linguistically diverse and noisy environments globally. SGER achieves 99.02% accuracy and an F1 of 0.994 on a held-out set of 50,000 real-world pairs, outperforming GPT-4o few-shot prompting and single-stage fine-tuning baselines. The system is fully deployed in production at Dream11, the world's largest fantasy sports platform, serving 250M+ users. Our results demonstrate that curriculum-guided training enables robust, high-precision entity resolution in real-world multilingual systems at scale. Accepted to ACL 2026. 8 pages, 1 figure, 2 tables",2026,Chourasia,Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (ACL),10.18653/v1/2026.acl-industry.101,,https://doi.org/10.18653/v1/2026.acl-industry.101,und,datacite:Audiovisual/Conference talk | datacite:Preprint/Article | openaire:Article; Preprint | openalex:conference-paper | openalex:other | s2:JournalArticle;Conference,openalex;s2;datacite;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0501,Financial Wellbeing and Gambling Risk in Australia,"This report examines gambling risk through a financial wellbeing lens. Rather than focusing only on individual indicators of problem gambling, it considers how gambling activity relates to household income, expenditure burden, financial stress, and economic vulnerability. Using wave 22 of the Household, Income and Labour Dynamics (HILDA) Survey, the analysis compares five major gambling activities: electronic gaming machines (EGMs), lotteries, sports betting, race betting, and casino table games. EGM participants had the lowest average household disposable income, the highest activity-specific expenditure burden, and the highest prevalence of financial stress among the gambling activity groups examined. After adjusting for expenditure and participant characteristics, EGM participation was associated with higher odds of both household financial stress and problem gambling. The results show that gambling-related harm is not evenly distributed across gambling activities. Lotteries was the most common activity, but problem gambling risk was more concentrated among EGM and casino table game participants. This pattern indicates that participation rates alone provide an incomplete picture of gambling risk. The report also draws on behavioural decision-making research and safer gambling intervention evidence. Gambling products differ in speed of play, frequency of outcomes, and opportunities for repeated spending. These features matter because financial risk may build through repeated decisions before individuals reassess their behaviour. Safer gambling policy should therefore consider who is financially vulnerable, how much is spent relative to income, and whether interventions reduce behaviour that may contribute to financial harm. Interventions should be evaluated using observable behavioural and financial wellbeing outcomes, including expenditure, betting intensity, limit-setting behaviour, and persistence after losses.",2026,Chung,University of Sydney Library,10.25910/rgj8-3p52,,https://doi.org/10.25910/rgj8-3p52,,datacite:Report/ | openalex:report,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0502,PHISHING EM PLATAFORMAS DE APOSTAS ONLINE: DETECÇÃO POR PLUGIN DE NAVEGADOR,"This article analyzes the growth of phishing attacks on online betting platforms in Brazil and proposes a preventive solution through a browser plugin. Fraudulent websites exploit user trust via page cloning, pop-ups, and malware, compromising sensitive information and transaction security. The hypothesis is that a plugin capable of analyzing domain reputation, SSL certificates, redirects, and download links can significantly reduce fraud risk. Three real phishing cases were examined, revealing technical and behavioral patterns that guided the tool’s development and validation. Results show that the proposed solution effectively mitigates attacks, enhances user protection, and strengthens trust in online betting platforms, contributing to a safer digital ecosystem",2026,Cintra,Revista Tecnológica da Fatec Americana,10.47283/9d3hze31,,https://doi.org/10.47283/9d3hze31,pt,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0503,Marketing d'influence sur Meta et paris sportifs en ligne : Quelle responsabilité sociale des influenceurs en Côte d'Ivoire ?,"Cette étude analyse l’impact du marketing d’influence et des facteurs sociaux, cognitifs et éthiques sur les comportements de pari sportif en ligne. Reposant sur un paradigme post-positisme, ce travail mobilise une approche mixte quantitative et qualitative. Les données sont analysées via l’analyse factorielle exploratoire (AFE) et l’analyse en composantes principales (ACP). Les résultats dénotent des indices KMO satisfaisant (0,84) et des tests de Bartlett significatifs ((χ² = 842,76 avec p < 0,001). Après rotation Varimax, trois dimensions se distinguent et expliquent 73,7% de variance totale. La première dimension porte sur l’influence numérique. La seconde dimension met en évidence les mécanismes de régulation individuelle et étatique. La troisième dimension traite de l’éthique et de la responsabilité sociale. Les coefficients de fiabilité (α de Cronbach) attestent une bonne cohérence interne des composantes (α = 0,91 ; α =0,79 ; α = 0,76).",2026,CISSE,Revue Française d'Economie et de Gestion,10.5281/zenodo.18508657,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18508657,fr,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
