R0604,Trajectories of unemployment and sport betting on antisocial behaviour among youth in Nigeria: demystifying the myth,,2026,Egbo,Alvan Journal of Social Sciences,10.67638/ajss-2026-v3-i2-005,,https://doi.org/10.67638/ajss-2026-v3-i2-005,,s2:JournalArticle,s2,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0605,APOSTAS ESPORTIVAS: UMA ANÁLISE DA AVERSÃO À PERDA EM DISCENTES DE GRADUAÇÃO EM UMA UNIVERSIDADE DO OESTE DE SANTA CATARINA,"O presente estudo teve como objetivo analisar de que forma a aversão à perda influencia o comportamento dos estudantes de graduação em Ciências Contábeis de uma Universidade do Oeste de Santa Catarina em relação às apostas esportivas. Para tal, realizou-se uma pesquisa descritiva, de abordagem quantitativa, utilizando-se de um questionário estruturado aplicado aos acadêmicos do curso (survey). A população da pesquisa compreendeu os acadêmicos do curso de Ciências Contábeis da Universidade do Oeste de Santa Catarina (UNOESC), campus de Joaçaba-SC, totalizando 153 alunos. A amostra da pesquisa foi composta por 108 respondentes, representando 70,53% da população total. Os dados utilizados no estudo foram coletados a partir de uma entrevista estruturada, com perguntas fechadas. Esse roteiro foi dividido em cinco partes, sendo elas: Bloco I - Perfil do respondente; Bloco 2 - Frequência de participação; Bloco 3 - Motivações e comportamentos; Bloco 4 - Gastos e resultados em apostas e Bloco 5 - Avaliação de aversão à perda. O questionário foi aplicado aos acadêmicos no mês de agosto de 2025. Os dados foram tabulados e analisados por meio de estatística descritiva, com uso do software IBM SPSS Statistics. Os resultados demonstram uma a baixa adesão dos participantes às apostas esportivas, uma vez que 73,15% declararam nunca ter apostado e, ainda 87,96% dos estudantes nunca destinaram recursos a apostas esportivas. Também foi constatada a ausência de comportamentos viciosos ou problemáticos relacionados a essa prática, posto que esses percentuais ficaram em torno de 0,93%.",2026,Einsweiller,Anuário Pesquisa e Extensão Unoesc Joaçaba,,,,pt,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0606,Online Sports Betting Is a Public Policy Issue and a Public Health Issue,"This Viewpoint discusses the potential harms of online sports betting, gaps in prevention and treatment, and protective policy shifts.",2026,Eisenberg,JAMA Health Forum,10.1001/jamahealthforum.2025.6926,41719029,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2025.6926,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,6,False,False,False,,,,,
R0607,"Celebrity Endorsements and Brand Awareness of Betking in Abak Metropolis, Nigeria","The increasing competition within Nigeria's sports betting industry has compelled operators to adopt innovative marketing communication strategies capable of strengthening brand awareness and attracting consumers. Among these strategies, celebrity endorsement has become increasingly prominent; however, empirical evidence regarding its effectiveness within the betting industry remains limited, particularly in Akwa Ibom State. This study examined the effect of celebrity endorsements on the brand awareness of BetKing in Abak metropolis. Specifically, the study investigated the influence of celebrity popularity, celebrity credibility and endorsement frequency on brand awareness. The study adopted a survey research design. The population comprised 181,717 registered BetKing customers in Abak metropolis, from which a sample of 399 respondents was determined using Taro Yamane's formula. Convenience sampling technique was employed in selecting respondents, while data were collected using structured questionnaires. A total of 397 valid questionnaires were analysed using descriptive statistics and multiple regression analysis with the aid of SPSS version 25. The findings revealed that celebrity popularity significantly enhanced brand awareness (β = 0.292, p 0.05). The study concluded that celebrity popularity and repeated endorsement exposure constitute the major celebrity endorsement dimensions influencing BetKing's brand awareness. The study recommended that BetKing should continue engaging highly recognised celebrities and increase the frequency of endorsement campaigns across multiple communication channels in order to strengthen consumer awareness and brand recall.",2026,EKPENYONG,AKSU Journal of Management Sciences,10.61090/aksujomas.11503,,https://doi.org/10.61090/aksujomas.11503,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0608,Local Fiscal Consequences of State Sports-Betting Tax Policy,"Thirty-eight states have legalized sports betting since 2018, yet policy debates ignore local governments. We ask whether state sports betting policy affects local fiscal health. Using municipal bond yields (2018-2024) and Census of Governments data (2017-2022), we estimate an imputation difference-indifferences model exploiting staggered legalization and cross-sectional variation in tax rates. Legalization improves local fiscal conditions, but higher state tax rates diminish these gains. Across all legalizing states, bond yields fall by approximately 10 basis points and municipal sales tax revenue rises by roughly $44,000 per municipality. At the median tax rate (15%), the yield reduction is approximately 14 basis points and the revenue gain is roughly $36,000. Each percentage point increase in the state tax rate erodes these benefits. We find no extensive-margin effect on intergovernmental transfers, suggesting effects flow through consumer spending rather than revenue-sharing.",2026,Ellis,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.6228258,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6228258,und,crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0609,Le droit de l'e-sport,"Introduction générale PARTIE I Un marché e-sport structuré mais normé par un droit incertain Chapitre 1 La structuration économique du marché e-sportif Section 1 L'écosystème e-sportif et ses acteurs A. Les acteurs de la compétition B. La place singulière de l'éditeur Section 2 Les flux économiques d'un marché structuré A. Les sources de revenus B. La fragilité structurelle du modèle Chapitre 2 Un marché difficilement appréhendé par les catégories juridiques traditionnelles Section 1 Une catégorie sui generis A. Ni tout à fait un sport B. Ni un simple jeu d'argent Section 2 Les qualifications mobilisables et leurs limites A. Une activité économique de droit commun B. Un objet de propriété intellectuelle sous contrôle de l'éditeur PARTIE II Les déséquilibres révélés par un droit fragmentaire et les voies d'adaptation Chapitre 1 Les dé s équilibres dans les relations contractuelles Section 1 La relation entre le joueur et la structure, une qualification disputée A. Le statut légal du joueur salarié B. La re-qualification par le juge, preuve du déséquilibre Section 2 L'image et le sponsoring, des contrats sous emprise A. Le droit à l'image du joueur B. Le sponsoring, contrat innommé déséquilibré Chapitre 2 La concentration du pouvoir et les voies d ' adaptation Section 1 La domination de l'éditeur à l'épreuve du droit de la concurrence A. Un pouvoir normatif privé fondé sur la propriété intellectuelle B. Vers un abus de position dominante ?Section 2 Un droit en quête d'adaptation A. L'adaptation par fragments B. Vers une régulation harmonisée ?La piste européenne Conclusion générale et Bibliographie Glossaire des principaux termes e-sportifsLe juge, dans son pouvoir d'appréciation n'est donc pas, contrairement à ce que disait Montesquieu « que la bouche de la loi qui prononcent la loi », il est une source de création des normes.Cette intervention du juge n'est pas un choix.En effet, l'article 4 du Code civil dispose que le juge qui refuse de juger, sous prétexte du silence, de l'obscurité ou de l'insuffisance de la loi, peut être poursuivi comme coupable de déni de justice 3 .Dès lors, même lorsque la loi est incomplète, le juge doit statuer.Il lui appartient d'interpréter les textes et de leur donner une portée face à des situations que le législateur n'avait pas expressément envisagées.Ce décalage, déjà relevé par Portalis en 1801, se vérifie encore aujourd'hui.En 2026, plus de deux siècles plus tard, sa formule conserve toute sa pertinence.Le phénomène e-sportif incarne, aujourd'hui, précisément ce « nouveau fait », découlant de l'innovation majeur et du développement d'internet et du numérique, que les catégories juridiques classiques n'avaient pas anticipé.L'e-sport constitue l'un des exemples récents les plus révélateurs de ce décalage.Bien avant que le législateur ne s'intéresse à ce phénomène, des équipes étaient déjà formées, des joueurs étaient déjà rémunérés et des compétitions internationales attiraient des millions de spectateurs.Dès la fin des années 1990, la Corée du Sud voyait émerger un véritable écosystème professionnel autour du jeu vidéo compétitif.L'essor de jeux tels que Dota 2, League of Legends ou Counter-Strike a ensuite porté ce phénomène à une échelle mondiale.Certains « prize pool » atteignaient des montants s'élevant à plusieurs millions de dollars pour les « teams d'e-sport » Le droit, lui, est longtemps demeuré silencieux.La France n'est véritablement intervenue qu'avec la loi « pour une République numérique » du 7 octobre 2016.Cette loi a notamment introduit un régime relatif aux compétitions de jeux vidéo et au joueur professionnel salarié de jeu vidéo compétitif 4 .L'article L. 321-8 du Code de la sécurité intérieure définit ainsi la compétition de jeux vidéo comme « l'affrontement, à partir d'un jeu vidéo, d'au moins deux joueurs ou équipes de joueurs, pour un score ou une victoire.5 » En Italie, en revanche, il n'existe toujours pas de cadre législatif général spécifiquement consacré à l'e-sport.3 Code civil, art. 4. 4 Loi n° 2016-1321 du 7 octobre 2016 pour une République numérique, art.102. 5 Code de la sécurité intérieure, art.L. 321-8.Le marché s'est structuré de manière inégale entre les Etats.Tandis que la France dispose d'un premier cadre, encore incomplet, l'Italie reste dans une situation plus incertaine, malgré une reconnaissance institutionnelle progressive du phénomène.Ainsi, cette différence est importante, car les deux pays appartiennent à une tradition juridique proche.Elle invite donc à s'interroger sur la manière dont deux droits répondent à un même phénomène économique.Avant d'analyser cette réponse juridique, il faut toutefois définir l'objet étudié.Le terme « e-sport » est la contraction de l'expression anglaise electronic sport, que l'on traduit généralement par « sport électronique ».Cette expression pose déjà une première difficulté, en rapprochant le jeu vidéo du sport, alors même que cette assimilation demeure discutée.Le sport renvoie traditionnellement à une activité physique, organisée autour de fédérations, de compétitions et de règles disciplinaires.L'e-sport, lui, repose sur un jeu vidéo.Ce jeu est un objet numérique.Il appartient à un éditeur privé.Il est protégé par le droit de la propriété intellectuelle.Cette spécificité distingue profondément l'e-sport du sport traditionnel.Matériellement, l'e-sport peut être défini comme la pratique compétitive et organisée du jeu vidéo, à un niveau amateur ou professionnel, il doit donc être distingué du gaming et du jeu d'argent, en effet :-Le gaming désigne la pratique de jeu vidéo à titre de loisir, dès lors il peut être occasionnel et individuel, tandis que l'esport suppose une confrontation organisée.-Autre distinction vis à vis du jeu d'argent, le joueur esport ne recherche pas l'aléa propre au pari ou à la loterie, il cherche a réaliser une performance, renvoyant à la performance sportive.Le droit français a préféré la notion de « compétition de jeux vidéos » au terme « e-sport », en effet, l'article L. 321-8 du Code de la sécurité intérieure dispose dans son deuxième alinéa « Une compétition de jeux vidéo confronte, à partir d'un jeu vidéo, au moins deux joueurs ou équipes de joueurs pour un score ou une victoire ».Le même texte écarte l'obligation d'une prise de paris sportifs préalable au déroulement de l'évènement.Cette définition, bien qu'elle pose un cadre, demeure limitée puisqu'elle vise surtout un affrontement direct entre joueurs ou équipes.Quid de certaines pratiques telles que le « speedrun » dans lequel le joueur doit finir un jeu le plus vite possible, se confrontant donc à un record et non à un joueur de manière directe.L'association française d'esport, nommée France Esports, donne une autre définition de ce phénomène en le qualifiant comme l'ensemble des pratiques permettant à des joueurs de se confronter, par l'intermédiaire d'un support électronique, principalement le jeu vidéo, de manière synchronique (en même temps) ou asynchronique (en décalé).6 6 France Esports, « Esport : pratiques et disciplines », 19 avr.2018.En revanche, si la carrière est courte, la reconversion devient nécessairement un enjeu majeur, alors même que le droit n'offre que très rarement au joueur e-sportif les mêmes garanties qu'au sportif professionnel traditionnel.La seconde spécificité tient à la transversalité qui existe entre le joueur et le créateur de contenu.Dans l'e-sport, la performance compétitive n'est pas toujours la seule source de valeur car un joueur peut également tirer des revenus du streaming, des réseaux sociaux, des partenariats commerciaux ou de la création de contenus.Cette situation brouille les frontières, un même individu pouvant être à la fois compétiteur, salarié, indépendant, influenceur, ambassadeur de marque et producteur de contenus, de sorte que la distinction entre sport, divertissement et activité commerciale devient de moins en moins nette.Cette porosité annonce déjà les difficultés de qualification qui seront abordées plus loin : le joueur est-il d'abord un sportif, un salarié, un prestataire, un artiste numérique, un influenceur, ou une figure hybride que le droit peine encore à cerner ?Autour du joueur se trouve ensuite la structure e-sportive, équivalent fonctionnel du club sportif.Son rôle est opérationnel puisqu'elle recrute les joueurs, constitue les équipes, organise les entraînements, négocie les contrats, assure la logistique et représente les joueurs dans les compétitions.Il serait donc erroné d'y voir de simples groupes amateurs de jeux vidéos : les structures les plus avancées disposent d'un encadrement étoffé, composé d'entraîneurs, d'analystes, de managers, de préparateurs, de responsables de communication, parfois même de psychologues ou de spécialistes de la performance.Certaines organisent des bootcamps, c'est-à-dire des périodes intensives de préparation, ou mettent à disposition des lieux de vie et d'entraînement inspirés des gaming houses, une même structure pouvant par ailleurs être présente sur plusieurs jeux, avec des rosters distincts selon les disciplines.Cette organisation confirme que l'e-sport s'est véritablement professionnalisé.La structure ne se limite pas à inscrire des joueurs à des compétitions : elle gère une activité économique à part entière, qui suppose de construire à la fois une stratégie sportive et une stratégie commerciale.Elle cherche évidemment à gagner, mais elle cherche tout autant à exister médiatiquement, ces deux objectifs se révélant indissociables l'un de l'autre.La Karmine Corp, de son côté, a construit un modèle communautaire d'une grande puissance, capable de mobiliser massivement son public, y compris dans des enceintes traditionnellement réservées au spectacle ou au sport.L'organisation d'événements tels que le KCX, notamment à l'Accor Arena, illustre bien cette capacité à transformer une simple équipe e-sportive en un véritable phénomène communautaire, dépassant le cadre strict de la compétition.LDLC OL constitue",2026,ERROHI,Electronic Theses and Dissertations Repository (University of Pisa),,,,it,openalex_non_en:dissertation,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0610,"Gambling behavior among adolescent and young recreational athletes: the role of gender, gambling modality, and sport characteristics.","Adolescence and youth are periods of increased vulnerability to risk behaviors, including gambling, particularly among athletes. The present study aimed to examine 1) the prevalence and severity of gambling, 2) gender differences in gambling-related behaviors, and 3) associations between sport modality, betting on one's own sport, and engagement in specific gambling activities with gambling severity and motives among recreational athletes. The sample consisted of 244 athletes aged 15 to 35 years ( M = 20.58, SD = 4.58), of whom 68% were male. Gambling severity and motives were assessed, along with sport type, involvement in different modalities of gambling activities, and betting on the sport they practiced. Results indicated that 73% of the participants had gambled in their lifetime, and 16.3% presented at-risk or problem gambling behaviors. Males showed higher involvement in strategic and online gambling, higher gambling severity, and stronger gambling motives than females. Regression analyses indicated that online gambling, card games, and sports betting were associated with higher gambling severity. Furthermore, betting on own's sport and participation in specific gambling types (particularly slot machines) were related to higher enhancement, social, coping, and financial motives. Early identification of at-risk athletes, with attention to gender differences in gambling behaviors and motives, may help in developing tailored educational and preventive strategies within recreational sports environments.",2026,Estévez,"Psychology, health & medicine",10.1080/13548506.2026.2659696,41989250,https://doi.org/10.1080/13548506.2026.2659696,eng,europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0611,Implications of Legalized Sports Betting for Family Well-Being and Tax Policy,"Sports betting has expanded rapidly in the United States since the 2018 Supreme Court decision in Murphy v. National Collegiate Athletic Association, which allowed states to legalize it. Since then, 38 states have legalized sports betting and 30 allow online sports betting. Recent work has documented negative effects of access to sports betting on household financial health and stability, but the downstream effects on the families and children of bettors are largely unknown. This project studies the causal effects of access to legal sports betting on family formation and well-being — including marriage, fertility, home ownership, earnings, and education. The identifying variation is the staggered rollout of sports-betting legalization (brick-and-mortar and online) across states beginning around 2018. A secondary, exploratory component examines compliance with tax-reporting requirements for gambling winnings and estimates the associated forgone tax revenue.",2026,Evans,Open Science Framework,10.17605/osf.io/tnzyk,,https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/tnzyk,,datacite:StudyRegistration/Pre-registration | openalex:other,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0612,Betting on Tomorrow: Prediction Markets and the Tax Treatment of Event Contracts&amp;nbsp;&amp;nbsp;,"Prediction markets purport to reveal the future. Yet they also present a more &lt;br&gt; immediate legal question: when an individual purchases a contract paying one &lt;br&gt; dollar if a candidate wins an election, is that individual investing or wagering? &lt;br&gt; The distinction matters because federal income tax law treats investment and &lt;br&gt; gambling quite differently. &lt;br&gt; Event-contract markets permit participants to wager on elections, inflation &lt;br&gt; reports, public-health developments, and numerous other contingencies. Artificial &lt;br&gt; intelligence and frictionless platform design can personalize wagering &lt;br&gt; opportunities, accelerate trading, and cause bets to resemble conventional &lt;br&gt; investments. Although many event contracts resemble recreational wagering in &lt;br&gt; their design, participation, and expected returns, their regulation under financial- &lt;br&gt; market statutes confers an investment-like identity. Tax law may therefore impose &lt;br&gt; different consequences on economically equivalent wagers merely because one &lt;br&gt; occurs through a sportsbook and another through a regulated exchange. &lt;br&gt; This Article examines that incongruity. It considers whether event contracts &lt;br&gt; should qualify as capital assets or instead fall within the tax rules applicable to &lt;br&gt; gambling. Drawing upon economic-substance principles and theories of tax &lt;br&gt; equity, the Article argues that most retail event-contract activity is better &lt;br&gt; understood as consumption-oriented wagering than as profit-motivated &lt;br&gt; investment. Capital treatment, it demonstrates, elevates form over substance, &lt;br&gt; undermines horizontal equity, and permits platforms and participants to exploit &lt;br&gt; regulatory classifications for tax advantage. &lt;br&gt; The Article then proposes a practical framework for harmonizing the taxation of &lt;br&gt; event contracts with existing gambling rules while preserving investment &lt;br&gt; treatment for bona fide commercial hedging. More broadly, it demonstrates how &lt;br&gt; tax law should respond when technological innovation erodes established &lt;br&gt; boundaries among finance, entertainment, and wagering.",2026,Eyal,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.7392018,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7392018,,crossref:posted-content | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0613,"Electronic gambling engagement and psychosocial outcomes among students at the University of Ibadan, Nigeria: a theory of planned behavior perspective.","BACKGROUND AND AIMS: The proliferation of electronic gambling in Nigeria presents a significant public health concern among youths facing economic adversity. This study applied the Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB) to investigate the socio-cognitive drivers and self-reported psychosocial consequences of electronic gambling engagement among students at the University of Ibadan. Electronic gambling is defined here as any gambling activity conducted through electronic devices, including mobile phones, laptops, and computers, encompassing mobile sports betting applications, web-based casino platforms, and online poker. METHODS: A descriptive cross-sectional survey design was employed. Data were collected from 403 undergraduate and postgraduate students at the University of Ibadan using stratified random sampling and a structured self-administered questionnaire adapted from validated instruments. Analyses included descriptive statistics, scale reliability assessment, and binary logistic regression. RESULTS: The lifetime prevalence of electronic gambling was 49.6%. Positive attitudes toward gambling, subjective norms involving peer approval, and perceived behavioral control were all significant predictors of gambling engagement (p < .001). Economic hardship was the strongest predictor in the model (OR = 3.06). Among participants who reported gambling, 50.9% reported increased stress and anxiety, 48.6% reported financial strain, and 42.6% reported negative academic consequences. PRACTICAL IMPLICATIONS: University health and counseling services should integrate validated gambling screening tools into routine student health assessments. Interventions should address both the economic motivations and the cognitive distortions sustaining gambling engagement. Prevention campaigns should target the specific TPB constructs validated in this study. Institutional policy must also address the structural driver of economic hardship through expanded work-study programs and student financial support initiatives.",2026,Fadipe,BMC public health,10.1186/s12889-026-27689-3,42098682,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12889-026-27689-3,eng,europepmc:research-article;Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0614,Betting on Gambling Diversion Courts to Handle the Risks of Online Gambling for Problem Gamblers,"1 Starting with Murphy v. NCAA, the Supreme Court held that a Professional and Amateur Sports Protection Act provision prohibiting states from authorizing sports gambling was unconstitutional.Murphy v. NCAA, 584 U.S. 453, 474 (2018).Thirtynine states and the District of Columbia allow legal sports betting as of February 27, 2025.",2026,Fajvan,Seton Hall Journal of Legislation and Public Policy,10.60095/wzez3262,,https://doi.org/10.60095/wzez3262,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0615,More frequent use of live sports-betting features is associated with increased risk of gambling harm: Evidence from a case-control design.,"BackgroundMany theories of gambling harm propose that interactive design features of sports-betting platforms confer risk of harm by enabling cyclical patterns of repeated gambling engagement. This exploratory study investigated whether such features were used more frequently by people at higher risk of gambling harm relative to a demographically matched lower-risk control group.MethodsThis study used a case-control design to compare a group of 85 people at higher risk of gambling harm (PGSI 5+) with an age- and gender-matched control group at lower risk of harm (n = 84, PGSI 0-4). We compared the frequency with which groups self-reported using in-play betting, instant cash-out, multi and micro bets, social betting, and viewing live-streamed events within a betting app.ResultsThe higher-risk group reported more frequent use of in-play betting and instant cash-out and were more likely to report streaming live sporting events within a betting app. Higher-risk participants were also more likely to make an immediate deposit to qualify for a marketing offer, and both groups reported being more likely to make risky bets when using bonus funds. We observed no group differences in the frequency of use of multis, micro-bets, or social betting.ConclusionsOur findings were broadly consistent with the theory that design features of modern betting platforms contribute to gambling harm by facilitating continuous engagement. A notable finding was that in-app streaming of live events was more common among people at higher risk of gambling harm, consistent with a novel prediction made by the motivating theory.",2026,Farrell,Journal of behavioral addictions,10.1556/2006.2025.00491,41723755,https://doi.org/10.1556/2006.2025.00491,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:brief-report;Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex:preprint | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,6,False,False,False,,R1603,,,
R0617,Effect of Sports Betting and its Potential Risk on Emotional Control and Entertainment of Players,,2026,Fatima,Advance Social Science Archive Journal,10.5281/zenodo.20315560,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.20315560,en,datacite:JournalArticle/ | openaire:Article | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0621,Testing the Probabilistic Calibration of NBA Trade Markets,"This paper tests whether NBA “Next Team” sportsbook markets are probabilistically calibrated at the trade deadline. Using an archival dataset of destination-specific player trade markets, this paper reconstructs 38 player-events across five pooled seasons from 2020–21 through 2025–26. Each event matches a sportsbook-implied probability for a player to join a specific team with the realized transaction outcome. Market efficiency is evaluated using the Brier Score, baseline forecast comparisons, and a calibration curve. The pooled Brier Score is 0.1825, compared to 0.1330 for a naïve base-rate forecast, yielding a Brier Skill Score of -0.373. The market therefore performs worse than a forecaster who assigns the same base-rate probability to every event. The calibration curve shows over-prediction in every probability bin, with the largest errors at higher quoted probabilities. These findings do not support the hypothesis that NBA trade markets are well calibrated.",2026,Felix-Padilla,ScholarlyCommons (University of Pennsylvania),,,,,openalex:report,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0622,"Sports Betting Behaviour in Portugal: A Descriptive and Bivariate Statistical Analysis of Betting Patterns, Profiles and Associated Factors Using SPSS",,2026,Fernandes,Unpublished,10.13140/rg.2.2.33025.42089,,https://doi.org/10.13140/rg.2.2.33025.42089,pt,datacite:Text/Research Proposal | openaire:Other literature type | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0623,Publicidad del juego en España:,"Esta investigación analiza comparativamente la publicidad de las apuestas deportivas privadas y de las loterías estatales en España, y su impacto en la percepción ciudadana. Mediante un diseño cualitativo que combina entrevistas semiestructuradas y análisis de contenido de cuatro spots emblemáticos, se observa una clara asimetría simbólica: las loterías se enmarcan como tradición afectiva y solidaridad, mientras las apuestas deportivas se asocian a riesgo, adicción y vicio. Los resultados muestran cómo la comunicación contribuye a normalizar el juego público y a estigmatizar el privado, tensionando los objetivos de protección al consumidor y recaudación estatal.",2026,Fernández,Questiones Publicitarias,10.5565/rev/qp.435,,https://doi.org/10.5565/rev/qp.435,es,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R0624,Brazil&amp;#39;s Ban On Online Betting And The Income That Does Not Return To The Market,"On 25 September 2026, Provisional Measure No. 1,394 prohibited the exploitation, offering and advertising of fixed-odds betting in Brazil, ending a regulated market that had operated since January 2025. This essay sets aside the polarised debate between economic freedom and public health to ask a prior, economic question: what happens to household income when it stops circulating through commerce, industry and services and begins to flow to betting platforms? Drawing on official data from the Central Bank of Brazil and the Ministry of Finance, and on estimates by the Celso Furtado Centre, the University of São Paulo's Made research centre, the National Confederation of Commerce and the Institute for Health Policy Studies, it distinguishes gross wagers from households' net loss, places that loss between a floor of roughly R$ 35 billion and a ceiling of R$ 62.5 billion for 2025, and traces where the retained revenue goes: modest tax collection, negligible formal employment, and profits that may be remitted abroad. It then confronts the classic 'mere transfer' objection of gambling economics and argues that, given the asymmetry of multipliers, the compromised nature of the bettor's choice and the externalities borne by third parties, the transfer is not neutral. The essay closes by examining the legal risks of the ban, including migration to illegal markets, compensation claims and the constitutional regime of provisional measures, and concludes that the measure's success will depend less on its text than on the State's capacity to prevent that income from merely changing address.",2026,Ferreira Marchetti Filho,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.7529824,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7529824,,crossref:posted-content | openalex:preprint,openalex;crossref,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R0625,"Digital Surveillance, Athlete Rights and Contractual Restrictions in High-Performance Sports: A Human Rights-Based Analysis","Amaç: Bu çalışmanın temel amacı, küresel bir endüstriye dönüşen yüksek performans sporunda, matbu sözleşmelerle profesyonel sporcuların sivil otonomileri ve serbest dolaşım hakları üzerinde kurulan kurumsal hegemonyanın (asimetrik ahlak hükümleri, transfer kısıtlamaları ve siber-fiziksel gözetim) ulusal normlar (TBK, KVKK) ve uluslararası insan hakları (AİHS, GDPR, AB Hukuku) bağlamındaki hukuki meşruiyetini incelemektir.Yöntem: Disiplinlerarası nitel araştırma desenlerinden geleneksel (narratif) derleme yöntemine dayanan bu araştırmada; güncel ampirik çalışmalar (2024-2026), ulusal spor regülasyonları ile Avrupa İnsan Hakları Mahkemesi (AİHM), Avrupa Adalet Divanı (CJEU) ve Uluslararası Spor Tahkim Mahkemesi (CAS) içtihatları analiz edilmiştir.Bulgular: Araştırma sonucunda, karşılıklılık ilkesinden yoksun ahlak hükümlerinin sporcuların sivil ifade özgürlüğünü daralttığı ve FIFA'nın transfer regülasyonlarının sporcuların serbest dolaşım hakkını ihlal ettiği saptanmıştır. Ayrıca, biyometrik veri izninin asimetrik güç dengesi nedeniyle hukuken sakat bir katılım ön şartına dönüştüğü, bu verilerin spor bahisleri sektörüyle kesişmesinin ciddi mahremiyet riskleri doğurduğu ve kesintisiz siber gözetimin sporcularda klinik düzeyde teknostres yarattığı doğrulanmıştır. AİHM'in Temmuz 2025 tarihli Semenya v. Switzerland Büyük Daire kararı ve CJEU'nun Ekim 2024 tarihli Lassana Diarra kararıyla Lex Sportiva'nın mutlak özerkliğinin kırılarak, insan hakları ve AB hukukunun spor yönetiminde zorunlu bir denetim standardı hâline geldiği belirlenmiştir.Sonuç: Sonuç itibarıyla, modern spor sözleşmelerindeki asimetrik hükümlerin ve dijital gözetimin sporcunun temel haklarını ölçüsüzce ihlal ettiği değerlendirilmiş olup; adil bir ekosistem için sporcu veri egemenliğinin tanınması, dağıtık veri sistemlerinin kullanılması ve fesih ile transferlerde hukuki karşılıklılığın tesisi gereklilik arz etmektedir.",2026,Fethi,DergiPark (Istanbul University),,,,tr,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R0626,Peripheral Mending at the Edges of Sports Journalism: Metajournalistic Discourse of the Terry Rozier Sports Gambling Arrest,"This study looks at opinion articles and sports columns in the aftermath of Terry Rozier’s illegal gambling arrest. Rozier was arrested for allegedly conspiring with organized crime rings and was accused of taking himself out of games to manipulate prop bets. The aftermath of the incident prompted a barrage of metajournalistic discourse, where writers attempted to paradigm repair sports media and the leagues relationships with sports betting corporations. Using textual analysis, this study analyzed 36 articles that wrote about sports gambling’s influence on the NBA and sports media. The findings suggest that journalists invoked paradigm repair tactics to defend the field of sports journalism from perceptions of illegitimacy but also attempted to repair the image of the NBA as an object of journalistic coverage. We dub this tactic peripheral mending , which demonstrates how mutualistic relationships further blur journalisms’ professional boundaries.",2026,Figueroa,Communication & Sport,10.1177/21674795261474796,,https://doi.org/10.1177/21674795261474796,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0627,"Prevalence, Patterns, and Sociodemographic Determinants of Gambling Participation Among Community Adults in Urban Nigeria: A Cross-Sectional Study from Obio-Akpor, Rivers State","Abstract Background Nigeria has experienced rapid expansion of mobile sports betting, yet community-level data on gambling prevalence, patterns, and sociodemographic determinants in the South-South geopolitical zone are entirely absent. This study examined gambling participation, primary gambling type, frequency, expenditure, and their sociodemographic correlates among adults in Obio-Akpor Local Government Area (LGA), Rivers State. Methods A quantitative cross-sectional survey was conducted in six wards of Obio-Akpor LGA using multistage sampling. A total of 468 adults participated (response rate 93.2%). Gambling prevalence, type, frequency, and weekly expenditure were assessed via structured questionnaire. Chi-square tests examined sociodemographic associations; binary logistic regression identified independent predictors of gambling participation. Ethics approval: UPH/CEREMAD/REC/MM118/087. Results Twelve-month gambling prevalence was 31.2% (n = 144/461). Sports betting predominated (49.6%), followed by online applications (34.8%). Daily gambling was reported by 40.3% of gamblers; 29.4% spent ₦10,000–19,999 weekly, representing 57–114% of weekly income for the lowest earners. Gender (χ²=36.815, p &lt; 0.001), educational attainment (χ²=7.369, p = 0.025), and monthly income (χ²=10.019, p = 0.018) were significantly associated with gambling participation. After adjustment, male gender was the sole independent predictor (AOR = 3.76; 95% CI: 2.36–6.01; p &lt; 0.001). Conclusion Gambling is prevalent and financially intensive in Obio-Akpor, disproportionately affecting educated young men. Findings support urgent regulatory reform, targeted digital prevention, and integration of gambling screening into primary care in Rivers State.",2026,Fii¹,Research Square,10.21203/rs.3.rs-9800243/v1,,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9800243/v1,und,citations:preprint | europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR | openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,europepmc;openalex;citations;openaire,4,False,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R0628,"Online sports betting in Brazil: regulation, socioeconomic impacts, and the protection of vulnerable consumers","Online sports betting has established itself as one of the fastest-growing economic and social phenomena in Brazil in recent years, especially after the legalization of fixed-odds betting by Law No. 13.756/2018 and the effective regulation promoted by Law No. 14.790/2023. This study analyzes the regulatory framework for online betting in Brazil, the social impacts resulting from the expansion of digital platforms, and the legal challenges related to the protection of vulnerable consumers. The research is characterized as applied, exploratory, and descriptive, using a mixed-methods approach (qualitative and quantitative), with analysis of legislation, judicial decisions, regulatory norms, and official statistical data. The results demonstrate that regulation represented an important advance for the organization of the sector; however, challenges related to oversight, abusive advertising, protection of highly vulnerable consumers, prevention of gambling addiction, and combating illegal operators persist. The jurisprudential analysis identified a trend towards applying the strict liability foreseen in the Consumer Protection Code to platforms that fail to implement effective responsible gambling mechanisms. A high incidence of debt among bettors, market concentration in economically vulnerable groups, and an increase in cases related to gambling disorder were also observed. It is concluded that the effectiveness of the Brazilian regulatory model depends on the adoption of integrated public policies, continuous oversight, and the strengthening of consumer protection mechanisms.",2026,Filho,Brazilian Journal of Development,10.34117/bjdv12n7-004,,https://doi.org/10.34117/bjdv12n7-004,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R0629,Long-distance yardage: the socioemotional functionality of fantasy football among American men,"Over 50 million people in the United States are active fantasy sports players, with Fantasy Football (FF) being the most popular. The present study examined potential social predictors of FF participation, with an emphasis on social inclusion – which was assessed by measuring one’s need to belong, fear of rejection, loneliness and depression. We hypothesised that these social variables would positively correlate with other motivators for playing FF. Two dependent measures were created to assess FF participation – Involvement and Time Commitment. Involvement was positively correlated with one’s sensitivity to rejection. Time Commitment was not significantly predicted by any of the above. Participants who have a greater sensitivity to rejection may be playing FF as a means of staying connected to friends, while not devoting significant amounts of time to ensuring their FF team(s) perform(s) well. Both FF Involvement and Time Commitment positively correlated with Dwyer and Kim’s (2011) Motivational Scale for FF Participation (MSFFP). Specifically, Involvement positively correlated with the MSFFP Competition subscale, and Time Commitment positively correlated with the Social Interaction and Entertainment/Escape subscales. Although loneliness did not correlate with FF Involvement and Time Commitment, loneliness positively correlated with the Social Interaction subscale, and negatively correlated with the Entertainment/Escape subscale.",2026,Fitzgerald,Leisure Studies,10.1080/02614367.2026.2701754,,https://doi.org/10.1080/02614367.2026.2701754,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
