R1074,Sport participation and gambling-related problems in adolescents: findings from a large school-based study,"Background Adolescent gambling is increasingly shaped by digital access, sports betting, and gambling-like mechanisms embedded in everyday youth culture. This study examined gambling-related problems among high school students and their associations with sociodemographic, family, mental health-related, and behavioral characteristics, with particular attention to sport participation. Methods A school-based cross-sectional study was conducted among 2,379 high school students in Novi Sad, Serbia, between 2024 and 2026. Participants completed a structured sociodemographic and behavioral questionnaire and the Serbian version of the Gambling Disorder Identification Test. Students were classified as non-gambling, recreational gambling, or having gambling-related problems. Associations between gambling categories and selected characteristics were examined using omnibus chi-square tests, Cramér's V, and post-hoc pairwise comparisons. Results Overall, 1,969 students (82.8%) were classified as non-gambling, 314 (13.2%) as recreational gambling, and 96 (4.0%) as having gambling-related problems. Gambling category was significantly associated with male sex, lower school achievement, living arrangement, parental marital status, sport participation, previous psychiatric treatment, family psychiatric history, smoking, alcohol use, and psychoactive substance use. In the univariate analyses, sport participation increased from 64.0% among non-gambling students to 75.2% among recreational gamblers and 78.1% among students with gambling-related problems. Post-hoc comparisons showed that sport participation differentiated non-gambling students from both gambling-involved groups, but not recreational gambling from gambling-related problems. However, after adjustment for sex and other relevant behavioral and mental health-related characteristics, sport participation was no longer independently associated with gambling-related problems. Male sex, previous psychiatric treatment, smoking, alcohol use, and psychoactive substance use remained the strongest independent correlates. Conclusion Gambling-related problems in adolescents were associated with a broader pattern of behavioral and mental health-related factors. Although sport participation was associated with gambling involvement in unadjusted analyses, this association did not remain significant after adjustment for sex and other relevant characteristics. These findings support understanding adolescent gambling within a broader risk-behavior and public health framework, with sport representing a relevant context for prevention rather than an independent risk factor.",2026,Ratković,Frontiers in Public Health,10.3389/fpubh.2026.1931143,,https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2026.1931143,en,citations:article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;citations,3,False,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1075,“The Thing I Don’t Take for Granted”: How the Audience Shapes the Fantasy Sport Podcasting Environment,"The rise in podcast creation and consumption has created a congested landscape that necessitates intentional consideration from podcasters to connect with their audience. Through the collaborative production of content, known as produsage, podcasters are motivated by and take value from engagement with their listeners. Currently, a gap exists specific to the fantasy sport genre in examining how the audience impacts the podcasting environment. Thus, the purpose of this qualitative study was to analyze the role of listeners on the experiences of fantasy sport podcasters. Semistructured interviews were conducted with 11 fantasy sport podcasters, with a thematic analysis revealing four main themes (i.e., meaningful audience, motivation from audience, leveraging feedback, and audience challenges). From a theoretical and practical perspective, findings advance understanding on produsage communities that elicit meaningful relationships as fantasy sport podcasters are reliant upon their audience to ensure positive outcomes.",2026,Ratts,International Journal of Sport Communication,10.1123/ijsc.2025-0217,,https://doi.org/10.1123/ijsc.2025-0217,,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1076,"Affected others’ perspectives on the burden, drivers, and prevention of gambling- related harms: a qualitative study","Abstract Background Gambling is increasingly recognised as a public health concern, with harms extending beyond individuals who gamble to their families, peers, and communities. However, young people and adults affected by others’ gambling remain under-represented in research on harm prevention messaging. Methods A critical qualitative inquiry approach was employed, involving four focus groups: two with adults affected by another person’s gambling (n = 10, aged 18–75 years) and two with young people living in households where an adult gambled (n = 10, aged 13–18 years). Participants were recruited through community networks and online platforms. Data were analysed thematically using the Framework Method. Results Three themes were identified. The first theme, Gambling-related harms are multifaceted and often hidden , highlights how harms extend beyond financial loss to include impacts on mental health, relationships, and family security, often reinforced by stigma and secrecy. Adults highlighted delayed recognition and minimisation of harm, while young people described diminished trust in adults who gambled. The second theme, Predatory tactics of the gambling industry , captures participants’ perceptions of aggressive marketing, celebrity endorsements, and the normalisation of gambling through sports and digital platforms. Adults expressed moral outrage and distrust, viewing the industry as exploitative and unethical, whereas young people emphasised the influence of celebrity and gaming-related promotions. The third theme, Harm prevention requires multi-level, targeted strategies , reflects participants’ views that industry slogans are ineffective and that prevention should instead emphasise credible, audience-specific messaging, lived-experience narratives, and systemic reforms. Adults underscored the invisibility of affected others and called for recognition and early support, while young people advocated for peer-led education integrated into schools and digital environments. Conclusions The findings underscore the need for targeted approaches to messaging that reflect the experiences of different groups affected by gambling and associated harms. Prevention strategies should combine tailored communication with upstream interventions—such as stricter regulation of advertising and affordability checks—to address the structural drivers of gambling-related harm. Acknowledging the moral and emotional dimensions of gambling-related harm is essential for rebuilding trust and ensuring that prevention messages are credible, compassionate, and effective.",2026,Ray,Research Square,10.21203/rs.3.rs-9131253/v1,,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9131253/v1,,europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR,europepmc,1,False,False,False,,R1077,,,
R1077,"Affected others’ perspectives on the burden, drivers, and prevention of gambling-related harms: a qualitative study","Abstract Background Gambling is increasingly recognised as a public health concern, with harms extending beyond individuals who gamble to families and communities. However, young people and adults affected by others’ gambling remain under-represented in research on harm prevention messaging. Methods A critical qualitative inquiry approach was employed, involving four focus groups: two with adults affected by another person’s gambling (n = 10, aged 18–75 years) and two with young people living in households where an adult gambled (n = 10, aged 13–18 years). Participants were recruited through community networks and online platforms. Data were analysed thematically using the Framework Method. Results Three themes were identified. The first theme, “gambling-related harms are multifaceted and often hidden”, highlights how harms extend beyond financial loss to include impacts on mental health, relationships, and family security, often reinforced by stigma and secrecy. Adults highlighted delayed recognition and minimisation of harm, while young people described diminished trust in adults who gambled. The second theme, “perceived predatory tactics of the gambling industry”, captures participants’ views on aggressive marketing, celebrity endorsements, and the normalisation of gambling through sports and digital platforms. Adults expressed moral outrage and distrust, often characterising industry practices as exploitative, whereas young people emphasised the influence of celebrities and gaming-related promotions. The third theme, “harm prevention requires multi-level, targeted strategies”, reflects participants’ views that industry slogans are ineffective, and prevention should instead emphasise credible, audience-specific messaging, lived-experience narratives, and systemic reforms. Adults underscored the invisibility of affected others and called for recognition and early support, while young people advocated for peer-led education in schools and digital environments. Conclusions The findings highlight the importance of prevention approaches that reflect the experiences of those affected by gambling-related harm. Effective prevention is likely to require tailored communication and structural measures addressing the social and commercial conditions in which gambling occurs. Although participants frequently discussed online gambling, digital advertising, and features of contemporary gambling environments, many harms were related to familiar family and relationship impacts. The findings also highlight the moral and emotional ways through which gambling-related harm is understood, with participants favouring prevention approaches that acknowledged these experiences.",2026,Ray,Harm Reduction Journal,10.1186/s12954-026-01513-y,,https://doi.org/10.1186/s12954-026-01513-y,en,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,R1076,,,
R1078,Reddy Book Anna – Top Online Cricket Betting Platform in India,"In India, cricket is not a simple sport. It is a feeling that unites individuals regardless of age, geography, and culture. As digital platforms continue to expand, so has the way individuals consume and use cricket. Online access has enabled users today to keep up-to-date, follow matches, and access structured sports-related services. Among the cricket fans seeking some structured and resourceful information, reddy books is a widely used query in this changing digital environment.",2026,Reddy Book Anna,Zenodo,10.5281/zenodo.18952461,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18952461,,datacite:Text/Presentation,datacite,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R1079,Covering the Spread: An Analysis of NCAA Proposition Betting Bans,"In 2018, the Supreme Court’s ruling in Murphy v. National Collegiate Athletic Association cleared the way for states to legalize sports gambling. As sports betting grows in popularity, states are faced with a new challenge: microbets known as “player propositions” have raised significant concerns about public health, player safety, and the integrity of the game. States have responded with blanket prohibitions, restrictions that limit player props to out-of-state teams, or by permitting the wagers. State restrictions on college player props came in two waves. The first wave came prior to 2024, when many states issued bans or restrictions as part of their roll-out of legalized sports gambling. The second wave came after NCAA president Charlie Baker publicly lobbied state legislatures to ban prop bets in early 2024. In 2024, four states passed new bans: Louisiana, Maryland, Ohio, and Vermont. These bans deviate from traditional models. They were passed by an amorphous coalition of states with different partisan agendas, betting markets, levels of religiosity, population size, and geographic location. Using evidence from Ohio, Maryland, and Louisiana, this paper argues that these states enacted bans on NCAA player proposition bets, while Arkansas, North Carolina, and Michigan did not, because of differences in the primary roles, structures, and powers of gambling regulators in each state. “Regulatory discretion” refers to these differences. The paper’s findings have implications for the NCAA’s lobbying campaign, state regulators and legislatures, and for those seeking to mitigate gambling-related harms.",2026,Reed,CU Scholar (University of Colorado Boulder),,,,,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1080,Demographic Propensity and Tail Risk in Online Sports Betting,"Online sports betting losses are not evenly distributed: average losses look modest, while a small group of bettors absorbs most of the harm. This paper builds a demographic Markov-chain Monte Carlo model to study how age, income, and gender shape annual sports-betting losses. Each simulated person's demographics are compressed into a single propensity score θ, which drives their probability of betting at all, their movement between non-, moderate, and heavy betting states over the year, their wager sizes, and their tendency to chase losses. Wagers face a house edge and an affordability cap tied to disposable income, and the model is calibrated to published aggregate anchors: overall participation, participation among men aged 18–49, and handle per bettor. The simulations show losses concentrating among younger, higher-income men, with the most severe outcomes in the lower tail rather than at the mean, while most of the simulated population loses little or nothing. A decomposition separates income's behavioural effect from its budget effect, a descriptive regression ranks the demographic drivers, and robustness checks show the gender gap is a consequence of the calibrated young-male participation boost rather than an independent empirical finding. Because transitions and loss-chasing are stylised rather than estimated from individual betting histories, results should be read as model-based estimates. The contribution is a transparent, fully reproducible framework — complete source code included — for studying how demographic risk factors translate into concentrated betting-loss distributions.",2026,Reeve,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.21809444,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21809444,en,datacite:Text/ | openaire:Other literature type | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1081,Relevance of legalized sports betting for the gendered nature of work and careers,"Purpose Legislative changes in various countries have allowed for considerable growth in the online sports betting industry. While impacts for financial well-being and health have been considered, the same attention has not been paid to careers. This is an oversight, as theoretical framings related to the gendered nature of work posit the workplace is rooted in, and rewards shows of, masculinity, while gambling is a prescribed masculine behavior. Thus, engaging in sports betting and having discussions of sports betting while at work may be a pathway to burnish masculinity and yield workplace rewards. Design/methodology/approach A conceptual pathway is provided based on the gendered nature of work frameworks. Findings Relationships and related boundary conditions are posited linking sports betting behavior to masculine displays, social network development and career outcomes. Originality/value The framework extends the gendered nature of work frameworks to consider how masculinity may be enacted in the workplace and yield rewards. This complements research suggesting gambling behavior can harm careers. Through these advances, sports betting is highlighted as a construct of interest within the workplace with wide reaching implications for careers.",2026,Regina,Career Development International,10.1108/cdi-04-2026-0297,,https://doi.org/10.1108/cdi-04-2026-0297,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1082,Translation and Adaptation of the Online Gambling Disorder Questionnaire (OGD-Q) into Brazilian Portuguese,"Online gambling has grown increasingly popular over the past decade, especially since the COVID-19 pandemic. Online gamblers tend to face more issues than those who gamble in traditional settings, leading to a higher risk of developing pathological gambling. In Brazil, gambling activities are culturally accepted and normalized, which encourages widespread participation among the population. Therefore, screening and assessing online gambling is now a public necessity. The Online Gambling Disorder Questionnaire (OGD-Q), developed by González-Cabrera et al. (2020), aims to evaluate symptoms of gambling disorder primarily online. Given the significance of this issue in Brazil, this study sought to adapt the scale for cross-cultural use and examine its psychometric properties. The sample included 298 participants who completed an online survey. Nomothetic validity was tested through Spearman’s Rho correlations between measures of depression, anxiety, stress, and online gaming behavior. Internal consistency was assessed using Cronbach’s alpha and Omega Coefficient, along with confirmatory factor analysis. Results showed strong reliability, with a Cronbach’s alpha of 0.92 and an Omega Coefficient of 0.93. The confirmatory factor analysis supported the proposed one-factor structure, demonstrating good fit indices and factor loadings. Significant correlations were found in the total sample between OGD-Q scores and measures of online gaming, depression, anxiety, and stress. In summary, the findings demonstrate that the Brazilian version of OGD-Q is an effective tool for assessing online gambling disorder in the Brazilian population.",2026,Rego,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10480-9,41794940,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10480-9,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1083,"Decision models and online betting behavior: Comparing mean-variance, expected utility, and prospect theory","We examine the drivers of decision making under risk by leveraging the odds offered by bookmakers in online sport betting. First, we use a dataset of over 1.6 million odds from ten years of soccer and 25 years of tennis online betting to estimate parameters for Expected Utility Theory (EUT), Cumulative Prospect Theory (CPT), and extended mean-variance models. Subsequently, we compare their out-of-sample predictive performance. We find that moment-based models that do not explicitly include nonlinear probability weighting perform on par with CPT. Our results further indicate that bettors are neither risk-averse (EUT) nor loss-averse (CPT) and exhibit preferences for skewness and kurtosis. Given the large share of adults who gamble regularly, these findings have broad relevance: We show that extended mean-variance frameworks, commonly used in finance, offer a simple alternative to prospect theory with comparable predictive power in online betting. Our study also offers insights into potential behavioral drivers of asset pricing anomalies, such as the underperformance of high-variance and high-skewness stocks. Furthermore, we provide parameter estimates for each framework that can be used in future empirical research, especially regarding the influence of gamblers in financial markets.",2026,Reichenbach,Journal of Economic Behavior & Organization,10.1016/j.jebo.2026.107563,,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jebo.2026.107563,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1084,Faith and the Odds: Religiosity as a Cultural Constraint on Online Sports Betting,"This study examines the role of religiosity in shaping gambling markets, focusing on online sports betting following legalization. It investigates whether states with higher levels of religious adherence showed slower growth in gambling tax revenue from online sports betting. By analyzing state-level revenue data alongside measures of religious participation, the research explores how cultural and moral factors influence the adoption and expansion of new gambling modalities. Findings aim to provide insights into the interplay between societal values and market behavior, offering guidance for policymakers and industry stakeholders.",2026,Reid,"Exhibit - A Showcase of Scholarship, Creativity and Preservation Provided by Xavier University Library (Xavier University)",,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1085,"High Stakes, Low Mood: Gambling Behavior and Mental Health Across Age Groups in Indiana","Gambling behavior is recognized as part of broader mental health outcomes across the lifespan. Prior research indicates that gambling problems are associated with psychological distress, including depression, anxiety, and suicidal ideation. Despite growing evidence of these links, few studies have examined gambling behavior and mental health patterns across multiple age groups within a single state population. This study examines gambling participation, problem-gambling severity, and self-reported mental health indicators among youth, college students, and adults in Indiana. Data were drawn from three statewide surveys conducted between 2023 and 2025. Measures included gambling frequency and type, validated problem-gambling indices, awareness and help-seeking behaviors, and self-reported mental health indicators such as stress, depression, and mentally unhealthy days. Across all age groups, gambling participation was common but varied by form. Youth and college students reported higher engagement in online and sports gambling, whereas adults more frequently reported lottery and casino play. Greater gambling severity was associated with elevated psychological distress and substance use across age groups, yet awareness of and engagement with treatment services remained low. These findings underscore the need for prevention and intervention strategies that address gambling and mental health through early intervention, education, and improved awareness of available support.",2026,Reynolds,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1086,Price in the Player? Testing Performance-Based Mispricing in NFT Fantasy Sports Markets,"This study examines behavioral pricing patterns in blockchain-based fantasy sports markets by analyzing non-fungible token (NFT) sales on Sorare.com, a decentralized platform where users buy, sell, and compete with digital player cards. Each NFT corresponds to a licensed professional soccer player and is traded in a secondary market on Ethereum-based infrastructure. We construct a unique dataset by linking player performance data with transaction-level NFT sales across multiple seasons, focusing on the Rare and Super Rare card tiers. To isolate the effects of recent performance on secondary market prices, we develop a matching algorithm that connects NFT transaction records to individual card IDs based on sale timestamps and card-specific metadata. This structure allows us to test whether player NFTs are priced efficiently, or whether recent on-field success leads to temporary price inflation consistent with hot hand bias. Our results align with the presence of performance-chasing or hot hand pricing, where recent form leads to a temporary inflation of perceived value. Our framework builds on prior work examining the hot hand hypothesis in traditional sports betting markets—including studies by Camerer (1989), Brown and Sauer (1993), and Paul and Weinbach (2005, 2011, 2014)—and extends this behavioral lens to tokenized digital assets.",2026,Riccardi,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1087,Sports Gambling: Savior or Mirage for the future growth of Gambling revenues,"Sports gambling represents one of the final opportunities for states to expand their gambling revenues. This paper will examines the effect that sports gambling had on the overall gambling revenues for five Northeastern states (NJ, PA, NY, MA and RI) with well established gambling sectors (lottery, casino, online gambling). Using ARIMA Intervention analysis, it is the aim of the paper to begin to answer three questions: (1) Is sports gambling a unique gambling market? (2) Does sports gambling canniablize other gamblign segments? (3) Is sports gambling the primary area of grwoth for gambling revenues?",2026,Richard,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1088,The State of Gambling Behaviors Among NCAA Student-Athletes: Findings from Five Waves of the National Study on Collegiate Wagering (2004-2024).,"Technological advances and regulatory changes have substantially expanded access to sports wagering in the United States, raising concerns about gambling behaviors among collegiate athletes. Since 2004, the National Collegiate Athletic Association (NCAA) has conducted a large-scale survey every four years to monitor gambling behaviors among student-athletes. The present study examined trends in gambling among NCAA student-athletes over a twenty-year period. Data were drawn from five waves of the NCAA National Study on Collegiate Wagering conducted in 2004, 2008, 2012, 2016, and 2024. Across these cohorts, more than 100,000 student-athletes completed anonymous surveys assessing gambling participation, sports wagering behaviors, attitudes toward gambling, and problem gambling severity. Gambling activities were measured using the Gambling Activities Questionnaire, and gambling severity was assessed using DSM-IV-TR criteria for pathological gambling. Descriptive analyses were used to examine trends across survey years. Overall gambling participation declined over time, with increasing proportions of non-gamblers and decreasing rates of social gambling among both men and women. Rates of at-risk and probable pathological gambling remained low and relatively stable. Male student-athletes consistently reported higher gambling participation and sports wagering than females. Notable shifts included increases in fantasy sports betting, parlay bets, live in-game betting, and prop bets, often conducted through mobile platforms. Although overall gambling participation has declined, evolving forms of sports wagering and greater accessibility through online platforms present new challenges. Continued prevention, education, and responsible gambling policies are needed to protect student-athletes and preserve the integrity of college sports.",2026,Richard,Journal of gambling studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10515-1,42322500,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10515-1,eng,europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1089,Improving the Detection of Gambling in Suicide Death: An Investigation to Inform Surveillance of Gambling Harms,"Abstract: Background: Previous studies have reported a positive association between gambling and suicide. Unlike other addictive behaviors like drugs or alcohol use disorders, there are no routine prompts for death investigators to capture whether gambling was a factor in a suicide death. This study was designed to understand how gambling suicides are investigated and ways to improve detection. Methods: Semi-structured interviews were conducted in Australia with 32 people who had either 1) survived a gambling suicide attempt, 2) been bereaved by gambling suicide, or 3) professionals who worked in areas aligned to this topic. Transcripts were analyzed using reflexive thematic analysis. Interpretation was considered in the context of the authors’ experiences identifying suicides in Australian mortality datasets. Results: Identifying gambling suicides is challenging due to the ways information is gathered, weighted, and reported during death investigations. This is compounded by the stigma and shame associated with gambling which can work to conceal gambling-related harms, and limit data available to death investigators. Conclusions: Advancing awareness of gambling as a driver of suicide is needed to improve surveillance. Policy options available to regulators include establishing centralized gambling transaction datasets. Giving death investigators access to data related to gambling engagement of people who have died unexpectedly would enable them investigate gambling in these deaths efficiently and comprehensively. This information could be used to inform the design of interventional support to users of gambling products.",2026,Rintoul,SUCHT - Zeitschrift für Wissenschaft und Praxis / Journal of Addiction Research and Practice,10.1024/0939-5911/a000977,,https://doi.org/10.1024/0939-5911/a000977,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1090,Prevalence of gambling behavior and risk for gambling disorder in Austria,"BACKGROUND: After gambling disorder (GD) was included as the first behavioral addiction in the Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders, fifth edition (DSM-5), many studies focused on epidemiology and the impact of different risk factors. In this context, online gambling seems to show especially high addictive potential and, additionally, lacks government control and restrictions. METHODS: In this study, we aimed to evaluate the prevalence of GD, as well as various aspects of gambling behavior in a representative Austrian sample (N = 3658). An adapted version of a standardized questionnaire was applied, which covered all nine DSM-5 criteria for GD. RESULTS: Results indicate a lifetime prevalence for gambling of 34.9%, with 4.5% of the whole population at high risk for GD. Males engaged in online gambling three times more often than female participants, and prevalence for online gambling was highest in the youngest cohort (16-29 years). CONCLUSION: Our findings suggest a general increase in gambling behavior, with clear indications for a higher vulnerability of younger participants as well as for males compared to females. Results are discussed in light of clinical relevance, and the need for further restrictions, especially for online gambling, is argued.",2026,Rittmannsberger,Neuropsychiatrie,10.1007/s40211-026-00586-2,42678480,https://doi.org/10.1007/s40211-026-00586-2,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1091,Duel.com DUEL5 Referral Code: Best Sports Betting Odds + 50% Enhanced Rakeback 2026 Guide,,2026,Rivers,Unpublished,10.13140/rg.2.2.36776.12806,,https://doi.org/10.13140/rg.2.2.36776.12806,en,openaire:External research report | openalex:article,openalex;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1092,"New Flames, Old Irons: A Striking Analysis of Florida's Gaming Compact with the Seminole Tribe","This Comment analyzes Florida’s gaming compact (the Compact) with the Seminole Tribe, focusing on the legality of allowing online sports betting under the Indian Gaming Regulatory Act (IGRA). The Compact permits the Seminole Tribe to offer statewide online sports betting by “deeming” all bets placed in Florida as occurring on tribal lands, where the Seminole betting servers are located. This Comment argues that such an interpretation stretches the plain meaning of “Indian lands” in IGRA, which traditionally refers to physical land within reservations. Notably, the Ninth Circuit and the D.C. Circuit have seemingly contradictory opinions on this issue. This Comment focuses on three key challenges to the Compact—one from the D.C. Circuit, one from the Florida Supreme Court, and one from the United States Supreme Court—further underscoring the need for clarification. This Comment concludes by asserting that Congress should amend IGRA to clarify the definition of “Indian lands” for online gaming ensuring, consistent application of the law and promoting judicial economy.",2026,Rizzuto-Flancbaum,bepress Legal Repository,,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1093,Betting on Boys: Understanding Gambling Among Adolescent Boys,"Anyone raising a son is all too familiar with the rhythms of digital life. Between the nonstop notifications, ceaseless social media scrolling, and late-night gaming sessions, boys are figuring out who they are online. Parents love to see their sons connect, but they're uneasy, too. And the data backs up their concern. Our newest research reveals that gambling has become common among adolescent boys, with more than a third of boys gambling before they turn 18. In ""Betting on Boys: Understanding Gambling Among Adolescent Boys,"" our second report about boys' online lives, we discovered that for boys today, gambling largely doesn't happen at casinos or card tables. It lives in sports betting and inside boys' favorite video games—in loot boxes, skin cases, and other reward systems that blur the line between playing and paying—and it's showing up in their social media feeds through algorithmic recommendations. Nearly half of boys who gamble see online material that promotes gambling, mostly through algorithmic exposure. Our research makes clear that we're at a pivotal moment for boys' well-being. We can either let gambling become normalized during a critical period of their development, or we can act now—with education, safeguards, and real accountability.",2026,Robb,Common Sense Media,10.65077/zsnc9706,,https://doi.org/10.65077/zsnc9706,,crossref:report | openalex:report | s2:CaseReport,openalex;s2;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1094,"Direct gambling marketing, direct harm: A randomised experiment.","Whether gambling marketing has a causal effect on harm is of regulatory interest. Direct marketing offers (emails, push notifications and text messages) are frequently received by people with active gambling accounts, but they can opt out. This study aimed to test whether opting out of direct marketing reduces betting and short-term gambling harms in a real-world gambling environment. Stratified randomised field experiment with a between-participants design, embedded in a 14-day ecological momentary assessment (EMA). Nationwide, Australia (July-August 2023). Participants (n = 227; 61.7% men; mean age = 45), 52.0% of whom scored in the moderate-risk or problem range on the Problem Gambling Severity Index (PGSI), were regular Australian sports and race bettors recruited from online panels who agreed in principle to opt out of receiving direct marketing from all wagering operators with whom they held accounts. Participants were stratified by PGSI risk category, age group and gender and then randomly allocated either to an opt-out condition (n = 96), in which they opted out of direct marketing from their wagering operators and provided proof, or to a control condition (n = 131) that continued to receive direct marketing as usual. Seven EMA surveys were administered every 48 hours over a 14-day period during a high-volume betting season. Outcomes were self-reported number of bets placed, gambling expenditure (in Australian dollars, AUD) and short-term gambling harms in the previous 48 hours, assessed with a 10-item adapted Short Gambling Harm Screen (SGHS). The opt-out group placed 23% fewer bets [B = -0.11, 95% confidence interval (CI) = -0.20, -0.03, P = 0.011], spent 39% less money (B = -0.53, 95% CI = -0.84, -0.21, P = 0.001) and reported 67% fewer short-term gambling harms (B = -0.22, 95% CI = -0.36, -0.07, P = 0.004) compared with the controls. Opting out of receiving direct marketing from wagering operators appears to be associated with statistically significantly fewer bets made, amount spent gambling and short-term gambling harms.",2026,Rockloff,"Addiction (Abingdon, England)",10.1111/add.70369,41850333,https://doi.org/10.1111/add.70369,eng,pubmed:Journal Article;Randomized Controlled Trial,pubmed,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1095,Persuasive Design in Esports Gambling: A Behavioral Analysis Through Cialdini’s Principles and the PSD Model,,2026,Rodrigo,Lecture notes in computer science,10.1007/978-3-032-29593-4_35,,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-032-29593-4_35,en,openaire:Part of book or chapter of book | openalex:conference-paper | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1096,Legal Moralism Reconsidered: Vice Jurisprudence and the Limits of Liberal Neutrality,,2026,Rogers,International Journal for the Semiotics of Law - Revue internationale de Sémiotique juridique,10.1007/s11196-026-10441-w,,https://doi.org/10.1007/s11196-026-10441-w,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1097,APOSTAS ESPORTIVAS ONLINE E SAÚDE MENTAL EM JOVENS ADULTOS: UMA REVISÃO INTEGRATIVA DA LITERATURA,"As apostas esportivas online expandem-se com a digitalização, a disponibilidade contínua das plataformas e sua crescente integração às práticas esportivas, suscitando preocupações acerca da saúde mental de jovens adultos. Este estudo analisa as evidências científicas sobre as relações entre apostas esportivas online, comportamento problemático e indicadores de saúde mental nessa população. Realizou-se uma revisão integrativa da literatura, de natureza bibliográfica e com síntese predominantemente qualitativa, mediante buscas nas bases SciELO, Scopus e Web of Science, considerando publicações de 2022 a 2026. Após a triagem, a retirada das duplicidades, a aplicação dos critérios de elegibilidade e a leitura integral, 12 artigos compuseram o corpus. Os dados foram submetidos à análise descritiva e comparativa e organizados em eixos temáticos. Os resultados indicam que a maior gravidade do comportamento problemático se associa de maneira mais consistente a sintomas de ansiedade, depressão, estresse e sofrimento psicológico. A participação ou a frequência das apostas, quando avaliadas sem medidas específicas de gravidade, não apresenta associação uniforme com esses desfechos. Fatores individuais, sociais, comerciais e tecnológicos também se relacionam a padrões de maior risco. As evidências não permitem estabelecer causalidade nem determinar a direção temporal dessas relações, permanecendo necessária a realização de estudos longitudinais direcionados aos jovens adultos e ao contexto brasileiro.",2026,Rolim,Revista Tópicos.,10.70773/revistatopicos/791152749,,https://doi.org/10.70773/revistatopicos/791152749,,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1098,"Stay updated on changes in sports‐betting restrictions for student‐athletes, staff, coaches","The sports‐betting world underwent significant transformation just over seven years ago when the U.S. Supreme Court decided ( Murphy v. NCAA , 584 U.S. 453 (2018)) to strike down as unconstitutional the Professional and Amateur Sports Protection Act of 1992. A total of 39 states, in addition to Washington, D.C., and Puerto Rico, now allow some form of legalized online or in‐person sports betting, with Missouri becoming the most recent as of Dec. 1, 2025 ( see www.legalsportsreport.com/sports-betting-states/ ). In states with legalized betting, anyone meeting the age requirement (21 in most of the states) can place bets on professional and college games with the caveat that there are restrictions regarding sharing insider information or betting on games within the conference that a student‐athlete's school participates. In addition, many state laws include language that restricts bets on game outcomes, bets on in‐state teams, and proposition bets (frequently referred to as “prop bets,” which are bets placed on specific events or individual performances within a game that aren’t directly tied to the final score or outcome of who wins or loses).",2026,Romano,College Athletics and the Law,10.1002/catl.70022,,https://doi.org/10.1002/catl.70022,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1099,The Art of Winning an Unfair Game: Modelling Strategies in the NHL Betting Market using Machine Learning,"This thesis develops and evaluates a profit-oriented machine-learning modelfor betting on regular-season National Hockey League (NHL) games, with thegoal of overcoming the bookmaker margin and achieving a positive return oninvestment (ROI). Game statistics from Natural Stat Trick are combined with moneyline odds fromThe Odds API and converted to decimal odds. Rolling ""form windows"" producehome-minus-away feature vectors, which feed logistic-regression, XGBoost, andsupport-vector-classifier models combined into a Sharpe-weighted ensemble.Value bets are identified and staked using two schemes (Flat and To-Win),evaluated over a holdout period (season 2024/25 and the ongoing 2025/26)across 15 random-seed Monte-Carlo runs. The model is benchmarked against three reference strategies (favorite,team-loyalty, and random betting) and against the S&P 500 and SMI indices.Financial risk metrics (ROI, maximum drawdown, Sharpe, Calmar, andrisk-reward ratio) are reported throughout. Under To-Win staking the modelreaches a modestly positive ROI (+1.47% and +1.51% in the two holdoutperiods), while a critical discussion addresses statistical significance,sample size, and the practical limits of the approach. This is the English translation of a German-language Maturaarbeit(Swiss upper-secondary thesis). The translation was produced with AIassistance; the German original is authoritative.",2026,Romano,Zenodo,10.5281/zenodo.21328310,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21328310,en,datacite:Dissertation/,datacite,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R1100,Darf die Polizei den Sportwetten-Sheriff vom Stadion fernhalten?,,2026,Romberg,MADOC (University of Mannheim),,,,de,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1101,Why Courts and Congress Should but Won’t Outlaw Prediction Markets,"The current confusion over prediction markets, mainly trading in futures on sports events, starts with society’s historic antipathy toward gambling. State legislatures passed statutes outlawing everything that looked like gambling. Following the Industrial Revolution this created conflicts with certain commercial activities, which actually served valuable public purposes. Among these were insurance, auctions and trading in securities and commodities. Courts struggled with these state restrictions until the Depression forced Congress to act. Beginning in 1933, federal statutes expressly exempted trades on nationally regulated markets from state anti-gambling laws. However, the items must meet specified criteria. Courts are now faced with the question of whether prediction markets meet those criteria. Even who should decide – courts or federal regulatory agencies – is in dispute. Courts should rule them illegal not only because they are, but also to protect states like Utah from being forced to have sports betting and states like Nevada being forced to have unregulated sports betting. But the wild card is the Trump tie-in, including naming Kalshi insiders as federal regulators. Not until Trump is gone will the right of states to make their own decisions about gambling once again prevail.",2026,Rose,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1102,"Changes in gambling behaviors among young adults in Rhode Island, 2022-2024.","Background The rapid expansion of legalized sports betting and mobile gambling in the U.S. has transformed how gambling is accessed and experienced. Young adults are particularly vulnerable to these changes; however, limited research has examined how gambling behaviors are shifting across multiple modalities during periods of expanding gambling availability. Methods We analyzed repeated cross-sectional data from the Rhode Island Young Adult Survey (RIYAS) collected in 2022 (n = 1,022) and 2024 (n = 1,008) among individuals aged 18-25 years. Outcomes included past-year participation in specific gambling behaviors, positive BBGS (Brief Biosocial Gambling Screen) screens, and overall gambling involvement measured as the number of gambling types endorsed. Multivariable logistic regression models estimated adjusted odds ratios for differences in individual gambling behaviors between survey waves, and Poisson/zero-inflated Poisson models estimated differences in gambling involvement. Results Gambling participation did not differ significantly between 2022 and 2024. However, among individuals reporting gambling, participation increased in several activities, including lottery tickets (AOR = 1.36, 95 % CI: 1.01-1.83), raffle gambling (AOR = 1.40, 95 % CI: 1.05-1.88), sports betting (AOR = 1.65, 95 % CI: 1.07-2.56), casino slot machines (AOR = 2.99, 95 % CI: 2.10-4.27), other slot machines (AOR = 2.30, 95 % CI: 1.23-4.33), and app-based gambling (AOR = 2.14, 95 % CI: 1.38-3.32). The number of gambling types endorsed increased (IRR = 1.28, 95 % CI: 1.16-1.41). Positive BBGS screens were more common in 2024 but not statistically significant after adjustment. Conclusions Findings suggest while overall gambling participation remained stable, engagement across multiple and mobile-based gambling activities increased between survey waves, potentially signaling emerging risk. Increasing diversification and digitalization of gambling behaviors requires continued surveillance and public health strategies addressing emerging gambling environments.",2026,Rosenthal,Addictive behaviors,10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108804,42497732,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108804,eng,europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1103,ESTRATEGIAS DE MARKETING EN LAS APUESTAS DEPORTIVAS: ANÁLISIS DE SU INFLUENCIA EN EL COMPORTAMIENTO Y FIDELIZACIÓN DE LOS JÓVENES,Trabajo Fin de Grado leído en la Universidad Rey Juan Carlos en el curso académico 2025/2026. Directores/as: Jesús Anguita Grajales,2026,Rosillo,BURJC Digital (King Juan Carlos University),,,,es,openalex_non_en:dissertation,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1104,Giocare per il denaro o giocare con il denaro? Una rassegna della letteratura economica sul valore edonistico delle scommesse sportive.,"La rassegna della letteratura economica suggerisce una duplice definizione delle scommesse sportive: attività finanziaria e/o bene di consumo. Infatti, l’ipotesi che l’obiettivo delle scommesse sportive sia unicamente il profitto contrasta con l’osservazione che il loro rendimento atteso è negativo. La partecipazione a lotterie da parte di agenti razionali è giustificabile allentando (almeno localmente) l’ipotesi di una loro avversione al rischio. Questa propensione al rischio può derivare dall’attribuire al gioco d’azzardo non solo una finalità finanziaria, ma anche un valore edonistico. L’ipotesi che scommettere sia divertente è sostenuta da varie osservazioni e studi empirici. In particolare, la domanda di scommesse sportive pare dipendere dalle stesse variabili che determinano la domanda di intrattenimento sportivo, suggerendo l’ipotesi di una complementarità tra scommesse e visione (dal vivo o televisiva) delle competizioni sportive. Sport bets are “state contingent assets”, with a negative expected return. Therefore, a rational agent who buys these lotteries behaves as a risk-lover. The rationality of bettors may be supported by the evidence on the informational efficiency of the sport betting market. While, theoretically, their (local) risk-loving behavior may be explained by adding a (small) utility of gambling in a bettors’ generally concave (risk-averse) utility function. If gambling is amusing, then the gamblers are consumers rather than investors, and the money that they spend on gambling should not be considered as a financial loss, but as the price paid for the pleasure of gambling. Actually, the empirical evidence suggests that the demand for sports entertainment includes sports betting. In particular, betting and watching a game appear to be strong complements, as if betting on the game watched on a media would make attending the game more exciting.",2026,Rossi,IRIS Research product catalog (Sapienza University of Rome),,,https://hdl.handle.net/11573/1769354,it,openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1105,Inteligencia sensible y apuestas deportivas: una lectura pedagógica sobre adolescentes y cultura digital,"Este ensayo conceptual analiza cómo las apuestas deportivas online configuran sensibilidades adolescentes y que implicancias pedagógicas se derivan para la educación del cuerpo y el ámbito escolar. A partir de una revisión crítica de literatura nacional e internacional (2010-2024) y del diálogo con las nociones de habitus emocional, reparto de lo sensible e inteligencia motriz, se propone la categoría inteligencia sensible para pensar como la percepción, emoción y acción se relacionan en prácticas de apuesta. El artículo discute la estetización del riesgo, la naturalización de la apuesta en contextos escolares y plantea propuestas pedagógicas orientadas a la alfabetización digital crítica y la educación emocional y corporal. Concluye que exceder el marco psicopatologizante abre posibilidades de intervención educativa que reconozcan aprendizajes y regulan riesgos.",2026,Roth,Revista Tempos e Espaços em Educação,10.20952/revtee.v19i38.24373,,https://doi.org/10.20952/revtee.v19i38.24373,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1106,Criminal Responsibility in Individuals with Gambling Disorder in Brazil,,2026,Roza,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10474-7,41706357,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10474-7,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1107,THE MOST IMPORTANT LEGAL BATTLE IN THE HISTORY of GAMBLING,"Prediction markets have quickly become the biggest legal issue in the gambling world since Murphy versus NCAA, the multi-year case that eventually resulted in the repeal of the Professional and Amateur Sports Protection Act (PASPA), paving the way for legalized sports betting and unbeknownst at the time, the rise of sports contracts on prediction markets. As of April 4, 2026, there are 10 active prediction market bills, and Kalshi alone is involved in 13 legal battles with various states.",2026,Ruddock,Gaming Law Review,10.1177/25725300261454045,,https://doi.org/10.1177/25725300261454045,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1108,Diseño y análisis psicométrico de un instrumento para determinar apuestas deportivas en universitarios,"El presente proyecto de investigación tuvo como propósito diseñar y analizar psicométricamente un instrumento para determinar las influencias que presentan las apuestas deportivas en estudiantes universitarios. La investigación se realizó ante el crecimiento de las plataformas digitales de apuestas deportivas y la necesidad de contar con herramientas que permitan identificar y caracterizar su presencia e influencia en la población universitaria. El estudio se elaboró con un enfoque exploratorio, mediante un muestreo no probabilístico de participación voluntaria, con una muestra de 40 estudiantes de la Facultad de Ciencias de la Salud de la Universidad de Ciencias Aplicadas y Ambientales (U.D.C.A.), pertenecientes a los programas de Ciencias del Deporte y Medicina Humana. Para la recolección de información se diseñó un instrumento metodológico tipo cuestionario, organizado en diferentes secciones relacionadas con las características sociodemográficas, el conocimiento y exposición a las plataformas de apuestas deportivas, la participación en estas actividades, las motivaciones y el gasto asociado, así como el impacto y la percepción de sus posibles efectos en la vida cotidiana de los estudiantes. El proceso de validación del instrumento se realizó mediante el Coeficiente de Validez de Contenido (CVC), considerando la valoración de los criterios en términos de suficiencia, claridad, relevancia y coherencia. Como resultado, se obtuvo un CVC global de 0,85, lo que corresponde a una buena validez y concordancia del instrumento. Los resultados permitieron identificar la participación de los estudiantes en diferentes plataformas de apuestas deportivas, destacándose BetPlay como una de las plataformas con mayor utilización. Asimismo, se evidenciaron diferentes motivaciones, niveles de participación y percepciones relacionadas con esta actividad. Entre los aspectos evaluados, la situación económica presentó el mayor porcentaje de impacto positivo percibido por los participantes. En conclusión, el instrumento diseñado presentó propiedades de validez de contenido favorables, por lo que constituye una herramienta pertinente para la identificación y caracterización de las influencias asociadas a las apuestas deportivas en estudiantes universitarios y puede servir como base para futuras investigaciones en esta población.",2026,Rueda,Repositorio - Universidad de Ciencias Aplicadas y Ambientales UDCA.,,,,es,openalex_non_en:dissertation,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1109,"Data-Driven Decision-Making in Fantasy Premier League : Correlations, predictions and optimization strategies","The increased amount of available information has, over the past decades, spread through every industry, and the world of sports has been no exception. Today, various statistics are available almost in real time and for nearly every event. This is naturally also the case with the world’s most popular football league, the English Premier League. This available information is utilized not only within teams themselves, but also widely for other purposes such as betting and fantasy games. Fantasy Premier League (FPL), which focuses on the English Premier League, and the statistics related to it, are at the center of this thesis. FPL provides an ideal environment for applying the principles of data-driven management within the constraints of limited resources. The research question of this thesis is: How can data-driven decision-making enhance performance in Fantasy Premier League (FPL) through correlation analysis, predictive modeling, and optimization strategies? The theoretical section of the thesis focuses on explaining the principles of data-driven management, predictive analytics, and optimization, particularly from the perspective of fantasy sports games. The methodology section describes the data analysis techniques used, presents the underlying mathematical formulas, and explains how the data was collected. The data used in this thesis is numerical, and the research method is quantitative. The empirical section of the thesis consists of a test created using the Python programming language. The first objective was to identify the factors that correlate most strongly with FPL points and recorded match events. After this, the mean absolute error and the root mean squared error were determined to evaluate the effectiveness of the predictive regression models. Finally, a simulation utilizing optimization was carried out, in which the created optimization model selected an FPL team and competed against a team representing a human minded, heuristic model. The results of the thesis indicate that data-driven management can also be applied in the world of fantasy sports. The created model did not outperform the human-like team in every individual round, but overall, it accumulated more points. In addition, it utilized the available budget considerably more efficiently. It should be remembered that FPL is a complex game, and this thesis alone cannot account for all variables, meaning that some simplifications had to be made. The observation period was also too short to draw broader conclusions. Nevertheless, it can be concluded that data-driven management and various forms of data analytics have become a permanent part of the lives of FPL managers going forward.",2026,Ruismäki,Osuva (University of Vaasa),,,,en,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1110,A Study on Gambling Effect on Youths and Their Lifestyle In Tumkuru District,"Abstract The rapid growth of online and offline gambling activities has become a major social concern among young people in recent years. This study focuses on analyzing the effects of gambling on the lifestyle, behavior, financial condition, and social relationships of youths in Tumakuru. The research aims to understand the reasons behind the increasing involvement of youths in gambling activities and to examine how such habits influence their daily lives and future well-being. The study is based on both primary and secondary data. Primary data were collected through questionnaires and direct interaction with selected respondents from different areas of Tumakuru. Secondary information was gathered from journals, articles, reports, and online sources related to gambling behavior among youths. The findings indicate that factors such as peer influence, easy access to online betting platforms, stress, unemployment, and the desire for quick money are major reasons for youth participation in gambling activities. The research further reveals that excessive gambling negatively affects academic performance, mental health, family relationships, financial stability, and social behavior. Many respondents reported experiencing anxiety, loss of concentration, debt, and reduced productivity due to gambling habits. The study also highlights that continuous exposure to gambling can lead to addiction and unhealthy lifestyle patterns among youths. The paper concludes that awareness programs, parental guidance, educational support, and stricter regulation of gambling platforms are necessary to reduce the harmful effects of gambling on young individuals. Creating employment opportunities and promoting healthy recreational activities can also help youths avoid gambling-related risks and develop a more balanced lifestyle .",2026,S,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.22283600,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22283600,,datacite:JournalArticle/ | openaire:Article | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1111,IPL Analysis Using Machine Learning,"The Indian Premier League (IPL), as one of the most popular Twenty-20 cricket leagues worldwide, generates vast amounts of data related to matches, players, teams, and playing conditions, making it an ideal domain for machine learning– based analysis. This paper presents a comprehensive study on the application of machine learning techniques for analyzing IPL data with the objectives of predicting match outcomes, evaluating player performance, and supporting strategic decision-making. Using historical IPL datasets comprising match details, player statistics, team compositions, pitch conditions, and weather factors, the data is preprocessed and transformed through feature engineering to enhance predictive capability. Various supervised machine learning algorithms, including regression, classification, ensemble, and deep learning models, are employed to forecast match results and performance metrics. The study builds upon existing research that demonstrates the effectiveness of models such as Random Forest, Support Vector Machines, and deep learning architectures in sports analytics. The results highlight the potential of machine learning to uncover meaningful patterns, improve prediction accuracy, and provide actionable insights for stakeholders such as team management, analysts, fantasy sports users, and broadcasters. Overall, the proposed approach emphasizes the growing role of data-driven methods in modern cricket analytics and their contribution to enhancing competitiveness, audience engagement, and informed decision-making in the IPL ecosystem.",2026,S,International Journal for Research in Applied Science and Engineering Technology,10.22214/ijraset.2026.77395,,https://doi.org/10.22214/ijraset.2026.77395,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1112,Sports betting as a social phenomenon: Public attitudes and motivation for participation,"This article examines bookmaking and sports betting as a social phenomenon emerging at the intersection of economic, cultural, and institutional processes. It analyzes the contradictory nature of public attitudes toward bookmaking. The theoretical basis of the study is formed by classical and modern sociological approaches to the analysis of gambling, deviant behavior, social inequality, and the rationalization of economic practices. The main focus is on the motivation for participation in sports betting, the role of the social environment, media, and digital communications in the process of involvement. Financial and non-financial incentives for participation, emotional involvement, as well as the characteristics of trust in advertising and informal sources of information are considered. The study is based on data from a sociological survey conducted in Tyumen and examines the relationships between the social context of involvement, motivation for participation, and subjective assessment of the consequences of betting. The range of social and economic consequences of participation in bookmaking practices, as well as strategies for limiting or terminating participation, are analyzed. In conclusion, the place of bookmaking in the structure of modern leisure and economic practices, its role in the reproduction of social differences and the formation of ambivalent public attitudes towards gambling are determined.",2026,S.,Вестник Тюменского государственного университета. Социально-экономические и правовые исследования,10.21684/2411-7897-2026-12-2-58-77,,https://doi.org/10.21684/2411-7897-2026-12-2-58-77,,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1113,"Kanavointiparadoksi: panosrajoittaminen, verotus ja kokeneiden vedonlyöjien poissulkeminen Suomen 2027 rahapeliuudistuksessa","This study examines whether a 90% channelisation target is achievable for the sports bettor segment under Finland’s 2027 gambling reform and investigates the structural mechanisms that may prevent this outcome. The Finnish Gambling Act of 2026 dismantles Veikkaus's decades-long monopoly in favour of a multioperator licensing model. While the reform aims to channel players away from unlicensed offshore operators, this study argues that it creates a Channelisation Paradox for high-turnover sophisticated bettors: the licensed market is commercially inaccessible due to systematic stake limiting by recreational operators, while the unlicensed market is financially unfeasible because positive wager-level gains from unlicensed sports betting may be taxable as earned income, while losses from losing wagers cannot be offset against them. The severity of this non-deductibility principle is illustrated by the Supreme Administrative Court ruling KHO 28.5.2025/1225. The study employs a pragmatist research philosophy using heuristic inquiry and autoethnography as its primary methodology. The empirical dataset comprises a systematically maintained personal betting log covering 23 months of sports betting activity from June 2024 to April 2026, encompassing 10,194 bets, €899,916 in total turnover, and a net return on investment of 2.38% across 33 operators. Seven documented limit events, instances of operator-imposed stake restriction, provide primary empirical evidence of systematic sophisticated bettor exclusion from the regulated market, including one case of restriction propagating automatically across operator brands within the same corporate group. The comparative policy analysis draws on Swedish channelisation data, the KHO ruling, and the Finnish Gambling Act of 2026. The findings demonstrate that operator-imposed stake limiting is driven by predicted future edge rather than realised profitability, as evidenced by one restriction applied at a negative return on investment. The study concludes that the 22% GGR tax rate structurally disadvantages sharp-friendly operator models, that recreational operator dominance of the licensed pool produces systematic exclusion of sophisticated bettors, and that the resulting Asymmetric Tax Trap renders both licensed and unlicensed markets non-viable for this segment. The Swedish precedent, where an identical tax rate has produced five consecutive years of missed channelisation targets, supports the thesis that the Finnish 90% target is structurally incompatible with the current framework for sports bettors.",2026,Saarinen,Aaltodoc (Aalto University),,,,en,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1114,Anatomy of Corruption: Biography of One Hundred Corrupt Politicians of India,"This book is a monumental scholarly investigation into the anatomy of political corruption in India. It is not a simple collection of scandalous stories or a partisan polemic. Instead it is a rigorous multidisciplinary analysis that examines the careers of one hundred politicians who held or sought public office in India and whose conduct was defined by serious corruption allegations, criminal charges, judicial condemnation, or credible documentation by independent sources. The work applies the full intellectual arsenal of criminology, criminal profiling, jurisprudence, political philosophy, and legislative inquiry to understand not only what happened but why it happened, how it was allowed to happen, and what systemic conditions made it possible. The book is organized into fifteen parts, each building on the theoretical and empirical foundations of the previous ones. Part One establishes the foundational architecture. It defines political corruption as the abuse of entrusted power for private gain and traces the historical trajectory from the colonial inheritance through the Nehruvian era, the Indira Gandhi period, the Rajiv Gandhi years, the economic liberalization of 1991, and up to the present. It explains how corruption evolved from the license raj rent seeking to the post liberalization regulatory capture that produced the 2G Spectrum scandal and the Coal Block Allocation scandal. The theoretical frameworks introduced include Merton's strain theory, Sutherland's differential association, rational choice theory, Sykes and Matza's techniques of neutralization, white collar crime theory, institutional corruption theory, and criminal profiling methodologies from the FBI and David Canter. The first part also presents the first seven profiles including Lalu Prasad Yadav and the Fodder Scam, A Raja and the 2G case, Suresh Kalmadi and the Commonwealth Games scandal, J Jayalalithaa and the disproportionate assets case, P Chidambaram and the INX Media case, Arvind Kejriwal and the Delhi Liquor Policy case, and Sukh Ram and the telecom scandal. Each profile is analyzed for criminological type, neutralization techniques used, institutional conditions that enabled the conduct, and legal outcomes. Part Two examines ideological corruption. It asks how political ideologies interact with corrupt practices. The book argues that strong ideological commitment can become a mechanism of moral disengagement, where politicians justify bending rules in service of a higher political project. The profiles in this part include B S Yediyurappa and the Karnataka mining scandal, Bangaru Laxman who was caught on camera accepting a bribe as BJP president, Madhu Koda the Jharkhand chief minister who laundered mining proceeds offshore, Dilip Singh Judeo and the cash for votes controversy, P V Narasimha Rao and the JMM bribery case, Chhagan Bhujbal and the Maharashtra money trail, Ajit Pawar and the irrigation scam, Mulayam Singh Yadav and disproportionate assets, Mayawati and the BSP political economy, and several Trinamool Congress leaders from the Narada sting operation. The part also analyzes the Coal Block Allocation scandal in depth and the defense sector corruption continuum from Bofors to Rafale. Part Three descends into the specific domain of land and mining corruption. It introduces the concept of extraction corruption, which combines financial harm with territorial dispossession and ecological degradation. The part examines the constitutional framework of the Fifth Schedule, the Forest Rights Act, and the Panchayats Extension to Scheduled Areas Act, showing how these protections have been systematically bypassed. Profiles include Hemant Soren who was arrested by the ED and then reelected as chief minister, Shibu Soren and the JMM bribery case, Ashok Chavan and the Adarsh Housing Society scam, Virbhadra Singh and the Himachal disproportionate assets case, D K Shivakumar and the cash haul controversy, Azam Khan and land grabbing in Rampur, and the criminal politicians Mukhtar Ansari and Atique Ahmed who were both killed while in custody. The part also analyzes the Muzaffarnagar riots and the political economy of communal violence. Part Four is titled Meta Corruption: the subversion of justice itself. This part argues that the most dangerous form of corruption is the corruption of the systems designed to detect and punish first order corruption. It examines investigative agency capture, prosecutorial manipulation, judicial pressure, witness management, legal process weaponization, and constitutional engineering of impunity. The profiles include P Chidambaram again but focused on his arrest and the PMLA proceedings, D K Shivakumar again for the ED investigation, Arvind Kejriwal and Manish Sisodia for the Delhi Liquor Policy case and their prolonged pre trial detention, Bhupesh Baghel and the Mahadev online betting app case, and the post mortem analysis of Mulayam Singh Yadav. The part also provides a comprehensive analysis of the Sita Soren judgment of 2024 which overruled the 1998 Narasimha Rao immunity for bribery of legislators, and the Electoral Bonds judgment of 2024 which struck down the opaque political financing scheme. Part Five examines electoral corruption. It presents the theory of electoral integrity and the democratic paradox where candidates with criminal records have a higher win rate than clean candidates. It analyzes vote buying, booth capturing, electoral violence, manipulation of electoral rolls, and the use of state resources for incumbent advantage. The quantitative data from the Association for Democratic Reforms shows that in the 2024 Lok Sabha elections, candidates with declared criminal cases had a success rate of 15.3 percent compared to only 4.4 percent for clean candidates. The part profiles the bahubali politicians of Uttar Pradesh and Bihar including Mukhtar Ansari, Atiq Ahmed, Pappu Yadav, D P Yadav, Azam Khan, and Prabhunath Singh. It also examines the Election Commission of India, the Model Code of Conduct, and the Supreme Court's jurisprudence on disqualification under Section 8 of the Representation of the People Act. Comparative international analysis includes the Philippines, Mexico, and Italy. Part Six analyzes the corporate political nexus in post liberalization India. It develops the frameworks of regulatory capture, crony capitalism, state capture, and structural corruption. The part examines the 2G Spectrum case in even greater detail, the Coalgate scandal, the Commonwealth Games procurement corruption, the Electoral Bonds data analysis, and the defense procurement scandals including Bofors and Rafale. Profiles include Manmohan Singh and the question of systemic responsibility for Coalgate, Naveen Patnaik and the Odisha mining economy, Y S Jagan Mohan Reddy and the disproportionate assets case, N Chandrababu Naidu and the skill development scam, Sharad Pawar and the architecture of long term political capital, and Jayant Sinha and the Jharkhand bail controversy. The part also examines the INX Media case, the Aircel Maxis case, and the role of corporate lobbying in shaping regulatory outcomes. Part Seven confronts the most morally urgent dimension: communal violence and state crime. It develops the theory of state facilitated communal violence using the frameworks of Penny Green and Tony Ward on state crime, Paul Brass on the institutionalized riot system, and Ashutosh Varshney on civic engagement. The part examines the major episodes of communal violence in independent India: the 1984 anti Sikh violence, the 1992 93 Mumbai riots following the Babri Masjid demolition, and the 2002 Gujarat violence. The profiles include Narendra Modi and the SIT closure report on Gujarat 2002, Amit Shah and the Sohrabuddin fake encounter case, L K Advani and the Babri Masjid demolition conspiracy case, Jagdish Tytler and Sajjan Kumar for the 1984 violence, Kalyan Singh for contempt of the Supreme Court in the Babri Masjid case, Maya Kodnani who was convicted then acquitted for the Naroda Patiya massacre, and Bal Thackeray for the organized intimidation culture of the Shiv Sena. The part also examines the Srikrishna Commission report and the failure to implement its recommendations. Part Eight examines the corruption of information itself. It analyzes media capture, paid news, IT cells, troll armies, legal coercion against journalists, and the weaponization of defamation and sedition laws. The theoretical framework draws on Habermas's public sphere, Herman and Chomsky's propaganda model, and the concept of manufactured uncertainty. Profiles include Subramanian Swamy as a political litigant using lawfare, Arnab Goswami and Republic TV for hyper partisan television journalism, Kapil Sibal for internet censorship controversies, Prannoy Roy and Radhika Roy for the hostile takeover of NDTV by the Adani Group, Prabir Purkayastha for the NewsClick case under UAPA, Ravish Kumar as a counter institutional figure, Devendra Fadnavis for election affidavit disclosure issues, Rahul Gandhi for defamation conviction and disqualification, Sudhir Chaudhary for the Doordarshan appointment controversy, and the figures from the Radia tapes revealing the corporate political media triangle. The part also examines the Pegasus spyware scandal and its implications for journalist source protection. Part Nine examines the post liberalization crony capitalism system. It analyzes the mechanisms of spectrum corruption, procurement corruption, regulatory forbearance, regulatory design corruption, post hoc regularization, and evergreening of bank loans. The profiles include Suresh Kalmadi for the Commonwealth Games, A Raja for the 2G acquittal, Vijay Mallya as the fugitive economic offender, Nirav Modi and Mehul Choksi for the Punjab National Bank fraud, Yediyurappa for the Karnataka mining scandal, Madhu Koda for Jharkhand corruption, P K Bansal for railway bribery through his nephew, Robert Vadra for land deals as proximity corru",2026,Sakib,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.19895575,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.19895575,und,datacite:Book/ | openaire:Book | openalex:book,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1115,Mediterranean Diet Adherence and Screening Indicators of Gambling-Related Problems in a Sample of Young Adults: An Exploratory Cross-Sectional Analysis of the EVA-Adic Study,"Background/Objectives: Gambling disorder is recognized as a behavioral addiction, and gambling-related problems represent an important public health concern among young adults. However, evidence regarding their association with overall diet quality, particularly adherence to the Mediterranean diet (MedDiet), remains scarce. This exploratory secondary analysis examined whether screening indicators of gambling-related problems were associated with MedDiet adherence in young adults. Methods: We conducted a cross-sectional analysis of 496 participants aged 18–34 years from the EVA-Adic study. MedDiet adherence was assessed using the 14-item Mediterranean Diet Adherence Screener (MEDAS-14). Gambling-related indicators were assessed using the Short Pathological Gambling Questionnaire (CBJP), the Lie/Bet Questionnaire, and their six individual items. Independent multiple linear regression models were fitted for each indicator. The fully adjusted Model 2 included age, sex, alcohol consumption, sleep quality, sitting time, marital status, and educational level. Given the secondary nature of the analysis, the absence of an a priori sample-size calculation for the present research question, and the limited number of participants with positive gambling-related indicators, the analyses were considered exploratory and hypothesis-generating rather than confirmatory. Results: The mean age was 26.60 ± 4.41 years; 276 participants (55.6%) were women, and 157 (31.7%) had high MedDiet adherence. In the fully adjusted Model 2, feeling guilty about gambling (Q2) was nominally associated with a lower MEDAS-14 score (B = −1.10; 95% CI, −1.97 to −0.22; p = 0.015). However, this association did not remain statistically significant after Benjamini–Hochberg correction (q = 0.116), and none of the eight primary Model 2 associations met the FDR-adjusted significance threshold. Conclusions: Several gambling-related screening indicators showed nominal inverse associations with MedDiet adherence, with gambling-related guilt showing the most consistent estimate across sensitivity analyses. However, no association remained statistically significant in the fully adjusted model after correction for multiple testing, and formal interaction analyses provided no robust evidence of effect modification by sex. Given the small number of positive screens and the cross-sectional design, these findings should be considered exploratory and hypothesis-generating.",2026,Saldaña-Ruiz,Nutrients,10.3390/nu18172919,42739091,https://doi.org/10.3390/nu18172919,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1116,A psicologia do vício em apostas virtuais: uma perspectiva integrativa e existencial do fenômeno,"Os jogos de sorte e o ato de apostar acompanham a trajetória cultural da humanidade, atravessando diferentes épocas e adquirindo características específicas em cada cultura. No Brasil, estima-se que a cultura das apostas tenha se consolidado a partir do século XVI, com a chegada dos jogos de cartas, expandindo-se posteriormente com o “jogo do bicho”, em 1892, e com a criação da Loteria Federal, em 1917. Mesmo após a proibição oficial dos cassinos em 1946, as apostas esportivas mantêm sua presença na sociedade brasileira. No século XXI, com o avanço do mercado de consumo capitalista e a expansão dos meios digitais, as apostas virtuais tornam-se amplamente acessíveis, favorecendo a exploração da realidade socioeconômica do país por meio de estratégias de marketing, publicidade massiva e influenciadores digitais. Nesse contexto, emergem problemas econômicos, psicossociais e comportamentais que se intensificam através de mecanismos de perpetuação do comportamento aditivo, como recompensas dopaminérgicas, estímulos sensoriais e esquemas de reforço variável. A manutenção do ciclo compulsivo, análoga aos danos multifatoriais observados no vício em apostas, evidencia também questões existenciais, como o vazio existencial e sua relação direta com comportamentos de fuga, autoentorpecimento e frustração de sentido. O presente trabalho realiza uma revisão narrativa da literatura, de caráter qualitativo, visando integrar as dimensões histórica, socioeconômica, comportamental e existencial do fenômeno, traçando um nexo causal entre o comportamento aditivo, os impactos psicossociais das casas de apostas virtuais e a dinâmica do vazio existencial à luz da Logoterapia e da análise existencial.",2026,Sales,Brazilian Journal of Health Review,10.34119/bjhrv9n1-083,,https://doi.org/10.34119/bjhrv9n1-083,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1117,PERSUASÃO DIGITAL E APOSTA ONLINE: UM EXPERIMENTO DE CAMPO SOBRE APELOS CENTRAIS E PERIFÉRICOS EM ANÚNCIOS DE APOSTAS ESPORTIVAS,,2026,Sales,Anais do VII Congresso Internacional de Sustentabilidade,10.51189/vii-coninters/83390,,https://doi.org/10.51189/vii-coninters/83390,,crossref:proceedings-article | openalex_non_en:conference-paper,openalex_non_en;crossref,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1118,Winning the Wager: Lessons from U.S. Sports Betting Markets for the Economic Design of Legislation in Texas,"Texas remains one of the few holdout states since Murphy v. NCAA (2018) opened the door to legalized sports betting across the country. Rather than asking whether Texas should legalize, this thesis asks how: specifically, how should the state design the economic framework for a legalized market? Through a comparative case study of six U.S. sports betting jurisdictions, it analyzes tax base design and market structure to recommend a gross gaming revenue-based, competitive multi-operator model suited to Texas's fiscal infrastructure and constitutional constraints. These findings are made more urgent by the state's existing ""grey market,"" which already delivers functional sports betting to Texans outside any regulatory or fiscal framework, exposing the state to consumer harms without the corresponding revenue benefits. The central argument is that the form of legalization is more consequential than the fact of it: for Texas, economic design carries both fiscal and meaningful social stakes.",2026,Sall,The University of Texas at Austin,10.26153/tsw/64147,,https://doi.org/10.26153/tsw/64147,en,datacite:Dissertation/Thesis | openaire:Thesis | openalex:dissertation,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1119,A RESPONSABILIDADE CIVIL DOS INFLUENCIADORES DIGITAIS NA PUBLICIDADE DE APOSTAS ESPORTIVAS À LUZ DO DIREITO DO CONSUMIDOR,"O presente artigo analisa a responsabilidade civil dos influenciadores digitais na publicidade de apostas esportivas, especialmente diante da expansão das plataformas conhecidas como bets e da crescente influência desses agentes sobre as decisões de consumo. O problema de pesquisa consiste em verificar em que medida os influenciadores podem ser responsabilizados civilmente quando divulgam apostas esportivas de forma inadequada, sobretudo nos casos de publicidade enganosa, omissão de riscos ou ausência de identificação clara do conteúdo patrocinado. O objetivo geral é examinar os limites jurídicos dessa atuação à luz do Código de Defesa do Consumidor, do Código Civil, da Lei n.º 14.790/2023 e das diretrizes de autorregulamentação publicitária. A pesquisa adota metodologia bibliográfica e documental. Conclui-se que, quando atuam mediante remuneração ou vantagem econômica, os influenciadores digitais podem assumir deveres de informação, transparência e boa-fé perante os consumidores, podendo ser responsabilizados civilmente quando sua conduta contribui para induzir o público em erro, ocultar riscos relevantes ou estimular expectativas irreais de ganho.",2026,Sanches,Aracê.,10.56238/arev8n5-126,,https://doi.org/10.56238/arev8n5-126,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1120,"Pleasure, Appetite, and the Things Christians Call Harmless","This chapter examines the moral and spiritual life of pleasure in a culture where appetite is increasingly treated as self-validating and where many practices are defended with the sentence, ""It is harmless."" Its governing claim is not that pleasure is evil, but that every pleasure must remain a gift under the lordship of Jesus Christ rather than becoming a master, an anesthetic, a substitute savior, or a rival source of identity. Scripture affirms wine, food, feasting, rest, music, celebration, recreation, beauty, friendship, and bodily enjoyment as creaturely goods, while also warning against drunkenness, intoxication, greed, dissipation, gluttony, sloth, occult participation, and every form of enslavement. The chapter therefore rejects both permissiveness and ascetic legalism. It refuses to call every enjoyable practice sin merely because it is pleasurable, and it refuses to call a practice safe merely because Scripture does not name its modern form explicitly. Using the biblical categories of command, principle, wisdom, conscience, liberty, stewardship, love, and mastery, the chapter evaluates alcohol, nicotine and vaping, cannabis and other recreational drugs, prescription-drug misuse, gambling and sports betting, speculative trading, gaming and streaming, artistic entertainment, occult-themed media and spiritual curiosity, celebrity culture and parasocial desire, food and fasting, sleep and leisure, and the broader problem of good gifts becoming controlling appetites. Contemporary public-health and behavioral evidence is used descriptively where it clarifies dependence, gambling harm, gaming disorder, binge-watching, parasocial engagement, obesity, sleep, and substance-related risk. Medical treatment is carefully distinguished from intoxication, necessary medication from misuse, and clinical research with psychedelics from recreational or spiritualized drug use. New Age, New Thought, manifestation, divination, entheogenic spirituality, and occult experimentation are examined where pleasure and altered consciousness are presented as routes to awakening, guidance, or divine access. The chapter argues that Christian liberty is not the right to ask how close one may live to enslavement while remaining technically permitted. Christian freedom is freedom to belong to Christ, love the neighbor, discipline appetite, receive created goods with thanksgiving, and surrender any gift that begins to rule the heart. The drunk, smoker, gambler, compulsive gamer, binge-watcher, comfort eater, celebrity-obsessed fan, exhausted workaholic, and fearful legalist are not identical cases and must not be treated with one slogan. Yet all are summoned to the same Savior. Christ alone justifies, abstinence cannot save, and pleasure cannot condemn the repentant believer who comes to Him. At the same time, grace must never be turned into permission for intoxication, bondage, waste, spiritual dullness, or idolatry. The chapter concludes by calling the reader from appetite as authority to Christ as Lord, from false reassurance to sober self-examination, and from temporary pleasure to holy joy, durable freedom, and readiness for His return.",2026,SANGWA,,10.65655/ocp.m22.c52,,https://doi.org/10.65655/ocp.m22.c52,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1121,A Study on Marketing Environment with Reference to Betting Apps in India,"This study explores the burgeoning online betting app market in India, highlighting its rapid expansion fueled by increased smartphone usage, widespread internet access, and the integration of digital payment systems like UPI. The rise of fantasy sports and real-money gaming platforms has transformed the way Indian users engage with sports and mobile entertainment. The research delves into the intricate regulatory environment, marked by central and state-level legal complexities, evolving definitions of \\\""games of skill\\\"" versus \\\""games of chance,\\\"" and advertising restrictions that pose significant challenges to market Players. The study emphasizes the growing need for localization strategies, including support for regional languages, cultural relevance, and sport-specific targeting—especially cricket, which remains the dominant sport influencing user engagement. Using strategic marketing frameworks such as PESTLE and 5Cs analysis, the research examines how betting and fantasy platforms deploy aggressive promotions, influencer marketing, and intuitive app design to attract and retain users in a highly competitive environment. Through secondary data analysis, the project identifies key trends in user behavior, regulatory shifts, and market dynamics. It concludes that while the Indian betting app market holds substantial growth potential, long-term success depends on how well platforms navigate legal uncertainties, build consumer trust, and adopt responsible marketing practices tailored to the Indian audience. The findings offer valuable insights for marketers, app developers, and policy-makers interested in the evolving landscape of digital gaming and betting in India.",2026,Sanketh,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.23180459,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23180459,en,datacite:JournalArticle/ | datacite:Text/ | openaire:Article | openaire:Report | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1122,Arbitragem de Apostas Esportivas Como um Problema de Programação Linear Inteira,Anais do Encontro Nacional de Modelagem Computacional e Encontro de Ciência e Tecnologia de Materiais (978-65-272-2245-3) - Arbitragem De Apostas Esportivas Como Um Problema De Programação Linear Inteira,2026,Santos,Encontro Nacional de Modelagem Computacional e Encontro de Ciência e Tecnologia de Materiais (ENMC/ECTM),10.29327/9786527222453.1200012,,https://doi.org/10.29327/9786527222453.1200012,und,crossref:proceedings-article | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:conference-paper,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1123,Perceived parental attitudes and gambling behavior in adults: the moderating role of resilience,"Introduction Although multiple risk factors for problematic gambling have been identified, evidence regarding perceived parental attitudes and resilience remains limited, particularly among adults in Türkiye. This study investigated the associations between perceived parental attitudes and gambling behavior and the moderating role of resilience. Methods A cross-sectional online correlational study was conducted in İstanbul, Türkiye, between October 2024 and May 2025 with 389 participants recruited via convenience sampling. Data were collected using the Abbreviated Perceived Parental Attitudes–Child Form, the Brief Psychological Resilience Scale, and the South Oaks Gambling Screen–Turkish Version. Moderator analyses with bias-corrected bootstrap confidence intervals were performed. Results Maternal rejection was positively associated with gambling severity (B = 0.106, 95% CI [0.053, 0.160], p < 0.001), as was paternal rejection (B = 0.123, 95% CI [0.073, 0.174], p < 0.001). Resilience significantly moderated both associations (maternal rejection × resilience: B = −0.017, 95% CI [−0.028, −0.005], p = 0.005; paternal rejection × resilience: B = −0.016, 95% CI [−0.028, −0.003], p = 0.012). Simple slope analyses showed that the associations between parental rejection and gambling severity were significant at low (maternal: B = 0.176, p < 0.001; paternal: B = 0.189, p < 0.001) and average (maternal: B = 0.106, p < 0.001; paternal: B = 0.123, p < 0.001) levels of resilience but were not significant at high resilience (maternal: B = 0.037, p = 0.265; paternal: B = 0.058, p = 0.092). Exploratory correlation analyses additionally showed that parental emotional warmth was positively associated with resilience. Discussion These findings suggest that resilience may buffer the association between perceived parental rejection and gambling severity.",2026,Sarıbudak,Frontiers in Psychology,10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1913942,42656468,https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyg.2026.1913942,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1124,Bad Bets: Examining the Legality of Sports-Related Prediction Markets,"Historically, the Commodity Futures Trading Commission (""CFTC"") employed a strict framework in which only prediction markets for research and academic value were permitted.This framework rejected political event contracts.In September 2024, a ruling from U.S. Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit as well as recent press releases from the Trump Administration have signaled a paradigm shift in the acceptance and legitimacy of prediction markets.This industry seized the moment and began offering event contracts over a broad swath of events including sports.These commercial platforms say their ability to offer sports-related event contracts are governed by the CFTC.However, the industry's position has drawn opposition from both state and Tribal Gaming Commissions, who view sports-related event contracts as sports gambling and subject to state law.This has led to an increase in legislation initiated by state and Tribal gaming regulators.This Note argues that sports-related event contracts are gambling and that the Supreme Court's ruling in Murphy v. NCAA prevents federal law from preempting state and Tribal gaming commissions from regulating these contracts.",2026,Saunders,University of North Carolina School of Law Scholarship Repository (University of North Carolina Hospitals),,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1125,White Label Prediction Market Platform,"Prediction markets are rapidly becoming one of the most engaging digital products across sports, finance, politics, entertainment, and current events. Companies like Polymarket and Kalshi have demonstrated how users are willing to trade on the probability of future events instead of relying on traditional betting or financial products. However, building a prediction market from scratch is a major engineering challenge. It requires a matching engine, liquidity management, settlement systems, KYC, compliance tools, market surveillance, risk controls, and a scalable backend capable of handling thousands of transactions per second. A White Label Prediction Market Platform removes this complexity by providing production-ready infrastructure that you can launch under your own brand. Whether you're an iGaming operator, media company, fintech startup, sports platform, or enterprise, a white-label solution enables you to enter the prediction market industry significantly faster while reducing development costs and technical risk. What Is a White Label Prediction Market Platform? A White Label Prediction Market Platform is a fully customizable software solution that allows businesses to launch their own branded prediction market without developing the underlying technology. Instead of spending 12–24 months building the platform internally, businesses receive a ready-to-deploy solution that includes: Complete branding customization Trading engine Market creation tools Liquidity management User wallets Settlement automation Administrative dashboard APIs Security infrastructure Your users see your brand, while the technology operates seamlessly in the background. Why Businesses Are Choosing White Label Platforms Launching a prediction market involves far more than creating a simple trading interface. A successful platform requires: Real-time market data Order matching Automated market makers (AMM) Central Limit Order Book (CLOB) Position management User authentication Payment integrations Settlement engines Fraud detection Market surveillance Analytics High availability infrastructure Developing these systems internally requires a multidisciplinary engineering team and a substantial investment. A white-label platform provides proven infrastructure that has already been tested in production environments. Key Features of a White Label Prediction Market Platform AI-Powered Market Creation Artificial intelligence can automatically generate markets based on trending topics, sports events, financial news, elections, entertainment, or custom categories. Benefits include: Faster market creation Trend detection Automated descriptions Suggested resolutions Market categorization Multiple Trading Models Modern prediction markets typically support: Central Limit Order Book (CLOB) Ideal for professional traders seeking transparent pricing and deep liquidity. Automated Market Maker (AMM) Ensures continuous liquidity even when trading volume is low. Many enterprise platforms support both models simultaneously. Complete White Label Branding Businesses can customize: Logo Domain Color scheme UI/UX Trading experience Emails Mobile applications Admin dashboard The platform appears entirely as your own product. Automated Market Settlement Manual settlement is time-consuming and error-prone. A professional platform automates: Market resolution Winning calculations Wallet credits Transaction history Notifications Settlement can be triggered using trusted data sources or oracle integrations. User Wallet Management Integrated wallet functionality includes: Deposits Withdrawals Balance tracking Transaction history Multi-currency support Crypto wallet support Fiat payment integration Risk & Market Monitoring Enterprise platforms provide: Position limits Market suspension User restrictions Fraud detection Trading surveillance Admin approvals These features help maintain platform integrity. Scalable Infrastructure High-profile events can generate sudden traffic spikes. A modern prediction market platform should support: Cloud deployment Auto scaling WebSocket infrastructure Real-time updates Low latency High availability Industries Using White Label Prediction Markets iGaming Operators Prediction markets provide a new revenue stream beyond traditional sportsbooks and casino games. Popular categories include: Sports outcomes Player performance Entertainment Elections Current events Media Companies Publishers can transform audience engagement into interactive experiences by allowing readers to predict news outcomes. Examples include: Election forecasts Business news Technology launches Entertainment awards Financial Platforms Prediction markets can complement trading products by enabling users to speculate on economic events, company announcements, inflation data, and market trends. Sports Organizations Sports teams and leagues can improve fan engagement by offering prediction markets around: Match results Player performance Tournament outcomes Fantasy sports events Enterprises Internal prediction markets help organizations forecast: Sales targets Product launches Demand forecasting Project completion Business KPIs Benefits of a White Label Prediction Market Platform Faster Time-to-Market Launch within weeks instead of spending years on development. Lower Development Costs Avoid hiring large engineering teams and reduce infrastructure investment. Proven Technology Production-tested architecture reduces technical risk and improves reliability. Customizable Platform Deliver a unique user experience while leveraging mature backend infrastructure. Enterprise Security Professional platforms include: Encryption Secure authentication DDoS protection Access controls Monitoring Backup systems Easy Integrations Connect with existing business systems using APIs, including: CRM Payment gateways Identity verification Analytics Marketing automation Data providers Essential Features to Look For Before choosing a provider, ensure the platform offers: AI-powered market creation CLOB & AMM trading Automated settlement White-label branding Wallet management KYC/AML integration Admin dashboard API-first architecture WebSocket support Mobile responsiveness Analytics & reporting High scalability Multi-language support Multi-currency support Cloud deployment Why AI Is Transforming Prediction Markets Artificial intelligence is reshaping prediction market operations by automating repetitive tasks and improving efficiency. AI can assist with: Market generation Trending event discovery Risk analysis User behavior insights Fraud detection Market moderation Personalized recommendations This enables operators to scale while reducing operational overhead. Future of White Label Prediction Markets As prediction markets continue to expand into finance, sports, media, and enterprise forecasting, demand for ready-to-launch infrastructure will grow. Organizations increasingly prefer white-label platforms because they offer: Faster product launches Lower technical complexity Proven infrastructure Reduced operational costs Greater flexibility Continuous innovation Businesses can focus on acquiring users, building their brand, and creating engaging markets instead of developing complex backend systems. Conclusion Launching a prediction market no longer requires years of development or significant engineering resources. A White Label Prediction Market Platform provides the technology needed to build a branded, scalable, and secure prediction exchange with significantly less time and investment. Whether you're an iGaming operator, fintech company, media brand, sports organization, or enterprise, a white-label solution lets you focus on growth while relying on enterprise-grade infrastructure for market creation, liquidity, settlement, and security. As interest in event-based trading and predictive intelligence continues to grow, adopting a white-label prediction market platform offers a practical path to entering this fast-evolving market with confidence.",2026,Saxena,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.21526616,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21526616,und,datacite:Text/ | openaire:Other literature type | openalex:article,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1126,From Murphy to Monopolies: Rethinking State Online Sports Betting Regulation,"Since the Supreme Court’s decision in Murphy v. NCAA invalidated the Professional and Amateur Sports Protection Act, states have assumed exclusive authority over the regulation of sports betting. This decentralization has produced a highly fragmented regulatory landscape, particularly in the online sports betting market, where some states permit robust competition among numerous operators while others affirmatively sanction monopolies or highly concentrated markets. These state-level choices interact with an already concentrated national market dominated by DraftKings and FanDuel, creating significant variation in consumer choice, pricing, and regulatory outcomes across jurisdictions. This Note examines the sources and consequences of market power in online sports betting, distinguishing between naturally occurring national concentration and state-created intra-state market power. It situates state-sanctioned monopolies within the broader theoretical framework of “justified monopolies,” drawing comparisons to industries such as alcohol distribution, pharmaceuticals, telecommunications, and securities markets. Although proponents argue that monopolistic regulation can generate higher tax revenues, enhance consumer protection, and promote long-term innovation, this Note contends that online sports betting does not satisfy the conditions necessary to justify such market concentration. This Note argues that competitive online sports betting markets are better suited to advancing consumer welfare and regulatory goals than state-level monopolies. It further suggests that the current patchwork of state regimes risks distorting consumer behavior, reducing effective competition, and undermining potential gains from innovation. Drawing on the regulatory logic of Regulation National Market System in U.S. securities law, the Note proposes that competition—rather than state-imposed exclusivity—offers a more effective and adaptable framework for regulating online sports betting in the post-Murphy era.",2026,Schachter,Columbia Business Law Review,10.52214/cblr.v2025i2.14673,,https://doi.org/10.52214/cblr.v2025i2.14673,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1127,AI-Assisted Bettors: Analyzing AI-Driven Betting Behavior through Cluster Analysis,"The use of artificial intelligence (AI) systems in consumer-facing decision-support systems (DSS) such as predictive analytics and automated recommendation platforms is growing in popularity in numerous domains, including sports betting. The degree to which users interact with the output of automated systems, calibrate trust, and exhibit automation bias–consistent behavior is largely unknown. In this study, we investigate the behavioral segments formed by bettors using AI-powered predictive sports betting DSS based on their shifts in confidence, risk-taking behavior, and bankroll management practices. We use survey data from a sample of 200 U.S.-based bettors and SPSS TwoStep Clustering to identify three distinct behavioral profiles: Traditional Bettors, AI-Influenced Confident Bettors, and AI-Adopting Risk-Takers, each with their own unique set of interactions with and through predictive DSS. The findings show that bettors can engage in responsible adoption through strategic bankroll management practices and a tempering of AI trust, while overreliance behaviors can be mitigated or amplified, respectively, by counter or co-aligning with individual differences. Framing betting platforms as real-time, in-the-wild, and consumer-deployed DSS contributes to IS research on algorithmic decision environments, user trust, and human–computer interaction. The results advance IS theory by contributing to the discussion of how cognitive biases, human decision behaviors, and confidence amplification in and through automated systems manifest in such domains. We conclude the paper with implications for responsible DSS design and deployment as well as practical guidelines for user segmentation in predictive analytics DSS environments.",2026,Schilhabel,Journal of the Association for Information Systems,10.17705/3jmwa.000097,,https://doi.org/10.17705/3jmwa.000097,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1128,Predicting and Understanding Mobile Sports Gambling with the Reasoned Action Approach.,"Mobile sports betting is an increasingly popular way to wager in the United States, but few studies have examined the social cognitive predictors of engaging in this specific form of gambling. The present studies were designed to do so with the application of the Reasoned Action Approach (RAA; Fishbein & Ajzen, 2010). Study 1 was a belief elicitation study that identified the most common behavioral, normative, and control beliefs regarding mobile sports betting. Study 1 also reported an initial test of the degree to which attitude, perceived normative pressure, and perceived behavioral control predict intentions to place mobile sports bets. Study 2 examined the predictive validity of the identified beliefs and tested whether future betting behavior can be predicted from the model. The RAA significantly predicted mobile sports gambling intentions and future behavior. The most important beliefs contributing to the prediction of mobile sports gambling were the degree that participants endorsed gambling is fun and easy, leads to addiction, and results in winning money. Implications for future research and interventions are discussed.",2026,Schlaffer,Journal of gambling studies,10.1007/s10899-025-10456-1,41207989,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-025-10456-1,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,6,False,False,False,,,,,
R1129,Risk Governance in Esports Gambling: Addressing Integrity and Regulation in a Rapidly Evolving Ecosystem,"Esports gambling has rapidly grown into a multi-billion-dollar global industry, incorporating traditional betting and fantasy esports. While this expansion brings exciting opportunities, it also presents significant risks, including threats to competitive integrity (match-fixing, cheating), financial vulnerabilities from unregulated skin-betting and cryptocurrency transactions, and social concerns like underage participation and problem gambling. Traditional risk management, which focuses on mitigating known risks within single organizations, is inadequate in this decentralized, transnational environment with diverse stakeholders: game publishers, tournament organizers, regulators, betting operators, and players. This presentation proposes risk governance as a more holistic approach to these challenges. Unlike reactive risk management, risk governance proactively anticipates, prioritizes, and addresses risks across organizational boundaries. It emphasizes collaboration between public and private sectors to create legitimacy, transparency, and trust while fostering innovation. By examining examples such as the Counter-Strike skin-betting scandal and Riot Games' evolving gambling policies in League of Legends, this presentation demonstrates how risk governance can address esports gambling’s systemic vulnerabilities. It advocates for governance frameworks that balance rapid technological innovation with consumer protection and competitive integrity.",2026,Scholz,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1130,Football World Cup 2026 Gambling Study,"The present study includes online research into betting behaviour within the real-life context of the 2026 World Cup. Variables aside from descriptives that will be investigated are sports betting consumption, gambling problems, bet amount, preferred odds, bet choice motivation, subjective probability of the real-life bets, and subjective probability of an urn-and-ball task. The manipulation will be in the verbal framing of the bet (aggregated vs. disaggregated), and the verbal and visual framing of the urn-and-ball task (aggregated vs. disaggregated). The present research will investigate whether aggregated vs. disaggregated framing of the bets will change sports betting behaviour (bet amount). Additionally, the sports betting behaviour will be investigated as a function of subjective probabilities. The overall aim is to discover whether framing can be applied as a way to increase subjective probability, to subsequently lessen the amounts bet by sports bettors. Furthermore, the present study will add to the literature on determinants of betting behaviour, to eventually be used to inform policy on how (sports) bettors can be better protected. We hypothesize that bet amounts will differ between aggregated and disaggregated bet framing conditions. Since the preparation timeline of the study is very short and dependent on the FIFA World Cup 2026, we did not have sufficient time to preregister all analyses, but we will add amendments to this pre-registration to further specify additional analyses before inspection of the data (aside from quality checks).",2026,Schuttinga,OSF Registries,10.17605/osf.io/fwq67,,https://doi.org/10.17605/osf.io/fwq67,,datacite:StudyRegistration/Pre-registration | openalex:preprint,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1131,Associations Between Gambling Motives and More Severe Gambling Disorder – Does Gender Matter?,"Gambling Disorder (GD) is a behavioural addiction with complex, detrimental psychological, economic, and social consequences. Previous research has examined gender differences in the prevalence of distinct gambling motives and has linked gambling motives to at-risk behaviour. Whether motives affect probabilities of transition to higher GD severity levels differently in males and females remains unclear. Using an online self-test, we assessed the prevalence distinct GD severity levels according to the DSM-5 criteria and gambling motives with items addressing domains of the ""Gambling Motives Questionnaire - Financial"" (GFQ-F). Associations between motives and GD severity at three transition points (no GD to mild, mild to moderate, moderate to severe GD) were analysed using age-adjusted logistic adjacent category regression models. Interaction terms (motive*gender) were examined to detect gender-specific ""effects"". Furthermore gender-stratified models were run. No significant gender*motive interactions emerged. In the total sample, a transition to a higher GD severity level was predicted by: ""(to) Forget worries"", ""When depressed,"" and ""(to) Earn money"" at each transition point; ""Thrill"" and ""Stress"" at the first two points, and ""Boredom"" at the first point. In males, the results were nearly identical. In females only ""Thrill"", ""Boredom"", and ""(to) Earn money"" were predictive at the first transition point. Given the prevalence of coping motives in females and the consistent detrimental ""effect"" of coping and financial motives in males, these motives should be prioritized in treatment and support offers for both genders. For prevention, strategies targeting enhancement motives may be particularly effective.",2026,Schwarzkopf,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-025-10470-3,41729485,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-025-10470-3,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1132,Beat the Sportsbook: The Theory and Practice of Successful Sports Betting,"Sports betting is a major sector of American Gaming economy. In 2024, Americans wagered almost $150 billion dollars in legal online and brick and mortar sportsbooks, resulting in record industry revenue of $13.7 billion. Moreover, sports betting holds a particular importance for gaming operators as it attracts younger and more affluent consumers, most of whom would be unlikely to participate in other casino games, such as slot machines. Some purists claim that betting on sports jeopardizes the integrity of competition; however that argument does not pass the test of time. As a matter of fact, historians have found that when sports emerged gambling did too. Separating the two may be impossible. Also, in many ways having sports betting operate in a regulated transparent market is superior to illegal betting and opaque rules. Despite the popularity of sports betting, public consensus holds that it is nearly impossible for bettors to overcome the vig (fee sportsbooks charge for booking a bet) and make money in the long run. While other forms of risk taking, such as investing in high-risk growth stocks, high-yield bonds, and cryptocurrencies, are viewed as daring risk taking, sports betting still has a negative social stigma. In this research we explore quantitative and qualitative strategies that allow sports bettors to overcome the inherent odds disadvantages and realize both the theoretical (expected value) and practical (actual results) positive returns on their sports wagers.",2026,Scott,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1133,Branding Through Sport,"Branding through sport encompasses the strategies of non-sport organizations to build and enhance their own brands through the vehicle of sport. The chapter explores how brands leverage fans’ existing emotional attachments toward players and teams to strengthen a given company’s/product’s brand equity. Relevant concepts are discussed including brand equity, image transfer, parasocial relationships, source credibility, and athlete endorsement. Emerging trends including artificial intelligence and sport betting are also overviewed in the context of branding through sport. The chapter closes with managerial implications for key stakeholders and an international case study exploring the strategic partnership between FC Barcelona and Spotify.",2026,Sears,Routledge eBooks,10.4324/9781003687016-17,,https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003687016-17,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1134,The Intersection of Sport Betting and Consumer Behavior: A Systematic Review,"Sport betting is a popular and lucrative entertainment segment of the sport industry. As such, understanding consumer behavior across the betting experiences is critical for the many stakeholders involved. The current study is a systematic quantitative literature review which assesses the current state of literature that intersects sport betting and consumer behavior literature, specifically cognitive, affective, conative, and consumption behavior of the sport bettors. The purpose of the systematic review is to (1) identify the most recent sport betting consumer behavior research, (2) compile and examine theoretical frameworks guiding sport gambling consumer research, (3) provide a descriptive summary of findings from previous sports betting consumer behavior research, and (4) provide an agenda for future research on the topic, identifying theoretical and practical implications for additional inquiry. This systematic quantitative literature review on 49 sports betting consumer behavior studies found no consistent application of theory, a bias toward quantitative research designs, and inconsistent scale usage to measure common sport betting related constructs. Future work should aim to publish more consistently in sport management or gambling studies journals, to employ scales to measure key constructs like sport betting behavior and diversify research design approaches.",2026,Sears,UNLV Gaming Research & Review Journal,10.9741/2327-8455.1523,,https://doi.org/10.9741/2327-8455.1523,en,crossref:journal-article | datacite:Text/Article | openalex:article | openalex:review | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;crossref;datacite,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1135,The Effects of Game-Related Experiences on Delinquent Behavior among Adolescents: The Mediating Roles of Gambling Problem Perception and Gambling Experience.,"This study aims to examine the effect of adolescents’ game-related experiences on deviant behavior and to analyze whether problem gambling perception and gambling experience serve as mediating variables in this relationship. In recent years, digital gaming environments have become a primary leisure space for adolescents. However, concerns have been raised as these environments increasingly incorporate gambling-like features, such as loot boxes, virtual item trading, and sports betting-related elements, which may lead to gambling behaviors and subsequent deviant activities. This study goes beyond examining direct effects by structurally testing a parallel mediation model in which problem gambling perception and gambling experience function as key mediators between game-related experiences and deviant behavior. The analysis utilized data from the “2022 National Survey on Youth Gambling Problems” conducted by the Korea Center on Gambling Problems, with a total sample of 18,944 adolescents, including both in-school and out-of-school youth. The results indicate that game-related experiences have a significant positive effect on deviant behavior. In addition, game-related experiences were found to negatively influence problem gambling perception while positively affecting gambling experience. The mediation analysis revealed that game-related experiences influence deviant behavior through both a cognitive pathway, which weakens problem gambling perception, and a behavioral pathway, which increases gambling experience. These findings suggest that adolescents’ gaming experiences extend beyond mere leisure activities and may contribute to deviant behavior through gambling-related perceptions and behaviors. This study highlights the need for preventive policy approaches that move beyond reactive control strategies. Specifically, it emphasizes the importance of proactively managing gambling-like elements within gaming environments and enhancing adolescents’ awareness of gambling risks as part of comprehensive prevention efforts.",2026,Security,The Korean Society of Private Security,10.56603/jksps.2026.25.1.191,,https://doi.org/10.56603/jksps.2026.25.1.191,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1136,Introduction,"This introduction traces a personal and intellectual journey rooted in childhood play, situating games as a foundational force in identity formation, creativity, and lifelong learning. Drawing on memories of growing up in a small village in upstate New York, the narrative explores informal, imaginative play – ranging from backyard sports and solitary role-playing to tabletop and early fantasy sports games – as a formative medium for social connection, emotional expression, and creative experimentation. These early experiences with play, chance, and invention are shown to anticipate later intellectual interests in game design, probability, imagination, and the pedagogical value of play. By weaving autobiography with cultural references to sport, film, literature, and philosophy, the introduction frames play not as trivial diversion but as a serious, generative activity that shapes cognition, relationships, and meaning across the lifespan. The chapter ultimately positions play as both a source of joy and a complex psychological force – capable of fostering creativity, mental health, and learning, while also carrying the potential for conflict and destruction – setting the stage for a broader psychoanalytic and educational exploration of play in the chapters that follow.",2026,Seelow,CRC Press eBooks,10.1201/9781003108979-1,,https://doi.org/10.1201/9781003108979-1,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0910;R1917;R2335;R2530;R5024;R5279;R5560,,,
R1137,Corporate Political Strategies and Discursive Frames of the Gambling Industry in Finland's Gambling Policy Reform,"Aims Finnish gambling policy, long based on a state-controlled monopoly, is set to shift to a license-based regime in 2027. The study investigates the Corporate Political Activity (CPA) strategies and discursive frames used by gambling industry actors, comparing their application among actors and phases of the legislative process in the context of Finland's gambling policy reform. Methods In total, 36 formal statements were analyzed, including submissions to the public consultation ( n = 19) and parliamentary hearings ( n = 17). A deductive content analysis was conducted, based on the previous CPA frameworks. Results The results indicate that gambling industry actors proposed alternative policies and sought to prevent certain parts of the legislation from being enacted. Discursive framings shifted throughout the legislative process: legal rights dominated consultations, while unintended effects, especially increased unlicensed gambling, became more salient in parliamentary hearings. Clear differences emerged among actors. International actors prioritized policy substitution and framed the proposed legislation as excessive and harmful to their legal rights, whereas the state monopoly operator did not prevent enactment of regulations. A further difference concerned proposals to replace some harm-reduction measures with more restrictive alternatives. Conclusions The growing influence of the gambling industry may risk deprioritizing the prevention and reduction of gambling harm in future gambling policy.",2026,Selin,Nordic Studies on Alcohol and Drugs,10.1177/14550725261453262,42255203,https://doi.org/10.1177/14550725261453262,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1138,How Indian Law Can Take On Prediction Market Giants,"&lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;Indian gaming has been the lifeblood for many tribes. In 2024, tribal gaming brought in $43.9 billion in gross revenue. But online sports betting poses “an existential threat to Tribal sovereignty and economies.” Prediction markets like Kalshi operate as “designated contract market[s]” accessible in anywhere in the country. These platforms tout themselves as “CFTC regulated” exchanges that allow users to “trad[e] on the outcome of future events.” Users can trade on sports events like NBA matches (and can even get a $10 trading bonus if they use a Kalshi promo code). With billions at stake, states and tribes have gone to court. There are at least nineteen pending federal lawsuits involving online prediction markets. Recently, the Third Circuit ruled in favor of Kalshi. Meanwhile, the Ninth Circuit just heard oral arguments, and observers there noted the bench’s skepticism towards the prediction markets. Tribes and tribal gaming associations have submitted amici urging courts to rule against these platforms. California tribes have sued Kalshi specifically for IGRA violations, and are currently appealing a district court’s decision.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;p&gt;&lt;span&gt;This paper argues that an IGRA-centered interpretation can resolve whether prediction markets’ sports-related contracts are “gaming” as it relates to tribal lands. By using traditional statutory interpretation principles to grapple with IGRA’s text, the law points to one conclusion: that Kalshi’s sports-related operations is class III gaming under IGRA. Therefore, absent a Tribal-State compact authorizing its operation, Kalshi’s operations on Indian lands violates the Unlawful Internet Gambling Enforcement Act (UIGEA). This paper does not substantively focus on whether sports-related events contracts are “swaps” under the Commodities Exchange Act (CEA). This paper does argue that even if they could be considered “swaps” under federal law, Indian pre-emption doctrine would preclude CEA to render IGRA surplusage. This paper also proposes that tribal-state compacting offers a solution. Of course, a statutory fix could resolve this problem in a broad stroke. But the “hub-and-spoke” tribal-state compact model could serve as a desirable solution for both states and tribes. As online prediction giants unlawfully threaten tribal gaming, courts need not look further than IGRA itself to resolve this dispute.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;",2026,Sepe,SSRN Electronic Journal,10.2139/ssrn.6690798,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6690798,und,openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,openalex;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1139,A qualitative investigation of the retrospective lived experiences and emotional trajectories of Australian gamblers,"PURPOSE: The current study sought to understand and explore how gamblers retrospectively characterise the shifts in their emotional valence across a gambling session, and how they interpret changes in their emotional arousal as a gambling session unfolds. METHODS: A total of 20 Australian gamblers, aged between 20 and 63 years (Mage = 34.9, SDage = 10.23), participated in retrospective semi-structured interviews. Participants were asked to reconstruct and narrate the emotional shifts experienced across gambling sessions and elucidate how they interpreted and understood the changes in their emotions as a gambling session unfolded. Data was analysed via reflexive thematic analysis using an interpretivist epistemological framework. RESULTS: Five key themes were constructed from the data: (1) Affective Recovery: Feelings of Pride and Competence Through Mastery; (2) Affective Shift: Isolation to Feelings of Belonging; (3) Navigating Experiences of Negative Valence; (4) Anticipatory Excitement to In-Game Arousal; and (5) Escalating Affective Intensity and Emotional Urgency. CONCLUSION: This study challenges traditional paradigms by demonstrating that both positive and negative emotions act as highly fluid drivers during and after a gambling session. These insights may prompt further research into investigating emotional mechanisms that drive and maintain an individual's gambling.",2026,Serafim,International Journal of Qualitative Studies on Health and Well-Being,10.1080/17482631.2026.2696637,42393841,https://doi.org/10.1080/17482631.2026.2696637,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1140,Beyond cognitive distortions: the mediating role of passion between erroneous cognitions and gambling severity,"Objective: While erroneous gambling cognitions and gambling passion (harmonious and obsessive) are recognised as key factors underpinning an individual's gambling, limited research has examined whether passion explains how cognitive distortions relate to gambling severity. Method: = 11.52). . Results: Controlling for gambling preference, frequency, and social desirability, predictive control exhibited a significant negative indirect path to gambling severity via harmonious passion, whereas obsessive passion positively mediated the relationship between illusion of control, interpretive bias, and gambling severity. Furthermore, obsessive passion significantly (negatively) moderated the relationship between interpretive bias and gambling severity. Conclusion: Findings suggest that when individuals overestimate predictive control, they may cognitively minimise risks associated with their gambling. Additionally, internal pressure from obsessive passion could inflate perceived control over random outcomes and heighten the tendency to attribute outcomes to skill. Moderation results indicated obsessive passion may exert a stronger influence on gambling severity than interpretive bias. Overall, findings highlight the need to target both gambling cognitions and passion when addressing an individual's gambling severity.",2026,Serafim,Australian Journal of Psychology,10.1080/00049530.2026.2726226,42769563,https://doi.org/10.1080/00049530.2026.2726226,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1141,Harmonious and obsessive gambling passion: a comparative analysis of non-problem to problem gamblers,"While researchers have investigated the link between gambling and passion, there is currently a paucity of literature detailing the precise relationship between harmonious and obsessive passion across the full spectrum of this behavior. The current study aimed to investigate the association between these passion constructs and empirically established levels of gambling severity: non-problem, low-risk, moderately at-risk, and problem gamblers. A total of (n = 630) gamblers aged between 18 and 73 (Mage = 38.56, SDage = 11.52) recruited via the general community and through Prolific.co, completed an online survey consisting of the Gambling Passion Scale (GPS), Problem Gambling Severity Index (PGSI) and the Short-Marlowe-Crowne Social Desirability Scale (S-MCSDS). Partial correlations revealed a significant weak negative correlation between harmonious passion and PGSI scores, and a strong positive partial correlation between obsessive passion and PGSI scores. Results from one-way ANOVAs showed that problem gamblers scored significantly higher on both harmonious and obsessive passion compared to the non-problem, low-risk and moderately at-risk gamblers. Findings from the current study provide insights into the role of passion in problem gambling and suggest that both may be salient toward an individual’s gambling behavior. Given these results, clinicians should consider focusing on the construct of passion when assessing and classifying a gambler’s risk profile. Focusing on passion may also elucidate a more holistic understanding of individuals’ perception of their own gambling behavior, which, in turn, could facilitate the development of more refined, personalized intervention strategies.",2026,Serafim,Addiction Research & Theory,10.1080/16066359.2026.2638744,,https://doi.org/10.1080/16066359.2026.2638744,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1142,Going for Broke: The Impact of Gambling Legalization on Larceny,"Since the Supreme Court overturned PASPA in 2018, many states have legalized sports betting while others have not. This has created an opportunity to study whether legalized sports betting is associated with changes in crime. While much of the existing research focuses on gambling addiction and financial hardship, fewer studies have examined whether legalization is related to changes in crimes. This project compares states with and without legalized sports betting, including North Carolina, South Carolina, Alabama, Mississippi, Wisconsin, Minnesota, and Pennsylvania. Annual data from 2011 through 2024 are being collected from sources such as the FBI Crime Data Explorer, the U.S. Census Bureau, the Bureau of Labor Statistics, and the Federal Reserve Economic Data database. In addition to crime, the analysis includes poverty, unemployment, educational attainment, population density, government safety net programs, and drug and alcohol use so that the effect of sports betting can be examined while accounting for other factors that influence crime. The goal of this research is to determine whether sports betting legalization has an independent relationship with crime after controlling for these economic and demographic variables. The findings may provide a better understanding of the broader social impacts of legalized sports betting and contribute to future policy discussions surrounding gambling expansion.",2026,Serxner,Digital Commons - Ursinus (Ursinus College),,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1143,NORMATIVE INFLUENCE ON SPORTS BETTING AMONG YOUNG MEN IN GHANA,"Sports betting has become an increasingly visible feature of everyday sport culture in Ghana, yet theory-driven communication research explaining how betting becomes socially normalized among young men remains limited. Drawing on the theory of normative social behavior and its communication-centered reconceptualization, this dissertation used an exploratory sequential mixed-methods design to examine how interpersonal communication, observation, and media exposure shape perceived norms, and how those norms relate to sports betting and its associated harms. Study One used semi-structured interviews with ten young Ghanaian men who bet on sports. Participants described a filtered communication environment in which wins circulate widely, losses are minimized or withheld, and audiences are carefully selected. Betting appeared ordinary because young men encountered it through friends, betting shops, phones, advertisements, sports media, and public debate. At the same time, participants described avoiding the people most likely to object, including parents, partners, religious figures, and non-betting peers. The qualitative findings showed that betting was widely normalized as participation, but not uniformly approved, and that help-seeking was constrained because admitting a desire to stop also meant admitting that betting was no longer working. Study Two tested the proposed communication-centered normative pathway in a survey of 314 young Ghanaian men who bet on sports. The results showed a consistent distinction between descriptive and injunctive norms. Perceived descriptive norms were predicted by observation of referent others, media exposure, and communication frequency, and were associated with betting frequency. Perceived injunctive norms were predicted only by positive and excitatory betting talk and were associated with PGSI-SB severity. Indirect-association models showed that descriptive norms accounted for pathways from communication frequency, observation, and media exposure to betting frequency, while injunctive norms accounted for the pathway from positive betting talk to PGSI-SB severity. Outcome expectations and pro-betting attitudes strengthened the association between injunctive norms and PGSI-SB severity. Together, the findings show that communication does more than accompany sports betting. It helps make betting visible, frames what is worth sharing, signals what appears common or approved, and shapes the conditions under which young men can disclose harm or seek support. Findings distinguish the normative processes associated with how often young men bet from those associated with how much harm they experience. The findings clarify communication mechanisms relevant to harm reduction, including the potential value of correcting one-directional information environments and of addressing the disclosure barriers that discourage help-seeking.",2026,Sesenu,UKnowledge (University of Kentucky),10.13023/etd.2026.408,,https://doi.org/10.13023/etd.2026.408,,datacite:Dissertation/ | openalex:article,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
