R1208,Understanding sports betting as an emerging behavioral addiction among young men in Ethiopia,"ABSTRACT Background Sports betting is one of the most popular, rapidly evolving, and attractive forms of gambling. It is associated with an increased risk of gambling-related harms like relationship difficulties, crime, and physical and mental health problems. Understanding the subjective experiences of sports betting is essential for accurately defining problematic betting and avoiding the over pathologization of the behavior. Hence, this study explored the perceptions and experiences of young men involved in sports betting in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia. Methods We employed a qualitative study design using a phenomenological approach. In-depth interviews were conducted with thirteen purposively selected participants in Addis Ababa. Participants were adults aged 18 years or older who had engaged in sports betting within the past 12 months. They were recruited at sports betting houses. All interviews were transcribed verbatim and translated into English. The data were then imported into OpenCode software and analysed using reflexive thematic analysis, in which themes were actively generated through an iterative and interpretative process by the research team. Findings The data resulted in five major themes: reasons for engagement in sports betting, unique and attractive features of sports betting, individuals experience of problematic betting, subjective harms due to sports betting. We found that young men engage in sports betting for reasons like financial interests, influence from peers and friends, and as a way of spending time and dealing with boredom. This study also identified several features that make sports betting more appealing, including ease of access, the possibility of gambling with a small amount of money, the benefit of avoiding confrontation, giving football fans the opportunity to gamble on something they like, enabling people to test their knowledge about the sport, the availability of betting options, and more rewards and reinforcements compared to other forms of gambling. Patterns suggestive of problematic betting include preoccupation and extensive planning, a tendency to bet successively and take more risky options after losing on a bet, unsuccessful attempts to quit, lying about involvement, social and occupational impairment, and negative emotional experiences. Conclusion Sports betting emerged in participants accounts as a growing behavioral practice among young men in Addis Ababa, Ethiopia, raising concerns for those who described their experiences While often initiated as a leisure activity, participants’ narratives suggested patterns that extended beyond casual engagement and shared features commonly associated with problematic gambling. Future studies could include women and use large-scale quantitative designs to explore the scope and distribution of sports betting-related harms at the population level and to help inform appropriate interventions.",2026,Teshome,medRxiv,10.64898/2026.10.01.26363633,,https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.10.01.26363633,,crossref:posted-content | europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR | openalex:preprint | s2:,europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1209,OPTIMIZING STATE REVENUE THROUGH REGULATORY REFORM IN VIETNAM’S GAMING AND LOTTERY SECTOR,"This article examines the determinants of state budget revenue from the prize-winning gaming sector in Vietnam, which encompasses traditional and computerized lotteries, casinos, sports betting, and electronic games for foreigners. As emerging markets actively seek to diversify their fiscal streams, the gaming sector presents a significant yet complex opportunity. Historically dominated by traditional lotteries, the Vietnamese gaming market has undergone a structural transformation toward controlled liberalization, designed to capture capital that previously flowed to informal markets. Utilizing a provincial panel data approach, this study analyzes how regulatory shifts and economic factors influence tax receipts across 63 provinces over the period from 2015 to 2025. The empirical analysis employs both Fixed Effects (FE) and Random Effects (RE) models to test the impact of specific drivers, including gross regional domestic product (GRDP), regulatory changes, and geographic market competition. The results provide strong evidence that income elasticity is a primary driver of sector growth, with a 1% increase in GRDP per capita associated with a 0.654% increase in gaming revenue within the preferred Fixed Effects model. Furthermore, regulatory liberalization, such as the introduction of modern lottery formats and casino pilots, yields a quantifiable fiscal benefit by increasing localized revenue. Conversely, increased geographic competition negatively impacts provincial gaming revenue, causing revenue attrition. Ultimately, optimizing state revenue requires a delicate balance between market liberalization and social cost management. The study concludes by proposing strategic policy recommendations, including product diversification, harmonized tax structures, geographic zoning optimization, and digital framework integration, to enhance long-term fiscal yields.",2026,Thang,Acta Innovations,10.62441/actainnovations.vi.818,,https://doi.org/10.62441/actainnovations.vi.818,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R1210,,,
