R1211,"Australia’s proposed gambling reforms may be good for industry, but not for public health","Gambling industry harms are a major global public health concern (Van Schalkwyk et al. 2021, Thomas et al. 2023a, Cowlishaw et al. 2025). They contribute to financial hardship, family violence, relationship breakdown, and, in severe cases, suicide (Marionneau et al. 2023). The rapid expansion of online betting and the embedding of gambling within sport and digital environments have intensified exposure and normalized participation (Forrest 2025, Mcgrane et al. 2025, Yeola et al. 2025). Central to this normalization are pervasive marketing systems that saturate everyday media and cultural environments (Pitt et al. 2024, McCarthy et al. 2026). These strategies consistently associate gambling with excitement, mateship, and sporting identity, reframing a high-risk product as an ordinary leisure activity (Coffey et al. 2026). Digital platforms and data-driven marketing extend this reach globally, while ‘dark nudges’ and inducement marketing amplify risks and harms (Mcgarrigle et al. 2026). Evidence shows that early and repeated exposure positively shapes gambling attitudes and promotes gambling as a normalized behaviour for young people (Nyemcsok et al. 2021, Thomas et al. 2023b, Royce et al. 2025). Despite this evidence, regulatory responses continue to prioritize industry interests over public health (Thomas et al. 2023a). Few jurisdictions have implemented comprehensive reforms explicitly aimed at protecting young people from commercial gambling practices (Constandt and De Jans 2024, Aonso-Diego et al. 2025). Most policy responses remain fragmented, incremental, and are arguably shaped through ongoing negotiation with industry actors (Van Schalkwyk and Cassidy 2024, Van Schalkwyk and Cassidy 2025). Even where major public inquiries have called for evidence-based reform, governments have failed to act (Thomas et al. 2023b). The Australian Government’s recent response to a comprehensive parliamentary inquiry into online gambling exemplifies this pattern, presented as significant progress but falling short of the structural reform required to address the drivers of harm. In 2022, growing concern about the normalization of gambling, especially through sport, prompted a cross party parliamentary inquiry into online gambling in Australia, chaired by the late Peta Murphy MP (Standing Committee on Social Policy and Legal Affairs 2022). Referred by the Minister for Social Services, the inquiry in the House of Representatives examined the full scope of online gambling—from products and advertising to regulation and support services. Its terms of reference were broadened early to adopt more inclusive, non-stigmatising language, shifting from ‘problem gamblers’ to ‘lived experience of gambling harm’. The inquiry received 161 submissions and 26 exhibits, and held 13 public hearings with a range of stakeholders, including the gambling industry, broadcasters, sporting codes, health organizations, academics and those with lived experience of gambling harm (together with some family members) (Standing Committee on Social Policy and Legal Affairs 2022). The resulting report ‘You win some, you lose more: Online gambling and its impacts on those experiencing gambling harm’ (which became widely known as the ‘Murphy Report’) set out a comprehensive suite of public health recommendations to prevent gambling harm (Standing Committee on Social Policy and Legal Affairs 2023). The 31 recommendations of the report included a phased in complete tobacco style ban on gambling advertising; a national regulator focused on harm reduction; stronger consumer protection measures such as banning inducements; and formal recognition of gambling as a public health issue. The Murphy Report quickly became a focal point for advocacy, with researchers, lived experience experts, politicians and the media drawing on its findings to call for meaningful online gambling reform (Cannane 2025, Daniel 2025, Australian Medical Association 2026, Pocock 2026). It also triggered pushback from groups with vested interests, who mobilized against key recommendations particularly in relation to the report’s recommendation for a full, phased in advertising ban. Industry stakeholders argued that tighter regulation would drive consumers to offshore and illegal gambling markets (Responsible Wagering Australia 2025), undermine the viability of free-to-air television (Free TV Australia 2023), and affect funding for grassroots and professional sport (Baum 2023). The Australian Government's response to the inquiry was due within six months (Chaney 2026). However, sustained delays and the absence of a substantive response drew criticism over industry influence, reliance on unevidenced statistics, and repeated appeals of ‘complexity’ to justify inaction (Cannane 2024b, Barlow 2026). On 2 April 2026, more than 1000 days after the release of the Murphy Report, the Government finally responded, announcing its planned reforms on the Thursday before the Easter long weekend—when media and political scrutiny are typically reduced, and amid intense media focus on escalating tensions with Iran. While claiming ‘strong action to protect Australians, particularly children and young people, from the harms of gambling’, the response fell short of the Murphy Report’s 31 recommendations, instead advancing a range of partial and individual responsibility measures (Prime Minister of Australia 2026). These included reducing television advertising caps (from 8 to 3 ads per hour between 6 am and 8.30 pm), restricted radio advertising during school drop-off and pick-up times, removal of gambling promotions from sports uniforms and stadiums, bans on celebrities and athletes appearing in advertising, and limits on digital gambling advertising unless users were logged in, over 18, and able to opt out of marketing. Importantly, these measures still need to progress through the parliamentary legislative process, where they will continue to face parliamentary scrutiny and stakeholder pressure. The Government has sought to present its position as signalling action and a step forward. But the measures involved also carry the hallmarks of political compromise, and avoidance of direct confrontation with powerful commercial actors who derive substantial revenue from gambling advertising and sponsorship. Arguing that ‘the solutions aren’t always just to ban things’ and that the Government had ‘got it right,’ the Prime Minister presented the reforms as a measured response to competing public expectations—one that preserved the ability to ‘have a punt’ while offering opt-out protections for those who wished to avoid exposure to marketing (Barlow 2026). In subsequent media interviews, senior government ministers stated that ‘pressure was brought to bear from all sides’ (Barlow 2026), highlighting the contested political environment in which the decisions were made and the influence of the views of those with political access. After reviewing the evidence, the Murphy Report concluded that ‘partial bans on gambling advertising do not work.’ (Standing Committee on Social Policy and Legal Affairs 2023, iv). By taking a partial approach, the Australian Government signalled its willingness to preserve large segments of the gambling marketing ecosystem, sustaining the industry’s capacity to promote its products in highly visible and sophisticated ways. The advertising ‘cap’ continues to normalize and legitimise gambling, and there is no clear evidence that the 8.30 pm cut off accurately reflects young peoples’ media viewing patterns, with studies showing that they continue to watch television after this time (Thomas et al. 2018, Cannane 2024a). Crucially, rather than reducing exposure to advertising, partial bans simply continue and redistribute it. In a context where gambling is already embedded across sport, media, and digital environments, limiting select channels will not meaningfully reduce overall exposure. By targeting only certain forms of advertising (and even those in a limited manner), the proposed reforms risk accelerating a shift to less visible, less regulated tactics. Tobacco control provides a clear precedent. Partial bans drove the expansion of special offers, creative packaging, and other forms of indirect promotion that proved just as influential as traditional advertising (Henriksen 2012, Ling et al. 2022). These risks are amplified in contemporary marketing environments. While the Australian reforms restrict some advertising and introduce opt-out mechanisms, some of the industry’s most powerful marketing tools are left untouched, such as influencer marketing which is highly appealing to young people (Pitt et al. 2024); algorithmic targeting (Parker et al. 2024); and inducements (linked to increased uptake and riskier betting) (Ó Ceallaigh et al. 2025). We can predict that marketing spend will also shift into less regulated spaces—such as podcasts which are widely consumed by younger audiences, and are increasingly attractive to advertisers (Taylor 2024; Brooks et al. 2022). Host-read promotions in podcasts also blur the line between content and advertising, carrying heightened persuasive influence, and are difficult to opt out from (Moe 2023, Wang and Chan-Olmsted 2024). On the basis of experience from this and other areas, we can safely predict that there will be further innovative approaches to marketing. More broadly, while framed as protective, measures such as online opt-outs and logged-in advertising restrictions shift responsibility onto individuals to manage exposure. These approaches are constrained by the complexity and limited transparency of digital advertising systems, which may reduce their capacity to meaningfully limit exposure (Goodwin 2022). Partial bans must be understood within the political economy that shapes regulatory decision making. Despite strong evidence and broad support for full implementation of the Murphy Report, the Government’s response reflects political compromise, raising serious concerns about the role of vested interests in shaping outcomes on major public health issues. As the literature on the commercial determinants of health demonstrates, harmful industries seek to actively shape policies to protect and extend their market power (Hawkins et al. 2012, Herzog-Hawelka and Gupta 2023, Miller et al. 2023, Watts et al. 2023). In this context, partial bans are not simply weak policies, they can indicate regulatory capture, creating the appearance of meaningful intervention while leaving the underlying drivers of harm intact. Indeed, by complying with limited restrictions, industries can also position themselves as part of the solution to the problems that they have caused. Such measures risk stabilizing and legitimizing harmful systems, compounded by the Government’s failure to follow the Murphy Report’s recommendation to establish a National Regulator with ‘the sole purpose of reducing harm and with responsibility for all licencing and regulation’ (Standing Committee on Social Policy and Legal Affairs 2023, xix). Effective reform requires comprehensive restrictions and strong, independent oversight. Without both, regulation remains fragmented, weak, and vulnerable to industry influence. The Murphy Report has been widely recognised as a comprehensive, evidence-based blueprint for preventing online gambling harm. The recommendations from the report have been backed by sustained advocacy from politicians, public health experts, and community groups, alongside strong public support for decisive action to protect children and young people. Meaningful gambling reform is both justified and expected. The Government’s delayed and partial response to the report should be the stimulus for further pressure and accountability for gambling reform. Limited advertising restrictions combined with the refusal to establish a National Regulator leave critical gaps that industry can readily exploit. This approach risks a familiar public health failure—when regulation lags behind industry innovation, harm persists. Without comprehensive reform, advertising will continue, and gambling will continue to be normalized for young people. Anything less than full implementation of the recommendations of the Murphy Report allows the problem to evolve faster than the policy designed to address it. None declared. S.T. has received funding for gambling research from the Australian Research Council, Victorian Responsible Gambling Foundation, VicHealth, Healthway, NSW Office of Responsible Gambling, Department of Social Services, ACT Gambling and Racing Commission, Healthway, and Deakin University. She holds the position of Editor-in-Chief for Health Promotion International. H.P. has received funding for gambling research from the Australian Research Council, Victorian Responsible Gambling Foundation, VicHealth, NSW Office of Responsible Gambling, Department of Social Services, ACT Gambling and Racing Commission, and Deakin University. She is a member of the Editorial Board for Health Promotion International. S.M. has received funding for gambling research from the Victorian Responsible Gambling Foundation, VicHealth, Department of Social Services, ACT Gambling and Racing Commission, and Deakin University. She is the social media coordinator for Health Promotion International. M.D. has received funding for gambling research from the Australian Research Council, Victorian Responsible Gambling Foundation, Healthway, ACT Gambling and Racing Commission. He is the Chair of the Editorial Boards for Health Promotion International.",2026,Thomas,Health Promotion International,10.1093/heapro/daag057,42028844,https://doi.org/10.1093/heapro/daag057,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1212,A systematic review of the prevalence of ADHD and autism among those experiencing gambling harm,"BACKGROUND: There is a growing body of research reporting complex correlations between neurodivergence and gambling - raising concern about targeted predatory gambling practices of operators, underlining the urgency for specialised harm prevention and reduction measures. This review provides new estimates of the prevalence of ADHD and autism in individuals experiencing gambling harm. METHODS: We performed a systematic review using frameworks by Munn et al. (2015), reviewing peer-reviewed observational studies across 4 databases - MEDLINE, PsycINFO, CINAHL and PubMed, searched on the 21st of May 2025. Results from each database were uploaded to Covidence as a RIS file to be screened and extracted. A meta-analysis was conducted included studies reporting ADHD data using a random effects model to estimate pooled prevalence, while included studies reporting data on autism had a narrative summary of their results. RESULTS: Our analysis included 14 studies, 2 of which reported autism data. The prevalence for ADHD in gambling harm was 0.24 (95 % CI 0.18; 0.29) or 24 %, compared with a prevalence of 2.58 % in the general population (Song et al., 2021). The data had a high rate of heterogeneity, suggesting the results of our meta-analysis may be inflated due to some studies reporting higher rates than others. CONCLUSION: Elevated prevalence of ADHD in those experiencing gambling harms is suggested by the results of this review. While autism is plausibly equally as important, more research is needed in this area to better understand the likely prevalence of this condition for those experiencing gambling harm.",2026,Timms-McLean,Addictive Behaviors,10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108776,42314257,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.addbeh.2026.108776,en,citations:review,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1214,"Correction to: Patterns of Gambling Behavior Influenced By Materialism, Financial Well-Being, and Monetary Motivation Among Sports Bettors: A Latent Profile Analysis.",,2026,Topbaş,Journal of gambling studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10488-1,41874905,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10488-1,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:Published Erratum|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:erratum | pubmed:Published Erratum | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,6,False,False,False,,,,,
R1215,(Mis)Comprehension and (Mistaken) Attractiveness of Financial Gambling Inducements among UK Bettors.,"Financial gambling inducements such as ‘free’ bets and welcome bonuses involve complex wagering requirements that many consumers miscomprehend. UK regulations cap these wagering requirements at x10 (i.e., users must wager x10 the bonus before withdrawal) but don’t require worked examples to aid comprehension. This study assessed UK bettors’ comprehension of inducement wagering requirements, whether comprehension varied by gambling severity, whether perceived attractiveness differed when worked examples were displayed, and bettors’ broader perceptions of inducements. A between-subjects, mixed-methods online experiment randomised UK bettors (n = 585) to view a welcome bonus inducement either with or without (control) a worked example detailing its wagering requirement. Participants completed measures of comprehension, perceived attractiveness, gambling severity, and qualitative questioning. Participants in the control condition significantly underestimated the inducement’s wagering requirements (p<.001, r=.94), with a median estimate of £500 versus the correct value of £750. Underestimation did not differ by gambling severity. The inducement was rated significantly less attractive (p<.001, η²=.18) when the worked example was displayed (M = 2.39, SD = 1.46) compared to the control (M = 3.75, SD = 1.48). However, this effect was moderated by gambling severity (B=-0.112, p=.005), with the reduction in perceived attractiveness associated with the worked example becoming smaller as gambling severity scores increased. Qualitative analysis identified three overarching perceptions of inducements: as manipulative, economically worthless, and requiring better regulation. UK bettors significantly underestimate inducements’ wagering requirements, while worked examples significantly reduce their mistaken attractiveness. These findings demonstrate how informed choice can be enhanced via worked examples.",2026,Torrance,Journal of gambling studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10491-6,41879944,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10491-6,eng,pubmed:Journal Article,pubmed,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1216,"‘It’s so difficult that you have to be fully in control of your emotions, you have to be fully understanding of what you’re betting on’: how professional sports bettors succeed by using rational thinking to cope with uncertainty","Background Sports betting is most often a losing endeavor, yet a small population of bettors can exercise enough skill to win or even bet professionally. While research has explored successful professional poker players, sports betting presents distinct challenges, including inherent outcome uncertainty and adversarial bookmaker relationships. Understanding the psychology of successful professional sports betting would provide a counterpoint to better understand the more common occurrence of harmful sports betting against.Methods In-depth qualitative interviews were conducted with five elite professional sports bettors. Interviews explored their progression from amateur to professional betting, their cognitive strategies for betting, their views toward the gambling industry, and their perceptions of other gambling products. Qualitative data were audio recorded, transcribed, and analyzed via Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis.Results Two main themes were identified. First, ‘Rational Thinking’ captured participants’ statistical approach to betting grounded in expected value calculations and their prioritization of process over outcome, supported by rigorous emotional regulation. Participants also maintained strict adherence to a defined circle of competence, by restricting themselves to bets for which they held informational advantages. Second, ‘Coping with Uncertainty’ reflected the ongoing challenges of adapting to evolving betting markets and navigating an adversarial environment where bookmakers restricted successful bettors. This involved using an actively open-minded thinking style, and coping with uncertainty regarding professional identity, social isolation, and career sustainability.Conclusions The difficulty of thinking rationally and coping with uncertainty in successful sports betting should temper the aspirations of recreational bettors who believe they can replicate such success.",2026,Torrance,Addiction Research & Theory,10.1080/16066359.2026.2693272,,https://doi.org/10.1080/16066359.2026.2693272,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1217,PERSONALITY TRAITS AND THEIR INFLUENCE ON FANTASY SPORTS GAMBLING,"Online fantasy sports gambling is a rapidly growing field of entertainment, in which players bet on athletes across various sports to win cash prizes. Online fantasy sports gambling’s rapid development raises concerns regarding the underlying factors that may drive problematic sports betting behavior (e.g. risky betting behavior). This study focuses on the association between the two personality domains of Neuroticism and Extraversion on fantasy sports gambling severity. A quantitative study was conducted utilizing college student fantasy sports betters. Participants (N = 98) reported engaging in fantasy sports in the past year and were assessed for as personality traits, income, engagement rate, number of bets placed on average, size of bets placed, motivational orientation, and impulsivity rate. Pearson’s correlations were used to assess associations between neuroticism, extraversion, and fantasy sport gambling severity. Multiple regressions utilizing variables were used to derive the best set of predictors for gambling severity. Findings indicated no statistically significant relationship between neuroticism and gambling severity (r [96] = -.10, p = .34). No significant linear associations were found between extraversion and gambling severity (r [96] = .10, p = .34). Neuroticism and extraversion were nonsignificant predictors of gambling severity, F(2,95) = 1.05, p = .35, R² = .02. The best predictors of fantasy sport gambling severity were external regulation motivational orientation, bet size, personal income, impulsivity, and engagement, F(5,92) = 13.43, p <.001, R² = .42. Findings suggest that behavioral factors may be more accurate predictors of problematic gambling rather than personality traits.",2026,Torre,Zenodo (CERN European Organization for Nuclear Research),10.5281/zenodo.21084664,,https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21084664,en,datacite:Dissertation/ | openaire:Doctoral thesis; Thesis | openalex:dissertation,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1218,Regulación de las apuestas deportivas en la literatura académica: Revisión de los debates más relevantes entre 2014 a 2026,,2026,Torres,Repositorio Institucional (Pontificia Universidad Javeriana),10.71618/7kt6-jz58,,https://doi.org/10.71618/7kt6-jz58,,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1219,"Understanding Gambling Behavior Among Hispanic College Students: The Role of Attitudes, Norms, Self-Efficacy, and Acculturation","Gambling has become increasingly accessible and socially normalized among young adults, particularly through mobile platforms and the expansion of legalized sports betting. Hispanic college students may be at heightened risk for gambling-related harms, given evidence of elevated rates of problem gambling, comorbid mental health concerns, and underrepresentation in empirical research. Cultural factors, such as acculturation, may further influence engagement in gambling behaviors and susceptibility to negative outcomes. Guided by the Theory of Planned Behavior, the present study investigated the relationships between gambling injunctive norms, gambling attitudes, and gambling self-efficacy across three outcomes: gambling frequency, problem gambling severity, and gambling intention. Additionally, this study examined whether acculturation and enculturation moderated these relationships. Participants included 318 Hispanic college students recruited from a U.S.-Mexico border university. Participants completed self-report measures assessing sociodemographic characteristics, gambling attitudes and norms, gambling intentions, gambling self-efficacy, gambling behaviors, problem gambling severity, and acculturation/enculturation. Two linear regression analyses were conducted to test main and interaction effects, controlling for age and sex, and negative binomial regression analysis was utilized to assess gambling frequency after it was observed to have a non-normal distribution to be zero inflated. Results indicated that gambling attitudes were positively associated with gambling frequency, problem gambling severity, and gambling intention. Gambling self-efficacy was negatively associated with gambling intention, problem gambling severity, gambling intention, suggesting a protective effect. Neither acculturation nor enculturation significantly moderated any of the examined relationships. These findings highlight the importance of cognitive and perceived control factors in understanding gambling behaviors among Hispanic college students. By integrating culturally relevant constructs within an established theoretical framework, this study contributes to the growing literature on gambling in underrepresented populations. Implications include the development of culturally responsive prevention and intervention strategies that target maladaptive attitudes and enhance self-efficacy to reduce gambling-related harm.",2026,Torres,DigitalCommons@UTEP (The University of Texas at El Paso),,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1220,Emotional Shocks and Intimate Partner Violence: Behavioural Responses Beyond Physical Abuse,,2026,Tovar,SSRN Electronic Journal,10.2139/ssrn.6833676,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6833676,,openalex:preprint,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1221,Edukasi Menghadapi Era Digital Keuangan & Resiko Teknologi Finansial Melalui “Sekolah Media Literasi” (SMILE),"The rapid growth of digital technology has transformed traditional gambling activities—such as poker, casinos, mahjong, sports betting, cockfighting, dice games, lottery, and numbers gambling—into increasingly popular online practices. Although often perceived as entertainment, online gambling has significant negative impacts on social life. Addressing this issue requires collaboration among various stakeholders, including educational institutions that play a vital role in promoting media and financial literacy. The Faculty of Communication Sciences at Gunadarma University has taken part through the SMILE (School of Media Literacy) program, which focuses on financial literacy education. This program is implemented through counseling, mentoring, and training using presentations, interactive media, and focus group discussions. The results indicate an improvement in community understanding of healthy financial management. Nevertheless, such educational efforts should not be one-time initiatives but rather continuous activities in order to build a society that is financially literate, independent, and resilient against the negative impacts of online gambling.",2026,Tunggali,Madani Indonesian Journal of Civil Society,10.35970/madani.v8i1.2944,,https://doi.org/10.35970/madani.v8i1.2944,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1222,Understanding Sports Betting Among Young People: Psychosocial and Structural Influences.,"Abstract This study explored participants’ experiences and perceptions of the psychosocial influences associated with sports betting and gambling practices among regular bettors in Masvingo, Zimbabwe. A qualitative ethnographic design was employed to examine the everyday experiences, meanings, and motivations attached to sports betting. Forty participants (N = 40), comprising 37 regular bettors and three key informants, were purposively recruited from four betting houses in Masvingo urban. Data were generated through in-depth interviews and participant observation conducted over a twelve-month period from January to December 2024. Participants reflected on their gambling histories, motivations for betting, and challenges associated with their involvement. Findings suggested that participants perceived peer relationships, social learning, economic insecurity, familial exposure, and emotional distress as important influences associated with their engagement in gambling. Economic hardship and youth unemployment further influenced engagement, as many viewed sports betting as a potential income-generating strategy and a means of coping with financial insecurity. Emotional distress, including stress, grief, frustration, and boredom, also motivated gambling, with betting serving as a temporary psychological escape from daily challenges. Family gambling practices were found to normalize risk-taking behaviors among some participants. Although betting houses provided social interaction and a sense of belonging, they simultaneously reinforced maladaptive coping strategies and heightened vulnerability to problem gambling. The study underscores the need for integrated interventions addressing mental health support, youth economic empowerment, family involvement, and community awareness of gambling-related harms in urban Zimbabwe.",2026,Tungwa,Journal of prevention (2022),10.1007/s10935-026-00952-4,42760485,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10935-026-00952-4,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref,5,False,False,False,,,,,
R1223,Lessons from Fan Studies for Sports Gambling Ventures,"Since the 1980s, volumes of scholarship on fandoms have detailed the ways that consumers have creatively interacted with cultural products. Media companies embraced fans as the default consumers for their products during Web 2.0. Sports media companies, Fox Sports and ESPN, attempted to migrate sports fans to sports gambling platforms and failed. Simultaneously, sports fans engaged in sports betting have often taken out their frustrations on athletes that have cost them money via their wagers. Our paper presentation reviews the work on sports fandom and sports gambling to identify the reasons for the disconnect between traditional ways to engage fans and the realities of sports gambling. Key to these insights, is that sports fandom is different from other kinds of media fandom as it is organized via “oppositional fandom” (Kido Lopez and Kido Lopez, 2017) and increasingly in “antagonist fandom” (Oates, 2023) practices. Similarly, sports gambling is a unique audience practice that while sharing some similarities with video games (Buehler, 2024) and narrative speculation (Mittell, 2015), has financially painful consequences that overshadow the social or emotional benefits of other kinds of fan investments. By cataloging best practice in sports fandom and analyzing failed efforts to translate fan practices into wagers, we delineate the limits of using a fandom to foster sports gambling.",2026,Tussey,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1224,"Pre-Service Teachers’ Readiness for Green Pedagogy: The Impact of Institutional Support, Environmental Awareness, and Sustainability Mindset","Abstract Green Pedagogical Skills are essential for preparing prospective teachers to act as agents of Education for Sustainable Development (ESD). However, previous studies tend to be fragmented, focusing on individual (psychological–pedagogical) or structural factors. This research analyzes the factors influencing GPS among 369 pre-service teachers (PSTs) who have completed teaching practice, using Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modeling (PLS-SEM). The result shows the strong predictive model (R² GPS = 0.795) with environmental awareness (EA) as the dominant predictor. Teaching readiness (TR) not only had a direct effect on GPS but also increased green self-efficacy (SE). Then, structural support (SS) had a dual effect: it strengthened GPS directly but weakened the EA-GPS relationship through negative moderation. The research concludes that GPS development requires integrating a holistic approach, strengthening individual capacity (knowledge– mindset–readiness), and designing context-based structural policies. The findings indicate that it is not enough to develop PSTs’ GPS by improving their environmental awareness; it also requires structural supports, their teaching readiness, and green self-efficacy in the curriculum.",2026,Tusyanah,Journal of Teacher Education for Sustainability,10.2478/jtes-2026-0005,,https://doi.org/10.2478/jtes-2026-0005,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1225,Effects of sports betting applications on youth developmental plans in Africa,"The rapid expansion of mobile technology and internet accessibility across Africa has significantly transformed youth engagement with digital platforms. Among the most influential developments is the proliferation of sports betting applications, including Betway, SportPesa, Bet365, 1xBet, BetKing, Premier Bet, Melbet, Odibets, Hollywoodbets, and Betika. While these platforms are often marketed as sources of entertainment and economic opportunity, their widespread adoption among African youth has raised serious concerns regarding long-term developmental planning. This study examines the effects of sports betting applications on youths’ educational aspirations, career planning, financial behavior, and psychosocial development. Drawing on existing literature, socio-economic observations, and behavioral trends, the research reveals that excessive engagement with betting apps undermines goal-oriented planning, promotes risky financial behaviors, and distorts perceptions of work, success, and delayed gratification. The findings suggest that without effective regulation, digital literacy, and youth-centered interventions, sports betting applications may significantly compromise sustainable youth development in Africa.",2026,Tutu,World Journal of Advanced Research and Reviews,10.30574/wjarr.2026.30.3.1720,,https://doi.org/10.30574/wjarr.2026.30.3.1720,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1226,Addictive behaviors among people with intellectual disability: An umbrella review,"INTRODUCTION: Addictive behaviors pose significant challenges for people with intellectual disability (ID), yet research remains limited. This umbrella review aims to synthesize evidence from systematic reviews on substance use (SU), gambling, and gaming among people with ID, focusing on prevalence and patterns, risk factors and motivations, and intervention approaches. Understanding these aspects is essential for developing effective prevention and treatment. METHOD: Following the Preferred Reporting Items for Overviews of Reviews (PRIOR) guidelines, two independent reviewers searched the Web of Science, PsycINFO, and the Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews for systematic reviews and meta-analyses focused on people with ID and SU, gambling, or gaming. Quality was assessed using the JBI Checklist for Systematic Reviews. RESULTS: No eligible studies were found for gambling and gaming. Nine reviews addressed SU (alcohol, tobacco, cannabis, solvents, stimulant, and depressant drugs), revealing higher prevalence among men, adults, and those with less complex support needs. Motivational, educational, and behavioral interventions were employed. Cognitive behavioral therapy, motivational enhancement therapy, and mindfulness showed potential efficacy in increasing knowledge, motivation, and reducing SU. CONCLUSIONS: The findings underscore the importance of inclusive prevention strategies that address social determinants of health (such as socioeconomic status and living conditions), transdiagnostic protective factors, and evidence-based, person-centered, and rights-based frameworks. While SU interventions for people with ID show promise, further research and more robust designs that incorporate these conceptual perspectives is needed. Moreover, it is essential to investigate polypharmacy and behavioral addictions to address these issues from a phenomenological and contextual perspective.",2026,Udeanu,Clinical Psychology Review,10.1016/j.cpr.2026.102701,41581374,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpr.2026.102701,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1227,RELIGIOUS PERSPECTIVES ON SPORTS BETTING AND ITS IMPLICATIONS FOR YOUTH SECURITY AND NATIONAL DEVELOPMENT IN NIGERIA,"Sports betting has become hugely popular among Nigerian youth, driven by online platforms and aggressive marketing strategies of gambling companies. While gambling can be a form of entertainment, excessive participation exposes youth to moral, social, and economic risks, including addiction, financial instability, and engagement in criminal activities. This study examines the religious perspectives on sports betting in Nigeria, assess its effects on youth security, and evaluate its implications for national development. Using a qualitative approach, insights from Christian, Islamic, and African Traditional religious frameworks were studied to understand the ethical, moral, and social guidance offered to youths. The findings show that religious institutions play a vital role in promoting moral education, offering counseling, and shaping behavioral norms that discourage gambling. The study concludes that a combination of religious moral guidance and targeted government policies is essential to reduce the negative impact of sports betting on Nigerian youth.",2026,UKPE,"SAPIENTIA GLOBAL JOURNAL OF ARTS, HUMANITIES AND DEVELOPMENT STUDIES",,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1228,"Sanctified Bets? Religiosity, Gender, and Sports Gambling in the United States","Over the past decade, the gambling landscape in the United States has shifted dramatically, with sports betting becoming more accessible and widespread than ever. Sports gambling is linked to sanctified constructs and rituals involving masculinity, leisure, sport, nation, and religiosity, complicating traditional moral deterrents related to it. To investigate connections between religiosity and sports gambling, we drew on the National Sports and Society Survey data ( N = 3,701) on U.S. adults. Findings challenge assumptions that religiosity uniformly suppresses gambling behavior. Those who attended religious services infrequently (e.g., once or twice a year) were more likely to gamble and wager more money than both weekly attenders and nonattenders. Catholics were especially likely to engage in sports gambling. Yet, gender moderated these relationships, with religious women significantly less likely to gamble on sports or wager large sums. Still, religiosity did not appear to determine sports gambling outcomes (i.e., wins/losses), among those who gambled. Overall, our findings reveal a complex, contingent relationship between religion and sports gambling.",2026,Upenieks,Sociology of Sport Journal,10.1123/ssj.2025-0136,,https://doi.org/10.1123/ssj.2025-0136,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1229,Quantifying the Illicit Ecosystem of Betting Apps in India,"Online betting and gambling apps in India have expanded rapidly, alongside growing concern about financial loss, debt stress, and addictive use patterns. Yet the ecosystem is difficult to quantify because recruitment and harm are observed in different places: users are often acquired through social media promotion, while harms become visible later inside apps and in user complaints. We address this measurement gap with a mixed-method, multi-source study that links promotion to downstream experience. We compile three complementary datasets. First, we collect and analyze tens of thousands of betting-related advertisements from Meta’s Ad Library using an extensive keyword strategy to measure scale and characterize persuasive frames. Second, we gather a purposive sample of organic Instagram posts from ten betting-linked hashtags to study how similar narratives circulate outside formal advertising, including through surrogate sports pages and influencer-style content. Third, we analyze over 300,000 Google Play reviews for a set of betting apps, using topic modeling to extract recurring user-reported problems that reflect a harm surface including financial loss, withdrawal friction, and customer support failure. We connect these layers by constructing a shared narrative codebook for paid and organic promotion and mapping those recruitment narratives to review topics. Across sources, we find a consistent mismatch between what is promised at recruitment and what users report after adoption. Paid ads frequently frame betting as simple, quick, and highly winnable, while reviews repeatedly describe difficulty winning, blocked or delayed withdrawals, unclear rules, and perceived extractive design. Organic promotion often uses more coded and informal presentation than official ads, potentially reducing detectability while funneling users toward the same apps and referral pathways. Together, these results provide one of the first large-scale, cross-source measurements of India’s online betting promotion ecosystem and its associated user-reported harms, and they offer a general approach for studying how potentially harmful digital services sustain growth through mainstream platforms even under evolving regulation.",2026,Vaidya,Proceedings of the International AAAI Conference on Web and Social Media,10.1609/icwsm.v20i1.42752,,https://doi.org/10.1609/icwsm.v20i1.42752,en,openaire:Article | openalex:conference-paper | s2:JournalArticle;Conference;Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1230,Does the Habit Theory of Addictions Extend to Disordered Gambling?,"Purpose of review: ‘Habit theory’ is a pervasive framework that describes addiction as a transition from goal-directed use (e.g. of drugs) to a habitual response, accompanied by a neurobiological shift in fronto-striatal brain circuitry. As a theory that has been explored in the context of substance addictions, this article summarizes recent work extending habit theory to gambling behavior and gambling disorder.Recent findings: Relevant research falls into two main themes. First, studies have compared behavioral markers of habit (e.g. two-step task, Pavlovian-to-Instrumental Transfer) in groups with and without gambling problems. These studies find limited direct support for the hypothesis. Second, psychological research has examined habit-like behaviors in naturalistic gambling. These studies find behavioral expressions consistent with habit formation, primarily during engagement with slot machines, but these studies are yet to test key tenets of habit theory such as outcome devaluation. Summary: Modern gambling products including slot machines and in-play sports betting involve a fast speed of betting and intense audiovisual feedback that creates a rich learning environment, which may be highly amenable to habit formation. Further research is needed to develop and validate new tools for testing habit strength and habit acquisition in the context of gambling.",2026,van Timmeren,Current addiction reports,10.1007/s40429-026-00715-5,41767824,https://doi.org/10.1007/s40429-026-00715-5,eng,europepmc:review-article;Review;Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article; Conference object; Review | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article;Review | s2:Review;JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;openaire,5,False,False,False,,,,,
R1232,Gambling Advertising and Problem Gambling in the United States: Multi‐Level Evidence Beyond Sports‐Betting Legal Status,"ABSTRACT US gambling debates often center on the legal status of sports‐betting, but advertising can also be used as a distinct policy lever. This paper presents the findings of two related studies. Study 1 links quarterly state‐level gambling advertising outlays across paid media (2014–2023) to Google Trends indices for 116 problem‐gambling help‐seeking queries across 41 states (1640 state‐quarters). Study 2 combines surveys with passive digital traces from 419 exposure‐eligible mobile‐platform users. Higher state‐level ad spend, but not sports‐betting legal status, was associated with greater help‐seeking search interest, and this association strengthened over time. Platform‐specific gambling‐ad exposure on Facebook, TikTok, and X was associated with gambler status, gambling frequency, and the probability of any observed gambling‐site visit. Clean gambling ads were also associated with higher 24‐h odds of subsequent gambling touchpoints, especially web visits; among gamblers, ad‐triggered touchpoints were associated with problem‐gambling scores more consistently than raw clean ad volume. Convergent state‐level and individual‐level evidence links gambling advertising with engagement and harm‐related indicators, while the observational designs do not identify causal effects. The findings support stronger restrictions on advertising volume, placement, inducements, and targeting.",2026,Vargo,Policy & Internet,10.1002/poi3.70060,,https://doi.org/10.1002/poi3.70060,en,citations:article | openalex:article,openalex;citations,2,False,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1233,"Apostas online, crime organizado e lavagem de dinheiro: a CPI das Bets como diagnóstico das vulnerabilidades institucionais do modelo regulatório brasileiro","Resumo: O presente artigo tem como objetivo analisar em que medida o relatório final da Comissão Parlamentar de Inquérito sobre a Manipulação de Jogos e Apostas Esportivas evidencia vulnerabilidades institucionais no modelo brasileiro de regulação e prevenção à lavagem de dinheiro no mercado de apostas online.Parte-se da hipótese de que a expansão desse setor ocorreu em um ambiente caracterizado por lacunas regulatórias e limitações na capacidade estatal de fiscalização.A pesquisa adota método qualitativo, com base em revisão bibliográfica e análise documental do referido relatório parlamentar.Os resultados indicam que o documento revela fragilidades estruturais relacionadas à fiscalização de operadores estrangeiros, ao monitoramento de transações financeiras e à atuação estatal, evidenciando limitações do modelo regulatório brasileiro diante da complexidade dos mercados digitais.Conclui-se que tais vulnerabilidades favorecem a utilização das plataformas de apostas para práticas ilícitas, especialmente lavagem de dinheiro, exigindo o aprimoramento dos mecanismos regulatórios e de cooperação institucional.",2026,Vaszkievicz,Revista Científica do CPJM,10.55689/rcpjm.2026.18.005,,https://doi.org/10.55689/rcpjm.2026.18.005,,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1234,Rentabilidad y eficiencia en los mercados de apuestas deportivas: el caso del Club Atlético Osasuna,"El presente TFG analiza la eficiencia y rentabilidad del mercado de apuestas deportivas en los partidos del Club Atlético Osasuna, con el objetivo de estudiar el comportamiento de las principales casas de apuestas, identificar los factores en la rentabilidad del apostante y determinar la existencia de posibles estrategias de apuestas sistemáticas rentables. Para ello, se analizan las cuotas ofrecidas por Bet365, Pinnacle y William Hill en los partidos de liga disputados por Osasuna entre las temporadas 2019/20 y 2024/25. Una vez procesados y depurados los datos, tras un laborioso proceso de recopilación y tratamiento manual de determinadas variables, los resultados respaldan la literatura previa y muestran que el mercado de apuestas deportivas se caracteriza por un elevado grado de eficiencia. Aun así, existen pequeñas ineficiencias capaces de generar rentabilidades limitadas. Del mismo modo, el margen aplicado por las operadoras constituye un factor relevante en la rentabilidad del apostante, observándose un aumento de este a medida que incrementa la incertidumbre y avanza la temporada.",2026,Velasco,Academica-e (Universidad Pública de Navarra),,,,es,openalex_non_en:dissertation,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1235,AS PROIBIÇÕES DE CASSINOS E A REGULAMENTAÇÃO DAS APOSTAS ESPORTIVAS NO BRASIL: UMA ANÁLISE SOB O PRINCÍPIO DA AUTONOMIA DO ESTADO,"Objetivo: O objetivo central deste estudo é analisar a proibição dos cassinos e a regulamentação das apostas esportivas no Brasil sob a ótica do princípio constitucional da autonomia dos entes federativos. Busca-se investigar se a atual centralização da regulamentação dos jogos de azar na União coaduna-se com o modelo federativo da Constituição Federal de 1988, avaliando a viabilidade jurídica e a possibilidade de uma descentralização normativa que confira maior autonomia aos estados para regulamentar e explorar essas atividades econômicas em seus territórios. Referencial Teórico: A sustentação teórica deste estudo articula-se a partir da repartição constitucional de competências, confrontando a exclusividade legislativa da União sobre sistemas de consórcios e sorteios com os princípios da livre iniciativa e da autonomia estadual. Essa discussão ganha contornos práticos com a análise da jurisprudência do Supremo Tribunal Federal, notadamente o entendimento fixado na ADPF 492, que consagra a competência material dos estados para a exploração de serviços lotéricos. A intervenção estatal sobre as escolhas individuais é problematizada sob a matriz liberal de John Stuart Mill, a qual estabelece a razoabilidade e a proporcionalidade como limites para a ingerência do Estado. Por fim, a pesquisa ancora-se nos critérios clínicos da Classificação Internacional de Doenças (CID-11) da Organização Mundial da Saúde para conceituar a ludopatia, e no direito comparado para examinar os modelos federalistas aplicados aos jogos nos Estados Unidos, no Canadá e na Alemanha. Método: A pesquisa adota uma abordagem qualitativa, desenvolvida por meio da Pesquisa Bibliográfica e Documental, analisando a doutrina jurídica, artigos científicos e dados econômico-sociais (como relatórios do Banco Itaú, dados do Serasa/Opinion Box e estimativas vinculadas ao IBGE). Constou ainda com a análise Normativa e Jurisprudencial: Exame exegético do Decreto-Lei nº 9.215/1946 (proibição de cassinos), do Decreto-Lei nº 3.688/1941 (Lei das Contravenções Penais), da Lei nº 13.756/2018 e da Lei nº 14.790/2023 (marco regulatório das apostas), além de acórdãos do STF (ADPF 492) e do STJ (REsp nº 1.968.167/SP). Por fim, a análise comparativa internacional face aos modelos federativos e regulatórios de jogos nos Estados Unidos, Canadá e Alemanha. Resultados e Discussão: Constatou-se uma contradição prática e jurídica no ordenamento brasileiro. Enquanto os cassinos físicos permanecem proibidos por fundamentos morais e religiosos de 1946, as plataformas de apostas online (""bets"") operam legalmente e oferecem jogos similares (como roletas online e slots machines) sob uma fiscalização predominantemente fazendária e tributária. A discussão jurisprudencial (STF) demonstra que, embora a União detenha a competência legislativa em sentido estrito, os estados possuem competência material legítima para organizar e explorar serviços públicos lotéricos, o que fundamenta a defesa de uma maior descentralização e flexibilização regional. A análise empírica apontou que o mercado regulado de apostas de quota fixa gera forte assimetria financeira. Citando dados do Banco Itaú, de R$ 68,2 bilhões gastos por apostadores, houve uma perda líquida de 35% (R$ 23 bilhões), com prêmios líquidos representando meros 0,29% do total. Ademais, as apostas consumiram 1,9% da massa salarial do trabalhador brasileiro, e a pesquisa Serasa indicou que 44% dos apostadores endividados continuam jogando na tentativa de quitar dívidas. O estudo demonstrou um incremento estatístico nos problemas de saúde mental: estimativas do século XXI apontam que cerca de 0,8% da população brasileira acima de 15 anos (1,4 milhão de pessoas) apresenta sintomas de jogo patológico, em contraste com os 0,4% registrados em estudos do século XX. Implicações da Pesquisa: O estudo sinaliza a necessidade premente de uma revisão constitucional e legislativa (como o andamento do PL nº 2.234/2022) para harmonizar o tratamento jurídico dado aos jogos no Brasil, superando a proibição absoluta dos cassinos em favor de um regime controlado de legalização em resorts integrados. Evidencia a urgência de estruturação de salvaguardas sociais robustas por parte do Estado, de órgãos reguladores (como a Secretaria de Prêmios e Apostas - SPA/MF) e do Sistema Nacional de Apostas (Sinapo). Tais medidas devem focar na fiscalização de crimes (como lavagem de dinheiro e evasão fiscal), na imposição de diretrizes rígidas de jogo responsável (Portaria SPA/MF nº 1.231/2024), na educação financeira e no tratamento da ludopatia na rede de saúde pública. Estimula os estados-membros a exercerem ativamente sua competência material e administrativa na instituição de loterias e regulação de jogos regionais, visando o incremento do turismo, a geração de empregos e a ampliação da arrecadação fiscal descentralizada. Originalidade/Valor: O valor deste trabalho reside na abordagem interdisciplinar que conecta o Direito Constitucional Federalista e a autonomia dos estados com dados concretos de saúde pública (psicopatologia do jogo compulsivo) e indicadores macroeconômicos recentes sobre o impacto das apostas na renda familiar. Afasta-se do debate puramente moralista ao propor soluções com base no direito comparado e na jurisprudência vinculante do STF (ADPF 492), servindo como referencial técnico para o aperfeiçoamento regulatório e para a superação de distorções normativas no mercado de jogos nacional.",2026,Vichesi,Journal of Law and Sustainable Development,10.55908/sdgs.v14i7.4678,,https://doi.org/10.55908/sdgs.v14i7.4678,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex_non_en:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;openalex_non_en;s2;crossref;openaire,5,False,False,False,,,,,
R1236,Las Vegas Casino Revenue in an Expanding Gambling Market,"This study examines whether expanding gambling substitutes disrupt the Las Vegas gambling industry using quarterly data from 2014 through 2025. Las Vegas casino revenue is used as the benchmark to gauge the health of Las Vegas, adjusted to Quarter 1 2014 dollars and using an OLS regression model. The results imply that online sports betting is a poor substitute for Las Vegas revenue, while online gambling shows a positive and statistically significant relationship with revenue. The findings indicate that Las Vegas is resilient despite the introduction of more competition and gambling alternatives within an industry it has traditionally known to dominate.",2026,Villa-Real,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),10.34917/40601203,,https://doi.org/10.34917/40601203,en,datacite:Text/Capstone | openalex:article,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1237,The sports gambling novice experience: an autoethnography of a woman bettor,"ABSTRACT Guided by scholarship on fandom, gendered leisure, and sports gambling, this study examines a woman’s novice experience with legalized sports betting in North America. Employing an analytic autoethnography, the study documents the lead author’s first-time engagement with sports betting during the 2024–2025 National Football League season and playoffs. Data were collected through structured, reflexive journaling across weekly wagers placed on two major online sportsbooks, and an iterative coding analysis led to five interrelated themes: (1) emotional volatility across wins, losses, and near-misses; (2) platform design as both an enabler and barrier to participation; (3) shifting social dynamics and inclusion within a male-dominated betting culture; (4) a learning trajectory from hesitant newcomer to confident participant; and (5) financial decision-making as an ongoing negotiation of risk, responsibility, and confidence. Findings demonstrate that while novice sports betting exposes women to emotional strain and escalating risk, it can also function as a mechanism for legitimacy and inclusion within contemporary sport fandom. The study extends gendered understandings of sports betting beyond male-centric narratives and offers empirical insight into how novice bettors navigate sports betting. Practically, the findings suggest that sportsbooks should implement novice-oriented onboarding programs that combine betting education with emotional regulation and financial risk-management tools to reduce harm while fostering sustainable engagement.",2026,Violin,Sport Management Review,10.1080/14413523.2026.2620213,,https://doi.org/10.1080/14413523.2026.2620213,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1238,Gambling disorder symptom severity and mental health: an item-response-theory analysis of DSM-5 criteria for gambling disorder,"Abstract The present study examined the relationship between the DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for gambling disorder (GD) and gambling severity via an item response theory (IRT) analysis in two large German population survey data sets (Buth et al. (2022, 2024)). IRT-based person fit analyses may reveal atypical response patterns (e.g. endorsing criteria linked to higher levels of disorder severity, but not criteria linked to lower levels). We examined the link of such atypical response patterns and mental health as measured by the MHI-5, employing a 2-parameter-logistic (2PL) IRT model and a linear mixed model with random intercepts. Results largely replicated previously reported item severity rankings across both samples: GD criteria such as loss chasing and a preoccupation with gambling were generally linked to lower severity levels, whereas criteria such as withdrawal symptoms or job/family problems where generally linked to higher severity levels. Modelling revealed a reduced assessment sensitivity in lower gambling severity ranges. Furthermore, person fit analyses suggest that atypical symptom patterns may be linked to poorer mental health (MHI-5). Implications for the interpretability of total scores of endorsed criteria and the validity of diagnostic practices determining eligibility for treatment and financial compensation are discussed.",2026,Vogl,medRxiv,10.64898/2026.08.12.26360267,,https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.08.12.26360267,,citations:preprint,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1239,The Social and Economic Impacts of Casinos in Massachusetts and Implications for Other Jurisdictions,"After casino gambling was legalized in Massachusetts in 2011, the Social and Economic Impacts of Gambling in Massachusetts (SEIGMA) study was launched to investigate the impacts of casinos in the state. Extensive social and economic data was collected prior to and after the opening of the new casinos to identify the impacts of their introduction. The study also allowed for consideration of the impacts of COVID-19 and the legalization of sports betting in Massachusetts on gambling behavior and gambling problems in the Commonwealth. The presentation will focus on the impacts identified through 2024 in three areas, including overall impacts, social and health impacts, and economic and fiscal impacts. Particular attention will be paid to notable differences in the impacts of the casinos in three distinct markets in urban and suburban areas of the state. The findings have particular relevance for the pending introduction of casino gambling at three locations in New York City, anticipated between 2026 and 2030.",2026,Volberg,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1240,"Gambling Prevalence, Determinants and Association with Anti-Retroviral Therapy Adherence Among Adolescents Living with HIV in Abuja","Background: Gambling, classified as a behavioural addiction in DSM-5, poses a public health challenge among adolescents, particularly those living with HIV (ALHIV), where it intersects with mental health disorders, substance use, and barriers to antiretroviral therapy (ART) adherence. In sub-Saharan Africa, where adolescent HIV prevalence remains high (approximately 1.01 million aged 15-19 years globally per UNICEF 2023), limited data exist on gambling’s prevalence, determinants, and impact on ART outcomes in urban ALHIV cohorts. This study addresses this gap by examining gambling patterns and their association with ART adherence in Abuja, Nigeria. Methods: A cross-sectional study was conducted from August to September 2025 among 140 ALHIV (aged 10-19 years) accessing care at purposively selected secondary and tertiary facilities in the Federal Capital Territory. Multistage sampling (purposive area council selection, random facility balloting, consecutive enrolment) yielded a sample adjusted for 10% non-response. Data were collected via interviewer-administered semi-structured questionnaires incorporating the Problem Gambling Severity Index (PGSI), Patient Health Questionnaire-9 (PHQ-9)/Generalized Anxiety Disorder-7 (GAD-7), AUDIT-C, and self-reported adherence (≥95% doses in past 30 days). Bivariate (χ²/Fisher’s exact) and multivariate logistic regression analyses were performed using SPSS v27, with p&lt;0.05 denoting significance. Results: Gambling prevalence was 32.1%, with 48.8% at moderate-to-high PGSI risk; sports betting (15.7%) and online gambling (12.9%) predominated. Bivariate analyses linked gambling to alcohol use (χ²=10.607, p=0.003) and smoking (χ²=5.232, p=0.037); multivariate regression confirmed alcohol as a determinant (adjusted OR=0.275, 95% CI: 0.071-1.056, p=0.034 for non-use vs. hazardous). Sociodemographic showed no associations. ART adherence was suboptimal (40.7% good), but no significant link emerged with gambling status (χ²=0.731, p=0.463) or PGSI category (χ²=3.690, p=0.282). Conclusion: Elevated gambling prevalence among urban ALHIV underscores modifiable risks like substance use, yet without direct ART adherence disruption. Integrated screening (PGSI/AUDIT-C) in HIV clinics is recommended to achieve UNAIDS 95-95-95 targets and SDG 3, with longitudinal studies needed for causality. &nbsp;",2026,Vongdip Nankpah Godsave,AIJR Abstracts,,,https://abstracts.aijr.org/index.php/index/article/view/665,eng,openaire:Article,openaire,1,False,True,False,,,,,
R1241,The Silent Epidemic: Early-Onset Ludopathy in Children and the Invisible Digital Threat,,2026,Vural,The Journal of Pediatrics,10.1016/j.jpeds.2026.114987,41548745,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jpeds.2026.114987,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1242,A Software Framework for Analysing Sports Betting Workflows,"Tato práce se zabývá návrhem a implementací software frameworku pro testování strojového učení v oblasti sportovního sázení. Framework navazuje na předchozí pokusy a klade důraz na pevné principy softwarového inženýrství, jako jsou modularita, rozšiřitelnost, jednoduchost použití, bezproblémová instalace a komplexní dokumentace s praktickými příklady. Framework integruje existující modely, data a sázkové strategie poskytnuté vedoucím práce a současně využívá osvědčené postupy softwarového vývoje a MLOps pro efektivní a reprodukovatelné experimentování. Práce zahrnuje studium relevantní předchozí práce, návrh robustní a modulární architektury a implementaci uživatelsky přívětivého balíčku se systémem verzování a snadnou instalací. Funkčnost frameworku je demonstrována na několika end-to-end pracovních postupech ve sportovním sázení, což dokládá jeho praktickou využitelnost a flexibilitu pro výzkum a vývoj v této oblasti.",2026,VĚTROVEC,Cvut DSpace (Czech Technical University),,,,en,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1243,How Bad Sports Betting Research Can Lead to Bad Sports Betting Regulations,"Academic research can provide important evidence to inform policy debates. Such has been the case with the ""gambling studies"" academic field, which has largely developed since 1990, when the casino industry began expanding outside of Nevada and Atlantic City, NJ. Recently, sports betting legalization across the United States has been controversial. Despite a significant body of published research analyzing the socioeconomic effects of expanded gambling opportunities, much of the recent public discussion has focused on just three working papers circulated in 2024. These papers have been cited as authoritative in media reports and have been presented before policymakers at the state and federal level. We highlight serious methodological problems in these papers to suggest that flawed research can lead to bad policy, and that the current sports betting debate has parallels to the 1990s debate over the ""social costs of gambling.""",2026,Walker,The Journal of Gambling Business and Economics,10.5750/jgbe.v19i1.2645,,https://doi.org/10.5750/jgbe.v19i1.2645,en,crossref:journal-article | crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex:conference-paper | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1244,"More Than a Bad Bet: Problem Gambling, Emotional Reactivity, and Athlete Cyberbullying Among Sports Bettors","Abstract Background The rapid expansion of legal sports betting in the United States since Murphy v. National Collegiate Athletic Association has coincided with a documented rise in online harassment of athletes, yet research has focused almost exclusively on athletes as victims rather than on bettors as perpetrators. Objective This study examined individual-level predictors of athlete cyberbullying (ACB) among sports bettors. Methods A survey of 1,013 U.S. sports bettors using a survey. Participants reported mood reactivity to betting outcomes, gambling-related harm, typical wager amount, age, and the frequency of seven cyberbullying behaviors directed at athletes and coaches. Logistic regression models predict any ACB and high-frequency ACB (several times weekly or more). Results More than half of respondents (57.0%) endorsed at least one cyberbullying behavior, and 35.7% reported at least one at high frequency. Supporting the hypothesis that mood reactivity independently predicted ACB. Odds increased steadily for the high frequency of ACB outcome with increased mood reactivity. Self-reported problem gambling more than tripled the adjusted odds of any ACB and showed a stronger association with high-frequency ACB. Larger wagers and younger age also predicted ACB. Mood reactivity did not moderate the problem-gambling association, indicating that the two operate as parallel rather than interacting pathways. Conclusions Gambling related harm extends beyond bettors and families to athletes and sport communities. Because the two risk pathways are additive rather than interacting, either can be addressed independently. Social work assessment of problem gambling should incorporate items on emotional reactivity to losses and post-loss online behavior.",2026,Walker,Research Square,10.21203/rs.3.rs-10733136/v1,,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-10733136/v1,,crossref:posted-content | europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR | openalex:preprint,europepmc;openalex;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1245,Sports Betting Access and Labor Market Outcomes: Evidence fromOnline and Retail Legalization,"Abstract Since the 2018 Murphy v. NCAA decision, U.S. states have expanded sports betting through retail-only, online-only, and mixed regulatory regimes. Using Current Population Survey (CPS)micro-level data and a staggered group-time difference-in-differences design, we examine how expanded sports betting access affects labor market outcomes. We find modest but modality-specific effects: online-only legalization has little aggregate impact, retail-only legalization reduces labor force participation and actual weekly hours, and combined adoption of both online and retail legalization increases participation and employment. These aggregate patterns mask heterogeneity: online access reduces participation among young adults, while retail access has stronger negative effects with working intensity among women and single individuals. Overall, the results highlight that labor market responses depend on access modality and demographic-specific labor supply elasticities rather than uniform labor market withdrawal. JEL Codes: D91; J22; K23",2026,Wang,Research Square,10.21203/rs.3.rs-9195227/v1,,https://doi.org/10.21203/rs.3.rs-9195227/v1,und,crossref:posted-content | europepmc:Preprint|src=PPR | openaire:Article | openalex:preprint,europepmc;openalex;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1246,Suicidal ideation among people with different gambling behaviour profiles: analysis of a longitudinal survey of people who gamble regularly in the UK.,"BACKGROUND: People who gamble experience elevated rates of suicidal thoughts and behaviours. Longitudinal studies have been scarce, and none has focused on those who regularly gamble in the UK. AIMS: To examine the relationship between specific products and locations of gambling activity (and their combinations) and risk of subsequent suicidal thoughts. METHOD: We analysed a UK longitudinal survey of 3927 adults (18 years old or over) who regularly bet on sports. Data were collected online between June and November 2020. Latent class analysis was used to identify groups of people with similar gambling profiles on the basis of 13 types of gambling activity. Weighted group characteristics are presented. Regression modelling was used to test associations between gambling groups and suicidal thoughts, adjusting for baseline characteristics. RESULTS: Five distinct groups were identified. One group (5.6% of the sample) reported multiple types of both in-person and online gambling. This group was the most likely to use electronic gambling machines. After adjustment for baseline suicidal thoughts, this group had significantly higher odds of subsequent suicidal thoughts (adjusted odds ratio 3.42; 95% CI: 1.18-9.89) than other groups. CONCLUSIONS: Although many profiles of gambling activity present suicide risk, some types present greater risk. National Institute for Health and Care Excellence guidelines recommend enquiry in primary care settings about gambling behaviours. Our findings suggest that clinicians should consider asking questions on mode (online or in-person) and product (especially electronic gambling machines) to identify those at heightened risk of suicidal ideation. Gambling should also be considered routinely in psychosocial assessments across clinical settings and incorporated into suicide prevention campaigns.",2026,Wardle,BJPsych open,10.1192/bjo.2025.10935,41536125,https://doi.org/10.1192/bjo.2025.10935,eng,europepmc:research-article;Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1247,Educating Novice Bettors: An Applied Framework for Risk-Aware Decision Making,"Sports betting participation has expanded rapidly, while most responsible gambling interventions rely on restriction-based tools such as limits, self-exclusion, and reactive controls. These approaches often fail to address a primary driver of harm among novice bettors: limited understanding of probability, risk, and decision quality. This paper presents an applied framework for betting literacy as a preventative responsible gambling strategy using AI-driven decision support systems. Drawing from real-world platform design and user engagement observations, the presentation examines how structured educational tools, probabilistic transparency, and decision-focused interfaces influence bettor behavior. Rather than emphasizing outcomes or picks, the framework promotes process-oriented learning, including variance awareness, line movement, and risk calibration prior to wager placement. The paper explores how novice bettors respond when predictive information is paired with contextual explanations and behavioral friction, encouraging reflection over impulsive action. Observations indicate improved risk awareness, more consistent staking behavior, and reduced emotionally driven wagering patterns. These findings support betting literacy embedded in product design as a complementary harm-reduction model for regulators, platform designers, and researchers, emphasizing prevention through understanding rather than restriction.",2026,Waters,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1249,Determinants of policy innovation diffusion in China's provincial sports industry funds: A mixed-methods analysis of strategic adaptation and institutional constraint.,"Existing policy-diffusion theories emphasize economic capacity, market maturity, and intergovernmental pressure, yet these factors may operate differently in specialized industrial policies. This study examines the diffusion of provincial sports industry development funds in China through a mixed-methods design combining discrete-time event history analysis of 31 provincial-level administrative regions from 2006 to 2022 with thematic analysis of 30 stakeholder interviews. The results show that fiscal self-sufficiency and sports lottery sales are positively associated with policy adoption, underscoring the importance of fiscal discretion and policy-specific resources. By contrast, the number of sports enterprises is negatively associated with adoption, contrary to the hypothesized positive relationship and consistent with a tentative compensatory interpretation in which dedicated funds respond to perceived industrial weakness. Administrative pressure is significant in several reduced models but not in the fully specified model, suggesting that national directives shape the institutional context without independently determining provincial adoption timing. Interview findings further reveal how strategic resource allocation, symbolic responses to public demand, formal compliance, and selective policy adaptation mediate the translation of policy conditions into local action. Together, the findings advance a context-sensitive account of diffusion organized around resource specificity, compensatory intervention, and strategic local translation, thereby identifying important boundary conditions of conventional policy-diffusion explanations.",2026,Wei,PloS one,10.1371/journal.pone.0354242,42627781,https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0354242,eng,europepmc:research-article;Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1250,Cross-Sport Substitution Effects on NBA Betting Volume: Evidence from the 2024–2025 Regular Season,"This study investigates the determinants of betting volume for individual National Basketball Association (NBA) games during the 2024–2025 regular season. Using betting volume data sourced from ActionNetwork.com, we employ linear regression models to assess the impact of concurrent live games from competing major sports leagues—National Football League (NFL), National Hockey League (NHL), and Major League Baseball (MLB)—on the same day. Models use the natural logarithm of betting volume as the dependent variable, with key independent variables capturing the presence of overlapping games from other leagues, alongside controls for NBA game-specific factors, including point spread, posted total, television network and start time. Findings indicate significant substitution effects: betting activity on NBA games declines when live events from competing leagues occur simultaneously, suggesting bettors reallocate wagers and attention across sports. Moreover, NBA game betting volume increases with larger posted point spreads and especially higher over/under totals, reflecting greater interest in matchups with perceived high scoring potential and greater uncertainty. These results highlight inter-league competition in legal sports betting markets and offer insights for sportsbook operators, NBA scheduling decisions, and responsible gambling initiatives in a time of expanding wagering access.",2026,Weinbach,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1251,Betting the House: Why the Safe Bet Act Is a Long Shot to Survive Constitutional Scrutiny Under the Commerce Clause and Anti-Commandeering Doctrine,"The rapid expansion of legalized sports gambling following Murphy v. NCAA has raised concerns about problem gambling, predatory marketing, and inconsistent state regulations. In response, the SAFE Bet Act, introduced by Representative Paul Tonko, seeks to impose nationwide standards on sports betting. However, its framework raises significant constitutional concerns under the Commerce Clause and the anti-commandeering doctrine. This Article argues that the Act’s enforcement mechanism likely violates the Tenth Amendment by coercing states into compliance, conflicting with Supreme Court precedent on federal conditional spending and preemption. Given these vulnerabilities, this Article explores three alternative approaches that would achieve similar regulatory goals while better-respecting state autonomy: (1) a Uniform Act through the Uniform Law Commission, (2) a revised SAFE Bet Act incorporating non-coercive incentive, and (3) a settlement-based regulatory model inspired by the Master Settlement Agreement with the tobacco industry. Ultimately, this Article contends that a cooperative regulatory model— rather than direct federal mandates—offers the most viable and constitutionally sound path forward. By learning from past federal regulatory frameworks and Supreme Court precedent on federalism, lawmakers can and should craft a legal structure that protects consumers, mitigates gambling-related harms, and preserves the constitutional balance of power between the federal government and the states.",2026,Weingard,eYLS (Yale Law School),,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1252,Evaluating Stringency of Age-Gating in Online Sports Betting Platforms,,2026,Wells,Oxford Journal of Student Scholarship,10.65161/recgylagc6jhubgbv,,https://doi.org/10.65161/recgylagc6jhubgbv,und,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1253,Exploring Gambling-like Behaviors in Children’s Cigarette Card Games and Parental Pro-game Perceptions in China: A Qualitative Study,"Cigarette card games emerged as a popular yet controversial pastime among children in China, raising concerns on the potential to foster gambling-like behaviors. This study explored children's experiences and perceptions of cigarette card games, as well as parental attitudes and facilitation practices. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with 21 child-parent dyads (19 boys, 2 girls) from September to December 2024. Purposive and snowball sampling techniques were adopted to recruit children who had played cigarette card games in the past 1 month. Separate interview protocols were developed for children and parents to ensure age-appropriate communication. Interviews were audio-recorded, transcribed verbatim, and analyzed thematically following Braun and Clarke's framework, with dual coding and triangulation. Four key themes emerged: (1) Chance and reward-driven engagement, where children linked the thrill of obtaining rare cards to gambling; (2) Peer influence and social reinforcement, which strengthened friendships but risked excluding non-players; (3) Parental misperceptions, viewing the games as healthier alternatives to screen time that promote outdoor activity; and (4) Parental facilitation, including sourcing cards through networks and rationalizing support as aiding socialization. These patterns indicate that cigarette card games may expose children to gambling-like reward systems and normalize tobacco-related imagery, with parental participation further amplifying such exposure and acceptance. Cigarette card games blend gambling-like features with early tobacco normalization, amplified by parental pro-game practices. Public health interventions should target both children and parents, promoting awareness of gambling risks and guiding families toward safe, prosocial forms of play.",2026,Weng,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10514-2,42209887,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10514-2,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1254,Online Sports Betting Legalization and Behavioral Health Care Use.,"Importance: The number of states with legal online sports betting has grown from 1 to 31 since 2018, expanding access to gambling. Despite the known behavioral health burdens associated with gambling, little is understood about legalization's impact on mental health and substance use treatment use. Objective: To evaluate the association between online sports betting legalization and treatment use for mental health conditions and substance use disorders (SUD). Design, Setting, and Participants: This was a quasi-experimental staggered difference-in-differences study using claims data from January 2016 to December 2023. The setting was a large, national convenience sample of people with employer-sponsored health insurance. Included in the analysis were men and women aged 18 to 64 years living in states that legalized online sports betting during 2018-2022 or had not by the end of the study period. Exposure: State legalization of online sports betting. Main Outcomes and Measures: Outcomes were measured at the state-year level, weighted by number of enrollees as follows: proportions of enrollees with 1 or more prescription fills for antidepressant, antianxiety, or SUD medications and proportions of enrollees with 1 or more outpatient, emergency department, or inpatient visits with a diagnosis for depression, anxiety, or SUD. Results: Among 61 951 305 enrollee-years, 31 914 852 (52%) were women, and the mean (SD) age was 41.9 (13.5) years. Online sports betting legalization was associated with increases in the probability of antidepressant use (men: 0.57 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.26-0.89 percentage points; women: 1.08 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.23-1.94 percentage points) and antianxiety use (men: 0.32 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.14-0.50 percentage points; women: 0.64 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.16-1.12 percentage points), but there was no detectable change in SUD medication use. Legalization was also associated with increases in the probability of an outpatient diagnosis for depression (men: 0.34 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.20-0.49 percentage points; women: 0.57 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.25-0.89 percentage points) and anxiety (men: 0.67 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.32-1.01 percentage points; women: 1.15 percentage points; 95% CI, 0.50-1.81 percentage points) but not SUD. Legalization was not associated with behavioral health diagnoses in the emergency department and inpatient settings. Conclusions and Relevance: Results of this quasi-experimental difference-in-differences study suggest that online sports betting legalization was associated with an increase in outpatient and prescription mental health care use among people with employer-sponsored health insurance. These findings underscore the importance of prevention and treatment of potential adverse health consequences of legalized online sports betting.",2026,Westermeyer,JAMA psychiatry,10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2026.3175,42873510,https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2026.3175,eng,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;openalex;s2;crossref,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1255,The economics of free bets,,2026,Whelan,Economics Letters,10.1016/j.econlet.2026.113289,,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.econlet.2026.113289,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1256,"Information, Uncertainty, and Betting Market Accuracy Across the NFL Season","This paper tests whether preseason uncertainty causes sportsbooks to make larger prediction errors in early-season NFL games than in late-season games, using game-level OLS regression on 6,185 regular season games from 2002 to 2025, where prediction error is measured as the absolute difference between the closing point spread and the actual game margin. Across the full sample, prediction error holds steady at roughly 10 points regardless of where in the season a game falls, and coaching changes do not amplify early-season error. Rather than amplifying error, larger draft capital gaps between teams are associated with slightly less prediction error, suggesting that observable roster investment signals are priced in rather than overlooked. A notable exception emerges in a subsample from 2014 to 2022, where games in the opening two weeks involving a new head coach show meaningfully higher prediction error than comparable late-season games — a localized pattern that the full-sample results obscure. Together, the findings are consistent with a market that is broadly efficient on average, where any inefficiencies appear too localized in sample and too narrow in scope to support systematic out-of-sample exploitation.",2026,White,Scholarship - Claremont (Claremont Colleges),,,,,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1257,Can simple models predict football — and beat the odds? Lessons from the German Bundesliga,"This study examines whether a simple model based on expected goals (xG) can generate accurate forecasts and identify profitable signals in football betting markets. The model uses recent xG to estimate win–draw–loss probabilities via a Skellam distribution, with isotonic regression applied for calibration, and evaluates the performance over eleven Bundesliga seasons (2014/15 through 2024/25). While bookmaker odds tend to exhibit superior statistical calibration, the xG-based model captures certain signals not fully reflected in market prices. In simulated betting, the model yields a return on investment of approximately 10% using average market odds, increasing to nearly 15% under the best available prices. Profits stem predominantly from home win bets, while backing away wins is consistently loss-making. The results show some robustness to modelling choices, though profitability varies considerably by season and bet type. The study highlights the potential of simple models as practical tools for identifying predictive value in structured football data.",2026,Wilkens,Journal of Sports Analytics,10.1177/22150218261416681,,https://doi.org/10.1177/22150218261416681,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | openalex:preprint | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1258,"Objective Performance, Subjective Appraisal, and Mood Across a Fantasy Premier League Season: A 38-Wave Longitudinal Study",,2026,Wilkins,SSRN Electronic Journal,10.2139/ssrn.7532302,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.7532302,,openalex:preprint,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1259,Canadian provincial channelization of online gambling in 2025: a cross-provincial analysis of player and revenue channelization,"There are widespread claims that most online gambling in Canada occurs on unsanctioned out-of-country sites rather than on provincial government platforms. However, these claims rely on black-box proprietary modeling and surveys with important sampling and measurement limitations. This study provides the first academic investigation of this issue and the first cross-provincial assessment of online gambling channelization in Canada. Player channelization was estimated in two ways: by comparing active registered players on provincial platforms in fiscal year 2024/25 with the number of past-year online gamblers in each province from the 2025 Rapid Response Gambling Module of the Canadian Community Health Survey (CCHS), and through a weighted national online panel survey of 12,970 adults that assessed patronization by gambling format. Expenditure channelization was also estimated from weighted self-reported spending in the panel survey. Results showed strong rank-order agreement across methods, with relatively high channelization in Quebec, Ontario, British Columbia, and the Atlantic Provinces, and lower channelization in Alberta and Saskatchewan. Channelization was highest for lotteries, instant lotteries, and bingo, and lowest for casino table games, sports betting, and other niche online gambling formats. Overall, channelization varied by province and gambling format and was higher in some provinces than prior estimates have suggested.",2026,Williams,International Gambling Studies,10.1080/14459795.2026.2708668,,https://doi.org/10.1080/14459795.2026.2708668,en,citations:article | openalex:article,openalex;citations,2,False,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1261,Sports Finance and Management,"As the sports business continues to evolve, so does the field of Sport Finance and Management. The first edition of this book provided an in-depth exploration of the dynamic changes within the sports industry, including the interconnected financial relationships between teams and their associated businesses, the influence of fan loyalty, and the impact of sponsorships on team revenues. Building on this foundation, the second edition updates these core elements, introduces new case studies, and examines the effects of changes in facility design, media opportunities, and league and conference policies. These factors are analyzed in relation to their impact on the economic success of teams, the salaries of professional players, and the financial landscape of collegiate athletics. This latest edition goes even further, incorporating discussions on emerging topics such as women's sports and sports betting. It also addresses the significant transformations the industry has experienced in recent years, offering a comprehensive and timely perspective on the evolving world of sports finance and management.",2026,Winfree,Taylor & Francis eBooks,10.4324/9781003659464,,https://doi.org/10.4324/9781003659464,en,openalex:book,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1262,Integrity at Stake: Statistical Detection of Anomalous In-Play Betting in Football,"Match-fixing undermines the integrity of sport by eroding public trust and threatening the financial sustainability of clubs and leagues. The expansion of sports betting markets has created new incentives and opportunities for manipulation, calling for rigorous, data-driven monitoring tools. Football, which accounts for the largest share of global betting turnover, remains particularly exposed: integrity reports continue to flag several suspicious matches, with past scandals in Italy and Turkey underlining the problem's persistence. This study uses high-frequency in-play (live) betting data from the Italian Serie B (seasons 2018/19--2020/21) to explore statistical approaches for detecting unusual betting behaviour. A state-space modelling framework is employed to describe standard betting market dynamics and to predict expected betting volumes conditional on match characteristics. Deviations from these expectations can then be analysed using outlier detection techniques to identify potentially suspicious betting activities. The results demonstrate how statistical modelling can contribute to the early identification of unusual betting patterns, thereby supporting integrity assurance in in-play sports betting markets.",2026,Winkelmann,arXiv (Cornell University),10.48550/arxiv.2605.30209,,https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2605.30209,und,datacite:Preprint/Article | openaire:Article; Preprint | openalex:preprint | s2:,openalex;s2;datacite;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1263,"The relationship between executive functions, decision-making, and changes in symptoms of gambling disorder in online sports bettors.","Deficits in executive functions (EFs) and impulsive decision-making are well-established cognitive mechanisms underlying gambling disorder (GD), though there are some inconsistent findings. This study investigated the relationship between changes in GD symptoms in online sports bettors and specific EFs as well as decision-making processes over time. A subsample of online sports bettors (n = 54, mean age = 33, 96% male) from a larger study underwent an in-person assessment. Initial GD symptom severity was measured online according to DSM-5 criteria and was low to mild in most participants. All other assessments took place an average of 443 days later, during the in-person assessment. We analyzed cross-sectional and longitudinal associations between three facets of EFs (response inhibition, cognitive flexibility, and working memory) and four decision-making facets (delay discounting, probability discounting for gains and losses, and loss aversion) and GD symptom severity and changes over time. Cross-sectional results showed that higher GD symptom severity was associated with poorer working memory and reduced loss aversion. Reduced loss aversion was also associated with greater symptom increase over time. However, these associations were not statistically significant after correction for multiple testing, so they should be interpreted with caution. No other significant relationships were found. These findings provide preliminary evidence that reduced loss aversion is one cognitive factor in disordered gambling among online sports bettors, a population with heterogeneous profiles of EFs and decision-making. Further longitudinal studies are needed to clarify the role of specific cognitive processes in the development and progression of GD to better inform treatment strategies. Supplementary Information The online version contains supplementary material available at 10.1038/s41598-026-48449-8.",2026,Wirkus,Scientific reports,10.1038/s41598-026-48449-8,41968207,https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-026-48449-8,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:research-article;Journal Article|src=MED | openaire:Article | openalex:article | pubmed:Journal Article | s2:JournalArticle,pubmed;europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,6,False,False,False,,,,,
R1264,"Kodex zum Umgang mit der Industrie, die abhängigkeitserzeugende Produkte vertreibt (z. B. Alkohol, Cannabis, Tabak, nicht pharmazeutische Nikotinprodukte, Glücksspiele, Sportwetten)","Zusammenfassung: Hintergrund: Industrien, deren Geschäftsmodell auf der Vermarktung abhängig machender oder gesundheitsschädlicher Produkte beruht, beeinflussen Forschung, Evidenzbildung und Gesundheitspolitik. Dazu zählen Tabak- und Nikotinindustrie, Alkohol- und Cannabisunternehmen sowie Glücksspiel- und Sportwettenanbieter, deren Strategien von industriefinanzierter Forschung über Sponsoring bis zu PR-Kampagnen und Lobbying reichen. Die vorliegenden Daten zeigen, dass dadurch systematische Interessenkonflikte entstehen, die wissenschaftliche Integrität und Glaubwürdigkeit gefährden. Position: Das Positionspapier formuliert einen Kodex für wissenschaftliche Fachgesellschaften im Umgang mit diesen Industrien. Es fordert die grundsätzliche Ablehnung von Geld- und Sachzuwendungen sowie wechselseitigen Gegenleistungen, die Vermeidung von Kooperationen mit industriegestützten Personen und Organisationen und eine konsequente Offenlegung aller relevanten finanziellen und nicht-finanziellen Interessenkonflikte der vergangenen fünf Jahre. Ausgenommen sind gesetzlich oder regulatorisch vorgeschriebene klinische Prüfungen unter Bedingungen strenger Transparenz, unabhängiger Governance und uneingeschränkter Publikationsrechte. Ziel ist es, Unabhängigkeit, Qualität und Vertrauenswürdigkeit der Suchtforschung zu sichern und den Einfluss kommerzieller Interessen auf Forschung, Lehre und gesundheitspolitische Empfehlungen wirksam zu begrenzen.",2026,Wodarz,SUCHT - Zeitschrift für Wissenschaft und Praxis / Journal of Addiction Research and Practice,10.1024/0939-5911/a001001,,https://doi.org/10.1024/0939-5911/a001001,de,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1265,Betting on Belief: How perceptions of increasing sport wagering shape fan trust in game,"The increased availability of legalized sport wagering across the United States has transformed the modern sport industry, redefining fan engagement practices. As betting partnerships, in-game odds integration, and gambling media content become increasingly embedded in sport broadcasts, understanding how fans interpret these changes is critical. This study examines how fans perceive the rise of sport wagering and how their perceptions influence their views of sport efficacy (i.e., integrity, authenticity, fairness, etc.). Participants will include adult sport fans, 18 or older, recruited through Murray State athletic events. Individuals recruited will be asked to voluntarily answer a four-section survey along with demographic questions. The first section will include the Sport Fandom Questionnaire (Wann, 2001), a five-item measure assessing the level of sport fandom. The next scale will be the five-item Fan Dysfunction Scale (Wakefield & Wann, 2006), designed to assess the degree to which fans are confrontational. The third scale will be the seven-item Sport Spectator Identification Scale – Revised (James, Delia, & Wann, 2019), completed to assess the level of identification with a favorite sport team. In the fourth and final section, the participants will be asked to complete a 6-item scale, assessing the level of an individual’s trust in sport following the increased availability of sport wagering.",2026,Wood,Murray State's Digital Commons (Murray State University),,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1266,Betting on Performance: Sports Betting Legalization and NBA Player Performance Relative to Sportsbook Expectations,"This paper presents some of the first empirical work examining how the legality of sports betting markets affects the relationship between player performance and sportsbook expectations and adds to a small but growing body of research on how legal sports betting changes player behavior. To achieve this, this paper evaluates the effect legalization of mobile sports betting has on NBA player performance relative to prominent sportsbook proposition line (or “prop line”) expectations. To determine that effect and assess whether these betting expectations impact player behaviors, a player-game level dataset is used that links FanDuel closing over/under lines to real game data for 178 NBA players across five different statistical categories. The dataset spans three distinct player positions (center, guard and forward) and covers games from the 2022 to the 2025 seasons. Using a difference-in-differences model, and exploiting staggered state-level legalization timing, the results show that players in legal markets deviate more from their prop line expectations, though not in any consistent direction. The primary outcome was the absolute value of the deviation between game performance and the FanDuel closing line (AbsDev). StatDiff measured the directional difference between real game performance and the closing line. Betting legality was associated with a statistically significant increase in AbsDev across three main specifications (beta range 0.266-0.396, all p < 0.05). Player performance in legalized markets deviated further from applicable prop lines in both directions without a consistent directional bias. This effect was most pronounced in the points market (beta = 0.665, p = 0.020), specifically with centers and forwards. For example, Dennis Schroder, who was traded to five different teams from 2022-2025 in markets that both offered and did not offer legalized betting, provides additional evidence and support for the conclusions in this paper.",2026,Wornow,Scholarship @ Claremont (The Claremont Colleges),,,,,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1267,"Does the consideration of market prices in model selection increase model profitability? Evidence from theory, artificial data and real-world data","It is well-acknowledged in the literature that besides accuracy, profitability (i.e. the economic value) of a forecast is crucial in domains including economics, energy, weather or sports forecasting. The present study argues that selection of profitable forecasting models benefits from consideration of market prices in-sample (i.e. when optimizing model parameters on training data). Such models can be specifically optimised for profitability instead of accuracy, as most commonly done so far. Previous literature suggests that, under specific circumstances, accuracy- and profitability-optimisation leads to equal model selection. We theoretically show that this result is contingent upon strict assumptions and does not hold anymore if relaxing any of these assumptions. Moreover, we present evidence that out-of-sample profitability (i.e. returns generated on unseen data) are actually increased for profitability-optimised models compared to accuracy-optimised models based on artificial and real-world data from the domain of sports betting. This result is explained by the ability of profitability-optimised models to implicitly detect and counter an existing market bias, provided that the model family allows to capture such biases. The present study intends to stimulate discussion on optimal model selection for profitable forecasting and to encourage researchers to consider profitability-optimisation in forecasting applications to real-world data.",2026,Wunderlich,International Journal of Forecasting,10.1016/j.ijforecast.2025.11.006,,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijforecast.2025.11.006,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1268,Forecasting the Winner of a Live Tennis Match,"With the rise of live sports betting in recent years, tennis forecasting has expanded from pre-match prediction to models that update win probabilities as a match unfolds. A central challenge in creating such a model is the constant need for models to adapt to score and performance changes. This study examines how pre-match and live information can be most effectively integrated into a model to produce accurate win-probability estimates. The analysis uses 8,222 Grand Slam matches containing a total of 1,505,355 points. Five models were evaluated using a chronological split, with matches from 2011-2021 used for training, 2022 for validation, and 2023-2024 for testing. Trace, a hybrid model, achieved accuracies of 76.06%, 82.15%, and 88.34% at 25%, 50%, and 75% match progress, suggesting that hybrid modeling is a practical approach to live tennis forecasting.",2026,Xie,arXiv (Cornell University),10.48550/arxiv.2609.07617,,https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2609.07617,,datacite:Preprint/Article | openalex:preprint | s2:,openalex;s2;datacite,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1269,Classification-Behavior Discordance and Problem Gambling: Differential Patterns for Fantasy Sports and Loot Boxes Among College Students.,"Fantasy sports and loot boxes share similarities with gambling yet remain largely unregulated as such, creating ambiguity in how individuals perceive and engage with these activities. While participation in both activities has been linked to problem gambling, whether individuals' classification of these activities as gambling is associated with differential risk has not been examined. This study introduces the concept of classification-behavior discordance, defined as the misalignment between how an individual classifies an activity and whether they participate in it. Using data from an online survey of 4,016 college students enrolled at public colleges and universities in Nevada, United States, we constructed four discordance groups for each activity based on the intersection of classification and participation: non-participants, aware participants, unaware participants, and non-participating classifiers. We conducted separate binary logistic regression models for fantasy sports and loot boxes to examine associations between discordance group membership and problem gambling. Results revealed divergent patterns across activities. For loot boxes, classifying the activity as gambling was associated with significantly elevated problem gambling risk among participants, whereas for fantasy sports, participating without classifying the activity as gambling was associated with significantly lower risk. These findings highlight the importance of considering individuals' subjective classification of gambling-like activities in problem gambling screening, prevention, and regulatory efforts.",2026,Xu,Journal of gambling studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10549-5,42711636,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10549-5,eng,crossref:journal-article | europepmc:Journal Article|src=MED | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,europepmc;openalex;s2;crossref,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1270,Religiosity and Online Gambling Addiction: A Serial Mediation Model of Perceived Social Support and Life Satisfaction within the Framework of Conservation of Resources Theory,,2026,YAM,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10531-1,42384293,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10531-1,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1271,The Impact of NBA-Related Signals and Market Factors on the Short-Term Price Movement of DKNG Stock,"This study investigates whether NBA-related signals and basic market factors can help predict the short-term behavior of DraftKings (DKNG), a major U.S. sports betting company whose business activity is closely tied to NBA games. Using daily price data, market benchmarks, technical indicators, and selected game-intensity variables, the study builds predictive models for two tasks: next-day return direction classification and next-day volatility regression. Three machine learning models—Ridge, Random Forest, and XGBoost—were trained and evaluated using time-ordered rolling validation. Results show that non-linear models significantly outperform linear baselines. Direction prediction improves only modestly, but calibration scores become more reliable. Volatility prediction shows stronger improvement, especially with technical indicators. The findings suggest that combining market information with NBA-related signals provides meaningful predictive power, especially during high-attention game periods. This work offers a foundation for future research incorporating higher-frequency betting data, injury reports, and sentiment indicators. Such extensions could enhance understanding of how sports-event dynamics interact with financial market behavior in real time.",2026,Yao,Journal of innovation and development,10.54097/gk4hj630,,https://doi.org/10.54097/gk4hj630,und,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1272,Responsibility and Liability in Autonomous Vehicle Accidents,"As algorithms get more advanced, they can take over and optimize human activities such as driving and sports betting. My capstone focuses on how algorithms and different data modalities can affect outcomes and accuracy in sports betting, while my STS project investigates how humans and an algorithmic system can share responsibility in case of an accident. In my technical capstone, I researched the impact that different datasets have on an algorithm’s accuracy. With the topic of NBA sports betting, I found three data modalities to train my algorithms on. The first was statistical data from box scores of games. The second combined the basic statistical data with spatial movement data from Sport VU. SportVU was a tracking system that the NBA implemented, where cameras all over the courts captured all the positions of the players and the ball at 25 frames per second. The third data was statistical data combined with textual data via a play-by-play commentary of the game. By training the same model on these three different types of data, I was able to analyze the importance of data and how it impacted the results of the model. While machine learning algorithms and models may produce better results on paper, it can be hard to trust them, as you can’t tell what factors the model considered and what it viewed as important. This leads to my STS project, where I analyzed how responsibility and liability should be distributed in cases of accidents occurring with autonomous vehicles (AV). As AVs get more prevalent in today’s society, it becomes important to have a fair framework to delegate responsibility when accidents will inevitably occur. To thoroughly examine this issue, I used current law regulations, a sociological framework via Actor Network Theory, and the consequentialist ethical framework. By using these three types of analysis, I was able to get to the root of the problem and not only determine all actors within this complex system of humans and computers, but also who/what is in control of the vehicle. This led to a more deterministic approach in deciding responsibility in AV accidents. Both of these topics have led me to realize how much computers and algorithms play a role in our lives. While autonomous driving and sports betting seem unrelated, they both heavily depend on computer analysis. This shows how much we currently rely on computers/algorithms and whether we should continue to rely this heavily on them.",2026,Yoo,Libra,10.18130/r49z-yw42,,https://doi.org/10.18130/r49z-yw42,und,datacite:Text/Dissertation | openaire:Doctoral thesis | openalex:dissertation,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1273,How sports betting is changing the way people watch sports,,2026,Young,The Conversation Media Group Ltd,10.64628/aam.swr794xan,,https://doi.org/10.64628/aam.swr794xan,und,crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex:other,openalex;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1274,Experimental Assortments for Choice Estimation and Nest Identification,"What assortments (subsets of items) should be offered, to collect data for estimating a choice model over $n$ total items? We propose a structured, non-adaptive experiment design requiring only $O(\log n)$ distinct assortments, each offered repeatedly, that consistently outperforms randomized and other heuristic designs across an extensive numerical benchmark that estimates multiple different choice models under a variety of (possibly mis-specified) ground truths. We then focus on Nested Logit choice models, which cluster items into ""nests"" of close substitutes. Whereas existing Nested Logit estimation procedures assume the nests to be known and fixed, we present a new algorithm to identify nests based on collected data, which when used in conjunction with our experiment design, guarantees correct identification of nests under any Nested Logit ground truth. Our experiment design was deployed to collect data from over 70 million users at Dream11, an Indian fantasy sports platform that offers different types of betting contests, with rich substitution patterns between them. We identify nests based on the collected data, which lead to better out-of-sample choice prediction than ex-ante clustering from contest features. Our identified nests are ex-post justifiable to Dream11 management.",2026,Yu,arXiv (Cornell University),10.48550/arxiv.2602.16137,,https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2602.16137,und,datacite:Preprint/Article | openaire:Article; Preprint | openalex:preprint | s2:,openalex;s2;datacite;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R1275,Scale development: betting in sport,"Scale development is widely used for systematically measuring individuals’ attitudes and behaviors. Scale development focuses on constructing psychometric instruments that ensure valid and reliable measurement of specific constructs. This chapter details the scale development procedure, followed by a case study demonstrating its application in identifying the motivations behind sports betting. Sports betting is considered a motivated behavior, and understanding the underlying drivers behind this behavior is crucial for sports management. The case study focuses on developing a valid and reliable measurement tool to assess how sports betting motivations influence consumer engagement and industry practices. As a result of the survey, seven core motivational dimensions emerged from the data analysis: Make Money, Fun, Socialization, Recreation-Escape, Knowledge of the Game, Interest in Sports, and Being in the Game. These findings provide valuable insights into the broader implications of sports betting motivations for stakeholders in sports management. The developed scale offers practical applications for industry professionals, regulatory bodies, and researchers, supporting the design of responsible gambling policies and effective consumer engagement strategies. The chapter concludes with recommendations for future research, emphasizing the potential for extending the scale's application to diverse populations and settings and further examining its impact on sports-related consumer behavior.",2026,Yüce,Edward Elgar Publishing eBooks,10.4337/9781035317226.00023,,https://doi.org/10.4337/9781035317226.00023,,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1276,"Stressful Life Events, Masculinity and Gambling Participation: An Age-Stratified Analysis of Australian Men","Gambling participation varies across genders and throughout the life course, yet little research has examined how stressful life events and masculinity-related factors influence gambling behaviour across different stages of adulthood among men. Using Wave 4 data from Ten to Men: The Australian Longitudinal Study on Male Health (n = 5,434), this study investigates age-stratified associations between stressful life events, self-perceived masculinity, masculinity strain and gambling frequency among Australian men. Negative binomial regression analysis reveals that masculinity and masculinity strain predict higher gambling frequency only among young men, highlighting the salience of masculine identity during early adulthood. Stressful life events also showed age-specific effects: job loss is associated with lower frequency of gambling among young men, while bereavement of loved ones is linked to higher gambling among middle-aged men. No stressful life events correlated with gambling among older men. Across all age groups, higher income was associated with more frequent gambling, while possessing a university degree was associated with less frequent gambling among those aged 30 and above only. These findings accentuate the importance of a life course perspective for understanding gambling participation and informing age-tailored interventions.",2026,Zahnow,Journal of Gambling Studies,10.1007/s10899-026-10506-2,42081058,https://doi.org/10.1007/s10899-026-10506-2,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1278,"O Novo Jogo dos Negócios: SAF, Apostas e Transformações no Futebol","O presente resumo analisa a metamorfose estrutural do futebol brasileiro a partir da transição do modelo associativo tradicional para um ecossistema complexo de entretenimento e negócios, fundamentando-se nas perspectivas de Evandro Ferreira Gomes sobre a implementação das Sociedades Anônimas do Futebol (SAFs) e a ascensão das casas de apostas. A discussão central demonstra que a adoção da SAF surge como uma resposta necessária ao endividamento histórico dos clubes, embora carregue o risco de uma financeirização excessiva que pode transformar as instituições em ativos meramente especulativos, distanciando investidores da identidade cultural das torcidas. Paralelamente, o estudo aborda a onipresença das empresas de apostas esportivas, destacando que, apesar do aporte financeiro vital para a competitividade técnica, essa dependência gera desafios éticos significativos, como o perigo da manipulação de resultados e os impactos sociais da ludopatia, fatores que colocam em risco a credibilidade e a integridade do esporte. O texto também ressalta o avanço na profissionalização da gestão e do marketing esportivo nos últimos quinze anos, enfatizando a importância da governança corporativa e da diversidade, com destaque para a crescente participação feminina em cargos diretivos. Por fim, argumenta-se que espaços de debate e inovação são essenciais para transformar o país em um polo de inteligência em gestão, concluindo que a sustentabilidade do futebol na era da economia criativa depende do equilíbrio rigoroso entre a busca pela rentabilidade financeira e a preservação da confiança do público e da ética esportiva.",2026,Zenone,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.6642621,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6642621,eng,crossref:posted-content | openaire:Article | openalex_non_en:preprint,openalex_non_en;crossref;openaire,3,False,False,False,,R1279,,,
R1279,"O Novo Jogo dos Negócios: SAF, Apostas e Transformações no Futebol","Esta resenha científica analisa as transformações contemporâneas no cenário do futebol brasileiro, fundamentando-se nas discussões promovidas pelo especialista Evandro Ferreira Gomes em entrevista concedida ao canal O Marketeiro, sob a curadoria de Luiz Cláudio Zenoni. O estudo debruça-se sobre a transição do esporte de um modelo puramente associativo para um complexo ecossistema de entretenimento e negócios, balizado pela implementação das Sociedades Anônimas do Futebol (SAFs) e pela onipresença das casas de apostas esportivas, as chamadas ""bets"". A análise busca compreender como a profissionalização da gestão e o influxo de novos capitais reconfiguram o marketing, a ética e a sustentabilidade financeira das instituições esportivas no Brasil.",2026,Zenone,Elsevier BV,10.2139/ssrn.6642501,,https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6642501,,crossref:posted-content | openalex_non_en:preprint,openalex_non_en;crossref,2,False,False,False,,R1278,,,
R1280,Guest editorial: Fundamentals of case study research for sport marketing studies,"Case study research is an important method to investigate social phenomena, which has attracted increasing attention in marketing research, but its implementation still faces challenges, mainly from misunderstandings of definition and methodological rigor. Early studies regarded case studies as unique events that could not produce universal laws, and confused them with ethnography, field research and teaching case development, leading to the criticism of being descriptive, qualitative and lacking rigor. In recent decades, with the application of grounded theory, optimization of qualitative data analysis and multi-perspective triangulation, the scientificity and rigor of case study research have been significantly improved (Eisenhardt, 1989).A case study is defined as an empirical study that deeply investigates contemporary phenomena in real contexts, especially when the boundary between phenomena and contexts is blurred (Yin, 2017). On the one hand, it adopts inductive logic to build theory, collecting qualitative data through interviews, document analysis and observation to derive new hypotheses; on the other hand, it is good at answering “how” and “why” questions, focusing on complex problems that are difficult to define and observe, and presenting vivid research scenarios. Compared with experiments and questionnaire surveys, case studies have higher applicability in natural environments with less variable control.Case studies are divided into single-case and multiple-case designs, as well as comparative and embedded case studies. Single-case studies are selected for their extremity, revelatory nature or subversion to existing theories (Eisenhardt and Graebner, 2007; Yin, 2017), supporting in-depth exploration (Mao and Chen, 2017), but have limited generalizability. Multiple-case studies follow the principle of replication, and the conclusions are more universal and robust (Yin, 2017). Comparative studies select contrasting cases, while embedded studies set sub-cases in the main case for multi-level analysis.The core advantage of case study is the inductive exploration of real and interesting questions, and high-quality case studies are recognized as the most valuable research (Bluhm et al., 2011; Eisenhardt et al., 2016; Gibbert et al., 2008; Gibbert and Ruigrok, 2010). This feature of ‘telling stories scientifically” has important value for sport marketing with complex practical topics.With global economic development and rising quality of life, the value and economic functions of sport have been increasingly recognized (Zhang et al., 2016). The sport industry is becoming a mainstream sector with growth potential, and areas like professional sport operations, event marketing, venue management, fitness club promotions, and digital sports evolve rapidly. Coupled with globalization and technological advancement, the complexity and uniqueness of sport marketing stand out, and many practical problems cannot be fully explained by universal marketing models, providing opportunities for case study research. Drawing on Mao and Chen (2017), three types of research questions are suitable for case study research in sport marketing.Case study research is first and foremost suitable for exploring new research questions where established theories are lacking or theoretical gaps exist. This category represents the main area where case study research in sport marketing is concentrated. The goal is theory building (Eisenhardt, 1989) based on inductive logic to explain “how” and “why” questions. Researchers do not predefine relationships between constructs and theory; instead, hypotheses emerge from multi-source qualitative data. For instance, Kellett and Russell (2009) conducted a case study on skateboarding clusters, offering insights for the extreme sport industry. Tripodi et al. (2009) examined sponsors’ exploitation strategies and ambush marketing during the Sydney 2000 Olympic Games. Recent case studies have examined the sustainability of major events (Jiang et al., 2026), legacy utilization (Park et al., 2020), smart sport venues (Wang and Li, 2025), smart sport communities (Chen et al., 2025), and digital sport digital marketing (Wang et al., 2025).Case study research is also well-suited for exploring complex sport marketing and operation problems, building process theories, or investigating questions with hard-to-measure constructs. This methodological approach enables researchers to delve deeply into marketing operations as they unfold over time. For instance, the analytical framework from the Bradford studies has been applied to analyze decision-making processes surrounding potential mergers between university athletic associations. Questionnaire surveys cannot capture stakeholder behavioral patterns in such contexts. Similar studies have analyzed strategic failures in developing community sports (Morrison and Misener, 2022), examined the EU's role in global sport regulations (García and Meier, 2017), and conducted longitudinal analysis of strategic decision-making in professional clubs (Marthinus et al., 2025), revealing dynamic mechanisms of developing strategies. These case studies are highly suitable when newly proposed constructs lack valid measurement scales.A third area where case study research excels is in addressing research questions that require in-depth investigation of extreme phenomena. For example, research on elite sport systems has often neglected small countries, leading to an analysis of extreme contexts like Greenland (Storm et al., 2023). A longitudinal single-case study on the Essendon Football Club focused on doping scandals (Halabi and O'Connell, 2021), providing rare empirical insights into sport operations during crises. Kellison (2021) examined a stadium development project in suburban Atlanta, exploring public attention. Extreme competitive pressure's impact on athlete performance has been studied through analysis of the Norwegian ski jumping team during the Beijing 2022 Winter Olympics (Kristiansen et al., 2023), offering insights into athlete psychological support and organizational communication. Such studies feature small sample sizes and limited reproducibility, but in-depth case analysis fosters resonance and understanding of these issues.Theoretical contribution stands as a core prerequisite for high-quality case study research (Pratt, 2008). As a methodological approach centered on theory development, case study research within sport marketing generates substantive scholarly value across theory building, theory testing, and theory transformation. A primary theoretical contribution lies in inductive theory building, a function widely recognized as central to the methodological value of qualitative case inquiry (Colquitt and Zapata-Phelan, 2011). This pathway focuses on addressing critical theoretical gaps and constructing novel theoretical frameworks to explain phenomena that existing marketing and management theories fail to adequately interpret. For novel formats and emerging trends in the sport industry that existing theories cannot adequately explain, case study research can identify key variables, clarify pathways of influence, and inductively derive new constructs and relationships among them through exploratory analysis. Final research outputs are typically formalized as testable theoretical propositions or distinct new conceptual constructs (Fisher and Aguinis, 2017). For instance, Pegoraro (2010) conducted an exploratory case study on athletes' social media use on Twitter and proposed an initial classification framework for athletes' social media communication, representing one of the early foundational contributions to research on athlete social media. Schulenkorf (2012) developed the Sport for Development and Peace Project Framework based on a comparative case analysis of multiple sport-for-development projects in the Asia–Pacific region, providing testable theoretical propositions for subsequent research on sport for social development. More recently, Banucha and Bielawa (2025) conducted an in-depth case study of top Polish basketball clubs and proposed the new construct of “goal alignment,” which suggests that aligning athletes' individual goals with team goals can significantly improve commitment and performance.Beyond novel theory construction, case study research also advances sport marketing scholarship through rigorous theory testing and incremental extension of existing theoretical frameworks (Colquitt and Zapata-Phelan, 2011). In this capacity, researchers design case studies, collect empirical data, and conduct systematic analysis based on established theories or conceptual models that offer partial explanatory power for target phenomena. Core objectives include validating construct relationships, testing the applicability of classic management theories in sport settings, expanding the theoretical application boundaries of existing theories, refining theoretical dimensions to enhance contextual fit for sport marketing dynamics, and reducing cross-context theoretical incompatibility. For instance, Mason et al. (2006) conducted a case study on marketing and communications associated with corruption issues that emerged during the IOC's Olympic bidding process, introducing classic principal-agent theory into research on the marketing and operation of non-profit international sport organizations and extending the application boundary of this theory in sport. Batuev and Robinson (2017) performed a cross-case study on the evolution of freestyle sports including snowboarding, skateboarding, and rock climbing, and constructed an analytical framework based primarily on neo-institutional theory supplemented by resource dependence theory, providing important theoretical insights for understanding the promotion and institutionalization of emerging sports. Miller (2026) used LeBron James's “The Decision” event as a teaching case to guide students in applying stakeholder theory to analyze how this decision would affect James's brand value, revealing the applicability of stakeholder theory in the context of athlete personal brand management.A final key contribution of case study research in sport marketing is embodied in theory transformation, which centers on bridging the translational gap between abstract theory and industry practice. Unlike research focused purely on theoretical construction, theoretical propositions derived from case study research originate from real-world sport marketing and operational contexts. This not only provides targeted guidance for decision-making in operating sport enterprises but also makes theoretical outcomes more easily accepted by industry practice. This stream of research places less emphasis on pure theory building and more on providing actionable guidance from a decision-making, consultation and marketing implications perspective. For example, in the field of elite athlete development, Berg et al. (2018) conducted a historical case study of the 1968 US Olympic delegation, analyzed the development pathways of elite athletes in different historical periods, and offered decision-making references for sport organizations to formulate long-term athlete development and promotional strategies. In the field of sport policy implementation, MacPherson and Moss (2025) performed a case study on the development and implementation of the Safe Sport Framework by Gymnastics Canada, translating the latest research findings into actionable practices and providing a referential model for sport managers. Baker and Gardner (2026) examined the complex decision-making dilemmas faced by USA Fencing in developing its national competition schedules and revealed how sport event managers should balance the demands of different stakeholders under such circumstances.From the perspective of theoretical contributions, whether providing new perspectives (Graebner, 2009), filling research gaps (Smith, 2014), resolving contradictions (Haveman, 1992), extending theoretical boundaries (Backhouse et al., 2006), or gaining new insights (Siggelkow, 2007), the case study's potential has been widely recognized. Of course, theoretical contributions require specific methods and strategies in case study design, including literature review, problem formulation, research design, theoretical sampling, data collection, data analysis, and writing (Eisenhardt, 2016).At present, no unified universal standard governs the design framework for case study research, though the process can generally be structured into a coherent, sequential workflow encompassing four core phases. The initial phase centers on problem formulation and data collection, during which researchers adopt an open, exploratory stance free from pre-determined assumptions. Facts and contextual details are gathered via a diverse range of data sources, including documents, archives, interviews, and observations. The entire process should be documented. Interviewing is the most common data collection method; questions should be open-ended to help generate research questions. This stage addresses how the case proceeds and why.Following the initial data collection phase, the research proceeds to a stage focused on analytical breakthroughs and rigorous data interpretation. Researchers systematically collect and analyze multi-source data to identify key elements. Data collection is saturated when further data gathering fails to yield new insights, analytical dimensions, or interpretive value. Core components of this analytical phase include narrative development, construct measurement, pattern identification, theory construction, and dialogue with existing literature. Recently, methods including econometric approaches, machine learning, and generative AI have been integrated into case study research. Regardless of method, high-quality case study research conveys value through vivid stories while forming a closed chain of evidence, building theory through iteration and comparison with existing literature.The subsequent phase focuses on the formal drafting and composition of the research manuscript, a step that directly reflects the researcher's scientific rigor and academic writing proficiency. Data findings and theoretical frameworks should be presented clearly through well-designed figures and tables, with logical conceptual models strongly recommended to visualize and clarify relationships between key theoretical constructs. When articulating core research propositions, researchers must conduct sufficient dialogue with existing literature to highlight theoretical contributions.The final phase of the process involves research verification and refinement. In this stage, researchers seek targeted feedback and solicit revision suggestions from key case informants and strengthen the discussion section to emphasize practical value. It is worth noting that alternative stage classifications exist within scholarly literature; for instance, some scholars have condensed the case study process into three overarching phases, including preparation, implementation, and dialogue (Chen and Liu, 2010). Researchers should develop comprehensive proposals including title, background, questions, theoretical foundation, design, methods, and references, particularly justifying case selection.To further elaborate the content of case study research, this study selects illustrative cases for concise analysis based on the combination of typicality and appropriateness. Anderski et al. (2024) analyzed athlete brands' role in international marketing of professional sports leagues, using the German Bundesliga's Asian market practices as a single case study. The research addressed two questions: athlete brands' current role and potential in international marketing, and their co-creation pathways toward league brand meaning. For case selection, the Bundesliga's uniqueness in terms of fan majority ownership, emphasis on local youth development, and high stadium attendance distinguishes its brand ecosystem, making it ideal for exploring athlete brands' role in marketing. The league's active expansion into the Asian market adds contextual relevance.The study employed semi-structured interviews with 17 stakeholders including Bundesliga executives, club managers, and broadcast media executives in Asian markets. Inductive thematic analysis across six phases integrated 40 codes through open coding. Following triangulation, theme refinement, and peer review, four themes emerged: athlete brands' international marketing role, league brand meaning co-creation pathways, brand co-creation prerequisites, and influence mechanisms within the brand ecosystem. Theoretically, it supplements understanding of athlete brands within sports brand ecosystems and refines co-creation pathways in cross-cultural contexts; methodologically, it demonstrates standardized application of interviews and thematic analysis; practically, it highlights risks of athlete brands in international marketing.Complementing this, our research team developed a case study protocol on the ATP Shanghai Masters 1000's localized development strategy in China. This longitudinal single case enables tracing the event's evolutionary trajectory over time. Following Yin's (2016) framework, we defined core parameters including research tasks, target groups, questions, and interviewee information. Supporting materials included checklist-based data collection, literature synthesis, and jointly refined interview questions, laying a foundation for subsequent data collection and analysis.As the lead article of this special issue on case-based research in sport marketing, this paper offers a foundational overview of case study methodology for diverse readers. Drawing on scholarly works and representative studies, it presents core definitions and classifications, reveals theoretical value and applicable characteristics, and provides intuitive explanations through typical examples.Admittedly, this paper lacks the detail required for a comprehensive step-by-step guide to rigorous case study research. Nevertheless, we hope this special issue stimulates innovative engagement with case study research in sport marketing and encourages more researchers to adopt this approach.We strongly encourage developing high-quality teaching cases tailored to sport marketing. The Harvard Case Library features rigorous sport-related cases, including Paris Saint-Germain: Building One of the World's Top Sports Brands, FC Barcelona: More Than a Club, More Than Data, Decathlon: Making Sports Accessible to All, Fantasy Sports: A Game of Skill or Chance, John Hancock Sports Sponsorship: 1993–2000 and Beyond, and Negotiating with Data: Analytics FC. These benchmark cases would greatly benefit sport marketing education and business decision-making.",2026,Zhang,International Journal of Sports Marketing and Sponsorship,10.1108/ijsms-05-2026-589,,https://doi.org/10.1108/ijsms-05-2026-589,en,openalex:editorial,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1281,Shared and Distinct Neural Signatures of Cue-Induced Response in Substance and Behavioral Addictions: A Coordinate-Based Neuroimaging Meta-Analysis,"Abstract As the global burden of addiction intensifies, the neurobiological commonalities and distinctions between substance use disorders (SUDs) and behavioral addictions (BAs) remain poorly characterized. This coordinate-based meta-analysis of 59 fMRI articles (n = 2,951) mapped the neural signatures of visual cue-reactivity across the addictive disorders. Our results revealed a universal core network shared by SUDs and BAs centered in the bilateral opercular inferior frontal gyrus, suggesting a shared disruption in inhibitory control. Distinctively, SUDs exhibited a stronger recruitment of a subcortical salience pathway, with greater involvement of the left thalamus ventral anterior nucleus than BAs, potentially reflecting pharmacologically amplified bottom-up salience attribution. Notably, recovery-related patterns diverged in the left medial superior frontal gyrus. Alcohol use disorder was associated with neural restoration, whereas heroin use disorder showed neural decompensation. These neural signatures establish a rigorous neurobiological basis for differentiating substance and behavioral phenotypes, supporting tailored circuit-based precision treatments.",2026,Zheng,medRxiv,10.64898/2026.01.23.26344591,,https://doi.org/10.64898/2026.01.23.26344591,,citations:preprint,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1282,Community Perspectives of MA Higher Education Students on their Exposure to and Participation in Sports Betting through Paid Media Advertising,"The introduction of legal sports betting to Massachusetts is anticipated to impose social, health, and educational impacts on higher education students (aged 18-25) in Massachusetts, particularly regarding their exposure to and participation in sports betting through paid media advertising (Vander Linden et al., 2022). Funded by the Massachusetts Gaming Commission, we used a community-engaged research approach to bring together a diverse, engaged student group from various higher education institutions in Massachusetts to form a community research team (CRT), who took the lead in exploring relevant issues in a multi-year research project. The specific research questions were developed by the CRT upon participating in a series of workshops with industry and community stakeholders. The CRT utilized journey mapping techniques, informed by a set of innovative methodologies that involve reflection journals, in-depth interviews, and auto-ethnography during primary sports events conducted by community members. This multi-method approach allow the investigators and the CRT to develop understanding about how college students experience, and are impacted by, paid sports betting advertising in Massachusetts, build community awareness about the practice and its impact, and inform community-minded interventions designed to encourage positive play, and reduce problematic sports betting behaviors.",2026,Zhu,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1284,Compressed Beliefs,"Subjective beliefs are central to economic inference, and incentive-compatible belief elicitation mechanisms are widely assumed to identify these latent objects. This paper shows that elicited belief reports causally depend on an uninformative cognitive default induced by the elicitation design. From the lab to sports betting to official inflation expectations, reported beliefs are highly malleable, even under theoretically and behaviorally compatible incentives. I propose experimentally varying the cognitive default during belief elicitation. This exogenous variation allows the construction of inferred beliefs that are stable across elicitation designs and empirically outperform incentivized reports in predicting realized outcomes and participants’ own behavior.",2026,Zihlmann,Working Papers SES,10.51363/unifr.ewp.fgx26v,,https://doi.org/10.51363/unifr.ewp.fgx26v,und,openaire:Report | openalex:report | s2:CaseReport,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1285,You Bet?! - A cross-cultural content analysis of sports betting advertising during a crisis,"This thesis examines how sports betting operators adapted their advertising communication during the disruption of live sport caused by the COVID-19 pandemic. It investigates whether communication strategies, thematic content, and emotional appeals differed across selected national contexts: the United States, Ireland, and the United Kingdom. Drawing on Hall’s high- and low-context communication theory and Taylor’s Six-Segment Message Strategy Wheel, the study applies a qualitative cross-cultural content analysis to 15 sports betting advertisements broadcast or distributed during the pandemic period. The advertisements were analysed using a deductive coding framework covering explicit informational appeals, implicit and relational appeals, emotional strategies, and regulatory elements, while allowing crisis-specific themes to emerge inductively. The findings indicate distinct patterns within the selected sample. Advertisements from the United States predominantly relied on explicit financial incentives, technological convenience, and efficiency-oriented messaging. Irish advertisements more frequently employed shared social references, humour, and ironic representations of lockdown experiences. UK advertisements combined emotional and socially contextualised messaging during the sports shutdown with more transactional appeals as live sport returned. These patterns suggest that crisis advertising in sports betting was not uniform across the examined markets, but reflected the interaction of communicative context, market conditions, and the changing availability of sport. The study contributes to research on cross-cultural advertising and gambling marketing by applying established communication frameworks to a crisis setting. Given the small purposive sample and focus on broadcast-style video advertising, the findings are analytically informative rather than statistically generalisable.",2026,Zimmerberger,University Library Linz repository (Johannes Kepler Universitat Linz),,,,en,openalex:dissertation,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1286,"GAMBLING INVOLVEMENT, HOPELESSNESS, AND SUICIDE-RELATED INDICATORS IN TURKİYE: A CROSS-SECTIONAL ONLINE SURVEY","ABSTRACT Objective: We aimed to estimate gambling participation and positive screening for probable gambling disorder and to examine their associations with hopelessness and suicide-related outcomes in a Turkish online sample. Methods: In this cross-sectional web-based survey, 1,057 adults completed the South Oaks Gambling Screen (SOGS), Beck Hopelessness Scale (BHS), and Suicide Probability Scale (SPS). Group differences were examined using non-parametric tests, followed by multivariable regression analyses. Results: Gambling participation was reported by 30.7% (95% CI 28.0–33.6) of participants; 4.9% (95% CI 3.8–6.4) screened positive for probable gambling disorder using the Turkish SOGS cut-off of ≥8. Participants reporting gambling had higher BHS and SPS scores, and differences were more pronounced among those screening positive for probable gambling disorder. The between-group difference in SPS scores among participants reporting gambling corresponded to a moderate-to-large effect (r=0.497, 95% CI 0.343–0.653). After covariate adjustment, gambling participation was associated with a 1.46-point higher BHS (95% CI 0.79–2.13) and 5.26-point higher SPS scores (95% CI 3.15–7.36). Among participants reporting gambling, positive screening was associated with 2.60-point higher BHS (95% CI 0.88–4.32) and 12.59-point higher SPS scores (95% CI 7.21–17.96). Gambling participation was also associated with histories of suicidal ideation (OR=1.71, 95% CI 1.17–2.50) and suicide attempt (OR=2.54, 95% CI 1.32–4.88). Conclusions: Gambling involvement, particularly positive SOGS screening, was associated with greater hopelessness and suicide-related symptom burden. The cross-sectional design and non-probability sampling preclude causal interpretation and population-level prevalence estimates.",2026,Özkoç,Kocaeli Üniversitesi Sağlık Bilimleri Dergisi,10.30934/kusbed.2013046,,https://doi.org/10.30934/kusbed.2013046,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1287,Gambling in Sports: Validity and Reliability Study of the Gambling Passion Scale,"Bu araştırmanın amacı, Rousseau ve arkadaşları (2002) tarafından geliştirilen “Bahis Tutkusu Ölçeği”nin Türk kültürüne uyarlanması, geçerlik ve güvenirlik analizlerinin gerçekleştirilmesidir. Araştırma, nicel araştırma desenlerinden tarama modeliyle yürütülmüştür. Uyarlama süreci literatürde önerilen beş aşamalı çeviri modeline göre gerçekleştirilmiştir: hedef dile çeviri, geri çeviri, uzman görüşü alınması, pilot uygulama ve geçerlik–güvenirlik analizleri. Araştırma grubu, 18–35 yaş aralığında yer alan ve aktif olarak spor bahsi oynayan toplam 344 katılımcıdan oluşmuştur (AFA=166; DFA=178). Elde edilen verilere ilişkin Açımlayıcı Faktör Analizi (AFA) sonucunda ölçeğin iki faktörlü (Obsesif Tutku ve Uyumlu Tutku) bir yapıya sahip olduğu ve toplam varyansın %63.9’unu açıkladığı belirlenmiştir (KMO=0.92; Bartlett χ²=801.495; p<0.001). Faktör yükleri 0.53 ile 0.92 arasında, Cronbach Alfa katsayıları 0.85–0.90, Omega güvenirlik katsayısı ise 0.90 olarak tespit edilmiştir. Doğrulayıcı Faktör Analizi (DFA) sonuçlarına göre uyum iyiliği indeksleri kabul edilebilir düzeydedir (χ²/sd=1.76; RMSEA=0.066; CFI=0.981; SRMR=0.292). Ayrıca yapı geçerliğini destekleyen CR (0.87–0.93) ve AVE (0.58–0.73) değerleri, ölçeğin birleşim ve ayrışım geçerliğini doğrulamaktadır. Bulgular, Türkçe Bahis Tutkusu Ölçeği’nin, bireylerin bahis tutkusu düzeylerini değerlendirmede geçerli ve güvenilir bir ölçme aracı olduğunu göstermektedir.",2026,Özsoy,DergiPark (Istanbul University),,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1288,Gambling Involvement and Problem Gambling: Finding from a General Population Survey,"Certain gambling formats, such as slot machines, have consistently been related to problem gambling. Extensive involvement in multiple gambling activities may also represent an important risk factor. This study explored the relationship between gambling involvement, gambling formats, and problem gambling in a subsample of 817 participants who reported engaging in gambling in the past month, drawn from a representative sample of Croatian residents aged 15-64 (N=4,988), Gambling involvement was assessed by the number of gambling formats and frequency of gambling in the past month, while problem gambling was assessed using the Problem Gambling Severity Index. Most participants (37.3%) engaged in one, 26.8% in two, 21.4% in three, and 14.5% in four or more gambling formats. Overall, 67.4% of participants were classified as non-problem, 10.1% as low-risk, 14.0% as moderate-risk, and 8.5% as participants with problem gambling. High gambling involvement was related to problem gambling, and frequent sports betting, casino games, and slot machines use. Participants with problem gambling most commonly engaged in four or more gambling formats. Findings suggest problem gambling is related not only to specific gambling formats but also to patterns of extensive and frequent involvement in multiple gambling activities, highlighting the importance of monitoring overall gambling behavior rather than focusing on specific games to identify high-risk individuals and inform prevention efforts.",2026,Škovrlj,Digital Scholarship - UNLV (University of Nevada Reno),,,,,openalex:conference-paper,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R0673,,,
R1289,"«Έπειτα γήπεδο, στοιχήματα, καυγάς» : δράσεις και αντιδράσεις κοινωνίας και πολιτικών φορέων στην ίδρυση του ΟΠΑΠ και την κυκλοφορία του ΠΡΟΠΟ (1958–1959)","This thesis examines the social and political framework surrounding the establishment of the Greek Organization of Football Prognostics (OPAP) and the introduction of the football betting game PROPO in Greece, focusing on the sociopolitical reactions provoked by the institutionalization of a state-controlled game of chance during the period 1958–1959. The main objective of the study is to investigate how Greek society, political parties, and institutional actors perceived and interpreted a new phenomenon that combined sports, entertainment, and economic risk. Through the analysis of primary archival material, the research seeks to reveal the contradictions, moral concerns, and political motivations that accompanied the emergence of the institution. The study shows that early public reactions were deeply ambivalent: part of society welcomed PROPO as a means of economic hope and as a mechanism for supporting Greek athletics, while others expressed fears of moral corruption and social decay, particularly among the youth. These perceptions were reflected in the press of the time, with characteristic examples including the Eikones magazine poll and the articles of Eleftheria and Avgi newspapers. At the same time, institutional bodies such as the Supreme Confederation of Parents’ Associations of Greek Pupils and the Panhellenic Union of National Lottery Agents highlighted distinct aspects of the issue—from moral anxiety about youth behavior to the economic interests of professional groups. Secondly, The public debate culminated in Parliament in April 1959, when members of the United Democratic Left (EDA) strongly criticized PROPO, denouncing it as a form of “state gambling” that undermined social ethics, while the government defended it as an instrument of modernization and athletic development. By juxtaposing these conflicting discourses, the study reveals the complexity of a phenomenon that transcended the boundaries of sports and leisure, reflecting broader social and ideological tensions in post–Civil War Greece. Η παρούσα εργασία εξετάζει το κοινωνικό και πολιτικό πλαίσιο ίδρυσης του Οργανισμού Προγνωστικών Αγώνων Ποδοσφαίρου (ΟΠΑΠ) και την κυκλοφορία του ΠΡΟΠΟ στην Ελλάδα, εστιάζοντας στις κοινωνικοπολιτικές αντιδράσεις που προκάλεσε η θεσμοθέτηση ενός κρατικά ελεγχόμενου τυχερού παιχνιδιού κατά την περίοδο 1958-1959. Στόχος της μελέτης είναι η διερεύνηση του τρόπου με τον οποίο η ελληνική κοινωνία, τα πολιτικά κόμματα και οι θεσμικοί φορείς προσέλαβαν και ερμήνευσαν ένα νέο φαινόμενο, που συνδύαζε τον αθλητισμό, τη διασκέδαση και το οικονομικό ρίσκο. Μέσα από την ανάλυση πρωτογενούς αρχειακού υλικού. Η εργασία φιλοδοξεί να αναδείξει τις αντιφάσεις, τις ηθικές ανησυχίες και τις πολιτικές στοχεύσεις που συνόδευσαν τη γέννηση του θεσμού. Ενώ παράλληλα, η μελέτη αποκαλύπτει, πως οι πρώτες αντιδράσεις της κοινής γνώμης ήταν αμφίθυμες: ένα τμήμα της κοινωνίας υποδέχθηκε το ΠΡΟΠΟ ως μέσο οικονομικής ελπίδας και ενίσχυσης του ελληνικού αθλητισμού, ενώ ένα άλλο εξέφρασε φόβους για ηθική διαφθορά και κοινωνικό εκφυλισμό, ιδίως μεταξύ των νέων. Οι απόψεις αυτές εκφράστηκαν μέσα από τον Τύπο της εποχής, με χαρακτηριστικά παραδείγματα το δημοψήφισμα του περιοδικού Εικόνες και τα άρθρα των εφημερίδων Ελευθερία και Αυγή. Ταυτόχρονα, θεσμικοί φορείς όπως η Ανώτατη Συνομοσπονδία Συλλόγων Γονέων Μαθητών Ελλάδος και η Πανελλήνια Ένωση Πρακτόρων Εθνικού Λαχείου ανέδειξαν διαφορετικές διαστάσεις του ζητήματος, από την ηθική αγωνία για τη νεολαία έως τα οικονομικά συμφέροντα επαγγελματικών κλάδων. Η αντιπαράθεση κορυφώθηκε στη Βουλή τον Απρίλιο του 1959, όπου η ΕΔΑ άσκησε έντονη κριτική για τον «κρατικό τζόγο», ενώ η κυβέρνηση υπερασπίστηκε το ΠΡΟΠΟ ως μέσο κοινωνικής και αθλητικής προόδου. Με τα σχετικά συμπεράσματα να αποτελούν το επιστέγασμα της μελέτης.",2026,Θεοδωρόπουλος,Πάντειον Πανεπιστήμιο Κοινωνικών και Πολιτικών Επιστημών,10.26250/heal.panteion.9602,,https://doi.org/10.26250/heal.panteion.9602,el,datacite:Other/master thesis | openaire:Master thesis,datacite;openaire,2,False,True,False,,,,,
R1290,Η ΧΡΗΣΗ ΤΗΣ ΤΕΧΝΗΤΗΣ ΝΟΗΜΟΣΥΝΗΣ ΣΤΑ ΜΕΓΑΛΑ ΑΘΛΗΤΙΚΑ ΓΕΓΟΝΟΤΑ. Η ΠΕΡΙΠΤΩΣΗ ΤΟΥ FIFA WORLD CUP 2025.,"Αντικείμενο της παρούσας διπλωματικής εργασίας αποτελεί η διερεύνηση του μετασχηματιστικού ρόλου της Τεχνητής Νοημοσύνης (ΤΝ) στη διαχείριση και την κατανάλωση περιεχομένου μεγάλων αθλητικών διοργανώσεων (Mega Events). Μέσα από τη μελέτη περίπτωσης (case study) του FIFA World Cup 2025, αναλύεται ο τρόπος με τον οποίο οι αναδυόμενες τεχνολογίες αναδιαμορφώνουν την εμπειρία των φιλάθλων και τη λειτουργία των αθλητικών οργανισμών. Η εργασία εξετάζει την εφαρμογή καινοτόμων συστημάτων, όπως το ημι-αυτοματοποιημένο οφσάιντ (SAOT 2.0), η τεχνολογία διασυνδεδεμένης μπάλας (Connected Ball Technology) και η χρήση των Smart Stadiums μέσω του Internet of Things (IoT). Τα ευρήματα καταδεικνύουν μια θεαματική τεχνολογική μετάβαση από το Παγκόσμιο Κύπελλο του 2022 στην εποχή της «Προληπτικής ΤΝ» του 2025, όπου η υπολογιστική ισχύς (Edge Computing) και η υπερ-υψηλή ταχύτητα επεξεργασίας δεδομένων μειώνουν δραστικά τον χρόνο λήψης αποφάσεων. Ιδιαίτερη έμφαση δίνεται στην εξατομίκευση της εμπειρίας του θεατή μέσω των AI-Generated Highlights και των FAST Channels, καθώς και στην παιχνιδοποίηση (gamification) της κατανάλωσης μέσω του Real-Time Fantasy Sports. Παράλληλα, η έρευνα προσεγγίζει κριτικά τις ηθικές προκλήσεις που προκύπτουν, όπως η ψηφιακή επιτήρηση και η προστασία των βιομετρικών δεδομένων των αθλητών. Συμπερασματικά, η εργασία αναδεικνύει ότι το FIFA World Cup 2025 αποτελεί το σημείο καμπής για τη μετάβαση σε ένα «Phygital» αθλητικό μοντέλο. Η Τεχνητή Νοημοσύνη δεν λειτουργεί πλέον ως επικουρικό εργαλείο, αλλά ως το θεμελιώδες λειτουργικό σύστημα που διασφαλίζει τη βιωσιμότητα, την ασφάλεια και την εμπορική επιτυχία του παγκόσμιου ποδοσφαίρου στην ψηφιακή εποχή.",2026,Παλιούρα,University of Peloponnese,10.26263/amitos-3217,,https://doi.org/10.26263/amitos-3217,el,datacite:Other/Μεταπτυχιακή διπλωματική εργασία | openalex:other,openalex;datacite,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R1291,"Σχεδίαση και ανάπτυξη προσωποποιημένης διαλογικής διεπαφής για εφαρμογή ""Fantasy football""","Fantasy Premier League (FPL) is one of the world’s most popular online fantasy sports games, in which millions of managers worldwide are required to make a series of critical decisions regarding their virtual teams every gameweek, in order to accumulate as many points as possible from the real-world performances of the footballers they select. However, the official platform primarily provides raw statistical information and limited decision-support capabilities, while existing third-party applications are mainly targeted at experienced users and frequently organise information through dense tables and long lists of metrics. Managers are therefore forced to switch between multiple websites and tools to analyse players, compare alternatives, and manage their squads, frequently through subscription-based services. They are left with a fragmented and demanding environment that imposes a considerable cognitive load and requires substantial time to learn, and that becomes difficult to use for those without much experience. This thesis presents the design, development, and evaluation of a complete full-stack Fantasy Football web application with a personalized conversational interface (AI chatbot). The central contribution is twofold: first, it implements the game itself and comprehensively redesigns the overall user experience (UI/UX) compared with both the official Fantasy Premier League application and existing third-party platforms, bringing together team management, data analysis, player-performance predictions, and decisionsupport functionalities within a single coherent and user-friendly environment. Second, it integrates a conversational Artificial Intelligence coaching assistant that personalises its recommendations according to each manager’s preferences, playing style, and current squad status, providing tailored explanations and decision support through natural language interaction. Underneath runs a machine-learning pipeline that forecasts points, minutes played and likelihood of appearance for the next gameweek, using gradient-boosted tree models with rolling-origin cross-validation and an acceptance check that retains the previous model whenever a retrained one performs worse. The resulting predictions are utilised by a hybrid recommendation engine that combines machine-learning techniques with heuristic rules, as well as by an integer linear programming optimiser for constructing the initial squad. A Large Language Model layer then turns these structured numerical recommendations into natural, explanatory dialogue. The platform is supported by a complete backend, a relational database, an automated data-synchronisation mechanism, a job-scheduling system, and user authentication. The implementation employs the Next.js and Express frameworks with TypeScript, Python for the machinelearning pipeline, and PostgreSQL for data management. The system was evaluated with respect to the accuracy of its predictive models, the quality of its recommendations, and the usability, clarity, and overall user experience delivered by the application.",2026,Τουβλέλιος,Νημερτής,,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1292,المكاسب المالية عبر وسائل التواصل المعاصرة (دراسة فقهية مقارنة) Financial Gains Through Modern Communication Media (A Comparative Jurisprudential Study),"تهدف الدراسة من هذا البحث إلى بيان المعاملات المالية التي انتشرت على وسائل التواصل المعاصرة ومن هذه المعاملات المالية التجارة الإلكترونية، التسويق الشبكي، المراهنات الإلكترونية منصات عرض المحتوى(Tik Tok) – فأردت أن أبين من الناحية الفقهية مدى شرعية هذه المعاملات المالية والمكاسب الناتجة عنها حتى يتبين ما هو مباح وندع منها ما خالف قواعد وأحكام الشريعة الغراء، ولقد نهجت في كتابة هذا البحث المنهج العلمي الاستقرائي، وذلك بتتبع المعلومات المتعلقة بمادة موضوع البحث من مظانها كلما اعتمدت على المنهج المقارن محاولة جمع آراء العلماء، والفقهاء، وجعلت الآراء المتفقة في قول واحد يجمع بين أصحابه وحدة الرأي في المسألة الواحدة كما قمت بعزو الآيات إلى سورها ،وكذلك قمت بتخريج الأحاديث وفق المنهج العلمي في التخريج من الصحيحين فإن لم يكن في الصحيحين بينت درجة الحديث والحكم عليه، كما ختمت هذا البحث بخاتمة ضمنتها أهم النتائج التي توصلت إليها في هذا البحث، وأهم نتائج البحث: المكاسب المادية الناتجة عن التجارة الإلكترونية إذا كانت الصفة مكتملة شروط الصحة وخالية من الموانع الشرعية فهي مباحة ومكاسبها المادية مباحة أما إذا كانت تحتوى على الغرر والجهالة فهي محرمة شرعاً وما نتج عنها من مكاسب مادية فهو محرم، التسويق الشبكي حرام شرعاً وما نتج عنه من مكاسب مالية فهي حرام، المراهنات الإلكترونية التي تعتمد على دفع أموال للفوز بمال، أو جوائز يعتبر من القمار المحرم شرعاً وما نتج عنه من مكاسب مالية فهو حرام – أما المراهنات الإلكترونية إذا كانت في مجال الرياضة وكانت الجائزة مقررة من ولى الأمر ولم يشترط فيها دفع مال فهي جائزة وما نتج عنها من أرباح مالية فهي حلال. This research aims to examine the financial transactions that have become prevalent on modern social media platforms. These transactions include e-commerce, network marketing, online betting, and content-sharing platforms (such as TikTok). The research seeks to determine the permissibility of these transactions and the resulting profits from a jurisprudential perspective, clarifying what is permissible and rejecting what violates the principles and rulings of Islamic sharia. The research employs an inductive scientific methodology, tracing information related to the research topic from its primary sources. Whenever the research relies on a comparative approach, it attempts to gather the opinions of scholars and jurists, presenting those who agree on a single issue. The research also cites Quranic verses to their respective chapters and authenticates hadiths according to the established methodology of hadith authentication, relying on the two Sahih collections (Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim). If a hadith is not found in these two collections, its authenticity and ruling are indicated. The research concludes with a summary of the most important findings. The most significant finding is the financial gains resulting from e-commerce. If a transaction fulfills all the conditions of validity and is free from any legal impediments, it is permissible, and its financial gains are permissible. However, if it involves uncertainty and ambiguity, it is forbidden according to Islamic sharia , and any resulting financial gains are also forbidden. Network marketing is forbidden according to Islamic sharia, and any financial gains resulting from it are also forbidden. Electronic betting, which involves paying money to win money or prizes, is considered gambling, which is forbidden according to Islamic sharia , and any resulting financial gains are forbidden. However, if electronic betting is in the field of sports and the prize is determined by the governing authority and does not require payment, then it is permissible, and any resulting financial profits are lawful.",2026,أحمد,مجلة کلیة الشریعة والقانون بأسیوط دوریة علمیة محکمة,10.21608/jfsu.2026.467017.1347,,https://doi.org/10.21608/jfsu.2026.467017.1347,ar,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1293,"المراهنات الإلكترونية "" دراسة فقهية"". Online Betting: A Jurisprudential Study",,2026,حسين,مجلة کلیة الشریعة والقانون بأسیوط دوریة علمیة محکمة,10.21608/jfsu.2026.506410.1368,,https://doi.org/10.21608/jfsu.2026.506410.1368,ar,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R1295,경륜(競輪) 고객의 심리 요인이 베팅 행동에 미치는 영향: 행동경제학 관점에서,,2026,김,한국체육학회지,10.23949/kjpe.2026.3.65.2.43,,https://doi.org/10.23949/kjpe.2026.3.65.2.43,en,citations:article,citations,1,True,False,False,cites Tran 2024,,,,
R1296,An Analysis of the Importance and Priority of Match Reference Factors in Football Sports Toto Decision-Making,"본 연구는 축구 스포츠토토 의사결정에서 경기 참고요인의 중요도와 우선순위를 분석하고, 참여자 특성에 따라 이러한 인식 구조가 어떻게 달라지는지를 확인하는 데 목적이 있다. 연구대상은 최근 1년 이내 축구 스포츠토토 구매 경험이 있는 성인 400명이었으며, 자료는 설문조사를 통해 수집하였다. 설문 문항은 축구 경기 결과 판단과 스포츠토토 의사 결정 과정에서 반복적으로 활용되는 경기 참고요인을 중심으로 구성하였다. 구체적으로 팀 시장가치, 팀 명성·인지도, 시즌 순위·승점, 최근 5경기 성적, 홈/원정 조건, 주전 선수의 부상 및 결장 정보, 일정 혼잡도·휴식일 차이, 최근 상대전적, 아시안핸디캡 개장정보, 유럽배당 개장정보 등 10개 요인의 중요도를 평정하도록 하였고, 실제 의사결정에서 우선적으로 고려하는 요인을 1~3순위로 선택하도록 하였다. 수집된 자료는 SPSS 통계프로그램을 활용하여 빈도분석, 기술통계 분석, Friedman 검정, 우선순위 분석 및 주요 하위집단 비교분석을 실시하였다. 분석 결과, 중요도 평균은 주전 선수 부상·결장 정보가 가장 높았고, 최근 5경기 성적, 일정 혼잡도·휴식일 차이, 아시안핸디캡 개장정보, 유럽배당 개장정보순으로 나타났다. 우선순위 분석에서는 최근 5경기 성적, 주전 선수 부상·결장 정보, 일정 혼잡도·휴식일 차이가 핵심 판단요인으로 확인되었다. 또한 구매경력과 배당 이해도가 높은 고경험·고배당이해 집단은 최근 경기 성적, 일정 관련 정보 및 배당정보를 상대적으로 더 중시하는 경향이 나타난 반면, 저경험·저배당이해 집단은 팀 시장가치와 팀 명성·인지도를 상대적으로 더 중요하게 평가하는 경향이 나타났다. 이러한 결과는 축구 스포츠토토 참여자의 경기 참고요인 인식이 단일한 구조가 아니라 참여 경험과 시장정보 이해 수준에 따라 달라질 수 있음을 시사한다.",2026,스포츠산업경영,The Korea Journal of Sport,10.46669/kss.2026.24.2.005,,https://doi.org/10.46669/kss.2026.24.2.005,,openalex_non_en:article,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1297,한국 스포츠토토 시장의 종목별 비교를 중심으로,"학위논문(석사) -- 서울대학교 대학원 : 사범대학 체육교육과(글로벌스포츠매니지먼트전공), 2026. 2. 김기한.",2026,양민성,Seoul National University Open Repository (Seoul National University),,,,ko,openalex_non_en:dissertation,openalex_non_en,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R1300,Chapter La prima vicenda del calcioscommesse in Italia (1980),"The football betting scandal that broke out in the first months of 1980, although it was the one that left the most unpleasant memories in the collective memory, especially due to the notoriety of the teams and players involved, was not the first scandal of its kind in our football. Between the fascist period, when football really began to be a mass phenomenon, and the eighties there were a series of “fixed” matches that dirty the image of our best-known sport, starting with the scandal involving the Juventus and national team full-back Luigi Allemandi in the 1926-1927 season. The paper analyzes this long period, marked by the events that between the fifties and seventies involved important clubs such as Milan, Udinese, Atalanta, Genoa and Foggia, and then faced the scandal of clandestine betting at the origin of the first major sporting process in our history.",2025,,OAPEN (The OAPEN Foundation),,,,it,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
