R1305,PROF. K C DUBE POSTER AWARD,"Betting on Neuromodulation: Efficacy of Adjunctive Theta HD-tACS in Gambling Disorder Aaditya S., Sourav Khanra, Premaja Bhatt Joshi, Anshul Yadav, Justin Raj PC, Sanjay Kumar Munda. Central Institute of Psychiatry, Ranchi. Introduction: Recent years have witnessed an unprecedented growth in commercial gambling, fueled by ready online access. The relapsing nature of gambling disorder, with poor retention on current management protocols, requires novel treatment modalities to be explored. Aim: To investigate the efficacy of neuromodulation in the form of theta high-definition transcranial alternating current stimulation (HD-tACS) as an adjunctive intervention for patients with gambling disorder. Methodology: Three inpatients from a tertiary care hospital - Mr. X, a 38-year old male with a ten-year history of online sports betting, Mr. Y, a 31-year old who had incurred a loss of eight lakhs on offline card gambling and Mr. Z, a 24-year old with a history of online casino gambling - were included in the case series. Appropriate clinical scales were applied to assess gambling related cognitions, severity and craving, along with any comorbid anxiety and depression. This was followed by Stroop Task, Iowa Gambling Task, qEEG and resting state fMRI. 10 sessions of 6Hz theta HD-tACS of 20 minutes each were then administered to the left DLPFC over 5 days, after which assessments and imaging were repeated. Results: All subjects showed improvements in gambling related cognitions, severity and craving. They also displayed reduced risk-taking behaviour on Iowa Gambling Task, and reduced anxiety and depressive symptoms. qEEG and resting state fMRI findings will be reported at the time of presentation. Discussion: Prefrontal theta asymmetry between the right and left cortices has been hypothesised to underlie increased risk-taking behaviour. Exogenous theta HD-tACS to the left DLPFC leading to entrainment of theta oscillations, thereby reducing interhemispheric asymmetry, could increase cognitive control and risk aversiveness in gambling. Conclusion: The successful application of theta HD-tACS represents a possible breakthrough in our quest to treat gambling disorder, and warrants urgent further research to generalise these findings. Keywords: Gambling, Neuromodulation, tACS, DLPFC. Capturing Real-Time Moments: Exploring Ecological Momentary Assessment for Alcohol Dependence in India Ankita Chattopadhyay, Shalini Singh, Rakesh Lal Jawaharlal Institute of Postgraduate Medical Education and Research (JIPMER), Puducherry, India, National Drug Dependence Treatment Centre (NDDTC), All India Institute of Medical Sciences (AIIMS), New Delhi, India Aims/Objectives: Ecological momentary assessment (EMA) is emerging as a valuable method for real-time behavioural monitoring in substance-using population in natural settings. However, most EMA research has been conducted in developed countries and predominantly among non-dependent or non-treatment-seeking individuals, often with varied protocols and heterogeneous samples. This study aims to explore feasibility of EMA in treatment-seeking alcohol-dependent patients in India. Methods: A total of 102 males diagnosed with alcohol-dependence were recruited during outpatient detoxification phase. Ethical approval was obtained, and participants provided written informed consent. Participants completed a socio-demographic-clinical proforma, alongwith assessment of alcohol use severity, affective states (depression, anxiety, psychological stress), and alcohol craving using standardized clinical scales. EMA prompts addressing mood (sadness), anxiety, stress, and craving were sent to participants’ phones thrice daily for one week. Additionally, participants were required to self-initiate a response once daily. Results: Overall response rate was 24.47%, comprising 0.08% for self-initiated responses and 32.35% for random prompts. Acceptance rate for participation was high at 91.07%, but retention rate was low (34.31%). Compliance assessed by tracking response frequency revealed decline in responses over study duration. Feedback from participants indicated that both the length and frequency of assessments were perceived as “just right.” Notably, no significant clinical differences emerged between responders and non-responders. Reliability analysis showed moderate reliability for mood and stress measures, but poor reliability for anxiety and craving. Discussion: This study is pioneering in assessing EMA feasibility among treatment-seeking alcohol dependent patients in India, with findings suggesting that EMA can be useful for monitoring drinking behavior and related parameters, but challenges in compliance must be addressed. Conclusion: EMA studies involving larger samples and strategies to enhance adherence, such as incentivization and personalized interventions should be conducted in India. Additionally, developing tailored digital applications for alcohol-dependent patients in India could improve efficacy of EMA methodologies. Navigating the Aftermath: The Impact of Patient’s Suicide on Mental Health Professionals Akansha Bharadwaj, Shipra Singh, Yogender Malik AIIMS, New Delhi Introduction: The impact of patient suicides on mental health professionals (MHPs) is profound, affecting their emotional well-being and profession. This study explores MHPs’ experiences with patient suicides and their adequacy of managing such incidents. Aim/Objectives: The aim of the study is to explore the effect of patient suicides on MHPs. Objectives include evaluating the perceived psychological and professional impacts, understanding the support MHPs need and receive, and assessing their views on training related to dealing with patient suicides. Methodology: A mixed-methods approach was employed, utilizing a semi-structured online survey, following Institutional Ethical approval. The questionnaire, validated by professionals, covered demographics, details of incidents, emotional and professional impacts and support systems. The survey was open for 8 weeks, and responses from 96 MHPs were analysed using descriptive statistics and thematic analysis to derive insights from both quantitative and qualitative data. Results: Among 96 MHPs surveyed, 51% had treated patients who died by suicide, with a majority being males aged 20-40 years, primarily diagnosed with psychotic or affective disorders. The emotional impact varied: 49% reported mild, 28% moderate effects, with sadness, regret, and guilt being common responses. Support-seeking behaviours were prevalent, with 52.2% of respondents finding support from colleagues, family, or professional communities helpful, but formal training on managing patient suicides was found lacking. Discussion: The impact of patient suicides on MHPs is multifaceted, affecting personal and professional aspects. A significant portion of MHPs experienced patient suicides after completing their training. Majority of suicides occurred outside controlled environments, highlighting need for stronger follow-up and community support. Conclusion: The emotional and professional consequences of patient suicides underscores importance of a supportive environment, integrating enhanced psychiatry training and fostering open dialogue. Addressing these needs can help mitigate adverse effects and promote resilience among MHPs, ultimately benefiting practitioners and quality of mental health services. A comparative study on Cognitive impairment among patients with chronic skin inflammatory disorder (Psoriasis and Vitiligo) Tarun Narang, Anupama Arora, Rahul Chakravarty, Aseem Mehra PGIMER, Chandigarh, Dr YS Parmar Government Medical College, Nahan Background: A limited number of studies have associated the prevalence of cognitive impairment among patient with chronic inflammatory skin disorders. Aim and objectives: To evaluate and compare the presence of cognitive impairment among patients with psoriasis and vitiligo. Methods: This was a cross-sectional study with a convenient sampling technique. A total of 200 patient with a diagnosis of Psoriasis and Vitiligo (100 each) were included. Participants were evaluated on the Hindi version of the Montreal Cognitive Assessment for cognitive impairment, Patient Health Questionnaire- 9 for depression, Generalized Anxiety Disorder-7 for anxiety disorder and Metabolic syndrome as per the consensus criteria given by World Health Organization. Results: A total of 200 patients were recruited. Those with Psoriasis were significantly older (p 10). Cardiovascular risk was significantly higher in inactive (6.36 ± 8.5, p =0 .015) group compared to HEPA active group (1.92 ± 4.0). There was no significant difference between HEPA and minimally active groups (p =0 .291). Age, but not duration of illness or antipsychotic exposure was positively associated with QRISK-3 score. Among antipsychotics, clozapine was significantly associated with increased BMI (p<0.001) but not with QRISK-3. Discussion: This aligns with previous research indicating that individuals with SMI, especially those on antipsychotics, are at increased cardiovascular risk due to adverse effects compounded with lifestyle factors such as physical inactivity and obesity.4 Conclusion: There is a need to emphasise lifestyle modifications and regular cardiovascular monitoring in women on antipsychotics. A patient presenting with acute vision loss having visual hallucinations: A case report Devang borisagarl, Vinod M. Darji, Bhaveshkumar M. Lakdawala, Vaishal D. Chokshi Narendra Modi Medical and Introduction: The in which visual with visual visual loss or visual loss is a syndrome The patient has or no control over the which are A old patient was to from as was has in right days, developed loss of vision in left reported and and a was was reported those to be more on and all was to higher mental were showed in left and and more in was on and was after patient was patient reported in of Pat