R6527,Substance-Related and Addictive Disorders,"Tomás Zambrano was a 36-year-old married first-generation Hispanic man who presented to the gambling clinic at a major medical center for evaluation and treatment. A football coach at a suburban high school, Mr. Zambrano had a 5-year-old son. He denied having any prior psychiatric or substance abuse history. He was appropriately dressed and groomed, expressed himself fluently in both English and Spanish, spoke in normal tone and volume, and on examination presented with intact cognitive function and average intelligence. Mr. Zambrano had been betting on sports and playing cards since childhood, noting in the evaluation that gambling “is part of our culture.” His own father’s favorite hobby and stress reliever had been to play poker with friends at night, and Mr. Zambrano remembered with affection the father-son moments they shared. Poker became for him a familial activity that he increasingly used to relieve work-related stress. He relished the excitement as well as the intellectual challenge and competition. Card games did not, however, remain a benign pastime for Mr. Zambrano. He had begun to lose more money than he could afford. Over the prior 2 years, he had gradually increased the frequency and stakes of his poker nights. When he lost, he placed even larger bets, convinced that the odds would favor him the next time. When he won, he felt great and would continue to play, convinced he was “on a streak.” Although losses made him feel unworthy, stupid, and irritable, he believed success would come if he could fine-tune his strategy. He felt a powerful, almost constant impulse to increase the tempo of his gambling and recover the money he had lost. When he tried to cut back on gambling, he felt irritable and preoccupied, and quickly returned to his poker nights. By the time Mr. Zambrano presented to the gambling clinic, he felt desperate. Nightly poker had led to daytime fatigue and poor performance at his coaching job, which he had previously loved. He was consumed with thoughts about his next poker game. His wife and son had long resented his time away from the family, but his wife had just found out that he had used up his son’s college fund plus accumulated $30,000 in credit card debt. When his wife threatened to file for divorce, he felt sad and depressed and decided to seek treatment.",2013,,American Psychiatric Association Publishing eBooks,10.1176/appi.books.9781585624836.jb16,,https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.books.9781585624836.jb16,en,openalex:book-chapter,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R6528,Table of Contents,"Abstract Cover Story Learn how to take proactive approach to prevent hazing tragedies Reduce hazing incidents with proactive approach Change the hazing culture Student‐Athlete Conduct Combat hazing by developing effective system The Two‐Minute Drill University fires AD for inappropriate relationships Higher incidences of depression found among student‐athletes Wrestling program nears end at Boston University NCAA won't sanction university for AD's sports bets Leaders & Innovators PEG BRADLEY‐DOPPES, VICE CHANCELLOR FOR ATHLETICS & RECREATION, UNIVERSITY OF DENVER Understand departmental legacies, traditions, culture Ensure student‐athletes achieve academic, athletic success Safety & Liability Improve safety of student‐athlete transportation Managing Your Office Retain talented staff with a sponsorship program Compliance Protect athletics from being tainted by sports wagering You Make The Call Did an NCAA bylaw violate anticompetitive statute? Lawsuits & Rulings RETALIATION Fired coach wins retaliation claim, but appeals court reverses DISCRIMINATION Deaf lifeguard gets chance to prove qualifications A Conversation With… KATHY TURPIN, SENIOR DIRECTOR OF SPORT DRUG TESTING THE NATIONAL CENTER FOR DRUG FREE SPORT, INC. Reduce drug testing mistakes, liability Resources/Ideas Defend sports Understand athletes Names in the News",2013,,College Athletics and the Law,10.1002/catl.20065,,https://doi.org/10.1002/catl.20065,en,openaire:Article | openalex:paratext,openalex;openaire,2,False,False,False,,R7555,,,
