R7575,A cross-sectional study of problem and pathological gambling in patients with schizophrenia/schizoaffective disorder.,"Community data suggest frequent co-occurrence between schizophrenia/schizoaffective disorder and problem gambling. However, gambling behaviors in large samples of patients with schizophrenia/schizoaffective disorder have not been systematically examined to date. A sample of outpatient subjects (N = 337) diagnosed with either schizophrenia or schizoaffective disorder and who were treated in either the VA Connecticut Healthcare System or the Connecticut Mental Health Center was interviewed in order to examine the prevalence estimates and clinical correlates of problem and pathological gambling. Multinomial logistic regression models investigated clinically relevant measures in recreational or problem/pathological gamblers, as compared to nongamblers. Data were collected between June 2002 and November 2003. Sixty-five participants (19%) met criteria for past-year problem/pathological gambling, with 10% meeting criteria for pathological gambling. Significant correlates of problem and pathological gambling from multivariable models included greater alcohol use severity (P = .007), higher depression scores (P = .04), and more outpatient mental health care utilization (P = .03). Participants with problem/pathological gambling were more likely than recreational gamblers to gamble for excitement, gamble more frequently and heavily, and report either sports or card gambling as favorite. A substantial proportion of individuals in treatment for psychotic disorders report past-year gambling problems. Patients with co-occurring alcohol use problems and depression may be at particularly high risk. These findings suggest the need for improved prevention and treatment efforts related to problem/pathological gambling in individuals with psychotic disorders.",2009,Desai,The Journal of clinical psychiatry,10.4088/jcp.08m04359,19538900,https://doi.org/10.4088/jcp.08m04359,eng,pubmed:Comparative Study;Journal Article,pubmed,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R7576,Suzan-Lori Parks (review),"Reviewed by: Suzan-Lori Parks Jon Dietrick Deborah R. Geis . Suzan-Lori Parks. Michigan Modern Dramatists Series. Ann Arbor: University of Michigan Press, 2008. Pp. viii + 176. $18.95 (Pb). As one of two volumes inaugurating the Michigan Modern Dramatist Series (a volume on Albee was released the same year and reviewed in Modern Drama 52.2), this book is aimed at a wide audience: ""general readers and spectators, teachers and directors, and students"" (2). This is no small challenge when writing about Parks's intellectually demanding and theoretically complex body of work, but Geis succeeds admirably. The book is structured as nine chapters, the first offering biographical, historical, thematic, and stylistic context, and each subsequent chapter looking at a group of works (with the exception of the fourth chapter, which concentrates solely on Venus). The groupings are made on the basis both of the plays' subject matter and style. Arriving at a sound organizational principle for the works of a playwright who seems to delight in revisiting and revising themes and characters is a challenge in itself, and Geis's choices make good sense: the early, short plays (Betting on the Dust Commander, Pickling, and Devotees in the Garden of Love) share a [End Page 497] chapter; the two ""Lincoln plays"" (The America Play and Topdog/Underdog) are discussed together, as are the ""Red Letter"" plays In The Blood and Fucking A; Imperceptible Mutabilities in the Third Kingdom and The Death of the Last Black Man in the Whole Entire World are paired based on their race politics and use of choral structure; and the final chapter gives an overview of Parks's ambitious 365 Plays project, her literary essay, ""New Black Math,"" and her forays into the novel and the screenplay. Geis rightly notes the varied stylistic and thematic influences present in Parks's work, from ""high"" modernists such as Joyce, Stein, Beckett, Faulkner, and Woolf, whose use of repetition and experimentation with language informs Parks's work, to black modernist writers like Baldwin and Hurston, both for their exploration of African Americans' complex relation to American history and Hurston, in particular, for her attention to black American language, with its ""punning, inversions, creative substitutions, and metaphor"" (7). As for influential dramatists, Geis notes Adrienne Kennedy's use of fragmentation and ""diminishment of plot in favor of repeating and unfolding stories"" and Ntozake Shange's rejection of white, western, ""standard"" English as important to Parks's work (9). But throughout the book, the influences Geis discusses most often are Brecht and Beckett. Geis brings our attention to Parks's Brechtian tendency to distance the audience from plot and character and notes that a Parks character tends to ""represent an idea or political or historical concept"" more than a real person. As for Beckett, Geis remarks on Parks's use of silence (though, curiously, Pinter goes unmentioned), her generous use of monologue, her emphasis on characters' being paralysed, and her explorations of watching and being watched. Finally, Geis notes Parks's use of ""the Africa-based tradition of 'signifyin'"" and also jazz music's version of variation on a theme, or ""rep and rev"" (for repetition and revision) (14). The chapters on groupings of plays are connected through their discussion of a set of themes: digging, remembering/dismembering, resurrection, and interrupted legacies. Each chapter presents synopses of the plays under discussion followed by analyses of themes as well as stylistic elements and, occasionally, critical controversies. If I find any fault with the book it concerns this last element, where Geis occasionally seems a bit too eager to defend Parks's work against criticism instead of presenting critical differences in a more objective way, as would befit a general, introductory survey. For instance, Geis answers the criticism that Parks's play Venus re-victimizes Saartjie Baartman by pointing out that ""Parks is using Baartman for theatrical purposes to create what is also a larger and more allegorical story – and one that is also an apt representation of her unique dramatic style"" (77). The fact that Parks uses the historical Baartman for theatrical purposes is not an argument against the claim that the play re-victimizes Baartman. Similarly, Geis answers the...",2009,Dietrick,Modern Drama,10.1353/mdr.0.0134,,https://doi.org/10.1353/mdr.0.0134,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R7577,The effects of fantasy football participation on NFL consumption: A qualitative analysis,"The growth of fantasy sports into a multi-billion dollar industry has made it an important component of the sports industry. Sport leagues must acknowledge the impact fantasy sports have on the way its participants consume their sport. This study examines the impact of fantasy football on the consumption of NFL-related products and services using an adapted version of the attitude/behavior model developed [Fazio, R. H., Powell, M. C., & Herr, P. M. (1983). Toward a process model of the attitude–behavior relation: Accessing one's attitude upon mere observation of the attitude object. Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 44(4), 723–735]. Using qualitative methods, the data indicate that fantasy football participants utilized various media sources, specifically the Internet, television, cellular telephones, and a variety of print media, at much higher levels as a result of their interest and participation in fantasy football. Further, contrary to previous literature on fan loyalty, team and player outcomes were found to alter perceptions towards a favorite team or a fantasy team.",2009,Drayer,Sport Management Review,10.1016/j.smr.2009.02.001,,https://doi.org/10.1016/j.smr.2009.02.001,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
