R7848,Fantasy Fans?: Comparing Team Identification Among Fantasy Football Players and Non-Fantasy Football Players,"It is the contention of some members of the media that individuals who play fantasy sports, particularly fantasy football, have less allegiance to their favorite sports teams than individuals who do not play fantasy sports. The following study aimed to evaluate the validity of these claims by comparing fantasy players and non-fantasy players in three respects: 1) their identification with their favorite NFL teams; 2) their identification with their favorite fantasy football players and favorite NFL players, respectively; and 3) their identification with several points of attachment for NFL football, including the teams, players, coaches, the sport, and the level of play. This study aimed to add to the existing body of research on team identification and fantasy sports, and it represents the first academic study to compare fantasy and non-fantasy players. A combination of paper and online surveys of 280 NFL fans at Florida State University revealed that fantasy football players identify with their favorite NFL teams at higher levels than do NFL fans that do not play fantasy football, contrary the contentions of the media. Fantasy players also identified with their favorite fantasy football players at similar levels to non-fantasy players' identification with their favorite NFL players. A number of other points of attachment for NFL fans were also addressed.",2007,,DigiNole (Florida State University),,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R7849,The Regulation of Gambling: European and National Perspectives,"In November 2005 Tilburg University hosted for the first time an international Colloquium on the European and National Perspectives of the Regulation of Gambling. The results of this exciting day are reflected in this book which brings together a wide range of perspectives from the contemporary debate surrounding the regulation of gambling markets within the context of the European Union. Not only does the book encompass both ends of the spectrum of the current discussion; it also brings together the perspectives of academics, lawyers and operators. The debate on the regulation of gambling has been gathering pace following a series of judgments of the ECJ. In 2006 gambling was excluded from the proposed Services Directive, and the European Commission commenced infringement proceedings against a handful of Member States regarding restrictions on the supply of sports betting services. Given these developments and being one of the very few publications concerned with this topic, this book will serve as a timely and valuable contribution for all those interested in this emerging and at times decisive debate.",2007,,Brill | Nijhoff eBooks,10.1163/ej.9789004154599.i-266,,https://doi.org/10.1163/ej.9789004154599.i-266,en,openalex:book,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R7850,,,
R7850,The Regulation of Gambling: European and National Perspectives,"In November 2005 Tilburg University hosted for the first time an international Colloquium on the European and National Perspectives of the Regulation of Gambling. The results of this exciting day are reflected in this book which brings together a wide range of perspectives from the contemporary debate surrounding the regulation of gambling markets within the context of the European Union. Not only does the book encompass both ends of the spectrum of the current discussion; it also brings together the perspectives of academics, lawyers and operators. The debate on the regulation of gambling has been gathering pace following a series of judgments of the ECJ. In 2006 gambling was excluded from the proposed Services Directive, and the European Commission commenced infringement proceedings against a handful of Member States regarding restrictions on the supply of sports betting services. Given these developments and being one of the very few publications concerned with this topic, this book will serve as a timely and valuable contribution for all those interested in this emerging and at times decisive debate.",2007,,Martinus Nijhoff Publishers eBooks,10.1163/ej.9789004154599.i-0,,https://doi.org/10.1163/ej.9789004154599.i-0,en,openalex:book,openalex,1,False,False,False,,R7849,,,
R7851,Freier Dienstleistungsverkehr auch für Glücksspiele? Zur Rechtsprechung des EuGH zum Glücksspielbereich*,"This article by former Advocate General in the Gambelli case, Siegbert Alber, gives a comprehensive overview of previous ECJ and EFTA Court case law on the cross-frontier provision and promotion of gambling in the Internal Market in terms of its relevance for European law. The author discusses the numerous pending cases relating to gambling as well as further considering the sports betting judgment by the German Federal Constitutional Court of March 2006. As the ECJ mainly regards the issue of the freedom to provide services as being affected, the article begins by giving a basic definition of the principles involved as well as the justifications for curtailing the freedom to offer (and receive) services. Further emphasis is placed on the problems involved in the liberalisation, or rather the lack of regulation, of gambling in secondary law. The author goes on to present in chronological order the cases relating to games of chance brought before the ECJ, with particular attention being given to the Gambelli and Placanica cases. The article concludes with a critical assessment of member states’ restrictive regulation of the market in gambling and games of chance, against the background of ECJ case law, in particular in the light of emerging new regulation of the German lottery and sports betting market by the draft Treaty on games of chance between the German federal states.",2007,Alber,ERA Forum,10.1007/s12027-007-0030-7,,https://doi.org/10.1007/s12027-007-0030-7,de,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:Review,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
