R8275,The Gambling Industry and Sports Gambling: A Stake in the Game?,This article describes developments in the American gambling industry during the decade of the 1990s in light of predictions made in the 1980s. Societal and legal trends in the 1990s are discussed in terms of their relevance for the future of the gambling industry in the first decade of the 21st century. Particular attention is addressed to sports gambling and Internet sports gambling as growth areas in the gambling industry.,2001,Claussen,Journal of Sport Management,10.1123/jsm.15.4.350,,https://doi.org/10.1123/jsm.15.4.350,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R8276,managing the action: sports bookmakers as entrepreneurs,"More and more states have moved to legalize various forms of gambling as a viable way to raise substantial revenues. And while some have considered adding sports betting to their menu of games, Nevada continues to be the only state where placing a bet on sports events is legal. This is both puzzling and ironic considering the popularity of gambling and the prominence of sports. Despite the lack of concrete knowledge about illegal sports betting, two justifications for keeping it illegal have dominated the public debate. The first has to do with whether legal sports betting could successfully compete with illegal operations were it to be legalized and the second has to do with a purported link between gambling on sports and organized crime. This paper focuses on the second line of argument and examines the social, organizational, and occupational features of bookmaking. If bookmakers are conduits for organized crime, as is claimed, there should be evidence of this in the day-to-day activities of bookmaking and in the career trajectories of bookmakers. The analysis is based upon interviews with 47 sports bookmakers working in the Rust Belt region. Drawing upon what sports bookies themselves have to say about their experiences in the business and their career trajectories, there appears to be little merit to the organized crime link argument. The analysis suggests that unlike other types of deviance, the social organization of bookmaking insulates bookies from the more typical consequences associated with frequent and prolonged deviant activity, for example, getting arrested, being prosecuted, serving time in prison, and forced association with other criminals. In fact, the findings suggest that bookies are more like entrepreneurs than criminals. While a single study cannot resolve the normative question about whether sports bookmaking should be criminalized, the findings do show that the social construction of bookmaking and bookmakers found in the public debate and popular culture does not reflect the reality of sports bookmaking.",2001,Coontz,Deviant Behavior,10.1080/01639620151096092,,https://doi.org/10.1080/01639620151096092,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R8277,Gambling Against the State: The State and the Legitimation of Gambling,"Gambling has come to be legitimated in many western countries since the reintroduction of lotteries as methods of revenue generation for the state in the 1960s and 1970s. Since then, state-sanctioned gambling opportunities have expanded to include casino games, sports betting, video lottery terminals, scratch and win games and others as governments seek to increase revenues. The article analyses the state’s role in the legitimation, expansion and marketing of gambling activities, and discusses the major cultural and ideological implications of this development. It argues that this legitimation must be seen in the context of broader social and economic forces related to the deregulation of markets. The legitimation of gambling illustrates the shift in social and economic morality away from the ‘rational’ basis of capitalism in Protestant ethics, and towards the deregulation of economic attitudes. While this shift suggests a further rationalizing of economic activity and attitudes, the analysis of the broader cultural implications illustrates the tension between the rational and irrational forces in late capitalism. The article focuses on developments in western countries, but state-sanctioned gambling is expanding in non-western countries as well.",2001,Cosgrave,Current Sociology,10.1177/0011392101495002,,https://doi.org/10.1177/0011392101495002,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R8278,On Study of Comparision Between Our Country andDeveloped Countries in Sports Lottery Tickets Industry,"Developed countries almost all issue sports lottery tickets,in addition,revenue of sports lottery tickets is a most important source of sports funds in a country.The benefit and effect of sports lottery tickest is beyond the reach of lottery tickest themselves.Authors depict the origin,development,management,suprvision of laws,allocation,the use of sports lottery tickets,and enlightenment of development of sports industry,and put forward several suggestions of improving sports lottery tickets in China.",2001,Ding,,,,,en,openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8279,Emerging Technology: Biometrics: They Want Your Body,,2001,Edwards,,10.1201/1079/43270.29.1.20010701/30413.9,,https://doi.org/10.1201/1079/43270.29.1.20010701/30413.9,,s2:,s2,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8280,Update on Pending Federal Legislation Regarding Betting on College Sports,"43 THE SENATE COMMERCE COMMITTEE met May 3, 2001 for the mark-up of S. 718, which would modify the Professional and Amateur Sports Protection Act (PASPA) to ban legal college sports wagering in Nevada. An amendment to the bill offered by Sen. John Ensign (RNev.) which would have essentially killed the legislation, failed to pass in a tie vote, 10-10. Amendments to S. 718 approved by the Senate Commerce Committee include those requiring colleges and universities to report statistics about gambling-related offenses; requiring colleges and universities to monitor students’ and employees’ Internet traffic and filter Internet gambling sites; exempting office pools from the betting ban; making credit card debts owed to offshore Internet gambling sites uncollectible; and providing an expedited judicial review in the event that this bill becomes law. The amendments that were not approved include those providing due process for college student-athletes and establishing 21 as the legal gambling age nationwide. The Senate Commerce Committee held a hearing April 26, 2001 on S. 718. Expert witnesses who testified against the bill included Hall of Fame basketball coach Pete Newell; Howard Shaffer, director of the Division on Addictions at Harvard Medical School; and Danny Sheridan, a sports analyst from USA Today. S. 718 was introduced by Sens. John McCain (R-Ariz.) and Sam Brownback (R-Kan.) on April 5, 2001. A similar bill, H.R. 1110, was introduced March 20, 2001, by Reps. Lindsey Graham (R-S.C.) and Tim Roemer (D-Ind.) and has been referred to the House Judiciary Committee. Both S. 718 and H.R. 1110 would prohibit college sports wagering in Nevada, the only state where it is legal. On Feb. 14, 2001, S. 338 and a House companion, H.R. 641, were introduced as part of an effort to address the real problem of illegal gambling. H.R. 641 was introduced by Reps. Jim Gibbons (R-Nev.) and Shelley Berkley (D-Nev.), and has acquired 88 bipartisan cosponsors. It was referred to the House Judiciary Committee and the Committee on Education and the Workforce. S. 338, introduced by Sens. Ensign and Harry Reid (DNev.), also has gained strong bipartisan support with cosponsors such as Sens. Orrin Hatch (R-Utah) and Max Baucus (D-Mont.). This bill has been referred to the Senate Judiciary Committee.",2001,Fahrenkopf,Gaming Law Review,10.1089/109218801750286831,,https://doi.org/10.1089/109218801750286831,eng,openaire:Article | s2:JournalArticle;Review,s2;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8281,Expekt - A study of Expekt.com from a strategic point of view,"The discussion during the last few years about the importance of working with strategy has been in some ways neglected because of the entrance of Internet. This issue has intrigued us and so we asked ourselves the question, what has happened with the traditional strategic business models that the business community for so many years have been using? \n\nMichael Porter created one of the most traditional business strategy models during the eighties. It became increasingly interesting for us to uncover if his theories still have a value in today's Internet economy. With this background in mind, we have formulated the following problems: In general, to what extent, are the existing strategic schools pertaining to the e-business companies? Specifically, is the strategic framework by Michael Porter suitable for the Internet based e-businesses? If this is not the case, might there be a need to complement Porters theory with other approaches?\n\nWe have chosen to answer our stated questions by following the start up process of one e-commerce business in United States. The company is called Expekt.com and they are in the beginning of their expansion as a startup. Expekt.com's business idea is to provide the market with comprehensive interactive online betting services on the Internet. We have taken an active part with them during their entrance into the US market \n\nTherefore we have decided to answer our problem questions by gathering data in two ways: From our own observations through the initial process of Expekt.com into the United States, and from interviews made with each market's manager for Sweden, Norway, Denmark, Finland and Germany.\n\nThe conclusions from this thesis are integrated with the issue that Porters theory of five-forces fulfills a very important purpose for e-commerce, but yet the theory needs to be complement by other strategic approaches to make it functionally viable for Expekt.com and other e-commerce companies.",2001,Gagneus,Gothenburg University Publications Electronic Archive (Gothenburg University),,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8282,The home field advantage revisited: a search for the bias in other sports betting markets,"Abstract Recent research describes a bias in the pricing of the home field advantage in the betting market on National Football League games with a national focus (Monday night and playoff games). Home teams, especially home team underdogs, win at a rate sufficient to reject both the unbiased forecast and absence of profit opportunities versions of market efficiency. This paper extends the examination of the pricing of the home field advantage, particularly in games with a national focus, to the point spread betting market on National Basketball Association games and the fixed odds betting market on Major League Baseball games. Contrary to the football results, we find little evidence of a mispricing of the home field advantage in either regular season or playoff games in both basketball and baseball. Further, betting on all home teams is never profitable in either league. Additionally, the paper examines the latest three football seasons and finds no mispricing in games with a national focus in this out-of-sample period. We conclude that these markets are, in general, efficient.",2001,Gandar,Journal of Economics and Business,10.1016/s0148-6195(01)00040-6,,https://doi.org/10.1016/s0148-6195(01)00040-6,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
