R8289,Survey of sports lottery market in Ningbo,"The questionnaire shows that most consumers have a limited knowledge about sports lottery. Only 18.3% of consumers have a good understanding, while 75.1% of them are just to take a chance to draw a lucky ticket or enjoy themselves, and merely 7.1% of consumers know that it is a key source to raise funds for sports. On the basis of statistics, we analyze the features of sports lottery market, and provide references to strategy-making for managing such market in Ningbo and Zhejiang province.",2001,Jian-yue,journal of physical education,,,,en,openalex:article | s2:Review,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8290,ANALYSIS ON THE LEGAL RELATIONSHIP IN THE TRADE OF THE SPORT LOTTERY,"By studying theories of administrative law and civil law, phenomena in sport lottery trade and related legal relationships were analyzed from points of view of law, which combined civil law and regulations as well as the sell management methods of sport lottery. It will be helpful to solve problems in trade of sport lottery and to promote it to develop orderly and healthily.",2001,Jun,China Sports,,,,en,openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8291,The Jay Cohen Affair: Lessons in the Legality of Internet Betting,"551 JAY COHEN AND HIS PARTNERS WERE AHEAD of the curve. Just before the great Internet boom of the late 1990s, they formed one of the world’s first off-shore Internet betting operations. Setting up shop in Antigua—where Internet gaming was legal and even licensed—Mr. Cohen’s company took the name “World Sports Exchange.” It proved a huge success, attracting eager U.S. customers who placed thousands of bets on professional and college sporting events. But the millions of dollars flowing into the company also attracted the attention of the Federal Bureau of Investigations and federal prosecutors based in Manhattan. After a six month investigation, Mr. Cohen became a pioneer of different sort: he became the first Internet gaming operator to be indicted for violating federal anti-gambling laws.1 The government charged Mr. Cohen with eight counts of violating, conspiring to violate, and aiding and abetting violations of the federal Wire Act, 18 U.S.C. § 1084. The Wire Act prohibits persons “engaged in the business of betting or wagering” from using the wires to transmit across state lines: (1) bets or wagers on sporting events; (2) any material that entitles the recipient to receive money or credit as a result of a bet or wager; or (3) any information that assists in the placing of bets or wagers.2 Because Congress was concerned with the breadth of the statute’s prohibitions, however, it also contains a “safe harbor,” which authorizes the use of the interstate wires to transmit information that would assist in the placing of bets or wagers “from a State or foreign country where betting on that sporting event or contest is legal into a State or foreign country where such betting is legal.”3 The government claimed that Mr. Cohen and World Sports Exchange had violated each of the three statutory prohibitions and, furthermore, that the safe harbor did not apply. Vowing to beat the charges, Mr. Cohen (unlike some of his partners) returned to the United States to stand trial.4 He did not seriously dispute the facts; he acknowledged that World Sports Exchange operated a substantial offshore Internet betting operation. He claimed, however, that the Wire Act—which was promulgated in the 1960s—did not prohibit offshore Internet gaming. Noting that the Wire Act’s drafters had never contemplated Internet transactions, Mr. Cohen argued that the statute could not be applied to online sports betting. Alternatively, Mr. Cohen contended that the Wire Act did not prohibit World Sports Exchange’s operations because the betting transaction actually took place in Antigua, where Internet gaming was entirely legal. Since the betting operation was authorized under the laws of Antigua, Mr. Cohen reasoned, the Wire Act’s safe harbor provision protected him from prosecution.5",2001,Kramer,Gaming Law Review,10.1089/109218801753336157,,https://doi.org/10.1089/109218801753336157,eng,openaire:Article | s2:JournalArticle,s2;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8292,The Net Tax Nightmare,,2001,Lewandowski,,10.1201/1079/43270.29.1.20010701/30412.8,,https://doi.org/10.1201/1079/43270.29.1.20010701/30412.8,,s2:,s2,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8293,Articles,"Isle of Man Issues Internet GamingLicenses to MGM Mirage, Littlewoods Leisure and Sun International Betandwin.com Offers Internet Gaming at the Gas Pump Ladbrokes and British Sky Broadcasting Form Joint Venture to Bring Sports Betting to Interactive Television On the Legal Front On the Financial Front IGI Newswire Web Watch: Poker.com Already Top Internet Gaming Portal Five Minutes With . . . Andrew McHenry of Aqua Online On Serving the Internet Gaming Industry",2001,Lîebert,Internet Gaming International,10.1089/10974460152642199,,https://doi.org/10.1089/10974460152642199,en,openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,R8270,,,
R8295,Online Sports Gambling-Regulation or Prohibition?,"may conceive to be injurious to the public health, morals, or welfare, even though the state has not sought to regulate their use"" (Darby, p.114).Thus, there is case precedent that would enable Congress, where it so inclined, to pass legislation prohibiting all gambling activity.On the other hand, gambling proponents argue in favor of continuing to regulate gambling rather than enacting a federal law banning all Internet and/or sports gambling activity.The following section summarizes arguments made (a) in support of state or federal laws that would legalize but regulate these segments of the industry, and (b) in support of federal law seeking to prohibit such gambling activities.A.The case for legalization with governmental regulationOpponents of prohibition assert that an outright ban on sport and/or Internet gambling could simply encourage illegal gambling and other un-scrupulous activities.As stated by Frank Miller, the former director of the Washington State Gambling Commission and former president of the North American Gaming Regulators Association, ""[o]ne might ask how it is possible to fashion an enforcement mechanism in an environment where prohibition will not work.The answer is, prohibition promotes unlawful activity, and regulation removes it"" (Kailus, 1999(Kailus, , p.1045)).From this perspective, the government would be futilely at-tempting to prohibit an otherwise prosperous industry with great market demand.Six primary arguments support the case for legalizing online sports gambling in a regulated fashion.",2001,Miller,Journal of Legal Aspects of Sport,10.1123/jlas.11.2.99,,https://doi.org/10.1123/jlas.11.2.99,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R8296,Testing whether a gambling system is profitable,"The project considers the analysis of data obtained from a sports gambling system. The practical inferential question that arises is whether the proposed system is profitable. To address the inferential question, models are proposed that take into account that different odds are posted on different matches. The translation of the practical problem is seen to be problematic in both the classical and Bayesian framework. We find that it is not so clear as to how the practical problem should be translated into statistical hypotheses involving parameters. In addition, computational difficulties arise for null and alternative hypotheses that form only a small subset of the parameter space. The project does not completely solve the applied problem but rather serves as an initial exploration into the problem with an emphasis on inference and computation.",2001,Mok,Simon Fraser University,10.11575/prism/9788,,https://doi.org/10.11575/prism/9788,en,datacite:Other/thesis | openaire:Thesis | openalex:dissertation,openalex;datacite;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R8297,A Study on the Consumption Behavior of Sports Lottery,,2001,Niu,,,,,,s2:,s2,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8298,Internet Gaming in Costa Rica,"89 COSTA RICA HAS BECOME A MAGNET for Internet casino sites and sports book operations. The exact number of operations located in Costa Rica is difficult to determine since there is no legal framework in the country for tracking them. The first operations arrived in Costa Rica in 1996 and current estimates indicate that there may be more than one hundred companies operating within the country.1 Many of these Internet gaming companies, whether sports books or virtual casinos, erroneously believe that they are allowed to operate within the country because there are no laws in Costa Rica that prohibit their operations. Although Costa Rica has no specific legislation governing Internet gaming, it does have gaming laws in effect that address sports wagering and laws which regulate games of chance. What has made Costa Rica appealing for Internet gaming operators is the tolerant attitude of the Costa Rican government; an abundance of educated and bilingual staff; an adequate telecommunications infrastructure; comfortable living conditions; and diversity of goods and services. To date the Costa Rican government has not taken any affirmative action to enforce the existing gaming laws against Internet gaming operations. However, the international media coverage which has appeared within the last year in major publications such as the Miami Herald and Washington Times has touted Costa Rica as “the Mecca of offshore wagering” due in part to “an anything goes governing attitude”, and has brought the issue to the forefront.2 The increased media coverage combined with the uncontrolled growth of gaming operations setting up in Costa Rica has forced the Costa Rican government into the corner. It must either attempt to regulate the industry or begin enforcement of existing laws to prohibit these entities from continuing to operate within the country. In order to address the issue, the Permanent Committee on Social Affairs of the Costa Rican Legislature3 named a special committee on gaming to compile information both on landbased casino gaming legislation and on Internet gaming. Their efforts resulted in the drafting of a legislative bill titled the “Gaming Law” (Ley de Juegos).4 The bill was published for public comment in the official gazette on January 17, 2001 but was never introduced to the legislature. Instead, the special committee opted to revamp legislative bill No. 13.034",2001,Petersen,Gaming Law Review,10.1089/10921880152403538,,https://doi.org/10.1089/10921880152403538,eng,openaire:Article | s2:JournalArticle,s2;openaire,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8299,"The Celebrated Captain Barclay : Sport, Gambling and Adventure in Regency Times","On 1st June 1809 Captain Robert Barclay undertook what was then the greatest sporting feat ever attempted - to walk 1000 miles in 1000 hours for 1000 guineas. Six weeks later, exhausted and on the verge of collapsing, he completed his challenge and instantly became the most famous sporting figure of Regency times. Gentleman, pugilist, athlete, trainer and soldier - Captain Barclay's life was reckless, extravagant and thrilling. Enormous sums of money were won and lost on him and he himself earned several fortunes, though he was to die in deranged poverty. His remarkable story opens a window on the world of 18th century sport - a rough, dangerous and often corrupt world driven by money.",2001,Radford,,,,,en,openalex:book | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
