R8329,J and sports betting,"Using J for Designing andTesting Sports Betting Algorithms One of the pop, dnr activities on the Internet is to gamble.The gaming indus try, as they like to call themselves, identifies three categories: casino games, lotteries and sports betting.Casino games include the well-known games of BlackJack and Roulette.Lotter/es are just what you would assume.Sports betting is a finanri, I contract with a contingent chim between two parties, typically a punter and his bookmaker.Depending on the outcome of some event e/ther party has a monetary ,-him on the other.(This is the principle.In many cases, the practice is for the punter to 'pre-pay' his potential debt so that further payments are unidirectional; from bookmaker to punter.)The two potential claims need not be, and rarely are, exactly symmetric.Also, in spite of the term, the event in question need not be in anyway connected with sports.The use of Intemet means that g-amblers increasingly ask for 24-hour, 7-days-a-week services making the automation of bookrv-h'/ng ~lrnost a necessity.This paper describes howJ was used to descz/be the math in one area of sports betting, to develop an automatic odds generator and to test this generator using robots.The author was not and is still not an expert in J.This is part of the point of this paper.J proved to be the quickest and most elegant way to produce results, even if the user was at the other end of the specttum compared 'with, say, Roger Hui.Therefore, the code in this paper is not Hui/Whitney/Ivcrson-spectacnhr, but it was dam useful code for a commexcial project.Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for pea~oual or classroom use is granted without fee provided that copies are not made or distributed for profit or comme~ial advanlage, anti that copies bear this noda= and the full citation oa tim first page.To copy otherwise, to republish, m pest on servers, 0r m redistaibute to fists, requires prior speeifific permission and/or a fee.APLOI, 06/01, New Haven, CT USA O2001",2000,Landherr,ACM SIGAPL APL Quote Quad,10.1145/570406.570409,,https://doi.org/10.1145/570406.570409,en,crossref:journal-article | openalex:article,openalex;crossref,2,False,False,False,,R8328,,,
R8330,Introduction: A Variety of Betting Lines,"From 1976 to 1996, legalized betting in the U.S. increased from $17.3 billion to $586.5 billion. Apart from legal betting, O’Brien1 estimates that illegal sports betting could amount to $88 billion annually with $4 billion to $5 billion wagered on the Super Bowl alone.",2000,Mallios,Springer eBooks,10.1007/978-1-4757-6713-1_1,,https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4757-6713-1_1,en,openaire:Other literature type; Part of book or chapter of book | openalex:book-chapter | s2:,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
