R8332,Il gioco eterno della norma in Cordero negatore,"The essay deals with the work of one of the most complex of Italian jurists, Franco Cordero. Author of a well-known treaty of criminal procedure, as well as of a four-volume reconstruction of the life of Girolamo Savonarola, essayist, novelist and commentator of St. Paul's Letter to the Romans, Cordero - at present a professor at Rome University - has elaborated an epistemological approach to legal experience based on the concept of play, in both the theoretical and the juridical dimension. The aim of the essay is, then, to show how feeling, play/bet and law contribute to the welfare of the community of the Observants, as Cordero terms it in one of his most impressive works in the field ot legal philosophy.",2000,Nisio,,10.1400/67540,,https://doi.org/10.1400/67540,it,openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8333,Educational Reform in America for the Enhancement of a Developing Culture of Peace,"Education for every child to reach academic and emotional maturity to his/her greatest potential continues to be the key to enhancement of a safe and secure environment and a developing culture of peace. Political entities, educators and the many communities they serve all seem to be united on the importance of a sound education which allows for the present and future success of individuals, Communities and the country/state as a whole. Needed Change in Social Attitude & Focus for Quality Education: An appropriate edt,cation which brings all ahead in a way that assures academic success, emotional stability and a healthy and peaceful place in which to grow, requires a considerable commitment inhuman resources/finance. Presently, a tremendous wealth is being amassed by business conglomerates, corporations and a limited but burgeoning number of individuals who have the money to go to expensive universities, become high paid executives and/or inherit family fortunes. At the same time, an even greater percentage of the population and communities in growing economically depreciated areas are rapidly falling behind in finances and critical public community services. A basic tenant of capitalism is to compete, control the market, make or provide a better product or service and keep more money and material possessions. To the extreme, someone would eventually own it all, but the most prudent, wise and long-lasting ability to do business and have a safe environment in which to function requires sharing an adequate amount of finances, goods and services to educate the total populace toward the most positive and peaceful existence. A political democracy with a capitalistic economic system and a penchant for spending on sports, gambling, weapons and jails can afford to enhance education and a culture of peace, but the generated capital will need to be shared more (invested to a much greater extent than ever imagined) for quality schools. Political leaders know the importance of education to their constituents and they do a good job of telling people how they will expect/require schools to be better. At the same time, many of these same politicians and fortunate financial barons call for cutting taxes and axing spending on schools. For a healthy and peaceful (less violent) democracy, the nation's leaders and economic giants will need to become even more committed in sharing and/or finding ways to redistribute that wealth. Some political and financial leaders seem to show disdain for public schools, as if public education were an unnecessary drag on economic expansion. Sometimes it appears that some of these leaders believe they should have no responsibility to contribute to public education. This mentality is causing great harm to the improvement of schools. Even if there were no other reason to support schools, it is an established legal requirement to do so -- it is a civil act of good citizenship (a patriotic duty) to educate the dependent children and needy citizens of this country. In addition, there are significant numbers of and adults who go far beyond the normal definition of dependency, they have been born into the world with genetic, biological, physical, mental limitations and illnesses. Many cannot function within a formal school setting which requires a basic academic curriculum. Psychological, medical and educational facilities and opportunities will need to be expanded for this growing number of individuals in our population. Political leaders should continue to encourage the highest quality of education for all, while sufficiently funding positive educational reform. This will take much more of a share of generated capitol, not less. It is well documented that the military/industrial complex generated a great deal of capitol for weapons of war. In the name of peace and security, extremely expensive missiles to carry nuclear warheads are still being built in large numbers; the military has been well overstocked with many outdated weapons systems for years. …",2000,Packard,The International Journal of Humanities and Peace,,,,en,openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8334,Discussion on Some Issues of Sports Lottery,"The State Council of China approved issuing of the welfare lottery and the sports lottery in 1987. Ever since then there has been argues on the issue of the sports lottery. The author holds that sports lottery is something that is different from gambling; as a special economic measure for funding, it should be properly used instead of misused. The issuing of sports lottery should be supervised and a fair and impartial sports lottery market should be established by legislation.",2000,Qiong,Sport science,,,,en,openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
