R8381,Les controverses sur le jeu dans la societe britannique : le cas des paris sportifs (1890-1961),"Betting on horses became a mass activity in the 1880s. As ready-money betting had been prohibited since 1853, most punters had to resort to illegal practices. A national anti- gambling league, strongly influenced by the nonconformist conscience, was set up in 1890 to fight mis evil. The return to office of a liberal government facilitated a tightening of prohibition in 1906. This measure failed to eradicate street-betting, which was condoned and even actively supported by wide sections of the public. Subsequent campaigns focused on football betting and greyhound racing. However, they could not stop the development of football pools and greyhound tracks in me 1930s. In spite of its failure, the prohibition of street betting remained on the statute-book for more than half a century. Until i960, the leisure activities of millions of british people remained outside the law. This study examines the protracted survival of an unenforceable piece of legislation. One factor may have been the puritanical background of some (mainly) liberal and labour leaders, which led them to uphold what was increasingly regarded as a class- discriminatory text. Secondly, the reluctant recognition of the legality of football pools in the 1930s eased tensions which might have been exacerbated by an overall prohibition. Lastly, the ambivalent attitude of the police amounted to a de facto regulation. Both bookmakers and anti- gambling campaigners had a vested interest in maintaining this status quo, albeit for conflicting reasons. This work also attempts to identify the various links between the anti-gambling campaign and other moral crusades. Placed in this perspective, the 1960 betting and gaming act can be regarded as a harbinger of the so-called permissive society, although the extent of its permissivity may be questioned. The second volume consists of a collection of relevant statutes.",1997,Roudaut,,,,,fr,openalex:dissertation | openalex_non_en:dissertation | s2:,openalex;openalex_non_en;s2,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R8382,Gambling on goals: A century of football betting,,1997,Sharpe,,,,,en,openalex:book | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8383,Efficiency in gambling markets involving spread: a corrected and simplified test,"A correction is proposed for a test of market efficiency that is commonly applied to sports betting markets. Markets offering spread and totals wagers, such as football and basketball, typically employ the ‘eleven for ten’ betting rule. The previously established test statistic for profitability is positively biased, so it could lead researchers to a false rejection of the efficient markets hypothesis.",1997,Woodland,Applied Economics Letters,10.1080/758526702,,https://doi.org/10.1080/758526702,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
R8384,"Northern Light, volume 16, number 13","Professor defends needle exchange study By: William K. Wolfrum (1) UA profits by using ’83 land rates By: Melissa Eichholz (1) Campus Center survey sparks ideas, complaints from 544 By: Mary Crego (1) New NSS head shares vision By: Melissa Eichholz (1) Library need serious help By: Unknown (2) Crazy Glue By: Scott Edward (2) Letters to the editor (2) Sexist dress code challenges singer’s basic rights By: Victoria Shaver (3) Subway takes bite out of cafeteria business Campus Center income down 50 percent By: Mary Crego (5) New Board of Regents’ president sets his agenda By: Stephen Morrisette (6) AFN celebrates 30 years By: Sue Cathcart (6) Campus Scanner (7) UAA Q&A By: Mary Crego (8) One-of-a-kind program offers degree for journeymen By: Melissa Eichholz (9) News Briefs (9) University police evacuate Business Education Building By: Northern Light Staff (10) Dorm life through older students’ eyes By: Rachel Grenier (11) Yupik dancers keep tradition alive By: Sue Cathcart (12-13) Top Ten the EDGE KRUA 88.1FM (14) Campus Calendar (14) Calendar Highlights (14) O’Donnel’s newest is ‘boring and mundane’ By: Michael Marcella (14) Catfish Haven fries up good greasy food By: Tracey Vorst (15) ‘A Piece of My Heart’ offers new look at Vietnam By: Gene Merrill (15) Week focuses on Japanese and their customs By: Grayling Martin (16) Alcohol issues addressed By: Northern Light Staff (17) Rubes By: Leigh Rubin (18) Jim’s Journal By: Jim (18) At The Zu By: Ron Ruelle (18) Dave By: David Miller (18) Chaos By; Brian Shuster (18) The Swan Factory By: Cuyler Black (18) Answers to Crossword (18) Crossword (19) Astrological Guru (19) Classified (20) Stay safe in the snow: winter sports tips By: Chris Curtis (21) America leads the way in butt-kicking sports By: William K. Wolfrum (23) Hockey team loses both games 3-1 By: Joe West (24) V-Ball splits series By: William K. Wolfrum (24) Dillon looks to leave UAA By: William K. Wolfrum (24)",1996,,ScholarWorks - UA (University of Alaska System),,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8385,Football Betting and the Efficient Market Hypothesis,"Three betting rules which had been nonrandomly profitable in both their initial application to the 1969–74 National Football League seasons and their replication during the years of 1975–81 were applied to all NFL games played between September 1984 and January 1994. One rule proved to be nonrandom and profitable for a third consecutive trial—a feat suggesting that bettors may be able to “beat the bookie” and, more broadly, that prices in competitive markets may not discount all available information in swift fashion.",1996,Badarinathi,The American Economist,10.1177/056943459604000207,,https://doi.org/10.1177/056943459604000207,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R8386,"Holyfield and Tyson - When Magic Play at Arena, City Loses Money","A week after the Boston College story broke the gamblers took a bath, not on college football but on the godfather of sports gambling, boxing. Evander Holyfield's upset of the century over Mike Tyson was a disaster for those who set the odds and cover the bets. With odds running 25-1 to open and dropping to 5-1 just before the fight, bookmakers lost several million dollars. Major money was placed on Holyfield in the last few days, at least some of it coming from the Holyfield camp itself, and the smart money on the favorite never surfaced as betting closed.",1996,Crepeau,Journal of International Crisis and Risk Communication Research,,,,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8387,A generalized model for testing the home and favorite team advantage in point spread markets,"Abstract Most sports teams play as either the favorite or the underdog and either at home or away. The failure to recognize the symmetric and interdependent relations between these characteristics has led previous researchers to use potentially biased methods to test for rationality and efficiency in football betting markets and thus to reach inappropriate conclusions. We develop a more general specification, which also incorporates ‘pick-em’ games and games played on neutral sites, and find little or no evidence against market efficiency in the NFL and college betting markets for regular season games. We do, however, uncover evidence of biased betting lines for Superbowls.",1996,Dare,Journal of Financial Economics,10.1016/0304-405x(95)00848-9,,https://doi.org/10.1016/0304-405x(95)00848-9,en,openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2;openaire,3,False,False,False,,,,,
