R8458,Gambling : views from the social sciences,"CONTENTS: Preface - William R. Eadington and Jame H. Frey; Legal Regulation of Gambling Since 1950 - G. Robert Blackey; The Casino Gambling Industry: A Study of Political Economy - William R. Eadington; Police Regulation of Illegal Gambling: Frustrations of Symbolic Enforcement - Peter Reuter; A Zoning Merit Model for Casino Gambling - Jerome H. Skolnick; Casino Gambling: Issues of Development and Redevelopment - Joseph Rubinstein; Significant Influences on the United Kingdom Casino Industry Since 1960 - Nigel Kent-Lemon; Management Policy and Practices in Modern Casino Operations - Dean M. Macomber; The Social and Economic Impact of State Lotteries - H. Roy Kaplan; Gambling: A Sociological Review - James H. Frey; Gambling as Play - James F. Smith and Vicki Abt; The Psychology as Gambling - Igor Kusyszyn; Pathological Gambling: Roots, Phases, and Treatment - Henry R. Lesieur and Robert L. Custer; The Professional Gambler: Fame, Fortune, and Failure - David M. Hayano; Sports Betting - George Ignatin. This is a special issue on gambling that appears in Volume 474, July 1984 of the Annals of The American Academy of Political and Social Science. Editors for this issue are James H. Frey and William R. Eadington.",1984,Frey,Sage Publications,10.11575/prism/9599,,https://doi.org/10.11575/prism/9599,en,datacite:Other/book | openaire:Book,datacite;openaire,2,False,True,False,,,,,
R8459,Gambling and College Sports: Views of Coaches and Athletic Directors,In light of the pervasiveness of sport betting this paper summarizes and presents data from a national study conducted by the Commission on the Review of the National Policy Toward Gambling. Data were collected from 214 coaches and 127 athletic directors from a sample of NCAA schools. Responses to Questionnaire items provided information on the perceived impact of betting and publicized point spreads on sport in general and on the behavior of coaches and players in particular. The phenomenon of sport betting is discussed in light of these attitudes.,1984,Frey,Sociology of Sport Journal,10.1123/ssj.1.1.36,,https://doi.org/10.1123/ssj.1.1.36,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:Review,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,,,,
R8460,Sports Betting,"This article discusses the type of people who wager on sports events and the reasons they gamble. It describes different types of bets on sports events, how betting lines are set, and the odds and probabilities involved. It examines in detail the difficulties in estimating the amounts of money wagered illegally in this country, and, after describing the nature of bookmaking operations, it concludes that total dollar amounts wagered with bookmakers are usually underestimated while the net income of bookmakers is typically overestimated.",1984,IGNATIN,The Annals of the American Academy of Political and Social Science,10.1177/0002716284474001015,,https://doi.org/10.1177/0002716284474001015,en,crossref:journal-article | openaire:Article | openalex:article | s2:JournalArticle,openalex;s2;crossref;openaire,4,False,False,False,,R1920;R1921;R2337;R2504;R3198;R3239;R4675,,,
R8461,The Profitability of Wagering on NFL Games,A reappraisal of previously suggested strategies for betting on football games using results of the period 1975–1981 casts considerable doubt on the profitability of relying on the past history of games for guidance in placing bets.,1984,Tryfos,Management Science,10.1287/mnsc.30.1.123,,https://doi.org/10.1287/mnsc.30.1.123,,crossref:journal-article,crossref,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8462,"Don't abandon synfuels, coal scientist pleads","The development of synthetic fuel is a case of ""damned if you do and damned if you don't,"" according to Irving Wender, the noted University of Pittsburgh coal scientist. From an economic standpoint, Wender says, if you do, you're playing ""bet your company."" But if you don't, you're playing ""bet your country."" In a plenary lecture to the 1983 International Conference on Coal Science, sponsored by the International Energy Agency and held in Pittsburgh, Wender observed that the current ""oil glut"" has brought about a sharp decline in coal consumption and has had a disastrous impact on funding for coal R&D. However, Wender warns, the present apparent abundance of oil and natural gas is at best a reprieve. He cites a study by the American Institute of Chemical Engineers: ""There is a highly probable need for commercial-scale synthetic fuel production in the U.S. by about the year 2000, with genuine possibilities for its abrupt ...",1983,,Chemical & Engineering News,10.1021/cen-v061n034.p007a,,https://doi.org/10.1021/cen-v061n034.p007a,en,openalex:other,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8463,Teleshopping,,1983,,Computer Communications,10.1016/0140-3664(83)90068-3,,https://doi.org/10.1016/0140-3664(83)90068-3,en,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
R8464,Football betting and football pools: a mathematical and statistical analysis.,,1983,Freedman,,,,,en,openalex:article | s2:,openalex;s2,2,False,False,False,,,,,
R8465,Biased evaluation and persistence in gambling.,"Perhaps the most striking aspect of gambling behavior is that people continue to gamble despite persistent failure. One reason for this persistence may be that gamblers evaluate outcomes in a biased manner. Specifically, gamblers may tend to accept wins at face value but explain away or discount losses. Experiment 1 tested this hypothesis by recording subjects' explanations of the outcomes of their bets on professional football games. The results supported the hypothesis: Subjects spent more time explaining their losses than their wins. A content analysis of these explanations revealed that subjects tended to discount their losses but ""bolster"" their wins Finally, subjects remembered their losses better during a recall test 3 weeks later. Experiments 2 and 3 extended this analysis by demonstrating that a manipulation of the salience or existence of a critical ""fluke"" play in a sporting event had a greater impact on the subsequent expectations of those who had bet on the losing team than of those who had bet on the winning team. Both the implications and the possible mechanisms underlying these biases are discussed.",1983,Gilovich,Journal of personality and social psychology,10.1037//0022-3514.44.6.1110,6875803,https://doi.org/10.1037//0022-3514.44.6.1110,eng,"pubmed:Journal Article;Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't;Research Support, U.S. Gov't, Non-P.H.S.",pubmed,1,False,False,False,,,,,
