R8505,Page 1,"SEVENTEENTH YEAR. RIGBY, JEFFERSON COUNTY. IDAHO, THURSDAY APRIL 10, 1919. NUMBER 17. MOODY BOUND OVER TO DISTRICT COURT. The South Fork River bridge com mission, consisting of t h e s t a t e engi neer. J. L. Jones, of Independ representing Madison county and J. W dinger, representing Jefferson, met this week and made an investi gation of affairs preparatory to com mencing action to hold back the flood waters with the $ 30,000 appropriat ed by the recent legislature. Examinations along the river were made by the commission from Heiae along the South Fork to points below the bridge at Lorenzo. In a talk with Mr. Clinger Wednesday he stated that the river had com menced to rise at the present time and that much water from the river was finding its way to the Great Feeder canal through the Lowder Slough. It is through this source that much damage was done last year and again threatens with even more danger this season should we have even normal high water. The engineer must 09 trvey of the work to be done, and then the same must be advertised for 3 0 and let by contract. Thus it will be seen that it will be some time be fore work can be commenced on the fight against the river, and thi lay in the face of the rising v. of the turbulent old Snake is a pro blem that is causing much alarm to the commission. Mr. Clinger stated that making endeavors to obtain em erg-money fr until the state aid is avail that work i: Mr. Clinger received a wire this morning from th. stating that Mr. Blossom would ac company the commission up the South Fork. Mr. Blossom at; here this morning and im mediately left for the upper . Boise, C. S. Moody, forme; j u t a n t general of Idaho, must stand trial in district court on the charge earing falsely to a voucher for traveling expenses. He was bound over to the district court Thursday afternoon by Judge Rust. His bond of $ 1500 put up at the time of his , was continued. The preliminary examination of Mr. Moody was brief. Two witnesses examined by the state. Capt. Frank A. McCall, United States dis bursing officer for Idaho, and E. Q. Gallett, state auditor. The ft testified to Moody presenting to him a claim for traveling expenses dur ing the month of De. In the sum of $ 176.63 to cover the expenses of a t r i p to Washington. The claim, he testified, was filed and paid 26, 1918. E. G. Gallett, s t a t e auditor, asked it he had on file an expense account of C. S. Moody for traveling during Dec. 15) 18, to which he repli ed that he had and prodm testified the account v rlgin-al one filed. E. S. Delana, at tor: the s t a t e , announced the state rest-that testimoir who with J. T. Pence and Herman J. Tay lor, represented the defendant, made a motion for dismissal on the gl t h a t the s t a t e had failed to pr any evidence in sn plaint and had failed to show that an offense had been against the s t a t e , or that any ot had been commit t Judge Rust denied the motio: iony was introduced by the de-int. TELLS OF VISIT TO T Commercial Club Meeting. The regular meeting of the com mercial club was held at the cluh rooms last Friday night, with a The proposition of a laundry talked of. lence Introd to the entire satisfaction nt. that the town ia too small to support such an enterprise at the present time. The mai Yellowstone highway was then brought before the meeting, and a report of the re-nieetlng at Idaho Falls was given by Geo. Hill. It was shown that if we are to secure state aid the Yellowstone highway must follow along the original line surveyed along the track north to Lorenzo. A strong resolution voicing the unani mous sentiment of the Rigby Club of Commerce as favoring the origi nal lines of the Highway, that the county may secure the s t a t e aid drafted and passed by the meeting, addressed to the county commission* erg. Mr. Cuddy, representing the Ida ho Falls Post, Peace and Prosperity edition, that will be issued next month, was then introduced. He ex plained the features of the publica tion and advantages to the local club participating in the issue. After discussion the club indorsed the move and Mr. Cuddy secured support the first of the week among the local business men. An invitation was extended to the Teton and Ririe Commercial clubs to visit Rigby Wednesday and to at tend a general gathering that night. The local club at t h e present time displays real pep and an i n t e r e s t tak en by the members, which is a very pleasing feature, and one that we hope will continue, as it insures ad ditional success for our l i t t le city. Circle Entertained. Members of the Rigby Study Circle were pleasantly entertained at the home of Mrs. Helen Lowder on last Wednesday evening. The occasion was an "" April Fool Party"" and was immensely enjoyed by those who participated, each member wearing a quaint costume to re-present a comic character. Musical numbers and recitations also added greatly^ to the merriment of the oc casion. The regular business and literary session was held. An elaborate luncheon was serv ed, assisting the hostess in serving were : Mrs. Ruth McCoy and Mrs. Ada Sage. The next meeting of the club will be held at the home of Mrs. J- S. Mason. Meeting Friday Night. An importa: lal cluh has been called for Fri day evening, April 11th., at S p. m. time, Gibson of the reclamation project coma will i or the purpose rfecting county organization. All who a r e it: are urged to attend as this Is of vital Interest U) the great pro; Dubois project. Big One Monday Night. inlay night at. the Orpheum a good wrestling card is in Btore for the fans, in fact it will probably be the best exhibition of strength and skill ever displayed here, when Ira Dern, of Salt Lake, meets the strong man. Tony Bernardl, of Pitts burg, PH. These boys recently wrestled here In a 16 minute match, Bet: agreeing to throw Ira in that time, and he failed to do so. Therefore a finish match was next in order and the fans will have the pleasure ot viewing the outcome Monday night. Bungalow Ranch Sold. The Bungalow ranch, owned by E. W. Hall, has been sold into smaller tracts, 7 60 acres being sold in the following tracts: to Henry Price of Rigby, 160 t\\ Rigby, 80 acres; Mr. Bone, of Utah. 80 acres- Mr. Jacobsen. of Bear Lake county; 120 acres; T. J. C bell of Rigby, 80 acres. and Mr Farnsworth, of Chester, Idaho, 240"" Mrs. A. L. Paul, county chairman of the clothes drive which has just been completed desires to thank all of the chairmen throughout the county for their loyal support. The drive was a complete success. Jeffer son county's allotment being 2 tons which was secured and a substantial surplus. Everyone who so kindly contributed are also due many t h a n k s . The clothes came in fine condition and the shipment when completed was a great credit to its contributors. Riley Dixon arrived home Wed nesday morning, having been dis charged from the army. He recently landed with the first units of the Ninty- First Division of which he was a member, having been in the first draft contingent to leave Jefferson county, In September 1917. Clarence K. Saunders was arrival last week from Camp Lewis, having received his discharge. He returned to Idaho Falls. Tuesday to resume U former work Sgt. Jack "" Zip"" Barrett was an arrival here Saturday morning for a visit with his sister, Mrs. H. A Anderson. '' Zip"" is now stationed at Camp Kearney. Since leaving Rigby he has spent three years in the navy and upon the declaration of war up on Germany by the United States he entered the army. He will spend a t h i r t y day furlough here. Marriage Licenses. Marriage licenses were issued Wednesday by County Clerk Zina H. Poole to Clyde H. Jessup, of Rigby and Nettie Hansen, of Idaho Falls; also to Nephl Hansen of Grant, and Miss Clesta Thornock* of Rigby. J. H. Steele and E. W. Hall of the Universal Elt. together with a number of the sab have just returned from Dayton. Ohio, where they spent a week attending the third annual Delco- Light vention.