R8506,Page 7,"Thursday, November 13, 1958. THE RIGBY STAR, RIGBY. IDAHO GARFIELD A program "" Praise Ye The Lord""' will be presented Tuesday eve ning at 8 p. m. Everyone ia Invited to attend. This is a combined festi val of speech, drama and muaic, to be presented by the Muli Mr. and Mrs. Joe Hershey, and family, formerly of Ammon, have purchased the home of Mr. and Mrs. Clyde Johnson. Mr, and Mrs. John son have purchased a home in Rigby. Mr. and Mrs. Wayne Barrows and family of Pocatello were visitors of the week end at the George Mugles ton home. The Primary organization will pre sent a program Sunday afternoon at 2 p. m. demonstrating what Primary ia doing. All parents are urged to attend. Friends and relatives of Mrs. Elma Baird are happy to leam she has been released from the hospital and Is convalescing at her home- Congratulations to Alonzo Cook in his new appointment as East Rigby Stake Sunday School superintendent. The Relief Society is planning a bazaar to be held the first week in December. Members are urged to complete their Bazaar articles in the near future. Members of the Stake Missionary Presidency were speakers at Sacra ment meeting Sunday evening. Pres ident Melnot Holbrook, Rendon Lov ell and Clay Graham. Miss Loa Joy Crystal played a piano solo. Exploring Family Trees By JOYCE DICE OWEN "" Remember your lineage' was the jonition given to young men at the ceremony making them knights- A person's ancestry is listed in all biographies in county histories., Who's Who,"" and obituaries. Pride of name goes back to Biblical days as the Bible demonstrates. Most all the important people mentioned in the e have their genealogy listed showing their blood line. We should always remember the inheritance left us by the ancestors who gave their lives and for- | any descendant of the Irving Brant uaaaaaaaaaauaaaaaaaaamnnnnnnnmaamnnnnnnnnnmMMMMnnnnnnmMm family, who were early settlers of I Congratulations to Mr. and Mrs. Hi_ i_ o_. p-_ e__,__ ___ I__ d___ a__ h_ _ o___._ ____ I__ r __ v! ing Brant laid out the town 0. era Ferry, Idaho."" Monteview/ Terreton And Mud Lake tunes t h a t we might live in a better world and e n j o y freedom. T h e colonist's courage and tlie pioneer's f e a r- Icssness all con tributed to the life we lead to- Hh^ HAVE ANOTHER BANDAGE, MA A lot of women work their fingers to the bone for their families and what do they get$ Sore fingers. Now's the time to order personalized TELEPHONE LISTINGS y o u can be listed in the new telephone directory even if . he telephone you use is under, someone else's name. Here's how. Just call your telephone business office and order a personalized listing. Whether you live in a club, hotel, with your family, or share on apartment, we'll be happy to list you and the num ber of the telephone you use. But h u r r y . The new telephone directory goes fo press soon, ( Want any new or changed listings or directory advertis ing? Call the business office today!) day. These are our forefathers and all should strive to be worthy pos terity. "" Some men by ancestry are only the shadow of a mighty name."" Lucan. A new genealogy entitled "" Descend ants of Jonathan Murray of East Guilford, Conn."" is now available through W. M. Murry, 104 S. Wal nut St., Kewane, 111. This book in cludes the Nathan Bradley line. Queries All readers are invited to send their problems pertaining to geneal ogy to The Rigby Star, in care of Joyce Owen. These problems will be printed free of charge in this column. From Dorothy S. Clark, Box 72, Halfway, Ore. "" Whi, Dewey Clark, born 30 Oct. 1806- 09 in N. Y.; migrated to Oregon 1848 and settled in Yamhill Co. He married a widow, Phebe O. Jaokson in 1848; she had three children, Louisa, Engeline, ( both bom in Iowa), and . Nancy, bom in Oregon. They had one son in 1853, Chase A. Clark, Any information would be welcome."" From Mrs. E. A. Reynolds, 617 N. 12th St., Boise Idaho "" Wish to locate A. Guy Christy, born in Winona, Mo. in 1890. He was last known to be an Akron, Ohio."" From Mrs. Joe Spence, Rt. 1, Nampa, Idaho "" Need the parentage of Richard Isaac Ledbetter of No. Carolina, oout 1890."" From Mrs. L. J. Prescott, 224 10th Ave. N., Nampa, Idaho Will correspond with persons working on these aumames; Neibour, Rosenbaum, and Tippets."" From J. L. Braden, 1705 Boise Ave., Boise I would like to correspond with all persons connected with the sur name Canada. Mary Canada, born in 1772 in Fairview, Buncombe Co., N. C., married William Whitaker. Her lather waa James Canada."" From Thelma A. Vincent, 2765 Ac acia Road, Walnut Creek, Calif. "" Were the parents of Charles Ben jamin Combs, born either in Boise or Pocatello on "" July 16. 1896, James and Minnie Hailing Combs? They may have been married in Pocatello An old leather case and the mother of a"" good friend furnished solutions to two queries in the paat month, these two Boiae women share the same surname but are not related. Mrs. Bertha Davis, of 2036 Harrison Blvd., haa been seeking information for the paat three years on Alfred f Anderson, who was aaid to have settled near Boiae in 1895. She de cided to place a query in this column. "" I never dreamed of an answer af so many discouragements,"" she said later. The day after her query appeared Mra. Lillie Roberts called, saying "" I am the daughter of Afred Anderson."" The following Saturday Mia. Roberts and her husband visit ed Mrs. Davis to discuss their fami lies. Airs. Roberta told of an old leather case left by her father, Alfred An derson, containing an old Virginia land grant and other personal pa pers. Another Boise woman, Mrs. Mary Jeffries called with informa-uon on the mother of Alfred Ander son. Mrs. Carrie Despian of Meridian, Idaho, mailed a query last week con taining the answer to Mrs. Adella Davis query on Georg Parkhurst, bom 1592, in England. Mrs. Davis had been searching 28 yeara for this material, and was surprised to find the letter was written by the mother of her mission companion. Mrs. Des-pain has been doing genealogy for almost 50 years. Letting others know your problem is sometimes the quickest way to the solution, remember every blank space on your pedigree chart is a query-lhe Idaho Genealogical Society has just launched a new project. They are collecting obituaries and filing them alphabetically by surname and placing them in the State Historical Library, where ail Idahoans will have access to them. Anyone having obitu-ries to donate to this project should send them to the Idaho Genealogical Society, 610 Parkway Dr., Boiae. Eastern Idaho genealogists would have access to this file by mail or when in Boise you are welcome to visit from 1 to 5 every day but Mon day. Queries Problems in genealogical research will be published in these coumns. Ralph Bean on a new baby boy. Mrs. Mary Martin, Mrs. M. B. Speelmon, and Mrs. Stella Hodgson are visiting in Ekalaka"", Mont, with friends and relatives. Mr, and Mrs. Raymond Osborne * vere weekend visitors at Bountiful, Utah with their son Kenneth and famliy. Congratulations to Mr. and Mrs. Karl Shuldberg on their new baby girl, bom Sunday night. Mi- and Mrs. Tommy Mitchell had the misfortune to have their home and all their belongings burn on el ection day. Everyone in the com munity is so thankful that the child ren were able to get out of the house and no one was hurt . Anyone wish ing to donate can contact the Relief Society. Max Osborne was a dinner guest last Saturday evening at the home of Dr. Peterson in Idaho Falls.. Stockholders of the Owsley Canal Company ask that the stockholders remember meeting next Monday af ternoon. Paul Osborne of Sugar City, who has been home on leave from the Air Force spent the weekend at the home of Mr. and Mrs. Ray Osborne. Don't forget the Relief Society Bazaar and supper. Henry Dunn has gone to Lake Mead to spend the winter. Miss Cyrie Jemmett of Idaho Falls has been visiting the last week with her grandparents, Mr. and Mrs, Bill Jemmett. Mr. and Mrs. Harvey Peterson spent last weekend at the home of Mr. and Mrs. Dennis Green, Harvey and Dennis spent Saturday up Crook ed Creek deer hunting. Mr. and Mrs. Ross Gibbs of Hay-ward, Calif., stopped to visit at the home of Mr. and Mrs. Merrill Jem mett on Saturday of last week. West Jefferson Panthers Speak By Barbara Shurtliff In a very thrilling and disappoint ing game for West Jefferson Friday, October 31 the Mackay Miners beat the Weat Jeferaon Panthers 28- 7. Although the Panthers played very good football the Miners played bet ter. After m u ch hard work and many hours of wondering and worrying t h e Yearbook Carni- ^ ueen, Linda Simmons w a s crowned. Her at tendants w e re L i n d a Wads- ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ ^ worth & Kathie Bybee. The queen and her attendants were chosen on the amount of tick ets each sold to the Carnival Dance, and the amount of cakes and other goodies donated by their friends. Edith Sanders, a very excited queen was crowned queen of the Homecoming Dance Friday, October 24. The queens attendants were Mary Alice Sant and Pam Harrell. The queens were chosen by votes the girl that had the most money in a bottle was queen. Hawker Servics November 11 Funeral services were conducted Tuesday, November 11 from the Buck Chapel, Idaho Falls, in memory of Irvin L. Hawker, 59, farmer in the Menan- Roberts area who died at the home of his sister in Idaho Falls Fri day afternoon. * Max C. Thomas, member of the Menan 2nd ward bishopric, conductel the service. Reared and educated at Menan, he was bom Aug. 22, 1899, aon of John S. and Lois Jane Firkins Hawker. He waa united in marriage to Etta Bell, St. Anthony, and she died in November of 1949. There are two , Blaine and Keith Hawker, of Roberts; and a daughter, Mrs. Le nore Squires of Rigby, who survive. Family prayer was offered by How ard Hawker. Invocation was ex pressed by Bishop Leo S. Waters of the Menan ward. The prelude and postlude music was played by Mrs. Leonard Mickelson. The se",,,Digital Collections at BYU,,,,,openalex:article,openalex,1,False,False,False,,,,,
